{"timestamp":"2026-10-07T07:56:05.367Z","ordinal":10754,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_094cad5b47a7c03f016ac5fb144a6087d2b4cb29be3db4dfa9","status":"completed","call_id":"call_2a49b8a50be043e990dbbed4f4306278","name":"exec","input":"const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:\"sed -n '1,260p' docs/agent-collaboration.md; sed -n '1,260p' .codex/skills/ai-create-knowledge/references/INDEXING-WORKFLOW.md; sed -n '1,260p' .codex/skills/ai-create-knowledge/references/DATABASE-CRUD.md; sed -n '1,260p' .codex/skills/ai-code-intelligence/references/AI-CODE-INTELLIGENCE.md; command -v node; command -v python; command -v ast-grep; command -v serena; python -c 'import tree_sitter_language_pack; print(\\\"tree-sitter available\\\")'\",\"workdir\":\"/home/kamaz/www/strateg-projects/strateg-export-products\",\"yield_time_ms\":10000,\"max_output_tokens\":13000});\ntext(r.output);\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a1155c-86e8-7eb1-9147-ce9f28491fa5","create_time":1791359764.3112693}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-07T07:56:05.430Z","ordinal":10755,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a0a02b-47ee-7913-b18b-d4d4b0be8b60","turn_id":"01a1155c-86e8-7eb1-9147-ce9f28491fa5","session_id":"01a0a02b-47ee-7913-b18b-d4d4b0be8b60","root_turn_id":"01a1155c-86e8-7eb1-9147-ce9f28491fa5","response_id":"resp_094cad5b47a7c03f016ac5fb12fce887d281f01b7b3027ba34","usage":{"input_tokens":47766,"cached_input_tokens":44800,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":309,"reasoning_output_tokens":105,"total_tokens":48075},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":263675,"cached_input_tokens":219648,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":1444,"reasoning_output_tokens":379,"total_tokens":265119},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":151740797,"cached_input_tokens":147333248,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":617336,"reasoning_output_tokens":319213,"total_tokens":152358133}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-07T07:56:05.450Z","ordinal":10756,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a1155c-cbca-7442-9289-28c17934f8bd","call_id":"call_2a49b8a50be043e990dbbed4f4306278","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.1 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"[← Users](user-resource.md) · [Back to README](../README.md) · [Code Intelligence →](ai-code-intelligence.md)\n\n# Agent Collaboration\n\nThis guide defines how the primary AI agent delegates complete, independently executable work to subagents in this repository.\n\nA subagent owns its assigned scope from investigation through implementation and verification unless the task brief explicitly limits it to research, review, testing, or another narrower responsibility.\n\nThe primary agent remains responsible for orchestration, integration, conflict resolution, final verification, and communication with the user.\n\n## Subagent orchestration policy\n\nUse subagents conservatively.\n\nSubagents exist to reduce context pressure, parallelize genuinely independent work, and provide specialized investigation or implementation. They must not be created merely because additional concurrency is available.\n\n### Hard limits\n\n- A root task may use **at most 4 subagents** unless the user explicitly requests a different limit.\n- The limit of 4 is a ceiling, not a target.\n- Do not spawn subagents for simple or straightforward tasks.\n- Prefer solving the task directly when delegation provides no clear benefit.\n- Prefer 1 subagent when one independent investigation or implementation scope is sufficient.\n- Prefer 2-4 subagents only when there are genuinely independent workstreams.\n- Never spawn additional subagents merely to increase parallelism.\n- Never spawn duplicate subagents investigating or implementing substantially the same scope.\n- Do not replace a completed subagent with another unless new independent work has actually been identified.\n- Prefer continuing an existing suitable subagent instead of spawning another one for closely related follow-up work.\n\nRecommended default:\n\n```text\nSimple task:\nMain Agent only\n\nModerate task:\nMain Agent\n└── 1 Subagent if useful\n\nComplex task:\nMain Agent\n├── Subagent 1\n└── Subagent 2\n\nLarge task with independent workstreams:\nMain Agent\n├── Subagent 1\n├── Subagent 2\n├── Subagent 3\n└── Subagent 4\n```\n\nNever create four subagents simply because four slots are available.\n\n### Nested delegation\n\nSubagents MUST NOT spawn other subagents.\n\nOnly the root/main agent may create subagents.\n\nEvery subagent task brief must explicitly include:\n\n> Do not spawn additional subagents. Complete the assigned task yourself.\n\nThe intended hierarchy is always:\n\n```text\nMain Agent\n├── Subagent\n├── Subagent\n├── Subagent\n└── Subagent\n```\n\nThe following is forbidden:\n\n```text\nMain Agent\n└── Subagent\n    └── Subagent\n        └── Subagent\n```\n\n### Before spawning\n\nBefore creating a subagent, the primary agent must verify that:\n\n1. The work is sufficiently independent to delegate.\n2. The scope can be clearly bounded.\n3. No existing subagent is already responsible for substantially the same work.\n4. Delegation provides meaningful benefit over completing the work directly.\n5. The expected benefit justifies the additional token, tool, and execution cost.\n6. The work can be performed without unsafe write conflicts with other active agents.\n\nIf these conditions are not satisfied, do not spawn a new subagent.\n\n### Token and cost discipline\n\nSubagents consume independent model context and tool execution.\n\nThe primary agent must therefore:\n\n- prefer the smallest useful number of subagents;\n- avoid assigning the same background investigation to multiple agents;\n- provide focused starting context rather than asking every subagent to rediscover the entire project;\n- give each subagent explicit files, symbols, services, or questions when known;\n- avoid broad prompts such as \"analyze the whole project\" unless that breadth is genuinely required;\n- request concise completion reports instead of unnecessary raw logs;\n- avoid spawning a new agent for a task that can be completed by extending an existing agent's assignment; and\n- stop delegating when sufficient evidence already exists to complete the task safely.\n\n### Lifecycle\n\nWhen a subagent completes its assigned task:\n\n1. collect its completion report;\n2. inspect the resulting changes or evidence;\n3. close/release the agent when supported;\n4. do not automatically create a replacement agent;\n5. integrate the result with findings from other agents;\n6. resolve contradictions before implementation or final reporting.\n\nA completed subagent's conclusion is evidence, not automatically accepted truth.\n\n### Default strategy\n\nFor a complex development task, a reasonable maximum decomposition is:\n\n```text\nMain Agent\n├── Subagent 1: primary investigation or backend scope\n├── Subagent 2: independent subsystem or database scope\n├── Subagent 3: verification, UI, testing, or review if necessary\n└── Subagent 4: independent implementation scope if necessary\n```\n\nThese roles are examples only.\n\nDo not automatically assign all four roles. The primary agent should create only the agents required by the actual task.\n\nA subagent may already own investigation, implementation, and verification for its bounded scope. A separate implementation subagent is unnecessary when an existing subagent can safely complete the work end to end.\n\n## Roles and authority\n\n| Role | Responsibility |\n|---|---|\n| Primary agent | Understands the user's goal, performs initial reconnaissance, divides work into bounded tasks, controls subagent count, assigns ownership, integrates and reviews results, resolves conflicts, performs final verification, reports to the user, and is the only agent allowed to run `git push`. |\n| Subagent | Completes its assigned work within the defined scope, uses available project tools, may modify authorized files or services, verifies its result, and reports evidence and remaining risks to the primary agent. |\n\nSubagents are full contributors, not inherently read-only reviewers.\n\nHowever, their authority is limited by the task brief.\n\nA subagent may be assigned as:\n\n```text\nresearch-only\nimplementation\nreview\ntesting\ndatabase\nUI/runtime investigation\ninfrastructure\ndocumentation\n```\n\nIf the task brief states that the assignment is read-only or research-only, the subagent must not modify project files, database state, services, Git state, or external systems.\n\nWithin their assignment and the current session's available tools and permissions, subagents may:\n\n- analyze requirements;\n- read source code and documentation;\n- search with `rg`, Serena/LSP, ast-grep, Tree-sitter, and the project index;\n- collect evidence from PostgreSQL and configured services while respecting project scope and read/write authorization;\n- inspect Docker services, PHP-FPM, NGINX, application servers, queues, and other runtime components when tools and permissions are available;\n- use host and container utilities, package managers, Node.js, Python, PHP, and other installed tools;\n- work with the shared `ai-code-intelligence` project to index this application, update project-scoped knowledge, or create embeddings when explicitly included in the assignment and authorized;\n- inspect browser/UI/runtime state using the configured browser tooling;\n- create, edit, or delete files within the assigned write scope; and\n- use Git to inspect changes and, when explicitly included in the assignment, stage and commit them.\n\n**Subagents must never run `git push`, in any form.**\n\nThis includes:\n\n```text\ngit push\ngit push --force\ngit push --force-with-lease\npushing tags\npushing branches\npushing refs\npushing mirrors\n```\n\nOnly the primary agent may push after reviewing the integrated result and confirming that the user's request authorizes the operation.\n\nDelegation does not expand a session's access.\n\nAgents must use only the tools and permissions actually available to them and must not bypass a denied command, sandbox restriction, authentication boundary, or approval gate.\n\nIf a task requires unavailable access or explicit approval, the subagent must report the blocker and a safe next step to the primary agent.\n\n## Delegation protocol\n\nEach subagent task brief must state:\n\n1. The desired outcome.\n2. The exact owned scope.\n3. Whether the assignment is read-only or permits writes.\n4. The files, modules, symbols, services, or operational responsibility involved when known.\n5. Relevant evidence and constraints already discovered by the primary agent.\n6. Required verification.\n7. Expected completion-report format.\n8. Whether Git staging or a commit is part of the assignment.\n9. The instruction that the subagent must not spawn other subagents.\n10. The instruction that `git push` is forbidden.\n\nA good task brief should resemble:\n\n```text\nGoal:\nDetermine why duplicate payment records can be created.\n\nScope:\n- PaymentService\n- payment repository/model\n- payment-related migrations\n- transaction and idempotency behavior\n\nAuthorization:\nResearch only. Do not modify files or database state.\n\nUse:\n- Serena/LSP\n- rg\n- PostgreSQL project index\n- Git history where useful\n\nDo not:\n- spawn subagents\n- modify files\n- modify database state\n- commit\n- push\n\nReturn:\n- status\n- findings\n- evidence\n- relevant files/symbols\n- verification performed\n- risks\n- unresolved questions\n```\n\n## Concurrent work and write scopes\n\nConcurrent subagents should have disjoint write scopes.\n\n# Indexing, Tools, Embeddings, and Retrieval\n\nThe shared repository is `/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/`. Start with its `AGENTS.md` and `README.md`, then read `docs/getting-started.md`, `docs/architecture.md`, `docs/database.md`, `docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md`, `docs/agent-workflow.md`, and `docs/security.md` as relevant. The DDL in `docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/` is authoritative.\n\n## Tool Placement and Commands\n\nCheck tools on the host. The local PostgreSQL/Node container does not need to contain parsers or language servers.\n\n```bash\ncommand -v ast-grep\nast-grep --version\nserena --version\n/home/kamaz/.venv/bin/python -c 'from tree_sitter_language_pack import get_parser; print(get_parser(\"php\"))'\n```\n\nUse the Python interpreter that can import `tree_sitter_language_pack`; verify rather than assuming `python3` points to it. Serena can install a PHP language server during initial indexing. If DNS/package registry access fails, treat it as a tool setup issue and retry when available.\n\nExamples:\n\n```bash\nast-grep run --lang php --pattern 'class $NAME extends $BASE' httpdocs\nserena --help\nserena project index\n```\n\nConfirm Serena's installed CLI syntax with `serena --help`. Test Tree-sitter on one representative file before a large traversal. If parsing crashes, stop the batch, document the fallback, and do not call line-based chunks syntax-aware. Record whether symbol relations were actually built.\n\n## Connect to PostgreSQL Through Docker\n\nRun from the shared repository root:\n\n```bash\ndocker compose -f .docker/dev/docker-compose.yml exec -T \\\n  ai-code-intelligence-postgres-postgresql sh -lc \\\n  'psql -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -P pager=off -U \"$POSTGRES_SIMPLE_USER\" -d \"$POSTGRES_DB\"'\n```\n\nTo execute a SQL file or script, send it through stdin to `psql`; keep environment values private. Resolve projects by slug:\n\n```sql\nSELECT id, name, slug\nFROM aic_projects\nWHERE slug = 'strateg-export-products'\n  AND active = TRUE;\n```\n\nThen scope all project-owned queries by that `id`. Do not use unscoped selects or infer a project ID from another task.\n\n## Indexing Sequence\n\n1. Inventory the repository and exclude private config, local environment files, secrets, vendor/dependency code, runtime/generated artifacts, and unapproved content. Record exclusions.\n2. Parse source with the best available syntax-aware tool. Use Serena/LSP for symbols/references, ast-grep for AST-shaped matches, Tree-sitter for parse boundaries, and `rg` for exact searches. Do not fabricate graph relations from approximate matches.\n3. Upsert one `aic_files` row per project/path, then store `aic_symbols`, verified `aic_symbol_relations`, `aic_documents`, evidence-backed `aic_knowledge_items`, and `aic_chunks` with the same `project_id`.\n4. Keep chunk source constraints valid: code chunks reference a file; document chunks reference a document; knowledge chunks reference a knowledge item. Use non-empty content and a SHA-256 `content_hash`.\n5. Record an `aic_indexing_runs` row with an honest `running` status and update its stats/metadata as stages finish.\n\nFor incremental indexing, compare file/chunk hashes. Re-parse and re-embed only changed content or when the parser/chunking/model version changes. Persist deletion state using the schema/indexing process; avoid deleting shared project data as a shortcut.\n\n## Paid Embeddings\n\nThe current utility accepts:\n\n```text\n<api-key> <result-file> <model> <text> [text...]\n```\n\nUse the requested/registered model and its exact dimensions. In this setup the OpenAI script is `/app/embedding.js`; the results directory is mounted persistently from:\n\n```text\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/app/embedding-results/\n```\n\nContainer output path: `/app/embedding-results/`. Use stable per-batch filenames. Before calling the API, compare files and chunk hashes so completed inputs are not charged twice. Keep a manifest mapping each output file to project ID, model ID, chunk IDs, content hashes, and dimensions.\n\nThe current script logs its key and input texts to stdout. Redirect stdout for the call and do not print it in captured output. Example for one approved, sanitized chunk:\n\n```bash\ndocker compose -f /home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/.docker/dev/docker-compose.yml \\\n  exec -T ai-code-intelligence-postgres-postgresql sh -lc '\n    node /app/embedding.js \"$OPENAI_API_KEY\" \\\n      /app/embedding-results/strateg-export-products/{year}/{month}/strateg-export-products-batch-0000.json \\\n      text-embedding-3-small \"sanitized source chunk\" >/dev/null\n  '\n```\n\nDo not save the only copy under `/tmp` inside the container. If DB insertion fails, reuse the persistent JSON and manifest; call the API again only for changed chunks or a changed model.\n\n## Persisting Vectors and HNSW Indexes\n\nRegister each provider/model identity once in `aic_embedding_models`; do not mutate its dimensions while vectors using that identity exist. Store embeddings in `aic_embeddings` with `project_id`, `chunk_id`, `embedding_model_id`, exact `dimensions`, vector value, and matching `source_content_hash`.\n\nCommit vector rows first. Then call the project/model-specific helper as the owner of `aic_embeddings`:\n\n```sql\nSELECT aic_create_project_embedding_hnsw_index(\n    '<project-uuid>'::uuid,\n    '<embedding-model-uuid>'::uuid\n);\n```\n\n`POSTGRES_SIMPLE_USER` may have insert grants but cannot necessarily create the index. If you see `must be owner of table aic_embeddings`, do not mark the run complete. Check whether vector inserts rolled back, reconnect as the authorized table owner, create the index separately, verify it in `pg_indexes`, then complete the run. Never expose database role passwords.\n\n## Search Vectors\n\nGenerate a query embedding with the same model and dimensions, then pass it to the exact or HNSW helper. SQL template for a parameterized client:\n\n```sql\nSELECT chunk_id, source_type, source_reference, title, distance, content\nFROM aic_search_chunks_vector_hnsw(\n    :project_id,\n    :embedding_model_id,\n    :query_embedding::vector,\n    20\n);\n```\n\nUse `aic_search_chunks_vector_exact(...)` when an approximate index is unavailable or exact results are required. For lexical search use `aic_search_chunks_text(...)`; for uncertain symbol names use `aic_search_symbols_fuzzy(...)`; graph traversal uses `aic_symbol_relations_walk(...)`. Confirm signatures in the current SQL. Combine candidates as needed, then open original files and verify the result. Similarity alone does not establish runtime behavior.\n\n## Index Record: Strateg Export Products\n \nThe current `strateg-export-products` index uses `text-embedding-3-small` (1536 dimensions), with 1,182 PHP files, 4,221 approximate symbol rows, 3,896 chunks/embeddings, four documents and four knowledge items, plus a project/model HNSW index. Serena indexed 964 PHP files. Tree-sitter batch node-field extraction segfaulted; saved code chunks therefore use 80-line boundaries and symbol names from declaration lines. `aic_symbol_relations` was not populated. The 325 output JSON files are in the persistent results directory. No batch-to-chunk manifest was produced for this completed run; do not regenerate its embeddings. Future indexes must produce a manifest before embedding.# Project-Scoped Database CRUD and Knowledge\n\nThe current schema uses `aic_*` table names. Confirm exact columns and constraints in `docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql` before applying these templates. Replace placeholders in a parameterized SQL client; examples are templates, not a command to mutate data without the user's request.\n\n## CRUD Guardrails\n\n- Resolve the project by `aic_projects.slug` first. Use the resulting `project_id` on every project-owned row and predicate.\n- Use explicit column lists, parameterized values, `RETURNING`, and transactions for related changes.\n- Preview `UPDATE`/`DELETE` rows with the same `WHERE` clause. Require the user's explicit request for destructive changes.\n- `aic_projects` owns files, symbols, relations, documents, knowledge, chunks, embeddings, and runs through cascading foreign keys. Prefer `active = FALSE` for a project that should stop being indexed; hard delete only after impact review and explicit approval.\n- The indexer owns derived tables (`aic_files`, `aic_symbols`, `aic_symbol_relations`, `aic_documents`, `aic_chunks`, `aic_embeddings`). Use the index pipeline for their normal CRUD so hashes, foreign keys, chunks, relations, and vectors stay consistent.\n\n## Resolve and Read a Project\n\n```sql\nSELECT id, name, slug, root_path, active\nFROM aic_projects\nWHERE slug = :project_slug;\n```\n\nCreate a project only when the slug is absent:\n\n```sql\nINSERT INTO aic_projects (name, slug, root_path, repository_url, default_branch)\nVALUES (:name, :slug, :root_path, :repository_url, :default_branch)\nRETURNING id, slug;\n```\n\nRead by stable identity, then scope all other operations by the returned UUID. For routine retirement, prefer:\n\n```sql\nUPDATE aic_projects\nSET active = FALSE\nWHERE id = :project_id\n  AND slug = :project_slug\nRETURNING id, slug, active;\n```\n\nHard-deleting a project cascades to all its records. Before such a delete, enumerate dependent counts and confirm the exact project with the user. Never issue an unqualified delete.\n\n## CRUD for Evidence-Backed Knowledge\n\nThe `aic_knowledge_items` table stores durable facts. Required fields are `project_id`, `kind`, `title`, and non-empty `content`. Valid statuses are `draft`, `active`, `superseded`, and `archived`; `confidence` must be between 0 and 1. Always set a source reference when evidence exists.\n\n### Create\n\n```sql\nINSERT INTO aic_knowledge_items (\n    project_id, kind, title, content, source_reference, status, confidence\n)\nVALUES (\n    :project_id, :kind, :title, :content, :source_reference, 'active', :confidence\n)\nRETURNING id, project_id, kind, title, status;\n```\n\nOnly store conclusions supported by source code, checked-in docs/DDL, tests, or verified runtime behavior. Keep temporary hypotheses out of durable knowledge.\n\n### Read\n\n```sql\nSELECT id, kind, title, content, source_reference, status, confidence,\n       valid_from, valid_until, metadata\nFROM aic_knowledge_items\nWHERE project_id = :project_id\n  AND status = 'active'\nORDER BY kind, title;\n```\n\n### Update / supersede\n\nFor a correction to an existing fact, inspect it first by both `project_id` and `id`. Preserve history where useful:\n\n```sql\nBEGIN;\n\nUPDATE aic_knowledge_items\nSET status = 'superseded'\nWHERE project_id = :project_id\n  AND id = :old_knowledge_id\nRETURNING id, status;\n\nINSERT INTO aic_knowledge_items (\n    project_id, kind, title, content, source_reference, status, confidence\n)\nVALUES (\n    :project_id, :kind, :title, :new_content, :source_reference, 'active', :confidence\n)\nRETURNING id;\n\nCOMMIT;\n```\n\nIf the same fact is merely being clarified and history is unnecessary, update the exact row with `WHERE project_id = :project_id AND id = :knowledge_id`, and use `RETURNING`.\n\n### Archive or delete\n\nPrefer archive/supersede over physical deletion:\n\n```sql\nUPDATE aic_knowledge_items\nSET status = 'archived'\nWHERE project_id = :project_id\n  AND id = :knowledge_id\nRETURNING id, status;\n```\n\nDelete only a confirmed duplicate or erroneous item when explicitly requested. A knowledge row can own chunks, and deleting it cascades to those chunks and their embeddings:\n\n```sql\nDELETE FROM aic_knowledge_items\nWHERE project_id = :project_id\n  AND id = :knowledge_id\nRETURNING id, title;\n```\n\nNever omit either predicate. Preview the target row and dependent chunk/vector counts before deletion.\n\n## Make Knowledge Searchable\n\nAn `aic_knowledge_items` row alone is not an FTS/vector chunk. To include a fact in retrieval, create a linked chunk with `source_type = 'knowledge'`, the same `project_id`, `knowledge_item_id`, a non-empty content string and its SHA-256 hash. The knowledge-source constraint requires file/document references to be `NULL`.\n\n```sql\nINSERT INTO aic_chunks (\n    project_id, source_type, knowledge_item_id, chunk_type,\n    sequence_number, content, content_hash\n)\nVALUES (\n    :project_id, 'knowledge', :knowledge_id, 'knowledge', 0,\n    :chunk_content, :sha256_of_chunk_content\n)\nRETURNING id;\n```\n\nGenerate an embedding for the chunk with the registered model and persist it to `aic_embeddings` through the indexing pipeline. If knowledge content changes, update/rebuild its linked chunk and regenerate only when the chunk hash changes. Keep `source_content_hash` equal to the indexed chunk's current `content_hash`.\n\n## CRUD for Other Tables\n\n| Table                                                                                               | Recommended owner     | Safe approach                                                                                                               |\n|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| `aic_projects`                                                                                      | Orchestrator          | Create/read/update by slug or ID; deactivate by default; hard delete is cascading and explicit only.                        |\n| `aic_knowledge_items`                                                                               | Agent/orchestrator    | Direct CRUD with project ID, evidence, status, and confidence. Maintain linked chunks when searchable content changes.      |\n| `aic_embedding_models`                                                                              | Administrator/indexer | Register provider/model once; do not change dimensions of an identity that already has vectors.                             |\n| `aic_indexing_runs`                                                                                 | Indexer               | Create `running`, then `completed`, `failed`, or `cancelled`; record truthful counts in stats.                              |\n| `aic_files`, `aic_symbols`, `aic_symbol_relations`, `aic_documents`, `aic_chunks`, `aic_embeddings` | Indexer               | Use the indexing pipeline; direct CRUD can break project-scoped FKs, content hashes, retrieval chunks, or vector freshness. |\n\nFor ordinary reads, use explicit columns and project filters, for example:\n\n```sql\nSELECT id, path, language, content_hash, is_deleted\nFROM aic_files\nWHERE project_id = :project_id\n  AND is_deleted = FALSE\nORDER BY path;\n```\n\nThe schema enforces project-scoped composite relationships. Never disable or bypass those constraints to force a write.\n\n## Embedding Upsert Template\n\nNormally let the indexing pipeline perform this upsert. If explicitly acting as the indexer, verify that the chunk belongs to the same project and that the source hash matches before writing:\n\n```sql\nINSERT INTO aic_embeddings (\n    project_id, chunk_id, embedding_model_id, dimensions, embedding, source_content_hash\n)\nVALUES (\n    :project_id, :chunk_id, :embedding_model_id, :dimensions,\n    :embedding::vector, :source_content_hash\n)\nON CONFLICT (chunk_id, embedding_model_id)\nDO UPDATE SET\n    dimensions = EXCLUDED.dimensions,\n    embedding = EXCLUDED.embedding,\n    source_content_hash = EXCLUDED.source_content_hash,\n    updated_at = NOW()\nWHERE aic_embeddings.project_id = EXCLUDED.project_id\nRETURNING id, chunk_id, dimensions;\n```\n\nThen commit vectors. Create the project/model HNSW index separately as the table owner and mark the indexing run completed only after verification. Never regenerate vectors just to recover from an index privilege error.# AI Code Intelligence: Tool and Database Reference\n\nUse this reference with the shared repository documentation. The DDL is authoritative; function names below reflect the current local AI Code Intelligence schema and must be checked against its SQL before use.\nPath to project root:\n\n```text\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/\n```\n\n## Documentation Map\n\n| Need                                              | Read                             |\n|---------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------|\n| Start database and call the embedding script      | `docs/getting-started.md`        |\n| Understand responsibilities and limits            | `docs/architecture.md`           |\n| Inspect tables, keys, and project ownership       | `docs/database.md`               |\n| Select tools, create indexes, and retrieve chunks | `docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md` |\n| Investigate and verify code changes               | `docs/agent-workflow.md`         |\n| Handle code, credentials, and shared data safely  | `docs/security.md`               |\n| Check the MCP/tool integration surface            | `docs/integrations.md`           |\n\nRead the shared `AGENTS.md` for repository commands and conventions. Read the relevant pages rather than dumping the entire documentation tree into context. Check `docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql`, `004_views_and_search_functions.sql`, and `005_vector_index_helpers.sql` before relying on copied SQL.\n\n## Tool Invocation and Boundaries\n\n### Host-side tools\n\nCheck the host, not only the database container:\n\n```bash\ncommand -v ast-grep\nast-grep --version\nserena --version\n/home/kamaz/.venv/bin/python -c 'from tree_sitter_language_pack import get_parser; print(get_parser(\"php\"))'\n```\n\nThe final Python command is illustrative for this workstation; use the Python interpreter that actually has `tree_sitter_language_pack` installed. Avoid printing environment files or secret values while checking setup.\n\n`ast-grep` structural search example:\n\n```bash\nast-grep run --lang php --pattern 'class $NAME extends $BASE' httpdocs\n```\n\nUse a small, known PHP file to validate Tree-sitter before a batch. Retain numeric node offsets and source text while traversing; some Python language-pack builds may segfault when node fields are accessed repeatedly. If it fails, stop the batch, document the parser/version and fallback, and do not claim AST-accurate chunks.\n\nFor Serena, inspect the installed CLI first (`serena --help`). Run project indexing from the repository root using the command exposed by that version (in this setup, `serena project index`). A PHP language server may be downloaded on first run; DNS failure to a package registry does not mean the source project is invalid. Retry only after network availability is restored.\n\n### Database container\n\nThe local service is named `ai-code-intelligence-postgres-postgresql`. It provides PostgreSQL and Node.js. Use the Compose file at:\n\n```text\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/.docker/dev/docker-compose.yml\n```\n\nUse `POSTGRES_SIMPLE_USER` for ordinary project data reads/writes where it has grants. Do not print database environment values. Index creation on `aic_embeddings` may require the table owner (`POSTGRES_USER`). Do not assume an unprivileged insert role can create an index.\n\n## Paid Embeddings and Safe Retries\n\nThe OpenAI embedding provider is called by `/app/embedding.js`. The current script accepts:\n\n```text\n<api-key> <result-file> <model> <text> [text...]\n```\n\nFor this setup, use model `text-embedding-3-small` and save result files at the persistent host path:\n\n```text\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/app/embedding-results/\n```\n\nThe same directory is visible in the container as `/app/embedding-results/`. Use deterministic batch filenames and a manifest containing each batch filename, chunk IDs, content hashes, model, and dimensions. Persist every successful batch before moving to the next one.\n\nThe script currently prints the API key and submitted texts to stdout after a successful call. Redirect stdout away from terminals and captured logs; do not echo result vectors unnecessarily. Never submit secrets or unapproved project content to the embedding API. Check existing files, chunk hashes, and DB state before retrying to avoid duplicate charges.\n\nSafe command shape for one approved, sanitized chunk:\n\n```bash\ndocker compose -f /home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/.docker/dev/docker-compose.yml \\\n  exec -T ai-code-intelligence-postgres-postgresql sh -lc '\n    node /app/embedding.js \"$OPENAI_API_KEY\" \\\n      /app/embedding-results/project-batch-0000.json \\\n      text-embedding-3-small \"sanitized source chunk\" >/dev/null\n  '\n```\n\nFor many chunks, call the script in bounded batches and store a mapping manifest. Do not build an enormous shell command or save the only output under `/tmp` in the container. If a later database insert or index creation fails, reuse the saved vectors; regenerate only when the source chunk hash or embedding model changed.\n\n## Database Write Sequence\n\n1. Resolve the project with `aic_projects.slug` and retain its `id`.\n2. Register the provider/model/dimensions in `aic_embedding_models`.\n3. Persist `aic_files`, `aic_symbols`, `aic_documents`, `aic_knowledge_items`, and `aic_chunks`, all scoped to the same project ID.\n4. Persist embedding results outside the container's ephemeral storage.\n5. Insert `aic_embeddings` in a transaction and commit.\n6. As the owner of `aic_embeddings`, call:\n\n   ```sql\n   SELECT aic_create_project_embedding_hnsw_index(:project_id, :embedding_model_id);\n   ```\n\n7. Verify the index in `pg_indexes`, compare row counts, then set the associated `aic_indexing_runs.status` to `completed` and record counts/fallbacks in `stats` or `metadata`.\n\nDo not put vector insertion and index creation in one transaction when they use different database roles. A `must be owner of table aic_embeddings` error rolls back inserts in that transaction. Check first; resume from durable embedding files and only the incomplete DB stage.\n\n## Search Correctly\n\nAlways resolve a project first. Examples of current helpers (verify exact signatures in `004_views_and_search_functions.sql` and `005_vector_index_helpers.sql`):\n\n- `aic_search_chunks_text(project_id, query, limit)` — lexical/full-text candidates.\n- `aic_search_chunks_vector_exact(project_id, model_id, query_vector, limit)` — exact vector comparison.\n- `aic_search_chunks_vector_hnsw(project_id, model_id, query_vector, limit)` — approximate search using a matching project/model HNSW index.\n- `aic_search_symbols_fuzzy(project_id, query, limit)` — fuzzy symbol lookup.\n\nThe query vector must use the registered model and expected dimensions (1536 for `text-embedding-3-small`). Pass both `project_id` and `embedding_model_id`; never search across projects by accident. Combine exact/symbol, FTS, and vector candidates where useful, then open source files to verify. Vector similarity is candidate discovery, not proof of a call path or runtime behavior./home/kamaz/.nvm/versions/node/v25.6.1/bin/node\n/home/kamaz/.local/bin/ast-grep\n/home/kamaz/.local/bin/serena\n/bin/bash: line 1: python: command not found\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a1155c-86e8-7eb1-9147-ce9f28491fa5","create_time":1791359765.4501474}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
