{"timestamp":"2026-10-07T07:56:10.854Z","ordinal":10764,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a0a02b-47ee-7913-b18b-d4d4b0be8b60","turn_id":"01a1155c-86e8-7eb1-9147-ce9f28491fa5","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-2f8d33d1-3b57-4dd6-be9b-1ee6c9758e74","process_id":"3542","command":["/bin/bash","-lc","sed -n '1,240p' AGENTS.md; sed -n '1,260p' README.md; sed -n '1,240p' docs/getting-started.md; sed -n '1,260p' docs/architecture.md; sed -n '1,260p' docs/database.md; sed -n '1,280p' docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md; sed -n '1,260p' docs/agent-workflow.md; sed -n '1,240p' docs/security.md; sed -n '1,260p' docs/integrations.md; ls -la /home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/app/embedding-results/; command -v python3; /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/python -c 'import tree_sitter_language_pack; print(\"tree-sitter available\")'"],"cwd":"file:///home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"sed -n '1,240p' AGENTS.md; sed -n '1,260p' README.md; sed -n '1,240p' docs/getting-started.md; sed -n '1,260p' docs/architecture.md; sed -n '1,260p' docs/database.md; sed -n '1,280p' docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md; sed -n '1,260p' docs/agent-workflow.md; sed -n '1,240p' docs/security.md; sed -n '1,260p' docs/integrations.md; ls -la /home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/app/embedding-results/; command -v python3; /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/python -c 'import tree_sitter_language_pack; print(\"tree-sitter available\")'"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"Failed to create stream fd: Operation not permitted\nFailed to create stream fd: Operation not permitted\nFailed to create stream fd: Operation not permitted\n# Repository Guidelines\n\n## Project Structure & Module Organization\n\nThis repository packages a shared PostgreSQL database for code intelligence. The root `docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/` contains ordered SQL initialization scripts: extensions, schema, indexes, search functions, and vector helpers. `.docker/dev/` contains the local development image, PostgreSQL configuration, Compose file, and environment files. `ai-code-intelligence/` is the standalone deployment package with its own Dockerfile, Compose file, SQL initialization scripts, example environment file, and README. `app/` contains the small Node.js embedding utility and provider implementation. The root README documents the schema and recommended agent workflow.\n\n## Build, Test, and Development Commands\n\n- `make up-dev` starts the development PostgreSQL service.\n- `make down-dev` stops the development service; it does not remove stored volumes.\n- `make build-dev` builds the development image.\n- `make restart-dev` restarts the service; `make rebuild-dev` rebuilds and starts it.\n- `npm install --prefix app` installs the embedding utility dependencies.\n- `node app/embedding.js <API_KEY> text-embedding-3-small \"text to embed\"` generates embeddings.\n\nThere is no configured automated test or lint command. For database changes, review SQL ordering and validate initialization against a disposable local database. Do not use destructive volume/database cleanup commands against the shared development data.\n\n## Coding Style & Naming Conventions\n\nKeep SQL readable, use uppercase SQL keywords, explicit column lists, and descriptive `snake_case` identifiers. Preserve the numeric prefix and dependency order of initialization scripts (for example, `003_indexes.sql`). Use two spaces for JSON and JavaScript indentation, ES modules, and descriptive camelCase JavaScript names. Keep deployment examples aligned between the root and standalone package when both represent the same behavior.\n\n## Testing Guidelines\n\nNo dedicated test suite is present. Verify SQL changes by applying initialization on a clean local database and checking the affected tables, indexes, or functions. For changes to `app/`, run the relevant command manually and avoid committing generated credentials or real API keys.\n\n## Commit & Pull Request Guidelines\n\nGit history is unavailable in this checkout, so no established commit pattern could be confirmed. Use concise imperative commit subjects, such as `Add vector search helper index`. Pull requests should explain the schema or runtime impact, list validation performed, call out environment/configuration changes, and include logs or screenshots only when they clarify behavior.\n\n## Security & Configuration\n\nNever commit `.docker/dev/env/` secrets or expose their values in documentation and logs. Use `.env.example` for safe placeholders. Scope all project-specific database queries by `project_id` or resolve a project by slug first. Persist paid embedding results under `app/embedding-results/`; inspect and reuse saved batches before retries. The current embedding script echoes its API key and input texts to stdout, so suppress stdout. Create project HNSW indexes as the table owner, after committing vector rows.\n\n## Documentation\n\n| Document               | Path                             | Description                                   |\n|------------------------|----------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|\n| README                 | `README.md`                      | Project landing page and guide index          |\n| Getting Started        | `docs/getting-started.md`        | Local database startup and embedding setup    |\n| Architecture           | `docs/architecture.md`           | Retrieval model and system responsibilities   |\n| Database Reference     | `docs/database.md`               | Schema, ownership, and SQL examples           |\n| Retrieval and Indexing | `docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md` | Search, indexing, and code intelligence tools |\n| Agent Workflow         | `docs/agent-workflow.md`         | Investigation and verification practices      |\n| Integrations           | `docs/integrations.md`           | MCP surface and upstream references           |\n| Security               | `docs/security.md`               | Local database and data protection guidance   |\n\n## AI Context Files\n\n| File                          | Purpose                                    |\n|-------------------------------|--------------------------------------------|\n| `.ai-factory/DESCRIPTION.md`  | Project scope and technology summary       |\n| `.ai-factory/ARCHITECTURE.md` | Repository architecture guidelines         |\n| `.ai-factory/rules/base.md`   | Detected naming and structural conventions |# AI Code Intelligence Database\n\n> Local, multi-project PostgreSQL storage for AI-assisted software development.\n\nThis repository combines PostgreSQL full-text search, fuzzy lookup, pgvector retrieval, and relational code relationships. It includes a Docker-based development database and a small Node.js embedding utility. Always scope project data by `project_id` or resolve the project by slug.\n\n## Quick Start\n\nRequires Docker Compose. Start the local database:\n\n```bash\nmake up-dev\n```\n\nCheck the service state:\n\n```bash\ndocker compose -f .docker/dev/docker-compose.yml ps\n```\n\nThe embedding utility uses the OpenAI API and `text-embedding-3-small`; see [Getting Started](docs/getting-started.md) for safe setup details.\n\n## Capabilities\n\n- PostgreSQL 18 with pgvector, full-text search, and pg_trgm.\n- Project, file, symbol, relationship, document, chunk, embedding, and indexing-run storage.\n- Ordered database initialization scripts for local development and standalone deployment.\n- A hybrid retrieval workflow that combines exact, structural, semantic, and graph search.\n\n## Documentation\n\n| Guide                                                    | Description                                          |\n|----------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------|\n| [Getting Started](docs/getting-started.md)               | Local database startup and embedding setup           |\n| [Architecture](docs/architecture.md)                     | Retrieval model and system responsibilities          |\n| [Database Reference](docs/database.md)                   | Schema, ownership, and SQL examples                  |\n| [Retrieval and Indexing](docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md) | Search, indexing, and code intelligence tools        |\n| [Agent Workflow](docs/agent-workflow.md)                 | Investigation, verification, and knowledge practices |\n| [Integrations](docs/integrations.md)                     | MCP surface and upstream references                  |\n| [Security](docs/security.md)                             | Local database and data protection guidance          |[Back to README](../README.md) · [Architecture and Retrieval Model →](./architecture.md)\n\n# Getting Started\n\n## Safe embedding workflow\n\n- Development environment files live in `.docker/dev/env/`. Never copy their values into docs, chat, logs, or command output.\n- The database container runs PostgreSQL and Node.js. Tools such as `/home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep`, Tree-sitter Python bindings, and Serena/LSP may be installed on the host; check the host environment and the exact Python interpreter instead of assuming they must exist in Docker.\n- Keep paid embedding results in the host directory `app/embedding-results/project-name/{year}/{month}/`, mounted in the container at `/app/embedding-results/project-name/{year}/{month}/`. Use stable batch filenames, check which files already exist before retrying, and resume from completed batches. Do not store paid results only in `/tmp` or another ephemeral container path.\n- The current `app/embedding.js` prints its API key and input texts to standard output. Suppress stdout for the embedding call; do not share captured logs. Only index sanitized, non-secret source content.\n- Use `text-embedding-3-small` for the existing model configuration. Keep batches bounded and persist each result file before inserting vectors into PostgreSQL.\n- The restricted `$POSTGRES_SIMPLE_USER` can write project records but may not own `aic_embeddings`. Create project/model HNSW indexes as `$POSTGRES_USER` (table owner). Commit vector rows before index creation and mark the indexing run complete only after the index is verified.\n- Never run `docker system prune`, `docker volume rm`, or `DROP DATABASE` against the shared development data. Before retrying, inspect project counts, run status, and saved embedding files; do not delete a project as a shortcut.\n\n### Run SQL through the PostgreSQL container\n\nFrom the ai-code-intelligence repository root, execute SQL with `psql` inside the database container:\n\n```bash\ndocker compose -f .docker/dev/docker-compose.yml exec -T \\\n  ai-code-intelligence-postgres-postgresql sh -lc \\\n  'psql -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -P pager=off -U \"$POSTGRES_SIMPLE_USER\" -d \"$POSTGRES_DB\" -c \"SELECT slug, id FROM aic_projects ORDER BY slug;\"'\n```\n\nUse `$POSTGRES_SIMPLE_USER` for normal project-scoped reads and writes. Resolve the target project's ID by slug, then include `project_id` in queries against its files, chunks, and embeddings. For vector and HNSW queries, see [Retrieval and Indexing](./retrieval-and-indexing.md).\n\nExample for one sanitized batch:\n\n```bash\ndocker compose -f /home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/.docker/dev/docker-compose.yml \\\n  exec -T ai-code-intelligence-postgres-postgresql sh -lc '\n    node /app/embedding.js \"$OPENAI_API_KEY\" \\\n      /app/embedding-results/project-name/{year}/{month}/example-batch-0000.json \\\n      text-embedding-3-small \"class OrderService {}\" >/dev/null\n  '\n```\n\nThe embedding file persists on the host at `/app/embedding-results/project-name/{year}/{month}/example-batch-0000.json`.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Database startup\n\n### Files about DB tables\n\nDB tables schemas:\ndocker-entrypoint-initdb.d:\n - 001_extensions.sql\n - 002_schema.sql\n - 003_indexes.sql\n - 004_views_and_search_functions.sql\n - 005_vector_index_helpers.sql\n\n---\n\n## See Also\n\n- [Architecture and Retrieval Model](./architecture.md)\n- [Database Reference](./database.md)\n- [Retrieval and Indexing](./retrieval-and-indexing.md)[← Getting Started](./getting-started.md) · [Back to README](../README.md) · [Database Reference →](./database.md)\n\n# Architecture and Retrieval Model\n\nThis page explains the code-intelligence retrieval model. For repository layers, SQL initialization order, deployment boundaries, and the embedding utility, see the [repository architecture guide](../.ai-factory/ARCHITECTURE.md).\n\n## 1. Architecture\n\n```text\nRepositories\n    |\n    +----------------------+----------------------+\n    |                      |                      |\n    v                      v                      v\nTree-sitter            Serena / LSP           Git\nAST + chunks           symbols/types          history\n    |                      |                      |\n    +----------------------+----------+-----------+\n                                       |\n                                       v\n                              Index / Context Layer\n                                       |\n               +-----------------------+-----------------------+\n               |                       |                       |\n               v                       v                       v\n          PostgreSQL FTS          pgvector RAG          relational graph\n               |                       |                       |\n               +-----------------------+-----------------------+\n                                       |\n                                       v\n                                  AI Agent\n                                       |\n                                       v\n                                  AI Factory\n                           plan -> implement -> verify\n```\n\n### Tool responsibilities\n\n| Tool                                                                     | Primary responsibility                                                                                                                     | Do not use it as                                     |\n|--------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------|\n| AI Factory                                                               | Project context, rules, spec-driven workflow, planning, implementation, verification                                                       | A code parser or search engine                       |\n| Serena                                                                   | Symbol-level semantic navigation, references, editing, refactoring, LSP-backed code intelligence                                           | A replacement for semantic RAG                       |\n| ripgrep (`rg`)                                                           | Fast exact text/regex search                                                                                                               | Structural AST matching                              |\n| /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep (`sg` / `/home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep`) | AST-aware structural search, linting, safe rewrite/codemod patterns                                                                        | Semantic business-language search                    |\n| Tree-sitter                                                              | Parsing, syntax trees, language-aware chunk boundaries, extraction input                                                                   | A complete type system or runtime call graph         |\n| PostgreSQL + pgvector                                                    | Persistent multi-project RAG, metadata, graph data, FTS, fuzzy lookup                                                                      | Proof that retrieved code is correct                 |\n| Git                                                                      | Diff/history/blame/commit context and change isolation                                                                                     | A static analyzer                                    |\n| Language servers                                                         | Definition/reference/type/navigation data                                                                                                  | Persistent RAG storage                               |\n| Tests/static analyzers                                                   | Verification after changes                                                                                                                 | Discovery/indexing layer                             |\n| `cua_repl` / `node_repl`                                                 | Primary runtime browser/UI inspection: rendered page, interaction, DOM, styles, geometry, console, network, screenshots, responsive states | Static source-code understanding                     |\n| Chrome DevTools MCP                                                      | Alternative browser/runtime inspection layer for DOM, CSS, console, network, performance, screenshots, and interaction                     | Persistent project knowledge or source-code indexing |\n\nRecommended verification tools depend on the project. Examples include PHPUnit/Pest, PHPStan/Psalm/PHPCS/PHP CS Fixer, ESLint, TypeScript, Vue/Nuxt type checks, Composer scripts, and application-specific integration tests.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 2. Why this is hybrid retrieval, not \"vector search only\"\n\nAn AI agent must select the search mechanism according to the question.\n\nUse **exact/symbol search** for questions such as:\n\n```text\nWhere is PaymentService::processPayment() called?\n```\n\nUse **structural search** for questions such as:\n\n```text\nFind all PHP methods that dispatch an event from inside a transaction.\n```\n\nUse **semantic/vector retrieval** for questions such as:\n\n```text\nWhere is failed payment retry logic implemented?\n```\n\nUse **graph traversal** for questions such as:\n\n```text\nWhat may be affected if PaymentGatewayInterface changes?\n```\n\nVector results identify candidates. They do **not** prove control flow, references, type relationships, or runtime behavior. Always verify important findings against source code and, when applicable, LSP/Serena, PostgreSQL `symbol_relations`, tests, and Git history.\n\n---\n\n## See Also\n\n- [Getting Started](./getting-started.md)\n- [Database Reference](./database.md)\n- [Retrieval and Indexing](./retrieval-and-indexing.md)[← Architecture and Retrieval Model](./architecture.md) · [Back to README](../README.md) · [Retrieval and Indexing →](./retrieval-and-indexing.md)\n\n# Database Reference\n\n## 6. PostgreSQL extensions\n\n`001_extensions.sql` enables:\n\n| Extension  | Purpose                                       |\n|------------|-----------------------------------------------|\n| `vector`   | Embedding storage and nearest-neighbor search |\n| `pgcrypto` | `gen_random_uuid()`                           |\n| `pg_trgm`  | Fuzzy symbol/path lookup                      |\n| `unaccent` | Optional normalization support                |\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 7. DDL model\n\nThe authoritative DDL is in `002_schema.sql`.\n\n### `projects`\n\nRoot tenant/entity for all stored projects.\n\n| Column           | Type        | Purpose                       |\n|------------------|-------------|-------------------------------|\n| `id`             | UUID PK     | Project identifier            |\n| `name`           | TEXT        | Human-readable name           |\n| `slug`           | TEXT UNIQUE | Stable local identifier       |\n| `root_path`      | TEXT        | Local repository path         |\n| `repository_url` | TEXT        | Optional Git remote           |\n| `default_branch` | TEXT        | Default branch                |\n| `active`         | BOOLEAN     | Whether the project is active |\n| `metadata`       | JSONB       | Project-specific metadata     |\n| timestamps       | TIMESTAMPTZ | Audit timestamps              |\n\nAll project-owned data cascades when a project is deleted. Composite project-scoped foreign keys also prevent accidental cross-project links between files, symbols, chunks, documents, knowledge, and embeddings.\n\n### `files`\n\nOne row per indexed file in a project.\n\nImportant columns:\n\n```text\nproject_id\npath\nlanguage\nfile_type\ncontent_hash\ngit_blob_sha\nlast_indexed_commit\nis_generated\nis_deleted\nindexed_at\n```\n\nUnique key:\n\n```text\n(project_id, path)\n```\n\n### `symbols`\n\nTree-sitter/LSP/Serena-derived symbols.\n\nTypical `kind` values:\n\n```text\nnamespace\nmodule\nclass\ninterface\ntrait\nenum\nmethod\nfunction\nproperty\nconstant\ntype\ncomponent\n```\n\nImportant columns:\n\n```text\nproject_id\nfile_id\nparent_symbol_id\nkind\nname\nqualified_name\nsignature\nvisibility\nstart_line/start_column\nend_line/end_column\ncontent_hash\nmetadata\n```\n\nThe schema intentionally does not constrain `kind` to a fixed enum because language ecosystems differ.\n\n### `symbol_relations`\n\nEdges in the relational code graph.\n\nTypical `relation_type` values:\n\n```text\ncalls\nreferences\nimports\nextends\nimplements\nuses\ndispatches\nlistens\npublishes\nsubscribes\nreads\nwrites\ninstantiates\n```\n\n`target_symbol_id` can be `NULL` for an unresolved external or not-yet-resolved target. In that case `target_qualified_name` must be present.\n\n`confidence` allows framework heuristics to be stored without pretending that every inferred edge is certain.\n\n### `documents`\n\nProject documentation and external/local reference material.\n\nExamples:\n\n```text\nREADME\nADR\nOpenAPI document\ninternal architecture document\nvendor API reference\nAI Factory generated research\n```\n\nA document must have either `path` or `source_uri`.\n\n### `knowledge_items`\n\nDurable, explicit project knowledge.\n\nRecommended `kind` values:\n\n```text\narchitecture\nbusiness_rule\nconstraint\nconvention\ndecision\nincident\nintegration\nsecurity\ndeployment\n```\n\nUse this table for concise, evidence-backed facts that should persist across agent sessions.\n\nDo not put temporary guesses here.\n\n### `chunks`\n\nCanonical RAG chunk table for all source types.\n\n`source_type` is one of:\n\n```text\ncode\ndocument\nknowledge\n```\n\nA chunk points to exactly one primary source:\n\n```text\ncode      -> file_id (+ optional symbol_id)\ndocument  -> document_id\nknowledge -> knowledge_item_id\n```\n\nImportant columns:\n\n```text\nchunk_type\nsequence_number\nstart_line/end_line\ncontent\ncontent_hash\ntoken_count\nmetadata\nsearch_vector\n```\n\n`search_vector` is generated automatically for PostgreSQL Full Text Search.\n\n### `embedding_models`\n\nRegistry of embedding configurations.\n\n```text\nprovider\nmodel\ndimensions\ndistance_metric\nenabled\n```\n\nSupported `distance_metric` values in this schema:\n\n```text\ncosine\nl2\ninner_product\n```\n\nThe model registry avoids hard-coding a single embedding dimension into the schema.\n\n### `embeddings`\n\nEmbeddings for chunks.\n\n```text\nproject_id\nchunk_id\nembedding_model_id\ndimensions\nembedding\nsource_content_hash\n```\n\nThe `embedding` column uses unconstrained `VECTOR`, allowing different models with different dimensions in one table.\n\n`source_content_hash` is critical: if it differs from the current chunk hash, the embedding is stale and must be regenerated.\n\n### `indexing_runs`\n\nTracks indexing/synchronization operations.\n\nTypical sequence:\n\n```text\nrunning\n    ->\ncompleted | failed | cancelled\n```\n\nStore counts and timing details in `stats`.\n\n---\n[← Database Reference](./database.md) · [Back to README](../README.md) · [Agent Workflow →](./agent-workflow.md)\n\n# Retrieval and Indexing\n\n## 4. Search decision matrix\n\n| Need                             | Preferred tool                                    |\n|----------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------|\n| Exact string/error/config key    | ripgrep                                           |\n| Class/method definition          | Serena/LSP                                        |\n| Find references/implementations  | Serena/LSP                                        |\n| Caller/callee path               | Serena/LSP + relational graph                     |\n| Blast radius                     | PostgreSQL relational graph                       |\n| AST code shape                   | /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep                    |\n| Large codemod                    | /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep + Git diff + tests |\n| Syntax-aware chunk boundaries    | Tree-sitter                                       |\n| Unknown business concept         | FTS + pgvector                                    |\n| Documentation/ADR/business rules | PostgreSQL RAG                                    |\n| Why/when code changed            | Git                                               |\n| Prove correctness                | tests/static analysis                             |\n| Rendered UI/layout/usability     | `cua_repl` / `node_repl`                          |\n| DOM/computed styles/geometry     | `cua_repl` / `node_repl`                          |\n| Browser console/runtime errors   | `cua_repl` / `node_repl`                          |\n| Network/API behavior in browser  | `cua_repl` / `node_repl`                          |\n| Browser runtime alternative      | Chrome DevTools MCP                               |\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## Reliable end-to-end indexing\n\nIndexing crosses host tools, a Docker container, a paid embedding API, and PostgreSQL. Verify each boundary explicitly:\n\n1. Check host tools with `command -v /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep`, `serena --version`, and the Python interpreter that has `tree_sitter_language_pack`. The AI database container is expected to provide PostgreSQL and Node.js, not every parser or language server.\n2. Run Serena/LSP indexing locally. A first run may try to download a language server (for PHP, Intelephense); DNS or registry failures indicate a tool-installation problem. Retry after network access is available and distinguish this from source parse errors.\n3. Parse a small representative file before processing the whole repository. Some Tree-sitter bindings can fail in batch traversal. If a fallback parser or line chunking is used, record the fallback and resulting limitations; do not label those chunks syntax-aware. Populate `symbol_relations` only when edges were actually derived.\n4. Resolve the project by slug and scope every file, symbol, chunk, knowledge, embedding, and run query by `project_id`. Exclude credentials, environment files, generated/runtime content, and any source not approved for the shared database.\n5. Generate embeddings in bounded batches. `app/embedding.js` currently prints the API key and input text to stdout, so suppress stdout and keep command logs private. Persist each batch under `/app/embedding-results/{year}/{month}/` (container path `/app/embedding-results/{year}/{month}/`) before starting another batch. Never rely on container `/tmp` for paid results.\n6. Before retrying, compare existing result files, chunk hashes, project counts, model ID, and indexing-run state. Reuse completed batches; do not call the API again for unchanged input. Keep a manifest mapping batch files to chunk IDs/content hashes when building a new index.\n7. Commit embeddings first. Then create the project/model HNSW index with `aic_create_project_embedding_hnsw_index(...)` using the table-owner role. The restricted `$POSTGRES_SIMPLE_USER` may insert rows but fail with `must be owner of table aic_embeddings` when creating the index. Mark the run `completed` only after the index exists and counts are checked.\n\nA failed index-creation statement inside the same transaction as vector inserts rolls back those inserts. Check database state before recovery; preserve saved batch files and resume the database write without generating embeddings again. Avoid broad deletes or project recreation when recovering partial runs.\n\n### Office Easyfit indexing record (2026-10-02)\n\nThe `office-easyfit` project uses `text-embedding-3-small` (1536 dimensions) and a project-specific HNSW index. The completed run contains 1,182 PHP files, 4,221 approximate symbol rows, 3,896 chunks/embeddings, four documents, and four knowledge items. Configuration, test, runtime, vendor/dependency, web asset/upload paths, and files matching the credential-like literal filter were excluded. Serena indexed 964 PHP files. The Tree-sitter Python binding segfaulted during batch node-field extraction, so code chunks used 80-line boundaries and symbol names used a declaration-line fallback; symbol relations were not populated. Use vector and symbol results as candidates and verify findings in the original source. The 325 embedding result JSON files were persisted under `app/embedding-results/`. This run did not produce a separate batch-to-chunk manifest; the database rows are complete, so do not regenerate them. Future runs should persist that mapping alongside each batch file.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Search rules\n\n### Rule 1 — Always scope by project\n\nBad:\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM chunks\nLIMIT 20;\n```\n\nGood:\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM chunks\nWHERE project_id = :project_id\nLIMIT 20;\n```\n\nCross-project retrieval is allowed only when the task explicitly requires it.\n\n### Rule 2 — Prefer exact symbol lookup before fuzzy/vector search\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n    s.*,\n    f.path\nFROM symbols s\nJOIN files f ON f.id = s.file_id\nWHERE s.project_id = :project_id\n  AND s.qualified_name = :qualified_name;\n```\n\n### Rule 3 — Fuzzy symbol search for uncertain names\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM search_symbols_fuzzy(\n    :project_id,\n    'PaymentServce',\n    20\n);\n```\n\n### Rule 4 — Full Text Search for lexical/business terms\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM search_chunks_text(\n    :project_id,\n    'failed payment retry',\n    20\n);\n```\n\n### Rule 5 — Exact vector retrieval\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM search_chunks_vector_exact(\n    :project_id,\n    :embedding_model_id,\n    :query_embedding::vector,\n    20\n);\n```\n\nThe application is responsible for generating `:query_embedding` with the same model used for stored embeddings.\n\n### Rule 6 — Graph traversal\n\nOutgoing dependencies:\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM symbol_relations_walk(\n    :project_id,\n    :symbol_id,\n    'outgoing',\n    3\n);\n```\n\nIncoming dependants / impact candidates:\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM symbol_relations_walk(\n    :project_id,\n    :symbol_id,\n    'incoming',\n    3\n);\n```\n\nTreat heuristic relations according to their `confidence`.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 12. Vector indexing\n\nA single `VECTOR` column can store embeddings of different dimensions. Approximate vector indexes, however, must target rows with a consistent dimension.\n\nThis repository therefore does **not** create one global HNSW index automatically.\n\nAfter registering an embedding model:\n\n```sql\nSELECT id, provider, model, dimensions\nFROM embedding_models;\n```\n\nCreate a **project + model specific** HNSW index:\n\n```sql\nSELECT create_project_embedding_hnsw_index(\n    :project_id,\n    :embedding_model_id\n);\n```\n\nProject-specific partial indexes are intentional. In a shared multi-project database, a single approximate index shared by all projects can lose recall when PostgreSQL applies `project_id` filtering after ANN candidate selection.\n\nThe helper uses:\n\n- `vector(...)` for dimensions up to 2000\n- `halfvec(...)` for dimensions from 2001 through 4000\n\nFor larger vectors, choose a deliberate strategy such as dimensionality reduction, binary quantization, or another model.\n\nTo remove an automatically named project/model index:\n\n```sql\nSELECT drop_project_embedding_hnsw_index(\n    :project_id,\n    :embedding_model_id\n);\n```\n\n### Important query note\n\nAn expression HNSW index is useful only when the retrieval query uses the corresponding cast/expression and matches the partial-index predicate. `search_chunks_vector_exact()` is deliberately an exact, generic fallback.\n\nFor high-volume approximate retrieval, use the helper after creating the HNSW index:\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM search_chunks_vector_hnsw(\n    :project_id,\n    :embedding_model_id,\n    :query_embedding::vector,\n    20\n);\n```\n\nThis function uses the same `vector(n)` or `halfvec(n)` expression as the model-specific HNSW index.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 13. Recommended hybrid RAG flow\n\nFor a natural-language question:\n\n```text\n\"Where is failed payment retry logic implemented?\"\n```\n\nRecommended pipeline:\n\n```text\n1. Resolve project_id\n2. FTS top N\n3. Vector top N\n4. Fuzzy symbol candidates\n5. Optional graph expansion around strong symbol candidates\n6. Merge / rerank candidates\n7. Read original source\n8. Verify references with Serena/LSP and PostgreSQL `symbol_relations`\n9. Answer or modify code\n```\n\nA practical RRF-style merge can happen in application code. Do not blindly concatenate dozens of chunks into the LLM context.\n\nPrefer small, evidence-rich context.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 14. Tree-sitter indexing rules\n\nTree-sitter should primarily produce structural metadata and semantic chunk boundaries.\n\nDo not embed a serialized raw AST unless a specific experiment proves it useful.\n\nPreferred code chunk:\n\n```text\nLanguage: PHP\nFile: app/Services/PaymentService.php\nNamespace: App\\Services\nClass: PaymentService\nMethod: processPayment\nSignature: processPayment(Order $order): Payment\nLines: 34-81\n\n<source code>\n```\n\nRecommended chunk boundaries:\n\n```text\nclass/interface/trait/enum\nmethod/function\ncomponent\ntemplate block\nconfiguration object\nquery/migration unit\n```\n\nFor very large symbols, split only after preserving:\n\n- symbol identity\n- parent symbol\n[← Retrieval and Indexing](./retrieval-and-indexing.md) · [Back to README](../README.md) · [Integrations and References →](./integrations.md)\n\n# Agent Workflow\n\n## 3. Required agent workflow\n\nThe following order is the default investigation workflow for a large project.\n\n### Phase A — Establish project scope\n\n1. Determine the repository root.\n2. Run `git status`.\n3. Determine the current branch and commit.\n4. Resolve the database project row by `slug` or `root_path`.\n5. Never query another project's rows accidentally.\n6. Load AI Factory project rules/context before modifying code.\n\nRecommended AI Factory entry points include:\n\n```text\n/aif-warmup\n/aif-grounded <question>\n/aif-explore <topic>\n/aif-plan <task>\n/aif-fix <problem>\n/aif-implement\n/aif-verify\n/aif-review\n```\n\nExact commands depend on the installed AI Factory version and configured agent.\n\n### Phase B — Search before reading broadly\n\nUse the cheapest precise source first:\n\n1. **Known symbol** -> Serena/LSP.\n2. **Call/dependency question** -> Serena/LSP and PostgreSQL `symbol_relations`.\n3. **Known literal, class name, route, config key, error text** -> `rg`.\n4. **Known code shape** -> /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep.\n5. **Unknown terminology / business concept** -> PostgreSQL FTS + vector retrieval.\n6. **Need syntax boundaries/chunk extraction** -> Tree-sitter.\n7. Read only the files/symbols returned by the search.\n\nDo not scan the whole repository if a precise search can narrow the scope.\n\n### Phase C — Build an evidence chain\n\nFor every non-trivial bug or feature, identify:\n\n```text\nentry point\n    ->\ncontroller/handler/command/job\n    ->\nservice/domain logic\n    ->\nrepository/model/external integration\n    ->\nevent/listener/queue/callback\n    ->\ntests\n```\n\nRecord unresolved questions instead of inventing relationships.\n\n### Phase D — Validate history when behavior is suspicious\n\nUse:\n\n```bash\ngit log -- <file>\ngit blame <file>\ngit show <commit>\ngit diff\n```\n\nHistory is especially useful for workarounds, regressions, legacy compatibility, and business decisions that are not obvious from current code.\n\n### Phase E — Modify minimally\n\n1. Follow project conventions.\n2. Prefer the smallest coherent change.\n3. Use symbol-aware edits for refactors where available.\n4. Use /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep rewrites only when the AST pattern is verified.\n5. Review `git diff` immediately after automated rewrites.\n6. Do not update persistent knowledge until the final behavior is verified.\n\n### Phase F — Verify\n\nRun the narrowest relevant tests first, then broader quality gates.\n\nTypical PHP/Laravel sequence:\n\n```bash\nphp artisan test --filter=<relevant-test>\nvendor/bin/phpstan analyse\nvendor/bin/phpcs\nvendor/bin/phpunit\n```\n\nTypical Nuxt/TypeScript sequence:\n\n```bash\nnpm run lint\nnpm run typecheck\nnpm test\nnpm run build\n```\n\nUse the project's actual scripts when they differ.\n\n### Phase G — Update index/knowledge\n\nAfter a verified change:\n\n1. Update changed `files`.\n2. Re-extract changed `symbols`.\n3. Replace affected `symbol_relations`.\n4. Rebuild affected `chunks`.\n5. Regenerate embeddings only for chunks whose `content_hash` changed.\n6. Store durable architecture/business knowledge only when it is supported by project evidence.\n7. Finish the `indexing_runs` row.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 19. Git rules for AI agents\n\nBefore editing:\n\n```bash\ngit status --short\ngit branch --show-current\ngit rev-parse HEAD\n```\n\nAfter editing:\n\n```bash\ngit diff --check\ngit diff\n```\n\nFor regressions/history:\n\n```bash\ngit log --oneline -- <path>\ngit blame <path>\ngit show <commit> -- <path>\n```\n\nNever discard existing user changes merely to make the working tree clean.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 20. Knowledge-writing policy\n\n`knowledge_items` must contain durable, evidence-backed information.\n\nGood:\n\n```text\n\"Payment webhooks are idempotent by provider_transaction_id.\"\n```\n\nBad:\n\n```text\n\"I think duplicate payments may come from Redis.\"\n```\n\nRecommended fields:\n\n```text\nkind = architecture | business_rule | decision | constraint | ...\nstatus = active\nconfidence = 0.0..1.0\nsource_reference = file/ADR/issue/commit/reference\n```\n\nWhen a fact changes, prefer marking the old row `superseded` and adding the new knowledge item rather than silently rewriting history.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 25. Final rule for the AI agent\n\nThe agent should follow this principle:\n\n> Retrieve broadly enough to discover candidates, but verify narrowly against authoritative source code, symbol relationships, tests, and Git before drawing conclusions or changing code.\n\nThe database is long-term project intelligence, not a substitute for the repository itself.\n\n## See Also\n\n- [Getting Started](./getting-started.md)\n- [Architecture and Retrieval Model](./architecture.md)\n- [Database Reference](./database.md)[← Integrations and References](./integrations.md) · [Back to README](../README.md)\n\n# Security\n\n## 23. Security\n\nThis stack is intended for local development.\n\nThe Compose file binds PostgreSQL to:\n\n```text\n127.0.0.1\n```\n\nrather than all network interfaces.\n\nStill:\n\n- change the default password\n- do not commit `.env`\n- do not expose port `5432` publicly\n- use a separate database user with restricted permissions if an untrusted agent/client can connect\n- back up the Docker volume if the accumulated project knowledge matters\n- never index secrets from source code in chunks, knowledge, or embeddings\n- treat embedding result files as source-derived data: keep them in `app/embedding-results/`, restrict access, and do not commit them unless the repository explicitly requires that\n- suppress stdout from the current embedding script because it prints the API key and input texts\n\n---\n\n## See Also\n\n- [Getting Started](./getting-started.md)\n- [Architecture and Retrieval Model](./architecture.md)\n- [Database Reference](./database.md)\n[← Agent Workflow](./agent-workflow.md) · [Back to README](../README.md) · [Security →](./security.md)\n\n# Integrations and References\n\n## 22. Suggested MCP surface for a future custom server\n\nA custom MCP server over this database could expose narrowly-scoped tools such as:\n\n```text\nproject_resolve\nsymbol_find\nsymbol_references\nsymbol_relations\ncode_search_text\ncode_search_semantic\nknowledge_search\ndocument_search\nindex_status\nindex_changed_files\nknowledge_upsert\n```\n\nAvoid exposing unrestricted SQL execution to an autonomous agent unless the environment is intentionally trusted.\n\nFor read tools, enforce `project_id` server-side.\n\nFor write tools, validate ownership and use transactions.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 24. Upstream references\n\n- AI Factory: https://github.com/lee-to/ai-factory\n- ast-grep: https://ast-grep.github.io/\n- Serena: https://github.com/oraios/serena\n- ripgrep: https://github.com/BurntSushi/ripgrep\n- Tree-sitter: https://tree-sitter.github.io/tree-sitter/\n- pgvector: https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector\n- Chrome DevTools MCP: https://github.com/ChromeDevTools/chrome-devtools-mcp\n\n---\n\n## See Also\n\n- [Getting Started](./getting-started.md)\n- [Architecture and Retrieval Model](./architecture.md)\n- [Database Reference](./database.md)\ntotal 56\ndrwxrwxr-x 7 kamaz kamaz 28672 Oct  6 15:38 .\ndrwxrwxr-x 5 kamaz kamaz  4096 Oct  3 14:30 ..\ndrwxrwxr-x 3 kamaz kamaz  4096 Oct  6 15:38 easyfit-pl\ndrwxrwxr-x 3 kamaz kamaz  4096 Oct  6 15:28 easyfit-ua\ndrwxrwxr-x 2 kamaz kamaz  4096 Oct  5 12:17 office-easyfit\ndrwxrwxr-x 3 kamaz kamaz  4096 Oct  6 18:46 pet-complaints-platform\ndrwxrwxr-x 2 kamaz kamaz  4096 Oct  5 12:15 yii3-office-easyfit\n/usr/bin/python3\ntree-sitter available\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"Failed to create stream fd: Operation not permitted\nFailed to create stream fd: Operation not permitted\nFailed to create stream fd: Operation not permitted\n# Repository Guidelines\n\n## Project Structure & Module Organization\n\nThis repository packages a shared PostgreSQL database for code intelligence. The root `docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/` contains ordered SQL initialization scripts: extensions, schema, indexes, search functions, and vector helpers. `.docker/dev/` contains the local development image, PostgreSQL configuration, Compose file, and environment files. `ai-code-intelligence/` is the standalone deployment package with its own Dockerfile, Compose file, SQL initialization scripts, example environment file, and README. `app/` contains the small Node.js embedding utility and provider implementation. The root README documents the schema and recommended agent workflow.\n\n## Build, Test, and Development Commands\n\n- `make up-dev` starts the development PostgreSQL service.\n- `make down-dev` stops the development service; it does not remove stored volumes.\n- `make build-dev` builds the development image.\n- `make restart-dev` restarts the service; `make rebuild-dev` rebuilds and starts it.\n- `npm install --prefix app` installs the embedding utility dependencies.\n- `node app/embedding.js <API_KEY> text-embedding-3-small \"text to embed\"` generates embeddings.\n\nThere is no configured automated test or lint command. For database changes, review SQL ordering and validate initialization against a disposable local database. Do not use destructive volume/database cleanup commands against the shared development data.\n\n## Coding Style & Naming Conventions\n\nKeep SQL readable, use uppercase SQL keywords, explicit column lists, and descriptive `snake_case` identifiers. Preserve the numeric prefix and dependency order of initialization scripts (for example, `003_indexes.sql`). Use two spaces for JSON and JavaScript indentation, ES modules, and descriptive camelCase JavaScript names. Keep deployment examples aligned between the root and standalone package when both represent the same behavior.\n\n## Testing Guidelines\n\nNo dedicated test suite is present. Verify SQL changes by applying initialization on a clean local database and checking the affected tables, indexes, or functions. For changes to `app/`, run the relevant command manually and avoid committing generated credentials or real API keys.\n\n## Commit & Pull Request Guidelines\n\nGit history is unavailable in this checkout, so no established commit pattern could be confirmed. Use concise imperative commit subjects, such as `Add vector search helper index`. Pull requests should explain the schema or runtime impact, list validation performed, call out environment/configuration changes, and include logs or screenshots only when they clarify behavior.\n\n## Security & Configuration\n\nNever commit `.docker/dev/env/` secrets or expose their values in documentation and logs. Use `.env.example` for safe placeholders. Scope all project-specific database queries by `project_id` or resolve a project by slug first. Persist paid embedding results under `app/embedding-results/`; inspect and reuse saved batches before retries. The current embedding script echoes its API key and input texts to stdout, so suppress stdout. Create project HNSW indexes as the table owner, after committing vector rows.\n\n## Documentation\n\n| Document               | Path                             | Description                                   |\n|------------------------|----------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|\n| README                 | `README.md`                      | Project landing page and guide index          |\n| Getting Started        | `docs/getting-started.md`        | Local database startup and embedding setup    |\n| Architecture           | `docs/architecture.md`           | Retrieval model and system responsibilities   |\n| Database Reference     | `docs/database.md`               | Schema, ownership, and SQL examples           |\n| Retrieval and Indexing | `docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md` | Search, indexing, and code intelligence tools |\n| Agent Workflow         | `docs/agent-workflow.md`         | Investigation and verification practices      |\n| Integrations           | `docs/integrations.md`           | MCP surface and upstream references           |\n| Security               | `docs/security.md`               | Local database and data protection guidance   |\n\n## AI Context Files\n\n| File                          | Purpose                                    |\n|-------------------------------|--------------------------------------------|\n| `.ai-factory/DESCRIPTION.md`  | Project scope and technology summary       |\n| `.ai-factory/ARCHITECTURE.md` | Repository architecture guidelines         |\n| `.ai-factory/rules/base.md`   | Detected naming and structural conventions |# AI Code Intelligence Database\n\n> Local, multi-project PostgreSQL storage for AI-assisted software development.\n\nThis repository combines PostgreSQL full-text search, fuzzy lookup, pgvector retrieval, and relational code relationships. It includes a Docker-based development database and a small Node.js embedding utility. Always scope project data by `project_id` or resolve the project by slug.\n\n## Quick Start\n\nRequires Docker Compose. Start the local database:\n\n```bash\nmake up-dev\n```\n\nCheck the service state:\n\n```bash\ndocker compose -f .docker/dev/docker-compose.yml ps\n```\n\nThe embedding utility uses the OpenAI API and `text-embedding-3-small`; see [Getting Started](docs/getting-started.md) for safe setup details.\n\n## Capabilities\n\n- PostgreSQL 18 with pgvector, full-text search, and pg_trgm.\n- Project, file, symbol, relationship, document, chunk, embedding, and indexing-run storage.\n- Ordered database initialization scripts for local development and standalone deployment.\n- A hybrid retrieval workflow that combines exact, structural, semantic, and graph search.\n\n## Documentation\n\n| Guide                                                    | Description                                          |\n|----------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------|\n| [Getting Started](docs/getting-started.md)               | Local database startup and embedding setup           |\n| [Architecture](docs/architecture.md)                     | Retrieval model and system responsibilities          |\n| [Database Reference](docs/database.md)                   | Schema, ownership, and SQL examples                  |\n| [Retrieval and Indexing](docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md) | Search, indexing, and code intelligence tools        |\n| [Agent Workflow](docs/agent-workflow.md)                 | Investigation, verification, and knowledge practices |\n| [Integrations](docs/integrations.md)                     | MCP surface and upstream references                  |\n| [Security](docs/security.md)                             | Local database and data protection guidance          |[Back to README](../README.md) · [Architecture and Retrieval Model →](./architecture.md)\n\n# Getting Started\n\n## Safe embedding workflow\n\n- Development environment files live in `.docker/dev/env/`. Never copy their values into docs, chat, logs, or command output.\n- The database container runs PostgreSQL and Node.js. Tools such as `/home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep`, Tree-sitter Python bindings, and Serena/LSP may be installed on the host; check the host environment and the exact Python interpreter instead of assuming they must exist in Docker.\n- Keep paid embedding results in the host directory `app/embedding-results/project-name/{year}/{month}/`, mounted in the container at `/app/embedding-results/project-name/{year}/{month}/`. Use stable batch filenames, check which files already exist before retrying, and resume from completed batches. Do not store paid results only in `/tmp` or another ephemeral container path.\n- The current `app/embedding.js` prints its API key and input texts to standard output. Suppress stdout for the embedding call; do not share captured logs. Only index sanitized, non-secret source content.\n- Use `text-embedding-3-small` for the existing model configuration. Keep batches bounded and persist each result file before inserting vectors into PostgreSQL.\n- The restricted `$POSTGRES_SIMPLE_USER` can write project records but may not own `aic_embeddings`. Create project/model HNSW indexes as `$POSTGRES_USER` (table owner). Commit vector rows before index creation and mark the indexing run complete only after the index is verified.\n- Never run `docker system prune`, `docker volume rm`, or `DROP DATABASE` against the shared development data. Before retrying, inspect project counts, run status, and saved embedding files; do not delete a project as a shortcut.\n\n### Run SQL through the PostgreSQL container\n\nFrom the ai-code-intelligence repository root, execute SQL with `psql` inside the database container:\n\n```bash\ndocker compose -f .docker/dev/docker-compose.yml exec -T \\\n  ai-code-intelligence-postgres-postgresql sh -lc \\\n  'psql -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -P pager=off -U \"$POSTGRES_SIMPLE_USER\" -d \"$POSTGRES_DB\" -c \"SELECT slug, id FROM aic_projects ORDER BY slug;\"'\n```\n\nUse `$POSTGRES_SIMPLE_USER` for normal project-scoped reads and writes. Resolve the target project's ID by slug, then include `project_id` in queries against its files, chunks, and embeddings. For vector and HNSW queries, see [Retrieval and Indexing](./retrieval-and-indexing.md).\n\nExample for one sanitized batch:\n\n```bash\ndocker compose -f /home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/.docker/dev/docker-compose.yml \\\n  exec -T ai-code-intelligence-postgres-postgresql sh -lc '\n    node /app/embedding.js \"$OPENAI_API_KEY\" \\\n      /app/embedding-results/project-name/{year}/{month}/example-batch-0000.json \\\n      text-embedding-3-small \"class OrderService {}\" >/dev/null\n  '\n```\n\nThe embedding file persists on the host at `/app/embedding-results/project-name/{year}/{month}/example-batch-0000.json`.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Database startup\n\n### Files about DB tables\n\nDB tables schemas:\ndocker-entrypoint-initdb.d:\n - 001_extensions.sql\n - 002_schema.sql\n - 003_indexes.sql\n - 004_views_and_search_functions.sql\n - 005_vector_index_helpers.sql\n\n---\n\n## See Also\n\n- [Architecture and Retrieval Model](./architecture.md)\n- [Database Reference](./database.md)\n- [Retrieval and Indexing](./retrieval-and-indexing.md)[← Getting Started](./getting-started.md) · [Back to README](../README.md) · [Database Reference →](./database.md)\n\n# Architecture and Retrieval Model\n\nThis page explains the code-intelligence retrieval model. For repository layers, SQL initialization order, deployment boundaries, and the embedding utility, see the [repository architecture guide](../.ai-factory/ARCHITECTURE.md).\n\n## 1. Architecture\n\n```text\nRepositories\n    |\n    +----------------------+----------------------+\n    |                      |                      |\n    v                      v                      v\nTree-sitter            Serena / LSP           Git\nAST + chunks           symbols/types          history\n    |                      |                      |\n    +----------------------+----------+-----------+\n                                       |\n                                       v\n                              Index / Context Layer\n                                       |\n               +-----------------------+-----------------------+\n               |                       |                       |\n               v                       v                       v\n          PostgreSQL FTS          pgvector RAG          relational graph\n               |                       |                       |\n               +-----------------------+-----------------------+\n                                       |\n                                       v\n                                  AI Agent\n                                       |\n                                       v\n                                  AI Factory\n                           plan -> implement -> verify\n```\n\n### Tool responsibilities\n\n| Tool                                                                     | Primary responsibility                                                                                                                     | Do not use it as                                     |\n|--------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------|\n| AI Factory                                                               | Project context, rules, spec-driven workflow, planning, implementation, verification                                                       | A code parser or search engine                       |\n| Serena                                                                   | Symbol-level semantic navigation, references, editing, refactoring, LSP-backed code intelligence                                           | A replacement for semantic RAG                       |\n| ripgrep (`rg`)                                                           | Fast exact text/regex search                                                                                                               | Structural AST matching                              |\n| /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep (`sg` / `/home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep`) | AST-aware structural search, linting, safe rewrite/codemod patterns                                                                        | Semantic business-language search                    |\n| Tree-sitter                                                              | Parsing, syntax trees, language-aware chunk boundaries, extraction input                                                                   | A complete type system or runtime call graph         |\n| PostgreSQL + pgvector                                                    | Persistent multi-project RAG, metadata, graph data, FTS, fuzzy lookup                                                                      | Proof that retrieved code is correct                 |\n| Git                                                                      | Diff/history/blame/commit context and change isolation                                                                                     | A static analyzer                                    |\n| Language servers                                                         | Definition/reference/type/navigation data                                                                                                  | Persistent RAG storage                               |\n| Tests/static analyzers                                                   | Verification after changes                                                                                                                 | Discovery/indexing layer                             |\n| `cua_repl` / `node_repl`                                                 | Primary runtime browser/UI inspection: rendered page, interaction, DOM, styles, geometry, console, network, screenshots, responsive states | Static source-code understanding                     |\n| Chrome DevTools MCP                                                      | Alternative browser/runtime inspection layer for DOM, CSS, console, network, performance, screenshots, and interaction                     | Persistent project knowledge or source-code indexing |\n\nRecommended verification tools depend on the project. Examples include PHPUnit/Pest, PHPStan/Psalm/PHPCS/PHP CS Fixer, ESLint, TypeScript, Vue/Nuxt type checks, Composer scripts, and application-specific integration tests.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 2. Why this is hybrid retrieval, not \"vector search only\"\n\nAn AI agent must select the search mechanism according to the question.\n\nUse **exact/symbol search** for questions such as:\n\n```text\nWhere is PaymentService::processPayment() called?\n```\n\nUse **structural search** for questions such as:\n\n```text\nFind all PHP methods that dispatch an event from inside a transaction.\n```\n\nUse **semantic/vector retrieval** for questions such as:\n\n```text\nWhere is failed payment retry logic implemented?\n```\n\nUse **graph traversal** for questions such as:\n\n```text\nWhat may be affected if PaymentGatewayInterface changes?\n```\n\nVector results identify candidates. They do **not** prove control flow, references, type relationships, or runtime behavior. Always verify important findings against source code and, when applicable, LSP/Serena, PostgreSQL `symbol_relations`, tests, and Git history.\n\n---\n\n## See Also\n\n- [Getting Started](./getting-started.md)\n- [Database Reference](./database.md)\n- [Retrieval and Indexing](./retrieval-and-indexing.md)[← Architecture and Retrieval Model](./architecture.md) · [Back to README](../README.md) · [Retrieval and Indexing →](./retrieval-and-indexing.md)\n\n# Database Reference\n\n## 6. PostgreSQL extensions\n\n`001_extensions.sql` enables:\n\n| Extension  | Purpose                                       |\n|------------|-----------------------------------------------|\n| `vector`   | Embedding storage and nearest-neighbor search |\n| `pgcrypto` | `gen_random_uuid()`                           |\n| `pg_trgm`  | Fuzzy symbol/path lookup                      |\n| `unaccent` | Optional normalization support                |\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 7. DDL model\n\nThe authoritative DDL is in `002_schema.sql`.\n\n### `projects`\n\nRoot tenant/entity for all stored projects.\n\n| Column           | Type        | Purpose                       |\n|------------------|-------------|-------------------------------|\n| `id`             | UUID PK     | Project identifier            |\n| `name`           | TEXT        | Human-readable name           |\n| `slug`           | TEXT UNIQUE | Stable local identifier       |\n| `root_path`      | TEXT        | Local repository path         |\n| `repository_url` | TEXT        | Optional Git remote           |\n| `default_branch` | TEXT        | Default branch                |\n| `active`         | BOOLEAN     | Whether the project is active |\n| `metadata`       | JSONB       | Project-specific metadata     |\n| timestamps       | TIMESTAMPTZ | Audit timestamps              |\n\nAll project-owned data cascades when a project is deleted. Composite project-scoped foreign keys also prevent accidental cross-project links between files, symbols, chunks, documents, knowledge, and embeddings.\n\n### `files`\n\nOne row per indexed file in a project.\n\nImportant columns:\n\n```text\nproject_id\npath\nlanguage\nfile_type\ncontent_hash\ngit_blob_sha\nlast_indexed_commit\nis_generated\nis_deleted\nindexed_at\n```\n\nUnique key:\n\n```text\n(project_id, path)\n```\n\n### `symbols`\n\nTree-sitter/LSP/Serena-derived symbols.\n\nTypical `kind` values:\n\n```text\nnamespace\nmodule\nclass\ninterface\ntrait\nenum\nmethod\nfunction\nproperty\nconstant\ntype\ncomponent\n```\n\nImportant columns:\n\n```text\nproject_id\nfile_id\nparent_symbol_id\nkind\nname\nqualified_name\nsignature\nvisibility\nstart_line/start_column\nend_line/end_column\ncontent_hash\nmetadata\n```\n\nThe schema intentionally does not constrain `kind` to a fixed enum because language ecosystems differ.\n\n### `symbol_relations`\n\nEdges in the relational code graph.\n\nTypical `relation_type` values:\n\n```text\ncalls\nreferences\nimports\nextends\nimplements\nuses\ndispatches\nlistens\npublishes\nsubscribes\nreads\nwrites\ninstantiates\n```\n\n`target_symbol_id` can be `NULL` for an unresolved external or not-yet-resolved target. In that case `target_qualified_name` must be present.\n\n`confidence` allows framework heuristics to be stored without pretending that every inferred edge is certain.\n\n### `documents`\n\nProject documentation and external/local reference material.\n\nExamples:\n\n```text\nREADME\nADR\nOpenAPI document\ninternal architecture document\nvendor API reference\nAI Factory generated research\n```\n\nA document must have either `path` or `source_uri`.\n\n### `knowledge_items`\n\nDurable, explicit project knowledge.\n\nRecommended `kind` values:\n\n```text\narchitecture\nbusiness_rule\nconstraint\nconvention\ndecision\nincident\nintegration\nsecurity\ndeployment\n```\n\nUse this table for concise, evidence-backed facts that should persist across agent sessions.\n\nDo not put temporary guesses here.\n\n### `chunks`\n\nCanonical RAG chunk table for all source types.\n\n`source_type` is one of:\n\n```text\ncode\ndocument\nknowledge\n```\n\nA chunk points to exactly one primary source:\n\n```text\ncode      -> file_id (+ optional symbol_id)\ndocument  -> document_id\nknowledge -> knowledge_item_id\n```\n\nImportant columns:\n\n```text\nchunk_type\nsequence_number\nstart_line/end_line\ncontent\ncontent_hash\ntoken_count\nmetadata\nsearch_vector\n```\n\n`search_vector` is generated automatically for PostgreSQL Full Text Search.\n\n### `embedding_models`\n\nRegistry of embedding configurations.\n\n```text\nprovider\nmodel\ndimensions\ndistance_metric\nenabled\n```\n\nSupported `distance_metric` values in this schema:\n\n```text\ncosine\nl2\ninner_product\n```\n\nThe model registry avoids hard-coding a single embedding dimension into the schema.\n\n### `embeddings`\n\nEmbeddings for chunks.\n\n```text\nproject_id\nchunk_id\nembedding_model_id\ndimensions\nembedding\nsource_content_hash\n```\n\nThe `embedding` column uses unconstrained `VECTOR`, allowing different models with different dimensions in one table.\n\n`source_content_hash` is critical: if it differs from the current chunk hash, the embedding is stale and must be regenerated.\n\n### `indexing_runs`\n\nTracks indexing/synchronization operations.\n\nTypical sequence:\n\n```text\nrunning\n    ->\ncompleted | failed | cancelled\n```\n\nStore counts and timing details in `stats`.\n\n---\n[← Database Reference](./database.md) · [Back to README](../README.md) · [Agent Workflow →](./agent-workflow.md)\n\n# Retrieval and Indexing\n\n## 4. Search decision matrix\n\n| Need                             | Preferred tool                                    |\n|----------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------|\n| Exact string/error/config key    | ripgrep                                           |\n| Class/method definition          | Serena/LSP                                        |\n| Find references/implementations  | Serena/LSP                                        |\n| Caller/callee path               | Serena/LSP + relational graph                     |\n| Blast radius                     | PostgreSQL relational graph                       |\n| AST code shape                   | /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep                    |\n| Large codemod                    | /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep + Git diff + tests |\n| Syntax-aware chunk boundaries    | Tree-sitter                                       |\n| Unknown business concept         | FTS + pgvector                                    |\n| Documentation/ADR/business rules | PostgreSQL RAG                                    |\n| Why/when code changed            | Git                                               |\n| Prove correctness                | tests/static analysis                             |\n| Rendered UI/layout/usability     | `cua_repl` / `node_repl`                          |\n| DOM/computed styles/geometry     | `cua_repl` / `node_repl`                          |\n| Browser console/runtime errors   | `cua_repl` / `node_repl`                          |\n| Network/API behavior in browser  | `cua_repl` / `node_repl`                          |\n| Browser runtime alternative      | Chrome DevTools MCP                               |\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## Reliable end-to-end indexing\n\nIndexing crosses host tools, a Docker container, a paid embedding API, and PostgreSQL. Verify each boundary explicitly:\n\n1. Check host tools with `command -v /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep`, `serena --version`, and the Python interpreter that has `tree_sitter_language_pack`. The AI database container is expected to provide PostgreSQL and Node.js, not every parser or language server.\n2. Run Serena/LSP indexing locally. A first run may try to download a language server (for PHP, Intelephense); DNS or registry failures indicate a tool-installation problem. Retry after network access is available and distinguish this from source parse errors.\n3. Parse a small representative file before processing the whole repository. Some Tree-sitter bindings can fail in batch traversal. If a fallback parser or line chunking is used, record the fallback and resulting limitations; do not label those chunks syntax-aware. Populate `symbol_relations` only when edges were actually derived.\n4. Resolve the project by slug and scope every file, symbol, chunk, knowledge, embedding, and run query by `project_id`. Exclude credentials, environment files, generated/runtime content, and any source not approved for the shared database.\n5. Generate embeddings in bounded batches. `app/embedding.js` currently prints the API key and input text to stdout, so suppress stdout and keep command logs private. Persist each batch under `/app/embedding-results/{year}/{month}/` (container path `/app/embedding-results/{year}/{month}/`) before starting another batch. Never rely on container `/tmp` for paid results.\n6. Before retrying, compare existing result files, chunk hashes, project counts, model ID, and indexing-run state. Reuse completed batches; do not call the API again for unchanged input. Keep a manifest mapping batch files to chunk IDs/content hashes when building a new index.\n7. Commit embeddings first. Then create the project/model HNSW index with `aic_create_project_embedding_hnsw_index(...)` using the table-owner role. The restricted `$POSTGRES_SIMPLE_USER` may insert rows but fail with `must be owner of table aic_embeddings` when creating the index. Mark the run `completed` only after the index exists and counts are checked.\n\nA failed index-creation statement inside the same transaction as vector inserts rolls back those inserts. Check database state before recovery; preserve saved batch files and resume the database write without generating embeddings again. Avoid broad deletes or project recreation when recovering partial runs.\n\n### Office Easyfit indexing record (2026-10-02)\n\nThe `office-easyfit` project uses `text-embedding-3-small` (1536 dimensions) and a project-specific HNSW index. The completed run contains 1,182 PHP files, 4,221 approximate symbol rows, 3,896 chunks/embeddings, four documents, and four knowledge items. Configuration, test, runtime, vendor/dependency, web asset/upload paths, and files matching the credential-like literal filter were excluded. Serena indexed 964 PHP files. The Tree-sitter Python binding segfaulted during batch node-field extraction, so code chunks used 80-line boundaries and symbol names used a declaration-line fallback; symbol relations were not populated. Use vector and symbol results as candidates and verify findings in the original source. The 325 embedding result JSON files were persisted under `app/embedding-results/`. This run did not produce a separate batch-to-chunk manifest; the database rows are complete, so do not regenerate them. Future runs should persist that mapping alongside each batch file.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Search rules\n\n### Rule 1 — Always scope by project\n\nBad:\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM chunks\nLIMIT 20;\n```\n\nGood:\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM chunks\nWHERE project_id = :project_id\nLIMIT 20;\n```\n\nCross-project retrieval is allowed only when the task explicitly requires it.\n\n### Rule 2 — Prefer exact symbol lookup before fuzzy/vector search\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n    s.*,\n    f.path\nFROM symbols s\nJOIN files f ON f.id = s.file_id\nWHERE s.project_id = :project_id\n  AND s.qualified_name = :qualified_name;\n```\n\n### Rule 3 — Fuzzy symbol search for uncertain names\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM search_symbols_fuzzy(\n    :project_id,\n    'PaymentServce',\n    20\n);\n```\n\n### Rule 4 — Full Text Search for lexical/business terms\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM search_chunks_text(\n    :project_id,\n    'failed payment retry',\n    20\n);\n```\n\n### Rule 5 — Exact vector retrieval\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM search_chunks_vector_exact(\n    :project_id,\n    :embedding_model_id,\n    :query_embedding::vector,\n    20\n);\n```\n\nThe application is responsible for generating `:query_embedding` with the same model used for stored embeddings.\n\n### Rule 6 — Graph traversal\n\nOutgoing dependencies:\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM symbol_relations_walk(\n    :project_id,\n    :symbol_id,\n    'outgoing',\n    3\n);\n```\n\nIncoming dependants / impact candidates:\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM symbol_relations_walk(\n    :project_id,\n    :symbol_id,\n    'incoming',\n    3\n);\n```\n\nTreat heuristic relations according to their `confidence`.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 12. Vector indexing\n\nA single `VECTOR` column can store embeddings of different dimensions. Approximate vector indexes, however, must target rows with a consistent dimension.\n\nThis repository therefore does **not** create one global HNSW index automatically.\n\nAfter registering an embedding model:\n\n```sql\nSELECT id, provider, model, dimensions\nFROM embedding_models;\n```\n\nCreate a **project + model specific** HNSW index:\n\n```sql\nSELECT create_project_embedding_hnsw_index(\n    :project_id,\n    :embedding_model_id\n);\n```\n\nProject-specific partial indexes are intentional. In a shared multi-project database, a single approximate index shared by all projects can lose recall when PostgreSQL applies `project_id` filtering after ANN candidate selection.\n\nThe helper uses:\n\n- `vector(...)` for dimensions up to 2000\n- `halfvec(...)` for dimensions from 2001 through 4000\n\nFor larger vectors, choose a deliberate strategy such as dimensionality reduction, binary quantization, or another model.\n\nTo remove an automatically named project/model index:\n\n```sql\nSELECT drop_project_embedding_hnsw_index(\n    :project_id,\n    :embedding_model_id\n);\n```\n\n### Important query note\n\nAn expression HNSW index is useful only when the retrieval query uses the corresponding cast/expression and matches the partial-index predicate. `search_chunks_vector_exact()` is deliberately an exact, generic fallback.\n\nFor high-volume approximate retrieval, use the helper after creating the HNSW index:\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM search_chunks_vector_hnsw(\n    :project_id,\n    :embedding_model_id,\n    :query_embedding::vector,\n    20\n);\n```\n\nThis function uses the same `vector(n)` or `halfvec(n)` expression as the model-specific HNSW index.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 13. Recommended hybrid RAG flow\n\nFor a natural-language question:\n\n```text\n\"Where is failed payment retry logic implemented?\"\n```\n\nRecommended pipeline:\n\n```text\n1. Resolve project_id\n2. FTS top N\n3. Vector top N\n4. Fuzzy symbol candidates\n5. Optional graph expansion around strong symbol candidates\n6. Merge / rerank candidates\n7. Read original source\n8. Verify references with Serena/LSP and PostgreSQL `symbol_relations`\n9. Answer or modify code\n```\n\nA practical RRF-style merge can happen in application code. Do not blindly concatenate dozens of chunks into the LLM context.\n\nPrefer small, evidence-rich context.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 14. Tree-sitter indexing rules\n\nTree-sitter should primarily produce structural metadata and semantic chunk boundaries.\n\nDo not embed a serialized raw AST unless a specific experiment proves it useful.\n\nPreferred code chunk:\n\n```text\nLanguage: PHP\nFile: app/Services/PaymentService.php\nNamespace: App\\Services\nClass: PaymentService\nMethod: processPayment\nSignature: processPayment(Order $order): Payment\nLines: 34-81\n\n<source code>\n```\n\nRecommended chunk boundaries:\n\n```text\nclass/interface/trait/enum\nmethod/function\ncomponent\ntemplate block\nconfiguration object\nquery/migration unit\n```\n\nFor very large symbols, split only after preserving:\n\n- symbol identity\n- parent symbol\n[← Retrieval and Indexing](./retrieval-and-indexing.md) · [Back to README](../README.md) · [Integrations and References →](./integrations.md)\n\n# Agent Workflow\n\n## 3. Required agent workflow\n\nThe following order is the default investigation workflow for a large project.\n\n### Phase A — Establish project scope\n\n1. Determine the repository root.\n2. Run `git status`.\n3. Determine the current branch and commit.\n4. Resolve the database project row by `slug` or `root_path`.\n5. Never query another project's rows accidentally.\n6. Load AI Factory project rules/context before modifying code.\n\nRecommended AI Factory entry points include:\n\n```text\n/aif-warmup\n/aif-grounded <question>\n/aif-explore <topic>\n/aif-plan <task>\n/aif-fix <problem>\n/aif-implement\n/aif-verify\n/aif-review\n```\n\nExact commands depend on the installed AI Factory version and configured agent.\n\n### Phase B — Search before reading broadly\n\nUse the cheapest precise source first:\n\n1. **Known symbol** -> Serena/LSP.\n2. **Call/dependency question** -> Serena/LSP and PostgreSQL `symbol_relations`.\n3. **Known literal, class name, route, config key, error text** -> `rg`.\n4. **Known code shape** -> /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep.\n5. **Unknown terminology / business concept** -> PostgreSQL FTS + vector retrieval.\n6. **Need syntax boundaries/chunk extraction** -> Tree-sitter.\n7. Read only the files/symbols returned by the search.\n\nDo not scan the whole repository if a precise search can narrow the scope.\n\n### Phase C — Build an evidence chain\n\nFor every non-trivial bug or feature, identify:\n\n```text\nentry point\n    ->\ncontroller/handler/command/job\n    ->\nservice/domain logic\n    ->\nrepository/model/external integration\n    ->\nevent/listener/queue/callback\n    ->\ntests\n```\n\nRecord unresolved questions instead of inventing relationships.\n\n### Phase D — Validate history when behavior is suspicious\n\nUse:\n\n```bash\ngit log -- <file>\ngit blame <file>\ngit show <commit>\ngit diff\n```\n\nHistory is especially useful for workarounds, regressions, legacy compatibility, and business decisions that are not obvious from current code.\n\n### Phase E — Modify minimally\n\n1. Follow project conventions.\n2. Prefer the smallest coherent change.\n3. Use symbol-aware edits for refactors where available.\n4. Use /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep rewrites only when the AST pattern is verified.\n5. Review `git diff` immediately after automated rewrites.\n6. Do not update persistent knowledge until the final behavior is verified.\n\n### Phase F — Verify\n\nRun the narrowest relevant tests first, then broader quality gates.\n\nTypical PHP/Laravel sequence:\n\n```bash\nphp artisan test --filter=<relevant-test>\nvendor/bin/phpstan analyse\nvendor/bin/phpcs\nvendor/bin/phpunit\n```\n\nTypical Nuxt/TypeScript sequence:\n\n```bash\nnpm run lint\nnpm run typecheck\nnpm test\nnpm run build\n```\n\nUse the project's actual scripts when they differ.\n\n### Phase G — Update index/knowledge\n\nAfter a verified change:\n\n1. Update changed `files`.\n2. Re-extract changed `symbols`.\n3. Replace affected `symbol_relations`.\n4. Rebuild affected `chunks`.\n5. Regenerate embeddings only for chunks whose `content_hash` changed.\n6. Store durable architecture/business knowledge only when it is supported by project evidence.\n7. Finish the `indexing_runs` row.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 19. Git rules for AI agents\n\nBefore editing:\n\n```bash\ngit status --short\ngit branch --show-current\ngit rev-parse HEAD\n```\n\nAfter editing:\n\n```bash\ngit diff --check\ngit diff\n```\n\nFor regressions/history:\n\n```bash\ngit log --oneline -- <path>\ngit blame <path>\ngit show <commit> -- <path>\n```\n\nNever discard existing user changes merely to make the working tree clean.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 20. Knowledge-writing policy\n\n`knowledge_items` must contain durable, evidence-backed information.\n\nGood:\n\n```text\n\"Payment webhooks are idempotent by provider_transaction_id.\"\n```\n\nBad:\n\n```text\n\"I think duplicate payments may come from Redis.\"\n```\n\nRecommended fields:\n\n```text\nkind = architecture | business_rule | decision | constraint | ...\nstatus = active\nconfidence = 0.0..1.0\nsource_reference = file/ADR/issue/commit/reference\n```\n\nWhen a fact changes, prefer marking the old row `superseded` and adding the new knowledge item rather than silently rewriting history.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 25. Final rule for the AI agent\n\nThe agent should follow this principle:\n\n> Retrieve broadly enough to discover candidates, but verify narrowly against authoritative source code, symbol relationships, tests, and Git before drawing conclusions or changing code.\n\nThe database is long-term project intelligence, not a substitute for the repository itself.\n\n## See Also\n\n- [Getting Started](./getting-started.md)\n- [Architecture and Retrieval Model](./architecture.md)\n- [Database Reference](./database.md)[← Integrations and References](./integrations.md) · [Back to README](../README.md)\n\n# Security\n\n## 23. Security\n\nThis stack is intended for local development.\n\nThe Compose file binds PostgreSQL to:\n\n```text\n127.0.0.1\n```\n\nrather than all network interfaces.\n\nStill:\n\n- change the default password\n- do not commit `.env`\n- do not expose port `5432` publicly\n- use a separate database user with restricted permissions if an untrusted agent/client can connect\n- back up the Docker volume if the accumulated project knowledge matters\n- never index secrets from source code in chunks, knowledge, or embeddings\n- treat embedding result files as source-derived data: keep them in `app/embedding-results/`, restrict access, and do not commit them unless the repository explicitly requires that\n- suppress stdout from the current embedding script because it prints the API key and input texts\n\n---\n\n## See Also\n\n- [Getting Started](./getting-started.md)\n- [Architecture and Retrieval Model](./architecture.md)\n- [Database Reference](./database.md)\n[← Agent Workflow](./agent-workflow.md) · [Back to README](../README.md) · [Security →](./security.md)\n\n# Integrations and References\n\n## 22. Suggested MCP surface for a future custom server\n\nA custom MCP server over this database could expose narrowly-scoped tools such as:\n\n```text\nproject_resolve\nsymbol_find\nsymbol_references\nsymbol_relations\ncode_search_text\ncode_search_semantic\nknowledge_search\ndocument_search\nindex_status\nindex_changed_files\nknowledge_upsert\n```\n\nAvoid exposing unrestricted SQL execution to an autonomous agent unless the environment is intentionally trusted.\n\nFor read tools, enforce `project_id` server-side.\n\nFor write tools, validate ownership and use transactions.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 24. Upstream references\n\n- AI Factory: https://github.com/lee-to/ai-factory\n- ast-grep: https://ast-grep.github.io/\n- Serena: https://github.com/oraios/serena\n- ripgrep: https://github.com/BurntSushi/ripgrep\n- Tree-sitter: https://tree-sitter.github.io/tree-sitter/\n- pgvector: https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector\n- Chrome DevTools MCP: https://github.com/ChromeDevTools/chrome-devtools-mcp\n\n---\n\n## See Also\n\n- [Getting Started](./getting-started.md)\n- [Architecture and Retrieval Model](./architecture.md)\n- [Database Reference](./database.md)\ntotal 56\ndrwxrwxr-x 7 kamaz kamaz 28672 Oct  6 15:38 .\ndrwxrwxr-x 5 kamaz kamaz  4096 Oct  3 14:30 ..\ndrwxrwxr-x 3 kamaz kamaz  4096 Oct  6 15:38 easyfit-pl\ndrwxrwxr-x 3 kamaz kamaz  4096 Oct  6 15:28 easyfit-ua\ndrwxrwxr-x 2 kamaz kamaz  4096 Oct  5 12:17 office-easyfit\ndrwxrwxr-x 3 kamaz kamaz  4096 Oct  6 18:46 pet-complaints-platform\ndrwxrwxr-x 2 kamaz kamaz  4096 Oct  5 12:15 yii3-office-easyfit\n/usr/bin/python3\ntree-sitter available\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":6703},"formatted_output":"Failed to create stream fd: Operation not permitted\nFailed to create stream fd: Operation not permitted\nFailed to create stream fd: Operation not permitted\n# Repository Guidelines\n\n## Project Structure & Module Organization\n\nThis repository packages a shared PostgreSQL database for code intelligence. The root `docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/` contains ordered SQL initialization scripts: extensions, schema, indexes, search functions, and vector helpers. `.docker/dev/` contains the local development image, PostgreSQL configuration, Compose file, and environment files. `ai-code-intelligence/` is the standalone deployment package with its own Dockerfile, Compose file, SQL initialization scripts, example environment file, and README. `app/` contains the small Node.js embedding utility and provider implementation. The root README documents the schema and recommended agent workflow.\n\n## Build, Test, and Development Commands\n\n- `make up-dev` starts the development PostgreSQL service.\n- `make down-dev` stops the development service; it does not remove stored volumes.\n- `make build-dev` builds the development image.\n- `make restart-dev` restarts the service; `make rebuild-dev` rebuilds and starts it.\n- `npm install --prefix app` installs the embedding utility dependencies.\n- `node app/embedding.js <API_KEY> text-embedding-3-small \"text to embed\"` generates embeddings.\n\nThere is no configured automated test or lint command. For database changes, review SQL ordering and validate initialization against a disposable local database. Do not use destructive volume/database cleanup commands against the shared development data.\n\n## Coding Style & Naming Conventions\n\nKeep SQL readable, use uppercase SQL keywords, explicit column lists, and descriptive `snake_case` identifiers. Preserve the numeric prefix and dependency order of initialization scripts (for example, `003_indexes.sql`). Use two spaces for JSON and JavaScript indentation, ES modules, and descriptive camelCase JavaScript names. Keep deployment examples aligned between the root and standalone package when both represent the same behavior.\n\n## Testing Guidelines\n\nNo dedicated test suite is present. Verify SQL changes by applying initialization on a clean local database and checking the affected tables, indexes, or functions. For changes to `app/`, run the relevant command manually and avoid committing generated credentials or real API keys.\n\n## Commit & Pull Request Guidelines\n\nGit history is unavailable in this checkout, so no established commit pattern could be confirmed. Use concise imperative commit subjects, such as `Add vector search helper index`. Pull requests should explain the schema or runtime impact, list validation performed, call out environment/configuration changes, and include logs or screenshots only when they clarify behavior.\n\n## Security & Configuration\n\nNever commit `.docker/dev/env/` secrets or expose their values in documentation and logs. Use `.env.example` for safe placeholders. Scope all project-specific database queries by `project_id` or resolve a project by slug first. Persist paid embedding results under `app/embedding-results/`; inspect and reuse saved batches before retries. The current embedding script echoes its API key and input texts to stdout, so suppress stdout. Create project HNSW indexes as the table owner, after committing vector rows.\n\n## Documentation\n\n| Document               | Path                             | Description                                   |\n|------------------------|----------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|\n| README                 | `README.md`                      | Project landing page and guide index          |\n| Getting Started        | `docs/getting-started.md`        | Local database startup and embedding setup    |\n| Architecture           | `docs/architecture.md`           | Retrieval model and system responsibilities   |\n| Database Reference     | `docs/database.md`               | Schema, ownership, and SQL examples           |\n| Retrieval and Indexing | `docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md` | Search, indexing, and code intelligence tools |\n| Agent Workflow         | `docs/agent-workflow.md`         | Investigation and verification practices      |\n| Integrations           | `docs/integrations.md`           | MCP surface and upstream references           |\n| Security               | `docs/security.md`               | Local database and data protection guidance   |\n\n## AI Context Files\n\n| File                          | Purpose                                    |\n|-------------------------------|--------------------------------------------|\n| `.ai-factory/DESCRIPTION.md`  | Project scope and technology summary       |\n| `.ai-factory/ARCHITECTURE.md` | Repository architecture guidelines         |\n| `.ai-factory/rules/base.md`   | Detected naming and structural conventions |# AI Code Intelligence Database\n\n> Local, multi-project PostgreSQL storage for AI-assisted software development.\n\nThis repository combines PostgreSQL full-text search, fuzzy lookup, pgvector retrieval, and relational code relationships. It includes a Docker-based development database and a small Node.js embedding utility. Always scope project data by `project_id` or resolve the project by slug.\n\n## Quick Start\n\nRequires Docker Compose. Start the local database:\n\n```bash\nmake up-dev\n```\n\nCheck the service state:\n\n```bash\ndocker compose -f .docker/dev/docker-compose.yml ps\n```\n\nThe embedding utility uses the OpenAI API and `text-embedding-3-small`; see [Getting Started](docs/getting-started.md) for safe setup details.\n\n## Capabilities\n\n- PostgreSQL 18 with pgvector, full-text search, and pg_trgm.\n- Project, file, symbol, relationship, document, chunk, embedding, and indexing-run storage.\n- Ordered database initialization scripts for local development and standalone deployment.\n- A hybrid retrieval workflow that combines exact, structural, semantic, and graph search.\n\n## Documentation\n\n| Guide                                                    | Description                                          |\n|----------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------|\n| [Getting Started](docs/getting-started.md)               | Local database startup and embedding setup           |\n| [Architecture](docs/architecture.md)                     | Retrieval model and system responsibilities          |\n| [Database Reference](docs/database.md)                   | Schema, ownership, and SQL examples                  |\n| [Retrieval and Indexing](docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md) | Search, indexing, and code intelligence tools        |\n| [Agent Workflow](docs/agent-workflow.md)                 | Investigation, verification, and knowledge practices |\n| [Integrations](docs/integrations.md)                     | MCP surface and upstream references                  |\n| [Security](docs/security.md)                             | Local database and data protection guidance          |[Back to README](../README.md) · [Architecture and Retrieval Model →](./architecture.md)\n\n# Getting Started\n\n## Safe embedding workflow\n\n- Development environment files live in `.docker/dev/env/`. Never copy their values into docs, chat, logs, or command output.\n- The database container runs PostgreSQL and Node.js. Tools such as `/home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep`, Tree-sitter Python bindings, and Serena/LSP may be installed on the host; check the host environment and the exact Python interpreter instead of assuming they must exist in Docker.\n- Keep paid embedding results in the host directory `app/embedding-results/project-name/{year}/{month}/`, mounted in the container at `/app/embedding-results/project-name/{year}/{month}/`. Use stable batch filenames, check which files already exist before retrying, and resume from completed batches. Do not store paid results only in `/tmp` or another ephemeral container path.\n- The current `app/embedding.js` prints its API key and input texts to standard output. Suppress stdout for the embedding call; do not share captured logs. Only index sanitized, non-secret source content.\n- Use `text-embedding-3-small` for the existing model configuration. Keep batches bounded and persist each result file before inserting vectors into PostgreSQL.\n- The restricted `$POSTGRES_SIMPLE_USER` can write project records but may not own `aic_embeddings`. Create project/model HNSW indexes as `$POSTGRES_USER` (table owner). Commit vector rows before index creation and mark the indexing run complete only after the index is verified.\n- Never run `docker system prune`, `docker volume rm`, or `DROP DATABASE` against the shared development data. Before retrying, inspect project counts, run status, and saved embedding files; do not delete a project as a shortcut.\n\n### Run SQL through the PostgreSQL container\n\nFrom the ai-code-intelligence repository root, execute SQL with `psql` inside the database container:\n\n```bash\ndocker compose -f .docker/dev/docker-compose.yml exec -T \\\n  ai-code-intelligence-postgres-postgresql sh -lc \\\n  'psql -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -P pager=off -U \"$POSTGRES_SIMPLE_USER\" -d \"$POSTGRES_DB\" -c \"SELECT slug, id FROM aic_projects ORDER BY slug;\"'\n```\n\nUse `$POSTGRES_SIMPLE_USER` for normal project-scoped reads and writes. Resolve the target project's ID by slug, then include `project_id` in queries against its files, chunks, and embeddings. For vector and HNSW queries, see [Retrieval and Indexing](./retrieval-and-indexing.md).\n\nExample for one sanitized batch:\n\n```bash\ndocker compose -f /home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/.docker/dev/docker-compose.yml \\\n  exec -T ai-code-intelligence-postgres-postgresql sh -lc '\n    node /app/embedding.js \"$OPENAI_API_KEY\" \\\n      /app/embedding-results/project-name/{year}/{month}/example-batch-0000.json \\\n      text-embedding-3-small \"class OrderService {}\" >/dev/null\n  '\n```\n\nThe embedding file persists on the host at `/app/embedding-results/project-name/{year}/{month}/example-batch-0000.json`.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Database startup\n\n### Files about DB tables\n\nDB tables schemas:\ndocker-entrypoint-initdb.d:\n - 001_extensions.sql\n - 002_schema.sql\n - 003_indexes.sql\n - 004_views_and_search_functions.sql\n - 005_vector_index_helpers.sql\n\n---\n\n## See Also\n\n- [Architecture and Retrieval Model](./architecture.md)\n- [Database Reference](./database.md)\n- [Retrieval and Indexing](./retrieval-and-indexing.md)[← Getting Started](./getting-started.md) · [Back to README](../README.md) · [Database Reference →](./database.md)\n\n# Architecture and Retrieval Model\n\nThis page explains the code-intelligence retrieval model. For repository layers, SQL initialization order, deployment boundaries, and the embedding utility, see the [repository architecture guide](../.ai-factory/ARCHITECTURE.md).\n\n## 1. Architecture\n\n```text\nRepositories\n    |\n    +----------------------+----------------------+\n    |                      |                      |\n    v                      v                      v\nTree-sitter            Serena / LSP           Git\nAST + chunks           symbols/types          history\n    |                      |                      |\n    +----------------------+----------+-----------+\n                                       |\n                                       v\n                              Index / Context Layer\n                                       |\n               +-----------------------+-----------------------+\n               |                       |                       |\n               v                       v                       v\n          PostgreSQL FTS          pgvector RAG          relational graph\n               |                       |                       |\n               +-----------------------+-----------------------+\n                                       |\n                                       v\n                                  AI Agent\n                                       |\n                                       v\n                                  AI Factory\n                           plan -> implement -> verify\n```\n\n### Tool responsibilities\n\n| Tool                                                                     | Primary responsibility                                                                                                                     | Do not use it as                                     |\n|--------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------|\n| AI Factory                                                               | Project context, rules, spec-driven workflow, planning, implementation, verification                                                       | A code parser or search engine                       |\n| Serena                                                                   | Symbol-level semantic navigation, references, editing, refactoring, LSP-backed code intelligence                                           | A replacement for semantic RAG                       |\n| ripgrep (`rg`)                                                           | Fast exact text/regex search                                                                                                               | Structural AST matching                              |\n| /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep (`sg` / `/home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep`) | AST-aware structural search, linting, safe rewrite/codemod patterns                                                                        | Semantic business-language search                    |\n| Tree-sitter                                                              | Parsing, syntax trees, language-aware chunk boundaries, extraction input                                                                   | A complete type system or runtime call graph         |\n| PostgreSQL + pgvector                                                    | Persistent multi-project RAG, metadata, graph data, FTS, fuzzy lookup                                                                      | Proof that retrieved code is correct                 |\n| Git                                                                      | Diff/history/blame/commit context and change isolation                                                                                     | A static analyzer                                    |\n| Language servers                                                         | Definition/reference/type/navigation data                                                                                                  | Persistent RAG storage                               |\n| Tests/static analyzers                                                   | Verification after changes                                                                                                                 | Discovery/indexing layer                             |\n| `cua_repl` / `node_repl`                                                 | Primary runtime browser/UI inspection: rendered page, interaction, DOM, styles, geometry, console, network, screenshots, responsive states | Static source-code understanding                     |\n| Chrome DevTools MCP                                                      | Alternative browser/runtime inspection layer for DOM, CSS, console, network, performance, screenshots, and interaction                     | Persistent project knowledge or source-code indexing |\n\nRecommended verification tools depend on the project. Examples include PHPUnit/Pest, PHPStan/Psalm/PHPCS/PHP CS Fixer, ESLint, TypeScript, Vue/Nuxt type checks, Composer scripts, and application-specific integration tests.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 2. Why this is hybrid retrieval, not \"vector search only\"\n\nAn AI agent must select the search mechanism according to the question.\n\nUse **exact/symbol search** for questions such as:\n\n```text\nWhere is PaymentService::processPayment() called?\n```\n\nUse **structural search** for questions such as:\n\n```text\nFind all PHP methods that dispatch an event from inside a transaction.\n```\n\nUse **semantic/vector retrieval** for questions such as:\n\n```text\nWhere is failed payment retry logic implemented?\n```\n\nUse **graph traversal** for questions such as:\n\n```text\nWhat may be affected if PaymentGatewayInterface changes?\n```\n\nVector results identify candidates. They do **not** prove control flow, references, type relationships, or runtime behavior. Always verify important findings against source code and, when applicable, LSP/Serena, PostgreSQL `symbol_relations`, tests, and Git history.\n\n---\n\n## See Also\n\n- [Getting Started](./getting-started.md)\n- [Database Reference](./database.md)\n- [Retrieval and Indexing](./retrieval-and-indexing.md)[← Architecture and Retrieval Model](./architecture.md) · [Back to README](../README.md) · [Retrieval and Indexing →](./retrieval-and-indexing.md)\n\n# Database Reference\n\n## 6. PostgreSQL extensions\n\n`001_extensions.sql` enables:\n\n| Extension  | Purpose                                       |\n|------------|-----------------------------------------------|\n| `vector`   | Embedding storage and nearest-neighbor search |\n| `pgcrypto` | `gen_random_uuid()`                           |\n| `pg_trgm`  | Fuzzy symbol/path lookup                      |\n| `unaccent` | Optional normalization support                |\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 7. DDL model\n\nThe authoritative DDL is in `002_schema.sql`.\n\n### `projects`\n\nRoot tenant/entity for all stored projects.\n\n| Column           | Type        | Purpose                       |\n|------------------|-------------|-------------------------------|\n| `id`             | UUID PK     | Project identifier            |\n| `name`           | TEXT        | Human-readable name           |\n| `slug`           | TEXT UNIQUE | Stable local identifier       |\n| `root_path`      | TEXT        | Local repository path         |\n| `repository_url` | TEXT        | Optional Git remote           |\n| `default_branch` | TEXT        | Default branch                |\n| `active`         | BOOLEAN     | Whether the project is active |\n| `metadata`       | JSONB       | Project-specific metadata     |\n| timestamps       | TIMESTAMPTZ | Audit timestamps              |\n\nAll project-owned data cascades when a project is deleted. Composite project-scoped foreign keys also prevent accidental cross-project links between files, symbols, chunks, documents, knowledge, and embeddings.\n\n### `files`\n\nOne row per indexed file in a project.\n\nImportant columns:\n\n```text\nproject_id\npath\nlanguage\nfile_type\ncontent_hash\ngit_blob_sha\nlast_indexed_commit\nis_generated\nis_deleted\nindexed_at\n```\n\nUnique key:\n\n```text\n(project_id, path)\n```\n\n### `symbols`\n\nTree-sitter/LSP/Serena-derived symbols.\n\nTypical `kind` values:\n\n```text\nnamespace\nmodule\nclass\ninterface\ntrait\nenum\nmethod\nfunction\nproperty\nconstant\ntype\ncomponent\n```\n\nImportant columns:\n\n```text\nproject_id\nfile_id\nparent_symbol_id\nkind\nname\nqualified_name\nsignature\nvisibility\nstart_line/start_column\nend_line/end_column\ncontent_hash\nmetadata\n```\n\nThe schema intentionally does not constrain `kind` to a fixed enum because language ecosystems differ.\n\n### `symbol_relations`\n\nEdges in the relational code graph.\n\nTypical `relation_type` values:\n\n```text\ncalls\nreferences\nimports\nextends\nimplements\nuses\ndispatches\nlistens\npublishes\nsubscribes\nreads\nwrites\ninstantiates\n```\n\n`target_symbol_id` can be `NULL` for an unresolved external or not-yet-resolved target. In that case `target_qualified_name` must be present.\n\n`confidence` allows framework heuristics to be stored without pretending that every inferred edge is certain.\n\n### `documents`\n\nProject documentation and external/local reference material.\n\nExamples:\n\n```text\nREADME\nADR\nOpenAPI document\ninternal architecture document\nvendor API reference\nAI Factory generated research\n```\n\nA document must have either `path` or `source_uri`.\n\n### `knowledge_items`\n\nDurable, explicit project knowledge.\n\nRecommended `kind` values:\n\n```text\narchitecture\nbusiness_rule\nconstraint\nconvention\ndecision\nincident\nintegration\nsecurity\ndeployment\n```\n\nUse this table for concise, evidence-backed facts that should persist across agent sessions.\n\nDo not put temporary guesses here.\n\n### `chunks`\n\nCanonical RAG chunk table for all source types.\n\n`source_type` is one of:\n\n```text\ncode\ndocument\nknowledge\n```\n\nA chunk points to exactly one primary source:\n\n```text\ncode      -> file_id (+ optional symbol_id)\ndocument  -> document_id\nknowledge -> knowledge_item_id\n```\n\nImportant columns:\n\n```text\nchunk_type\nsequence_number\nstart_line/end_line\ncontent\ncontent_hash\ntoken_count\nmetadata\nsearch_vector\n```\n\n`search_vector` is generated automatically for PostgreSQL Full Text Search.\n\n### `embedding_models`\n\nRegistry of embedding configurations.\n\n```text\nprovider\nmodel\ndimensions\ndistance_metric\nenabled\n```\n\nSupported `distance_metric` values in this schema:\n\n```text\ncosine\nl2\ninner_product\n```\n\nThe model registry avoids hard-coding a single embedding dimension into the schema.\n\n### `embeddings`\n\nEmbeddings for chunks.\n\n```text\nproject_id\nchunk_id\nembedding_model_id\ndimensions\nembedding\nsource_content_hash\n```\n\nThe `embedding` column uses unconstrained `VECTOR`, allowing different models with different dimensions in one table.\n\n`source_content_hash` is critical: if it differs from the current chunk hash, the embedding is stale and must be regenerated.\n\n### `indexing_runs`\n\nTracks indexing/synchronization operations.\n\nTypical sequence:\n\n```text\nrunning\n    ->\ncompleted | failed | cancelled\n```\n\nStore counts and timing details in `stats`.\n\n---\n[← Database Reference](./database.md) · [Back to README](../README.md) · [Agent Workflow →](./agent-workflow.md)\n\n# Retrieval and Indexing\n\n## 4. Search decision matrix\n\n| Need                             | Preferred tool                                    |\n|----------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------|\n| Exact string/error/config key    | ripgrep                                           |\n| Class/method definition          | Serena/LSP                                        |\n| Find references/implementations  | Serena/LSP                                        |\n| Caller/callee path               | Serena/LSP + relational graph                     |\n| Blast radius                     | PostgreSQL relational graph                       |\n| AST code shape                   | /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep                    |\n| Large codemod                    | /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep + Git diff + tests |\n| Syntax-aware chunk boundaries    | Tree-sitter                                       |\n| Unknown business concept         | FTS + pgvector                                    |\n| Documentation/ADR/business rules | PostgreSQL RAG                                    |\n| Why/when code changed            | Git                                               |\n| Prove correctness                | tests/static analysis                             |\n| Rendered UI/layout/usability     | `cua_repl` / `node_repl`                          |\n| DOM/computed styles/geometry     | `cua_repl` / `node_repl`                          |\n| Browser console/runtime errors   | `cua_repl` / `node_repl`                          |\n| Network/API behavior in browser  | `cua_repl` / `node_repl`                          |\n| Browser runtime alternative      | Chrome DevTools MCP                               |\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## Reliable end-to-end indexing\n\nIndexing crosses host tools, a Docker container, a paid embedding API, and PostgreSQL. Verify each boundary explicitly:\n\n1. Check host tools with `command -v /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep`, `serena --version`, and the Python interpreter that has `tree_sitter_language_pack`. The AI database container is expected to provide PostgreSQL and Node.js, not every parser or language server.\n2. Run Serena/LSP indexing locally. A first run may try to download a language server (for PHP, Intelephense); DNS or registry failures indicate a tool-installation problem. Retry after network access is available and distinguish this from source parse errors.\n3. Parse a small representative file before processing the whole repository. Some Tree-sitter bindings can fail in batch traversal. If a fallback parser or line chunking is used, record the fallback and resulting limitations; do not label those chunks syntax-aware. Populate `symbol_relations` only when edges were actually derived.\n4. Resolve the project by slug and scope every file, symbol, chunk, knowledge, embedding, and run query by `project_id`. Exclude credentials, environment files, generated/runtime content, and any source not approved for the shared database.\n5. Generate embeddings in bounded batches. `app/embedding.js` currently prints the API key and input text to stdout, so suppress stdout and keep command logs private. Persist each batch under `/app/embedding-results/{year}/{month}/` (container path `/app/embedding-results/{year}/{month}/`) before starting another batch. Never rely on container `/tmp` for paid results.\n6. Before retrying, compare existing result files, chunk hashes, project counts, model ID, and indexing-run state. Reuse completed batches; do not call the API again for unchanged input. Keep a manifest mapping batch files to chunk IDs/content hashes when building a new index.\n7. Commit embeddings first. Then create the project/model HNSW index with `aic_create_project_embedding_hnsw_index(...)` using the table-owner role. The restricted `$POSTGRES_SIMPLE_USER` may insert rows but fail with `must be owner of table aic_embeddings` when creating the index. Mark the run `completed` only after the index exists and counts are checked.\n\nA failed index-creation statement inside the same transaction as vector inserts rolls back those inserts. Check database state before recovery; preserve saved batch files and resume the database write without generating embeddings again. Avoid broad deletes or project recreation when recovering partial runs.\n\n### Office Easyfit indexing record (2026-10-02)\n\nThe `office-easyfit` project uses `text-embedding-3-small` (1536 dimensions) and a project-specific HNSW index. The completed run contains 1,182 PHP files, 4,221 approximate symbol rows, 3,896 chunks/embeddings, four documents, and four knowledge items. Configuration, test, runtime, vendor/dependency, web asset/upload paths, and files matching the credential-like literal filter were excluded. Serena indexed 964 PHP files. The Tree-sitter Python binding segfaulted during batch node-field extraction, so code chunks used 80-line boundaries and symbol names used a declaration-line fallback; symbol relations were not populated. Use vector and symbol results as candidates and verify findings in the original source. The 325 embedding result JSON files were persisted under `app/embedding-results/`. This run did not produce a separate batch-to-chunk manifest; the database rows are complete, so do not regenerate them. Future runs should persist that mapping alongside each batch file.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Search rules\n\n### Rule 1 — Always scope by project\n\nBad:\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM chunks\nLIMIT 20;\n```\n\nGood:\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM chunks\nWHERE project_id = :project_id\nLIMIT 20;\n```\n\nCross-project retrieval is allowed only when the task explicitly requires it.\n\n### Rule 2 — Prefer exact symbol lookup before fuzzy/vector search\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n    s.*,\n    f.path\nFROM symbols s\nJOIN files f ON f.id = s.file_id\nWHERE s.project_id = :project_id\n  AND s.qualified_name = :qualified_name;\n```\n\n### Rule 3 — Fuzzy symbol search for uncertain names\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM search_symbols_fuzzy(\n    :project_id,\n    'PaymentServce',\n    20\n);\n```\n\n### Rule 4 — Full Text Search for lexical/business terms\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM search_chunks_text(\n    :project_id,\n    'failed payment retry',\n    20\n);\n```\n\n### Rule 5 — Exact vector retrieval\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM search_chunks_vector_exact(\n    :project_id,\n    :embedding_model_id,\n    :query_embedding::vector,\n    20\n);\n```\n\nThe application is responsible for generating `:query_embedding` with the same model used for stored embeddings.\n\n### Rule 6 — Graph traversal\n\nOutgoing dependencies:\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM symbol_relations_walk(\n    :project_id,\n    :symbol_id,\n    'outgoing',\n    3\n);\n```\n\nIncoming dependants / impact candidates:\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM symbol_relations_walk(\n    :project_id,\n    :symbol_id,\n    'incoming',\n    3\n);\n```\n\nTreat heuristic relations according to their `confidence`.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 12. Vector indexing\n\nA single `VECTOR` column can store embeddings of different dimensions. Approximate vector indexes, however, must target rows with a consistent dimension.\n\nThis repository therefore does **not** create one global HNSW index automatically.\n\nAfter registering an embedding model:\n\n```sql\nSELECT id, provider, model, dimensions\nFROM embedding_models;\n```\n\nCreate a **project + model specific** HNSW index:\n\n```sql\nSELECT create_project_embedding_hnsw_index(\n    :project_id,\n    :embedding_model_id\n);\n```\n\nProject-specific partial indexes are intentional. In a shared multi-project database, a single approximate index shared by all projects can lose recall when PostgreSQL applies `project_id` filtering after ANN candidate selection.\n\nThe helper uses:\n\n- `vector(...)` for dimensions up to 2000\n- `halfvec(...)` for dimensions from 2001 through 4000\n\nFor larger vectors, choose a deliberate strategy such as dimensionality reduction, binary quantization, or another model.\n\nTo remove an automatically named project/model index:\n\n```sql\nSELECT drop_project_embedding_hnsw_index(\n    :project_id,\n    :embedding_model_id\n);\n```\n\n### Important query note\n\nAn expression HNSW index is useful only when the retrieval query uses the corresponding cast/expression and matches the partial-index predicate. `search_chunks_vector_exact()` is deliberately an exact, generic fallback.\n\nFor high-volume approximate retrieval, use the helper after creating the HNSW index:\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM search_chunks_vector_hnsw(\n    :project_id,\n    :embedding_model_id,\n    :query_embedding::vector,\n    20\n);\n```\n\nThis function uses the same `vector(n)` or `halfvec(n)` expression as the model-specific HNSW index.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 13. Recommended hybrid RAG flow\n\nFor a natural-language question:\n\n```text\n\"Where is failed payment retry logic implemented?\"\n```\n\nRecommended pipeline:\n\n```text\n1. Resolve project_id\n2. FTS top N\n3. Vector top N\n4. Fuzzy symbol candidates\n5. Optional graph expansion around strong symbol candidates\n6. Merge / rerank candidates\n7. Read original source\n8. Verify references with Serena/LSP and PostgreSQL `symbol_relations`\n9. Answer or modify code\n```\n\nA practical RRF-style merge can happen in application code. Do not blindly concatenate dozens of chunks into the LLM context.\n\nPrefer small, evidence-rich context.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 14. Tree-sitter indexing rules\n\nTree-sitter should primarily produce structural metadata and semantic chunk boundaries.\n\nDo not embed a serialized raw AST unless a specific experiment proves it useful.\n\nPreferred code chunk:\n\n```text\nLanguage: PHP\nFile: app/Services/PaymentService.php\nNamespace: App\\Services\nClass: PaymentService\nMethod: processPayment\nSignature: processPayment(Order $order): Payment\nLines: 34-81\n\n<source code>\n```\n\nRecommended chunk boundaries:\n\n```text\nclass/interface/trait/enum\nmethod/function\ncomponent\ntemplate block\nconfiguration object\nquery/migration unit\n```\n\nFor very large symbols, split only after preserving:\n\n- symbol identity\n- parent symbol\n[← Retrieval and Indexing](./retrieval-and-indexing.md) · [Back to README](../README.md) · [Integrations and References →](./integrations.md)\n\n# Agent Workflow\n\n## 3. Required agent workflow\n\nThe following order is the default investigation workflow for a large project.\n\n### Phase A — Establish project scope\n\n1. Determine the repository root.\n2. Run `git status`.\n3. Determine the current branch and commit.\n4. Resolve the database project row by `slug` or `root_path`.\n5. Never query another project's rows accidentally.\n6. Load AI Factory project rules/context before modifying code.\n\nRecommended AI Factory entry points include:\n\n```text\n/aif-warmup\n/aif-grounded <question>\n/aif-explore <topic>\n/aif-plan <task>\n/aif-fix <problem>\n/aif-implement\n/aif-verify\n/aif-review\n```\n\nExact commands depend on the installed AI Factory version and configured agent.\n\n### Phase B — Search before reading broadly\n\nUse the cheapest precise source first:\n\n1. **Known symbol** -> Serena/LSP.\n2. **Call/dependency question** -> Serena/LSP and PostgreSQL `symbol_relations`.\n3. **Known literal, class name, route, config key, error text** -> `rg`.\n4. **Known code shape** -> /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep.\n5. **Unknown terminology / business concept** -> PostgreSQL FTS + vector retrieval.\n6. **Need syntax boundaries/chunk extraction** -> Tree-sitter.\n7. Read only the files/symbols returned by the search.\n\nDo not scan the whole repository if a precise search can narrow the scope.\n\n### Phase C — Build an evidence chain\n\nFor every non-trivial bug or feature, identify:\n\n```text\nentry point\n    ->\ncontroller/handler/command/job\n    ->\nservice/domain logic\n    ->\nrepository/model/external integration\n    ->\nevent/listener/queue/callback\n    ->\ntests\n```\n\nRecord unresolved questions instead of inventing relationships.\n\n### Phase D — Validate history when behavior is suspicious\n\nUse:\n\n```bash\ngit log -- <file>\ngit blame <file>\ngit show <commit>\ngit diff\n```\n\nHistory is especially useful for workarounds, regressions, legacy compatibility, and business decisions that are not obvious from current code.\n\n### Phase E — Modify minimally\n\n1. Follow project conventions.\n2. Prefer the smallest coherent change.\n3. Use symbol-aware edits for refactors where available.\n4. Use /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep rewrites only when the AST pattern is verified.\n5. Review `git diff` immediately after automated rewrites.\n6. Do not update persistent knowledge until the final behavior is verified.\n\n### Phase F — Verify\n\nRun the narrowest relevant tests first, then broader quality gates.\n\nTypical PHP/Laravel sequence:\n\n```bash\nphp artisan test --filter=<relevant-test>\nvendor/bin/phpstan analyse\nvendor/bin/phpcs\nvendor/bin/phpunit\n```\n\nTypical Nuxt/TypeScript sequence:\n\n```bash\nnpm run lint\nnpm run typecheck\nnpm test\nnpm run build\n```\n\nUse the project's actual scripts when they differ.\n\n### Phase G — Update index/knowledge\n\nAfter a verified change:\n\n1. Update changed `files`.\n2. Re-extract changed `symbols`.\n3. Replace affected `symbol_relations`.\n4. Rebuild affected `chunks`.\n5. Regenerate embeddings only for chunks whose `content_hash` changed.\n6. Store durable architecture/business knowledge only when it is supported by project evidence.\n7. Finish the `indexing_runs` row.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 19. Git rules for AI agents\n\nBefore editing:\n\n```bash\ngit status --short\ngit branch --show-current\ngit rev-parse HEAD\n```\n\nAfter editing:\n\n```bash\ngit diff --check\ngit diff\n```\n\nFor regressions/history:\n\n```bash\ngit log --oneline -- <path>\ngit blame <path>\ngit show <commit> -- <path>\n```\n\nNever discard existing user changes merely to make the working tree clean.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 20. Knowledge-writing policy\n\n`knowledge_items` must contain durable, evidence-backed information.\n\nGood:\n\n```text\n\"Payment webhooks are idempotent by provider_transaction_id.\"\n```\n\nBad:\n\n```text\n\"I think duplicate payments may come from Redis.\"\n```\n\nRecommended fields:\n\n```text\nkind = architecture | business_rule | decision | constraint | ...\nstatus = active\nconfidence = 0.0..1.0\nsource_reference = file/ADR/issue/commit/reference\n```\n\nWhen a fact changes, prefer marking the old row `superseded` and adding the new knowledge item rather than silently rewriting history.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 25. Final rule for the AI agent\n\nThe agent should follow this principle:\n\n> Retrieve broadly enough to discover candidates, but verify narrowly against authoritative source code, symbol relationships, tests, and Git before drawing conclusions or changing code.\n\nThe database is long-term project intelligence, not a substitute for the repository itself.\n\n## See Also\n\n- [Getting Started](./getting-started.md)\n- [Architecture and Retrieval Model](./architecture.md)\n- [Database Reference](./database.md)[← Integrations and References](./integrations.md) · [Back to README](../README.md)\n\n# Security\n\n## 23. Security\n\nThis stack is intended for local development.\n\nThe Compose file binds PostgreSQL to:\n\n```text\n127.0.0.1\n```\n\nrather than all network interfaces.\n\nStill:\n\n- change the default password\n- do not commit `.env`\n- do not expose port `5432` publicly\n- use a separate database user with restricted permissions if an untrusted agent/client can connect\n- back up the Docker volume if the accumulated project knowledge matters\n- never index secrets from source code in chunks, knowledge, or embeddings\n- treat embedding result files as source-derived data: keep them in `app/embedding-results/`, restrict access, and do not commit them unless the repository explicitly requires that\n- suppress stdout from the current embedding script because it prints the API key and input texts\n\n---\n\n## See Also\n\n- [Getting Started](./getting-started.md)\n- [Architecture and Retrieval Model](./architecture.md)\n- [Database Reference](./database.md)\n[← Agent Workflow](./agent-workflow.md) · [Back to README](../README.md) · [Security →](./security.md)\n\n# Integrations and References\n\n## 22. Suggested MCP surface for a future custom server\n\nA custom MCP server over this database could expose narrowly-scoped tools such as:\n\n```text\nproject_resolve\nsymbol_find\nsymbol_references\nsymbol_relations\ncode_search_text\ncode_search_semantic\nknowledge_search\ndocument_search\nindex_status\nindex_changed_files\nknowledge_upsert\n```\n\nAvoid exposing unrestricted SQL execution to an autonomous agent unless the environment is intentionally trusted.\n\nFor read tools, enforce `project_id` server-side.\n\nFor write tools, validate ownership and use transactions.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 24. Upstream references\n\n- AI Factory: https://github.com/lee-to/ai-factory\n- ast-grep: https://ast-grep.github.io/\n- Serena: https://github.com/oraios/serena\n- ripgrep: https://github.com/BurntSushi/ripgrep\n- Tree-sitter: https://tree-sitter.github.io/tree-sitter/\n- pgvector: https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector\n- Chrome DevTools MCP: https://github.com/ChromeDevTools/chrome-devtools-mcp\n\n---\n\n## See Also\n\n- [Getting Started](./getting-started.md)\n- [Architecture and Retrieval Model](./architecture.md)\n- [Database Reference](./database.md)\ntotal 56\ndrwxrwxr-x 7 kamaz kamaz 28672 Oct  6 15:38 .\ndrwxrwxr-x 5 kamaz kamaz  4096 Oct  3 14:30 ..\ndrwxrwxr-x 3 kamaz kamaz  4096 Oct  6 15:38 easyfit-pl\ndrwxrwxr-x 3 kamaz kamaz  4096 Oct  6 15:28 easyfit-ua\ndrwxrwxr-x 2 kamaz kamaz  4096 Oct  5 12:17 office-easyfit\ndrwxrwxr-x 3 kamaz kamaz  4096 Oct  6 18:46 pet-complaints-platform\ndrwxrwxr-x 2 kamaz kamaz  4096 Oct  5 12:15 yii3-office-easyfit\n/usr/bin/python3\ntree-sitter available\n"},"started_at_ms":1791359770854,"completed_at_ms":1791359770854}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-07T07:56:10.858Z","ordinal":10765,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a1155c-e0ea-7f33-bf8b-6fa3d4eeee81","call_id":"call_6425b868871c4d5186d0808319856ebf","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.2 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"Failed to create stream fd: Operation not permitted\nFailed to create stream fd: Operation not permitted\nFailed to create stream fd: Operation not permitted\n# Repository Guidelines\n\n## Project Structure & Module Organization\n\nThis repository packages a shared PostgreSQL database for code intelligence. The root `docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/` contains ordered SQL initialization scripts: extensions, schema, indexes, search functions, and vector helpers. `.docker/dev/` contains the local development image, PostgreSQL configuration, Compose file, and environment files. `ai-code-intelligence/` is the standalone deployment package with its own Dockerfile, Compose file, SQL initialization scripts, example environment file, and README. `app/` contains the small Node.js embedding utility and provider implementation. The root README documents the schema and recommended agent workflow.\n\n## Build, Test, and Development Commands\n\n- `make up-dev` starts the development PostgreSQL service.\n- `make down-dev` stops the development service; it does not remove stored volumes.\n- `make build-dev` builds the development image.\n- `make restart-dev` restarts the service; `make rebuild-dev` rebuilds and starts it.\n- `npm install --prefix app` installs the embedding utility dependencies.\n- `node app/embedding.js <API_KEY> text-embedding-3-small \"text to embed\"` generates embeddings.\n\nThere is no configured automated test or lint command. For database changes, review SQL ordering and validate initialization against a disposable local database. Do not use destructive volume/database cleanup commands against the shared development data.\n\n## Coding Style & Naming Conventions\n\nKeep SQL readable, use uppercase SQL keywords, explicit column lists, and descriptive `snake_case` identifiers. Preserve the numeric prefix and dependency order of initialization scripts (for example, `003_indexes.sql`). Use two spaces for JSON and JavaScript indentation, ES modules, and descriptive camelCase JavaScript names. Keep deployment examples aligned between the root and standalone package when both represent the same behavior.\n\n## Testing Guidelines\n\nNo dedicated test suite is present. Verify SQL changes by applying initialization on a clean local database and checking the affected tables, indexes, or functions. For changes to `app/`, run the relevant command manually and avoid committing generated credentials or real API keys.\n\n## Commit & Pull Request Guidelines\n\nGit history is unavailable in this checkout, so no established commit pattern could be confirmed. Use concise imperative commit subjects, such as `Add vector search helper index`. Pull requests should explain the schema or runtime impact, list validation performed, call out environment/configuration changes, and include logs or screenshots only when they clarify behavior.\n\n## Security & Configuration\n\nNever commit `.docker/dev/env/` secrets or expose their values in documentation and logs. Use `.env.example` for safe placeholders. Scope all project-specific database queries by `project_id` or resolve a project by slug first. Persist paid embedding results under `app/embedding-results/`; inspect and reuse saved batches before retries. The current embedding script echoes its API key and input texts to stdout, so suppress stdout. Create project HNSW indexes as the table owner, after committing vector rows.\n\n## Documentation\n\n| Document               | Path                             | Description                                   |\n|------------------------|----------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|\n| README                 | `README.md`                      | Project landing page and guide index          |\n| Getting Started        | `docs/getting-started.md`        | Local database startup and embedding setup    |\n| Architecture           | `docs/architecture.md`           | Retrieval model and system responsibilities   |\n| Database Reference     | `docs/database.md`               | Schema, ownership, and SQL examples           |\n| Retrieval and Indexing | `docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md` | Search, indexing, and code intelligence tools |\n| Agent Workflow         | `docs/agent-workflow.md`         | Investigation and verification practices      |\n| Integrations           | `docs/integrations.md`           | MCP surface and upstream references           |\n| Security               | `docs/security.md`               | Local database and data protection guidance   |\n\n## AI Context Files\n\n| File                          | Purpose                                    |\n|-------------------------------|--------------------------------------------|\n| `.ai-factory/DESCRIPTION.md`  | Project scope and technology summary       |\n| `.ai-factory/ARCHITECTURE.md` | Repository architecture guidelines         |\n| `.ai-factory/rules/base.md`   | Detected naming and structural conventions |# AI Code Intelligence Database\n\n> Local, multi-project PostgreSQL storage for AI-assisted software development.\n\nThis repository combines PostgreSQL full-text search, fuzzy lookup, pgvector retrieval, and relational code relationships. It includes a Docker-based development database and a small Node.js embedding utility. Always scope project data by `project_id` or resolve the project by slug.\n\n## Quick Start\n\nRequires Docker Compose. Start the local database:\n\n```bash\nmake up-dev\n```\n\nCheck the service state:\n\n```bash\ndocker compose -f .docker/dev/docker-compose.yml ps\n```\n\nThe embedding utility uses the OpenAI API and `text-embedding-3-small`; see [Getting Started](docs/getting-started.md) for safe setup details.\n\n## Capabilities\n\n- PostgreSQL 18 with pgvector, full-text search, and pg_trgm.\n- Project, file, symbol, relationship, document, chunk, embedding, and indexing-run storage.\n- Ordered database initialization scripts for local development and standalone deployment.\n- A hybrid retrieval workflow that combines exact, structural, semantic, and graph search.\n\n## Documentation\n\n| Guide                                                    | Description                                          |\n|----------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------|\n| [Getting Started](docs/getting-started.md)               | Local database startup and embedding setup           |\n| [Architecture](docs/architecture.md)                     | Retrieval model and system responsibilities          |\n| [Database Reference](docs/database.md)                   | Schema, ownership, and SQL examples                  |\n| [Retrieval and Indexing](docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md) | Search, indexing, and code intelligence tools        |\n| [Agent Workflow](docs/agent-workflow.md)                 | Investigation, verification, and knowledge practices |\n| [Integrations](docs/integrations.md)                     | MCP surface and upstream references                  |\n| [Security](docs/security.md)                             | Local database and data protection guidance          |[Back to README](../README.md) · [Architecture and Retrieval Model →](./architecture.md)\n\n# Getting Started\n\n## Safe embedding workflow\n\n- Development environment files live in `.docker/dev/env/`. Never copy their values into docs, chat, logs, or command output.\n- The database container runs PostgreSQL and Node.js. Tools such as `/home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep`, Tree-sitter Python bindings, and Serena/LSP may be installed on the host; check the host environment and the exact Python interpreter instead of assuming they must exist in Docker.\n- Keep paid embedding results in the host directory `app/embedding-results/project-name/{year}/{month}/`, mounted in the container at `/app/embedding-results/project-name/{year}/{month}/`. Use stable batch filenames, check which files already exist before retrying, and resume from completed batches. Do not store paid results only in `/tmp` or another ephemeral container path.\n- The current `app/embedding.js` prints its API key and input texts to standard output. Suppress stdout for the embedding call; do not share captured logs. Only index sanitized, non-secret source content.\n- Use `text-embedding-3-small` for the existing model configuration. Keep batches bounded and persist each result file before inserting vectors into PostgreSQL.\n- The restricted `$POSTGRES_SIMPLE_USER` can write project records but may not own `aic_embeddings`. Create project/model HNSW indexes as `$POSTGRES_USER` (table owner). Commit vector rows before index creation and mark the indexing run complete only after the index is verified.\n- Never run `docker system prune`, `docker volume rm`, or `DROP DATABASE` against the shared development data. Before retrying, inspect project counts, run status, and saved embedding files; do not delete a project as a shortcut.\n\n### Run SQL through the PostgreSQL container\n\nFrom the ai-code-intelligence repository root, execute SQL with `psql` inside the database container:\n\n```bash\ndocker compose -f .docker/dev/docker-compose.yml exec -T \\\n  ai-code-intelligence-postgres-postgresql sh -lc \\\n  'psql -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -P pager=off -U \"$POSTGRES_SIMPLE_USER\" -d \"$POSTGRES_DB\" -c \"SELECT slug, id FROM aic_projects ORDER BY slug;\"'\n```\n\nUse `$POSTGRES_SIMPLE_USER` for normal project-scoped reads and writes. Resolve the target project's ID by slug, then include `project_id` in queries against its files, chunks, and embeddings. For vector and HNSW queries, see [Retrieval and Indexing](./retrieval-and-indexing.md).\n\nExample for one sanitized batch:\n\n```bash\ndocker compose -f /home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/.docker/dev/docker-compose.yml \\\n  exec -T ai-code-intelligence-postgres-postgresql sh -lc '\n    node /app/embedding.js \"$OPENAI_API_KEY\" \\\n      /app/embedding-results/project-name/{year}/{month}/example-batch-0000.json \\\n      text-embedding-3-small \"class OrderService {}\" >/dev/null\n  '\n```\n\nThe embedding file persists on the host at `/app/embedding-results/project-name/{year}/{month}/example-batch-0000.json`.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Database startup\n\n### Files about DB tables\n\nDB tables schemas:\ndocker-entrypoint-initdb.d:\n - 001_extensions.sql\n - 002_schema.sql\n - 003_indexes.sql\n - 004_views_and_search_functions.sql\n - 005_vector_index_helpers.sql\n\n---\n\n## See Also\n\n- [Architecture and Retrieval Model](./architecture.md)\n- [Database Reference](./database.md)\n- [Retrieval and Indexing](./retrieval-and-indexing.md)[← Getting Started](./getting-started.md) · [Back to README](../README.md) · [Database Reference →](./database.md)\n\n# Architecture and Retrieval Model\n\nThis page explains the code-intelligence retrieval model. For repository layers, SQL initialization order, deployment boundaries, and the embedding utility, see the [repository architecture guide](../.ai-factory/ARCHITECTURE.md).\n\n## 1. Architecture\n\n```text\nRepositories\n    |\n    +----------------------+----------------------+\n    |                      |                      |\n    v                      v                      v\nTree-sitter            Serena / LSP           Git\nAST + chunks           symbols/types          history\n    |                      |                      |\n    +----------------------+----------+-----------+\n                                       |\n                                       v\n                              Index / Context Layer\n                                       |\n               +-----------------------+-----------------------+\n               |                       |                       |\n               v                       v                       v\n          PostgreSQL FTS          pgvector RAG          relational graph\n               |                       |                       |\n               +-----------------------+-----------------------+\n                                       |\n                                       v\n                                  AI Agent\n                                       |\n                                       v\n                                  AI Factory\n                           plan -> implement -> verify\n```\n\n### Tool responsibilities\n\n| Tool                                                                     | Primary responsibility                                                                                                                     | Do not use it as                                     |\n|--------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------|\n| AI Factory                                                               | Project context, rules, spec-driven workflow, planning, implementation, verification                                                       | A code parser or search engine                       |\n| Serena                                                                   | Symbol-level semantic navigation, references, editing, refactoring, LSP-backed code intelligence                                           | A replacement for semantic RAG                       |\n| ripgrep (`rg`)                                                           | Fast exact text/regex search                                                                                                               | Structural AST matching                              |\n| /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep (`sg` / `/home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep`) | AST-aware structural search, linting, safe rewrite/codemod patterns                                                                        | Semantic business-language search                    |\n| Tree-sitter                                                              | Parsing, syntax trees, language-aware chunk boundaries, extraction input                                                                   | A complete type system or runtime call graph         |\n| PostgreSQL + pgvector                                                    | Persistent multi-project RAG, metadata, graph data, FTS, fuzzy lookup                                                                      | Proof that retrieved code is correct                 |\n| Git                                                                      | Diff/history/blame/commit context and change isolation                                                                                     | A static analyzer                                    |\n| Language servers                                                         | Definition/reference/type/navigation data                                                                                                  | Persistent RAG storage                               |\n| Tests/static analyzers                                                   | Verification after changes                                                                                                                 | Discovery/indexing layer                             |\n| `cua_repl` / `node_repl`                                                 | Primary runtime browser/UI inspection: rendered page, interaction, DOM, styles, geometry, console, network, screenshots, responsive states | Static source-code understanding                     |\n| Chrome DevTools MCP                                                      | Alternative browser/runtime inspection layer for DOM, CSS, console, network, performance, screenshots, and interaction                     | Persistent project knowledge or source-code indexing |\n\nRecommended verification tools depend on the project. Examples include PHPUnit/Pest, PHPStan/Psalm/PHPCS/PHP CS Fixer, ESLint, TypeScript, Vue/Nuxt type checks, Composer scripts, and application-specific integration tests.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 2. Why this is hybrid retrieval, not \"vector search only\"\n\nAn AI agent must select the search mechanism according to the question.\n\nUse **exact/symbol search** for questions such as:\n\n```text\nWhere is PaymentService::processPayment() called?\n```\n\nUse **structural search** for questions such as:\n\n```text\nFind all PHP methods that dispatch an event from inside a transaction.\n```\n\nUse **semantic/vector retrieval** for questions such as:\n\n```text\nWhere is failed payment retry logic implemented?\n```\n\nUse **graph traversal** for questions such as:\n\n```text\nWhat may be affected if PaymentGatewayInterface changes?\n```\n\nVector results identify candidates. They do **not** prove control flow, references, type relationships, or runtime behavior. Always verify important findings against source code and, when applicable, LSP/Serena, PostgreSQL `symbol_relations`, tests, and Git history.\n\n---\n\n## See Also\n\n- [Getting Started](./getting-started.md)\n- [Database Reference](./database.md)\n- [Retrieval and Indexing](./retrieval-and-indexing.md)[← Architecture and Retrieval Model](./architecture.md) · [Back to README](../README.md) · [Retrieval and Indexing →](./retrieval-and-indexing.md)\n\n# Database Reference\n\n## 6. PostgreSQL extensions\n\n`001_extensions.sql` enables:\n\n| Extension  | Purpose                                       |\n|------------|-----------------------------------------------|\n| `vector`   | Embedding storage and nearest-neighbor search |\n| `pgcrypto` | `gen_random_uuid()`                           |\n| `pg_trgm`  | Fuzzy symbol/path lookup                      |\n| `unaccent` | Optional normalization support                |\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 7. DDL model\n\nThe authoritative DDL is in `002_schema.sql`.\n\n### `projects`\n\nRoot tenant/entity for all stored projects.\n\n| Column           | Type        | Purpose                       |\n|------------------|-------------|-------------------------------|\n| `id`             | UUID PK     | Project identifier            |\n| `name`           | TEXT        | Human-readable name           |\n| `slug`           | TEXT UNIQUE | Stable local identifier       |\n| `root_path`      | TEXT        | Local repository path         |\n| `repository_url` | TEXT        | Optional Git remote           |\n| `default_branch` | TEXT        | Default branch                |\n| `active`         | BOOLEAN     | Whether the project is active |\n| `metadata`       | JSONB       | Project-specific metadata     |\n| timestamps       | TIMESTAMPTZ | Audit timestamps              |\n\nAll project-owned data cascades when a project is deleted. Composite project-scoped foreign keys also prevent accidental cross-project links between files, symbols, chunks, documents, knowledge, and embeddings.\n\n### `files`\n\nOne row per indexed file in a project.\n\nImportant columns:\n\n```text\nproject_id\npath\nlanguage\nfile_type\ncontent_hash\ngit_blob_sha\nlast_indexed_commit\nis_generated\nis_deleted\nindexed_at\n```\n\nUnique key:\n\n```text\n(project_id, path)\n```\n\n### `symbols`\n\nTree-sitter/LSP/Serena-derived symbols.\n\nTypical `kind` values:\n\n```text\nnamespace\nmodule\nclass\ninterface\ntrait\nenum\nmethod\nfunction\nproperty\nconstant\ntype\ncomponent\n```\n\nImportant columns:\n\n```text\nproject_id\nfile_id\nparent_symbol_id\nkind\nname\nqualified_name\nsignature\nvisibility\nstart_line/start_column\nend_line/end_column\ncontent_hash\nmetadata\n```\n\nThe schema intentionally does not constrain `kind` to a fixed enum because language ecosystems differ.\n\n### `symbol_relations`\n\nEdges in the relational code graph.\n\nTypical `relation_type` values:\n\n```text\ncalls\nreferences\nimports\nextends\nimplements\nuses\ndispatches\nlistens\npublishes\nsubscribes\nreads\nwrites\ninstantiates\n```\n\n`target_symbol_id` can be `NULL` for an unresolved external or not-yet-resolved target. In that case `target_qualified_name` must be present.\n\n`confidence` allows framework heuristics to be stored without pretending that every inferred edge is certain.\n\n### `documents`\n\nProject documentation and external/local reference material.\n\nExamples:\n\n```text\nREADME\nADR\nOpenAPI document\ninternal architecture document\nvendor API reference\nAI Factory generated research\n```\n\nA document must have either `path` or `source_uri`.\n\n### `knowledge_items`\n\nDurable, explicit project knowledge.\n\nRecommended `kind` values:\n\n```text\narchitecture\nbusiness_rule\nconstraint\nconvention\ndecision\nincident\nintegration\nsecurity\ndeployment\n```\n\nUse this table for concise, evidence-backed facts that should persist across agent sessions.\n\nDo not put temporary guesses here.\n\n### `chunks`\n\nCanonical RAG chunk table for all source types.\n\n`source_type` is one of:\n\n```text\ncode\ndocument\nknowledge\n```\n\nA chunk points to exactly one primary source:\n\n```text\ncode      -> file_id (+ optional symbol_id)\ndocument  -> document_id\nknowledge -> knowledge_item_id\n```\n\nImportant columns:\n\n```text\nchunk_type\nsequence_number\nstart_line/end_line\ncontent\ncontent_hash\ntoken_count\nmetadata\nsearch_vector\n```\n\n`search_vector` is generated automatically for PostgreSQL Full Text Search.\n\n### `embedding_models`\n\nRegistry of embedding configurations.\n\n```text\nprovider\nmodel\ndimensions\ndistance_metric\nenabled\n```\n\nSupported `distance_metric` values in this schema:\n\n```text\ncosine\nl2\ninner_product\n```\n\nThe model registry avoids hard-coding a single embedding dimension into the schema.\n\n### `embeddings`\n\nEmbeddings for chunks.\n\n```text\nproject_id\nchunk_id\nembedding_model_id\ndimensions\nembedding\nsource_content_hash\n```\n\nThe `embedding` column uses unconstrained `VECTOR`, allowing different models with different dimensions in one table.\n\n`source_content_hash` is critical: if it differs from the current chunk hash, the embedding is stale and must be regenerated.\n\n### `indexing_runs`\n\nTracks indexing/synchronization operations.\n\nTypical sequence:\n\n```text\nrunning\n    ->\ncompleted | failed | cancelled\n```\n\nStore counts and timing details in `stats`.\n\n---\n[← Database Reference](./database.md) · [Back to README](../README.md) · [Agent Workflow →](./agent-workflow.md)\n\n# Retrieval and Indexing\n\n## 4. Search decision matrix\n\n| Need                             | Preferred tool                                    |\n|----------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------|\n| Exact string/error/config key    | ripgrep                                           |\n| Class/method definition          | Serena/LSP                                        |\n| Find references/implementations  | Serena/LSP                                        |\n| Caller/callee path               | Serena/LSP + relational graph                     |\n| Blast radius                     | PostgreSQL relational graph                       |\n| AST code shape                   | /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep                    |\n| Large codemod                    | /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep + Git diff + tests |\n| Syntax-aware chunk boundaries    | Tree-sitter                                       |\n| Unknown business concept         | FTS + pgvector                                    |\n| Documentation/ADR/business rules | PostgreSQL RAG                                    |\n| Why/when code changed            | Git                                               |\n| Prove correctness                | tests/static analysis                             |\n| Rendered UI/layout/usability     | `cua_repl` / `node_repl`                          |\n| DOM/computed styles/geometry     | `cua_repl` / `node_repl`                          |\n| Browser console/runtime errors   | `cua_repl` / `node_repl`                          |\n| Network/API behavior in browser  | `cua_repl` / `node_repl`                          |\n| Browser runtime alternative      | Chrome DevTools MCP                               |\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## Reliable end-to-end indexing\n\nIndexing crosses host tools, a Docker container, a paid embedding API, and PostgreSQL. Verify each boundary explicitly:\n\n1. Check host tools with `command -v /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep`, `serena --version`, and the Python interpreter that has `tree_sitter_language_pack`. The AI database container is expected to provide PostgreSQL and Node.js, not every parser or language server.\n2. Run Serena/LSP indexing locally. A first run may try to download a language server (for PHP, Intelephense); DNS or registry failures indicate a tool-installation problem. Retry after network access is available and distinguish this from source parse errors.\n3. Parse a small representative file before processing the whole repository. Some Tree-sitter bindings can fail in batch traversal. If a fallback parser or line chunking is used, record the fallback and resulting limitations; do not label those chunks syntax-aware. Populate `symbol_relations` only when edges were actually derived.\n4. Resolve the project by slug and scope every file, symbol, chunk, knowledge, embedding, and run query by `project_id`. Exclude credentials, environment files, generated/runtime content, and any source not approved for the shared database.\n5. Generate embeddings in bounded batches. `app/embedding.js` currently prints the API key and input text to stdout, so suppress stdout and keep command logs private. Persist each batch under `/app/embedding-results/{year}/{month}/` (container path `/app/embedding-results/{year}/{month}/`) before starting another batch. Never rely on container `/tmp` for paid results.\n6. Before retrying, compare existing result files, chunk hashes, project counts, model ID, and indexing-run state. Reuse completed batches; do not call the API again for unchanged input. Keep a manifest mapping batch files to chunk IDs/content hashes when building a new index.\n7. Commit embeddings first. Then create the project/model HNSW index with `aic_create_project_embedding_hnsw_index(...)` using the table-owner role. The restricted `$POSTGRES_SIMPLE_USER` may insert rows but fail with `must be owner of table aic_embeddings` when creating the index. Mark the run `completed` only after the index exists and counts are checked.\n\nA failed index-creation statement inside the same transaction as vector inserts rolls back those inserts. Check database state before recovery; preserve saved batch files and resume the database write without generating embeddings again. Avoid broad deletes or project recreation when recovering partial runs.\n\n### Office Easyfit indexing record (2026-10-02)\n\nThe `office-easyfit` project uses `text-embedding-3-small` (1536 dimensions) and a project-specific HNSW index. The completed run contains 1,182 PHP files, 4,221 approximate symbol rows, 3,896 chunks/embeddings, four documents, and four knowledge items. Configuration, test, runtime, vendor/dependency, web asset/upload paths, and files matching the credential-like literal filter were excluded. Serena indexed 964 PHP files. The Tree-sitter Python binding segfaulted during batch node-field extraction, so code chunks used 80-line boundaries and symbol names used a declaration-line fallback; symbol relations were not populated. Use vector and symbol results as candidates and verify findings in the original source. The 325 embedding result JSON files were persisted under `app/embedding-results/`. This run did not produce a separate batch-to-chunk manifest; the database rows are complete, so do not regenerate them. Future runs should persist that mapping alongside each batch file.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Search rules\n\n### Rule 1 — Always scope by project\n\nBad:\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM chunks\nLIMIT 20;\n```\n\nGood:\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM chunks\nWHERE project_id = :project_id\nLIMIT 20;\n```\n\nCross-project retrieval is allowed only when the task explicitly requires it.\n\n### Rule 2 — Prefer exact symbol lookup before fuzzy/vector search\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n    s.*,\n    f.path\nFROM symbols s\nJOIN files f ON f.id = s.file_id\nWHERE s.project_id = :project_id\n  AND s.qualified_name = :qualified_name;\n```\n\n### Rule 3 — Fuzzy symbol search for uncertain names\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM search_symbols_fuzzy(\n    :project_id,\n    'PaymentServce',\n    20\n);\n```\n\n### Rule 4 — Full Text Search for lexical/business terms\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM search_chunks_text(\n    :project_id,\n    'failed payment retry',\n    20\n);\n```\n\n### Rule 5 — Exact vector retrieval\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM search_chunks_vector_exact(\n    :project_id,\n    :embedding_model_id,\n    :query_embedding::vector,\n    20\n);\n```\n\nThe application is responsible for generating `:query_embedding` with the same model used for stored embeddings.\n\n### Rule 6 — Graph traversal\n\nOutgoing dependencies:\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM symbol_relations_walk(\n    :project_id,\n    :symbol_id,\n    'outgoing',\n    3\n);\n```\n\nIncoming dependants / impact candidates:\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM symbol_relations_walk(\n    :project_id,\n    :symbol_id,\n    'incoming',\n    3\n);\n```\n\nTreat heuristic relations according to their `confidence`.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 12. Vector indexing\n\nA single `VECTOR` column can store embeddings of different dimensions. Approximate vector indexes, however, must target rows with a consistent dimension.\n\nThis repository therefore does **not** create one global HNSW index automatically.\n\nAfter registering an embedding model:\n\n```sql\nSELECT id, provider, model, dimensions\nFROM embedding_models;\n```\n\nCreate a **project + model specific** HNSW index:\n\n```sql\nSELECT create_project_embedding_hnsw_index(\n    :project_id,\n    :embedding_model_id\n);\n```\n\nProject-specific partial indexes are intentional. In a shared multi-project database, a single approximate index shared by all projects can lose recall when PostgreSQL applies `project_id` filtering after ANN candidate selection.\n\nThe helper uses:\n\n- `vector(...)` for dimensions up to 2000\n- `halfvec(...)` for dimensions from 2001 through 4000\n\nFor larger vectors, choose a deliberate strategy such as dimensionality reduction, binary quantization, or another model.\n\nTo remove an automatically named project/model index:\n\n```sql\nSELECT drop_project_embedding_hnsw_index(\n    :project_id,\n    :embedding_model_id\n);\n```\n\n### Important query note\n\nAn expression HNSW index is useful only when the retrieval query uses the corresponding cast/expression and matches the partial-index predicate. `search_chunks_vector_exact()` is deliberately an exact, generic fallback.\n\nFor high-volume approximate retrieval, use the helper after creating the HNSW index:\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM search_chunks_vector_hnsw(\n    :project_id,\n    :embedding_model_id,\n    :query_embedding::vector,\n    20\n);\n```\n\nThis function uses the same `vector(n)` or `halfvec(n)` expression as the model-specific HNSW index.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 13. Recommended hybrid RAG flow\n\nFor a natural-language question:\n\n```text\n\"Where is failed payment retry logic implemented?\"\n```\n\nRecommended pipeline:\n\n```text\n1. Resolve project_id\n2. FTS top N\n3. Vector top N\n4. Fuzzy symbol candidates\n5. Optional graph expansion around strong symbol candidates\n6. Merge / rerank candidates\n7. Read original source\n8. Verify references with Serena/LSP and PostgreSQL `symbol_relations`\n9. Answer or modify code\n```\n\nA practical RRF-style merge can happen in application code. Do not blindly concatenate dozens of chunks into the LLM context.\n\nPrefer small, evidence-rich context.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 14. Tree-sitter indexing rules\n\nTree-sitter should primarily produce structural metadata and semantic chunk boundaries.\n\nDo not embed a serialized raw AST unless a specific experiment proves it useful.\n\nPreferred code chunk:\n\n```text\nLanguage: PHP\nFile: app/Services/PaymentService.php\nNamespace: App\\Services\nClass: PaymentService\nMethod: processPayment\nSignature: processPayment(Order $order): Payment\nLines: 34-81\n\n<source code>\n```\n\nRecommended chunk boundaries:\n\n```text\nclass/interface/trait/enum\nmethod/function\ncomponent\ntemplate block\nconfiguration object\nquery/migration unit\n```\n\nFor very large symbols, split only after preserving:\n\n- symbol identity\n- parent symbol\n[← Retrieval and Indexing](./retrieval-and-indexing.md) · [Back to README](../README.md) · [Integrations and References →](./integrations.md)\n\n# Agent Workflow\n\n## 3. Required agent workflow\n\nThe following order is the default investigation workflow for a large project.\n\n### Phase A — Establish project scope\n\n1. Determine the repository root.\n2. Run `git status`.\n3. Determine the current branch and commit.\n4. Resolve the database project row by `slug` or `root_path`.\n5. Never query another project's rows accidentally.\n6. Load AI Factory project rules/context before modifying code.\n\nRecommended AI Factory entry points include:\n\n```text\n/aif-warmup\n/aif-grounded <question>\n/aif-explore <topic>\n/aif-plan <task>\n/aif-fix <problem>\n/aif-implement\n/aif-verify\n/aif-review\n```\n\nExact commands depend on the installed AI Factory version and configured agent.\n\n### Phase B — Search before reading broadly\n\nUse the cheapest precise source first:\n\n1. **Known symbol** -> Serena/LSP.\n2. **Call/dependency question** -> Serena/LSP and PostgreSQL `symbol_relations`.\n3. **Known literal, class name, route, config key, error text** -> `rg`.\n4. **Known code shape** -> /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep.\n5. **Unknown terminology / business concept** -> PostgreSQL FTS + vector retrieval.\n6. **Need syntax boundaries/chunk extraction** -> Tree-sitter.\n7. Read only the files/symbols returned by the search.\n\nDo not scan the whole repository if a precise search can narrow the scope.\n\n### Phase C — Build an evidence chain\n\nFor every non-trivial bug or feature, identify:\n\n```text\nentry point\n    ->\ncontroller/handler/command/job\n    ->\nservice/domain logic\n    ->\nrepository/model/external integration\n    ->\nevent/listener/queue/callback\n    ->\ntests\n```\n\nRecord unresolved questions instead of inventing relationships.\n\n### Phase D — Validate history when behavior is suspicious\n\nUse:\n\n```bash\ngit log -- <file>\ngit blame <file>\ngit show <commit>\ngit diff\n```\n\nHistory is especially useful for workarounds, regressions, legacy compatibility, and business decisions that are not obvious from current code.\n\n### Phase E — Modify minimally\n\n1. Follow project conventions.\n2. Prefer the smallest coherent change.\n3. Use symbol-aware edits for refactors where available.\n4. Use /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep rewrites only when the AST pattern is verified.\n5. Review `git diff` immediately after automated rewrites.\n6. Do not update persistent knowledge until the final behavior is verified.\n\n### Phase F — Verify\n\nRun the narrowest relevant tests first, then broader quality gates.\n\nTypical PHP/Laravel sequence:\n\n```bash\nphp artisan test --filter=<relevant-test>\nvendor/bin/phpstan analyse\nvendor/bin/phpcs\nvendor/bin/phpunit\n```\n\nTypical Nuxt/TypeScript sequence:\n\n```bash\nnpm run lint\nnpm run typecheck\nnpm test\nnpm run build\n```\n\nUse the project's actual scripts when they differ.\n\n### Phase G — Update index/knowledge\n\nAfter a verified change:\n\n1. Update changed `files`.\n2. Re-extract changed `symbols`.\n3. Replace affected `symbol_relations`.\n4. Rebuild affected `chunks`.\n5. Regenerate embeddings only for chunks whose `content_hash` changed.\n6. Store durable architecture/business knowledge only when it is supported by project evidence.\n7. Finish the `indexing_runs` row.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 19. Git rules for AI agents\n\nBefore editing:\n\n```bash\ngit status --short\ngit branch --show-current\ngit rev-parse HEAD\n```\n\nAfter editing:\n\n```bash\ngit diff --check\ngit diff\n```\n\nFor regressions/history:\n\n```bash\ngit log --oneline -- <path>\ngit blame <path>\ngit show <commit> -- <path>\n```\n\nNever discard existing user changes merely to make the working tree clean.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 20. Knowledge-writing policy\n\n`knowledge_items` must contain durable, evidence-backed information.\n\nGood:\n\n```text\n\"Payment webhooks are idempotent by provider_transaction_id.\"\n```\n\nBad:\n\n```text\n\"I think duplicate payments may come from Redis.\"\n```\n\nRecommended fields:\n\n```text\nkind = architecture | business_rule | decision | constraint | ...\nstatus = active\nconfidence = 0.0..1.0\nsource_reference = file/ADR/issue/commit/reference\n```\n\nWhen a fact changes, prefer marking the old row `superseded` and adding the new knowledge item rather than silently rewriting history.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 25. Final rule for the AI agent\n\nThe agent should follow this principle:\n\n> Retrieve broadly enough to discover candidates, but verify narrowly against authoritative source code, symbol relationships, tests, and Git before drawing conclusions or changing code.\n\nThe database is long-term project intelligence, not a substitute for the repository itself.\n\n## See Also\n\n- [Getting Started](./getting-started.md)\n- [Architecture and Retrieval Model](./architecture.md)\n- [Database Reference](./database.md)[← Integrations and References](./integrations.md) · [Back to README](../README.md)\n\n# Security\n\n## 23. Security\n\nThis stack is intended for local development.\n\nThe Compose file binds PostgreSQL to:\n\n```text\n127.0.0.1\n```\n\nrather than all network interfaces.\n\nStill:\n\n- change the default password\n- do not commit `.env`\n- do not expose port `5432` publicly\n- use a separate database user with restricted permissions if an untrusted agent/client can connect\n- back up the Docker volume if the accumulated project knowledge matters\n- never index secrets from source code in chunks, knowledge, or embeddings\n- treat embedding result files as source-derived data: keep them in `app/embedding-results/`, restrict access, and do not commit them unless the repository explicitly requires that\n- suppress stdout from the current embedding script because it prints the API key and input texts\n\n---\n\n## See Also\n\n- [Getting Started](./getting-started.md)\n- [Architecture and Retrieval Model](./architecture.md)\n- [Database Reference](./database.md)\n[← Agent Workflow](./agent-workflow.md) · [Back to README](../README.md) · [Security →](./security.md)\n\n# Integrations and References\n\n## 22. Suggested MCP surface for a future custom server\n\nA custom MCP server over this database could expose narrowly-scoped tools such as:\n\n```text\nproject_resolve\nsymbol_find\nsymbol_references\nsymbol_relations\ncode_search_text\ncode_search_semantic\nknowledge_search\ndocument_search\nindex_status\nindex_changed_files\nknowledge_upsert\n```\n\nAvoid exposing unrestricted SQL execution to an autonomous agent unless the environment is intentionally trusted.\n\nFor read tools, enforce `project_id` server-side.\n\nFor write tools, validate ownership and use transactions.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 24. Upstream references\n\n- AI Factory: https://github.com/lee-to/ai-factory\n- ast-grep: https://ast-grep.github.io/\n- Serena: https://github.com/oraios/serena\n- ripgrep: https://github.com/BurntSushi/ripgrep\n- Tree-sitter: https://tree-sitter.github.io/tree-sitter/\n- pgvector: https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector\n- Chrome DevTools MCP: https://github.com/ChromeDevTools/chrome-devtools-mcp\n\n---\n\n## See Also\n\n- [Getting Started](./getting-started.md)\n- [Architecture and Retrieval Model](./architecture.md)\n- [Database Reference](./database.md)\ntotal 56\ndrwxrwxr-x 7 kamaz kamaz 28672 Oct  6 15:38 .\ndrwxrwxr-x 5 kamaz kamaz  4096 Oct  3 14:30 ..\ndrwxrwxr-x 3 kamaz kamaz  4096 Oct  6 15:38 easyfit-pl\ndrwxrwxr-x 3 kamaz kamaz  4096 Oct  6 15:28 easyfit-ua\ndrwxrwxr-x 2 kamaz kamaz  4096 Oct  5 12:17 office-easyfit\ndrwxrwxr-x 3 kamaz kamaz  4096 Oct  6 18:46 pet-complaints-platform\ndrwxrwxr-x 2 kamaz kamaz  4096 Oct  5 12:15 yii3-office-easyfit\n/usr/bin/python3\ntree-sitter available\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a1155c-86e8-7eb1-9147-ce9f28491fa5","create_time":1791359770.8583312}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
