{"timestamp":"2026-10-07T08:27:34.461Z","ordinal":11648,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a0a02b-47ee-7913-b18b-d4d4b0be8b60","turn_id":"01a11579-64a7-7683-8971-6858f57dcfc9","session_id":"01a0a02b-47ee-7913-b18b-d4d4b0be8b60","root_turn_id":"01a11579-64a7-7683-8971-6858f57dcfc9","response_id":"resp_094cad5b47a7c03f016ac60272971c87d2a127c898401b59ee","usage":{"input_tokens":33433,"cached_input_tokens":11008,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":332,"reasoning_output_tokens":150,"total_tokens":33765},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":236892,"cached_input_tokens":213504,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":1005,"reasoning_output_tokens":150,"total_tokens":237897},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":164586746,"cached_input_tokens":159942528,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":696472,"reasoning_output_tokens":368985,"total_tokens":165283218}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-07T08:27:34.463Z","ordinal":11649,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a0a02b-47ee-7913-b18b-d4d4b0be8b60","turn_id":"01a11579-64a7-7683-8971-6858f57dcfc9","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-1a440c21-9cdf-4077-9837-9980eed0be95","process_id":"69657","command":["/bin/bash","-lc","cat /home/kamaz/www/strateg-projects/strateg-export-products/.codex/skills/ai-code-intelligence/SKILL.md && rg -n --hidden --glob '*.sql' --glob '*.md' 'v_retrieval_chunks|v_aic_retrieval_chunks|aic_v_retrieval_chunks' /home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence"],"cwd":"file:///home/kamaz/www/strateg-projects/strateg-export-products","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"cat /home/kamaz/www/strateg-projects/strateg-export-products/.codex/skills/ai-code-intelligence/SKILL.md","name":"SKILL.md","path":"/home/kamaz/www/strateg-projects/strateg-export-products/.codex/skills/ai-code-intelligence/SKILL.md"},{"type":"search","cmd":"rg -n --hidden --glob '*.sql' --glob '*.md' 'v_retrieval_chunks|v_aic_retrieval_chunks|aic_v_retrieval_chunks' /home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence","query":"v_retrieval_chunks|v_aic_retrieval_chunks|aic_v_retrieval_chunks","path":"ai-code-intelligence"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"---\nname: ai-code-intelligence\ndescription: Explains how to use the shared ai-code-intelligence documentation, host-side code tools, PostgreSQL retrieval, and paid embeddings safely. Use when indexing or searching a project in the shared intelligence database, or when creating/updating project documentation about that integration, especially docs/ai-code-intelligence.md.\nargument-hint: \"[task or repository]\"\nmetadata:\n  author: AI Factory\n  version: \"1.0\"\n  category: documentation-and-code-intelligence\n---\n\n# AI Code Intelligence\n\nUse this skill to work with the shared `ai-code-intelligence` project and document that workflow accurately. Treat the repository as an evidence store, not as an authority over the source code.\n\n## 1. Read the Documentation in Context\n\n1. Identify the current repository root and read its `AGENTS.md` first. Follow direct links to the shared `ai-code-intelligence` project; do not guess its path.\n2. Read the shared project's `AGENTS.md` and `README.md`. Then read only the pages needed for the task:\n   - `docs/getting-started.md` for Docker, database, and embedding setup.\n   - `docs/architecture.md` for tool responsibilities and retrieval boundaries.\n   - `docs/database.md` for table ownership, constraints, and project-scoped records.\n   - `docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md` for search selection, index creation, retry behavior, and chunking.\n   - `docs/agent-workflow.md` for investigation and verification sequence.\n   - `docs/security.md` for secret handling and shared-data safeguards.\n   - `docs/integrations.md` for available database/MCP surfaces.\n3. Check the current database schema and scripts when documentation might be stale. Prefer the checked-in DDL and functions over copied examples.\n4. Resolve the target project by its stable slug, then scope every project-owned database read/write by `project_id`.\n\nFor tool commands, index recovery, vector search, and common failures, read [the detailed workflow reference](references/AI-CODE-INTELLIGENCE.md).\n\n## 2. Choose and Invoke Tools Correctly\n\nUse the cheapest precise search first:\n\n- Exact path, string, route, config key, or error: `rg`.\n- Class/method definitions and references: Serena/LSP.\n- Repeated code shape: `ast-grep`.\n- Syntax-aware PHP metadata and chunk boundaries: Tree-sitter.\n- Unknown business wording: PostgreSQL full-text and vector retrieval.\n- Runtime page behavior: the available browser inspection tool.\n\nCheck which environment has the tools. In this setup, PostgreSQL and Node.js run in Docker; `ast-grep`, Tree-sitter Python bindings, and Serena are host tools. A missing command inside the database container does not establish that it is absent on the host. Check `command -v`, `serena --version`, and the Python interpreter that can import `tree_sitter_language_pack`.\n\nSerena may need to download a PHP language server on its first run. A DNS or package-registry failure is a tool-installation problem; retry after network access is available. Tree-sitter bindings can fail during large or repeated AST traversals. Parse a representative file first. If a fallback is required, record it and do not describe line-based chunks as syntax-aware.\n\n## 3. Treat Embeddings as a Paid, Persistent Artifact\n\nEmbedding calls use the OpenAI API and can incur cost. Generate or refresh them only when the user's request authorizes that work. Before any call:\n\n1. Inspect existing result files, project/chunk counts, chunk hashes, model registration, and indexing-run status.\n2. Reuse complete results. Do not re-embed unchanged chunks just because a later database step failed.\n3. Exclude secrets, credentials, local environment files, generated/runtime data, and unapproved source. Embeddings remain source-derived data.\n4. Write each batch immediately to the configured persistent `app/embedding-results/` directory. Never keep the only copy in container `/tmp`.\n5. Keep a batch manifest mapping each result file to chunk IDs and content hashes so interrupted database writes can resume without new API calls.\n\nThe current `app/embedding.js` prints its API key and input texts to stdout. Suppress that output and do not expose captured logs. In the local Compose setup, the host results directory is mounted at `/app/embedding-results/` inside the PostgreSQL/Node container. See [the paid-embedding and recovery instructions](references/AI-CODE-INTELLIGENCE.md#paid-embeddings-and-safe-retries).\n\n## 4. Write and Verify the Index in Safe Stages\n\n1. Create or resolve the project row; never infer a project ID from another project.\n2. Upsert model metadata and persist files, symbols, documents, knowledge, and chunks with the same project ID.\n3. Generate and persist embedding batch files, then write vectors to PostgreSQL.\n4. Commit vector rows before creating the project/model HNSW index. In the local service, the restricted `$POSTGRES_SIMPLE_USER` can write rows but may not own `aic_embeddings`; use the table-owner role for `aic_create_project_embedding_hnsw_index(...)`.\n5. Verify file, symbol, chunk, embedding, document, knowledge, and index counts. Mark the `aic_indexing_runs` record `completed` only after those checks pass.\n6. If a stage fails, inspect the database and persistent batch files before retrying. Do not broadly delete project data or recreate a project as a shortcut.\n\nFor vector retrieval, generate the query vector with the same registered model and dimensions as stored vectors. Resolve the project's slug and pass its project ID and model ID to `aic_search_chunks_vector_hnsw(...)` (or the exact-search helper when required). Review source files for all important results; vector similarity proposes candidates and does not prove behavior.\n\n## 5. Create and Maintain Project Documentation\n\nFor a first documentation pass, inspect the real repository structure, framework, dependencies, runtime configuration, database access, tests, integrations, and existing docs before writing. Never invent available tools or commands. Keep the README a concise landing page and put setup, architecture, configuration, API, deployment, testing, security, and code-intelligence details in focused pages under the configured documentation directory.\n\nWhen a project uses the shared intelligence database, create a `docs/ai-code-intelligence.md` page. Document:\n\n- the shared database's purpose and the project's stable slug;\n- how to locate and read the shared documentation;\n- which tools run on the host and which services run in Docker;\n- verified commands, model, dimensions, result-file location, and project-scoped search function;\n- API cost, stdout/logging, secret exclusion, persistent batch recovery, and database-role nuances;\n- actual index counts, limitations, parser fallbacks, and whether symbol relations were populated;\n- relative links to relevant project docs and shared documentation.\n\nFor existing docs, read the configured docs path, README, AGENTS.md, and linked pages first. Make targeted updates without replacing unrelated content. Keep every claim tied to source/config/DDL or a verified run. Update the README documentation table and the `AGENTS.md` Documentation table when pages are added or removed. External shared-project docs are a separate project: edit them only when the user explicitly asks.\n\nUse [the documentation checklist and page outline](references/PROJECT-DOCUMENTATION.md) for first-time documentation and `docs/ai-code-intelligence.md` updates.\n\n## 6. Final Review\n\nBefore finishing, check all modified pages and examples against the actual project and database schema. Verify Markdown links, commands, code-fence balance, and README/AGENTS documentation indexes. Report any tool failure, fallback, missing relation data, or incomplete indexing clearly. Do not claim successful semantic indexing when only line chunks or declaration-line matches were stored./home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/004_views_and_search_functions.sql:35:CREATE OR REPLACE VIEW v_aic_retrieval_chunks AS\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/004_views_and_search_functions.sql:90:    FROM v_aic_retrieval_chunks v\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/004_views_and_search_functions.sql:194:         JOIN v_aic_retrieval_chunks v\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/005_vector_index_helpers.sql:184:         JOIN v_retrieval_chunks v\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: ai-code-intelligence\ndescription: Explains how to use the shared ai-code-intelligence documentation, host-side code tools, PostgreSQL retrieval, and paid embeddings safely. Use when indexing or searching a project in the shared intelligence database, or when creating/updating project documentation about that integration, especially docs/ai-code-intelligence.md.\nargument-hint: \"[task or repository]\"\nmetadata:\n  author: AI Factory\n  version: \"1.0\"\n  category: documentation-and-code-intelligence\n---\n\n# AI Code Intelligence\n\nUse this skill to work with the shared `ai-code-intelligence` project and document that workflow accurately. Treat the repository as an evidence store, not as an authority over the source code.\n\n## 1. Read the Documentation in Context\n\n1. Identify the current repository root and read its `AGENTS.md` first. Follow direct links to the shared `ai-code-intelligence` project; do not guess its path.\n2. Read the shared project's `AGENTS.md` and `README.md`. Then read only the pages needed for the task:\n   - `docs/getting-started.md` for Docker, database, and embedding setup.\n   - `docs/architecture.md` for tool responsibilities and retrieval boundaries.\n   - `docs/database.md` for table ownership, constraints, and project-scoped records.\n   - `docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md` for search selection, index creation, retry behavior, and chunking.\n   - `docs/agent-workflow.md` for investigation and verification sequence.\n   - `docs/security.md` for secret handling and shared-data safeguards.\n   - `docs/integrations.md` for available database/MCP surfaces.\n3. Check the current database schema and scripts when documentation might be stale. Prefer the checked-in DDL and functions over copied examples.\n4. Resolve the target project by its stable slug, then scope every project-owned database read/write by `project_id`.\n\nFor tool commands, index recovery, vector search, and common failures, read [the detailed workflow reference](references/AI-CODE-INTELLIGENCE.md).\n\n## 2. Choose and Invoke Tools Correctly\n\nUse the cheapest precise search first:\n\n- Exact path, string, route, config key, or error: `rg`.\n- Class/method definitions and references: Serena/LSP.\n- Repeated code shape: `ast-grep`.\n- Syntax-aware PHP metadata and chunk boundaries: Tree-sitter.\n- Unknown business wording: PostgreSQL full-text and vector retrieval.\n- Runtime page behavior: the available browser inspection tool.\n\nCheck which environment has the tools. In this setup, PostgreSQL and Node.js run in Docker; `ast-grep`, Tree-sitter Python bindings, and Serena are host tools. A missing command inside the database container does not establish that it is absent on the host. Check `command -v`, `serena --version`, and the Python interpreter that can import `tree_sitter_language_pack`.\n\nSerena may need to download a PHP language server on its first run. A DNS or package-registry failure is a tool-installation problem; retry after network access is available. Tree-sitter bindings can fail during large or repeated AST traversals. Parse a representative file first. If a fallback is required, record it and do not describe line-based chunks as syntax-aware.\n\n## 3. Treat Embeddings as a Paid, Persistent Artifact\n\nEmbedding calls use the OpenAI API and can incur cost. Generate or refresh them only when the user's request authorizes that work. Before any call:\n\n1. Inspect existing result files, project/chunk counts, chunk hashes, model registration, and indexing-run status.\n2. Reuse complete results. Do not re-embed unchanged chunks just because a later database step failed.\n3. Exclude secrets, credentials, local environment files, generated/runtime data, and unapproved source. Embeddings remain source-derived data.\n4. Write each batch immediately to the configured persistent `app/embedding-results/` directory. Never keep the only copy in container `/tmp`.\n5. Keep a batch manifest mapping each result file to chunk IDs and content hashes so interrupted database writes can resume without new API calls.\n\nThe current `app/embedding.js` prints its API key and input texts to stdout. Suppress that output and do not expose captured logs. In the local Compose setup, the host results directory is mounted at `/app/embedding-results/` inside the PostgreSQL/Node container. See [the paid-embedding and recovery instructions](references/AI-CODE-INTELLIGENCE.md#paid-embeddings-and-safe-retries).\n\n## 4. Write and Verify the Index in Safe Stages\n\n1. Create or resolve the project row; never infer a project ID from another project.\n2. Upsert model metadata and persist files, symbols, documents, knowledge, and chunks with the same project ID.\n3. Generate and persist embedding batch files, then write vectors to PostgreSQL.\n4. Commit vector rows before creating the project/model HNSW index. In the local service, the restricted `$POSTGRES_SIMPLE_USER` can write rows but may not own `aic_embeddings`; use the table-owner role for `aic_create_project_embedding_hnsw_index(...)`.\n5. Verify file, symbol, chunk, embedding, document, knowledge, and index counts. Mark the `aic_indexing_runs` record `completed` only after those checks pass.\n6. If a stage fails, inspect the database and persistent batch files before retrying. Do not broadly delete project data or recreate a project as a shortcut.\n\nFor vector retrieval, generate the query vector with the same registered model and dimensions as stored vectors. Resolve the project's slug and pass its project ID and model ID to `aic_search_chunks_vector_hnsw(...)` (or the exact-search helper when required). Review source files for all important results; vector similarity proposes candidates and does not prove behavior.\n\n## 5. Create and Maintain Project Documentation\n\nFor a first documentation pass, inspect the real repository structure, framework, dependencies, runtime configuration, database access, tests, integrations, and existing docs before writing. Never invent available tools or commands. Keep the README a concise landing page and put setup, architecture, configuration, API, deployment, testing, security, and code-intelligence details in focused pages under the configured documentation directory.\n\nWhen a project uses the shared intelligence database, create a `docs/ai-code-intelligence.md` page. Document:\n\n- the shared database's purpose and the project's stable slug;\n- how to locate and read the shared documentation;\n- which tools run on the host and which services run in Docker;\n- verified commands, model, dimensions, result-file location, and project-scoped search function;\n- API cost, stdout/logging, secret exclusion, persistent batch recovery, and database-role nuances;\n- actual index counts, limitations, parser fallbacks, and whether symbol relations were populated;\n- relative links to relevant project docs and shared documentation.\n\nFor existing docs, read the configured docs path, README, AGENTS.md, and linked pages first. Make targeted updates without replacing unrelated content. Keep every claim tied to source/config/DDL or a verified run. Update the README documentation table and the `AGENTS.md` Documentation table when pages are added or removed. External shared-project docs are a separate project: edit them only when the user explicitly asks.\n\nUse [the documentation checklist and page outline](references/PROJECT-DOCUMENTATION.md) for first-time documentation and `docs/ai-code-intelligence.md` updates.\n\n## 6. Final Review\n\nBefore finishing, check all modified pages and examples against the actual project and database schema. Verify Markdown links, commands, code-fence balance, and README/AGENTS documentation indexes. Report any tool failure, fallback, missing relation data, or incomplete indexing clearly. Do not claim successful semantic indexing when only line chunks or declaration-line matches were stored./home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/004_views_and_search_functions.sql:35:CREATE OR REPLACE VIEW v_aic_retrieval_chunks AS\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/004_views_and_search_functions.sql:90:    FROM v_aic_retrieval_chunks v\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/004_views_and_search_functions.sql:194:         JOIN v_aic_retrieval_chunks v\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/005_vector_index_helpers.sql:184:         JOIN v_retrieval_chunks v\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":6903},"formatted_output":"---\nname: ai-code-intelligence\ndescription: Explains how to use the shared ai-code-intelligence documentation, host-side code tools, PostgreSQL retrieval, and paid embeddings safely. Use when indexing or searching a project in the shared intelligence database, or when creating/updating project documentation about that integration, especially docs/ai-code-intelligence.md.\nargument-hint: \"[task or repository]\"\nmetadata:\n  author: AI Factory\n  version: \"1.0\"\n  category: documentation-and-code-intelligence\n---\n\n# AI Code Intelligence\n\nUse this skill to work with the shared `ai-code-intelligence` project and document that workflow accurately. Treat the repository as an evidence store, not as an authority over the source code.\n\n## 1. Read the Documentation in Context\n\n1. Identify the current repository root and read its `AGENTS.md` first. Follow direct links to the shared `ai-code-intelligence` project; do not guess its path.\n2. Read the shared project's `AGENTS.md` and `README.md`. Then read only the pages needed for the task:\n   - `docs/getting-started.md` for Docker, database, and embedding setup.\n   - `docs/architecture.md` for tool responsibilities and retrieval boundaries.\n   - `docs/database.md` for table ownership, constraints, and project-scoped records.\n   - `docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md` for search selection, index creation, retry behavior, and chunking.\n   - `docs/agent-workflow.md` for investigation and verification sequence.\n   - `docs/security.md` for secret handling and shared-data safeguards.\n   - `docs/integrations.md` for available database/MCP surfaces.\n3. Check the current database schema and scripts when documentation might be stale. Prefer the checked-in DDL and functions over copied examples.\n4. Resolve the target project by its stable slug, then scope every project-owned database read/write by `project_id`.\n\nFor tool commands, index recovery, vector search, and common failures, read [the detailed workflow reference](references/AI-CODE-INTELLIGENCE.md).\n\n## 2. Choose and Invoke Tools Correctly\n\nUse the cheapest precise search first:\n\n- Exact path, string, route, config key, or error: `rg`.\n- Class/method definitions and references: Serena/LSP.\n- Repeated code shape: `ast-grep`.\n- Syntax-aware PHP metadata and chunk boundaries: Tree-sitter.\n- Unknown business wording: PostgreSQL full-text and vector retrieval.\n- Runtime page behavior: the available browser inspection tool.\n\nCheck which environment has the tools. In this setup, PostgreSQL and Node.js run in Docker; `ast-grep`, Tree-sitter Python bindings, and Serena are host tools. A missing command inside the database container does not establish that it is absent on the host. Check `command -v`, `serena --version`, and the Python interpreter that can import `tree_sitter_language_pack`.\n\nSerena may need to download a PHP language server on its first run. A DNS or package-registry failure is a tool-installation problem; retry after network access is available. Tree-sitter bindings can fail during large or repeated AST traversals. Parse a representative file first. If a fallback is required, record it and do not describe line-based chunks as syntax-aware.\n\n## 3. Treat Embeddings as a Paid, Persistent Artifact\n\nEmbedding calls use the OpenAI API and can incur cost. Generate or refresh them only when the user's request authorizes that work. Before any call:\n\n1. Inspect existing result files, project/chunk counts, chunk hashes, model registration, and indexing-run status.\n2. Reuse complete results. Do not re-embed unchanged chunks just because a later database step failed.\n3. Exclude secrets, credentials, local environment files, generated/runtime data, and unapproved source. Embeddings remain source-derived data.\n4. Write each batch immediately to the configured persistent `app/embedding-results/` directory. Never keep the only copy in container `/tmp`.\n5. Keep a batch manifest mapping each result file to chunk IDs and content hashes so interrupted database writes can resume without new API calls.\n\nThe current `app/embedding.js` prints its API key and input texts to stdout. Suppress that output and do not expose captured logs. In the local Compose setup, the host results directory is mounted at `/app/embedding-results/` inside the PostgreSQL/Node container. See [the paid-embedding and recovery instructions](references/AI-CODE-INTELLIGENCE.md#paid-embeddings-and-safe-retries).\n\n## 4. Write and Verify the Index in Safe Stages\n\n1. Create or resolve the project row; never infer a project ID from another project.\n2. Upsert model metadata and persist files, symbols, documents, knowledge, and chunks with the same project ID.\n3. Generate and persist embedding batch files, then write vectors to PostgreSQL.\n4. Commit vector rows before creating the project/model HNSW index. In the local service, the restricted `$POSTGRES_SIMPLE_USER` can write rows but may not own `aic_embeddings`; use the table-owner role for `aic_create_project_embedding_hnsw_index(...)`.\n5. Verify file, symbol, chunk, embedding, document, knowledge, and index counts. Mark the `aic_indexing_runs` record `completed` only after those checks pass.\n6. If a stage fails, inspect the database and persistent batch files before retrying. Do not broadly delete project data or recreate a project as a shortcut.\n\nFor vector retrieval, generate the query vector with the same registered model and dimensions as stored vectors. Resolve the project's slug and pass its project ID and model ID to `aic_search_chunks_vector_hnsw(...)` (or the exact-search helper when required). Review source files for all important results; vector similarity proposes candidates and does not prove behavior.\n\n## 5. Create and Maintain Project Documentation\n\nFor a first documentation pass, inspect the real repository structure, framework, dependencies, runtime configuration, database access, tests, integrations, and existing docs before writing. Never invent available tools or commands. Keep the README a concise landing page and put setup, architecture, configuration, API, deployment, testing, security, and code-intelligence details in focused pages under the configured documentation directory.\n\nWhen a project uses the shared intelligence database, create a `docs/ai-code-intelligence.md` page. Document:\n\n- the shared database's purpose and the project's stable slug;\n- how to locate and read the shared documentation;\n- which tools run on the host and which services run in Docker;\n- verified commands, model, dimensions, result-file location, and project-scoped search function;\n- API cost, stdout/logging, secret exclusion, persistent batch recovery, and database-role nuances;\n- actual index counts, limitations, parser fallbacks, and whether symbol relations were populated;\n- relative links to relevant project docs and shared documentation.\n\nFor existing docs, read the configured docs path, README, AGENTS.md, and linked pages first. Make targeted updates without replacing unrelated content. Keep every claim tied to source/config/DDL or a verified run. Update the README documentation table and the `AGENTS.md` Documentation table when pages are added or removed. External shared-project docs are a separate project: edit them only when the user explicitly asks.\n\nUse [the documentation checklist and page outline](references/PROJECT-DOCUMENTATION.md) for first-time documentation and `docs/ai-code-intelligence.md` updates.\n\n## 6. Final Review\n\nBefore finishing, check all modified pages and examples against the actual project and database schema. Verify Markdown links, commands, code-fence balance, and README/AGENTS documentation indexes. Report any tool failure, fallback, missing relation data, or incomplete indexing clearly. Do not claim successful semantic indexing when only line chunks or declaration-line matches were stored./home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/004_views_and_search_functions.sql:35:CREATE OR REPLACE VIEW v_aic_retrieval_chunks AS\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/004_views_and_search_functions.sql:90:    FROM v_aic_retrieval_chunks v\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/004_views_and_search_functions.sql:194:         JOIN v_aic_retrieval_chunks v\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/005_vector_index_helpers.sql:184:         JOIN v_retrieval_chunks v\n"},"started_at_ms":1791361654462,"completed_at_ms":1791361654462}}
