{"timestamp":"2026-10-08T14:44:58.351Z","ordinal":11763,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a0a02b-47ee-7913-b18b-d4d4b0be8b60","turn_id":"01a11bf9-7de2-72a3-b949-e54787d105e6","item":{"type":"UserMessage","id":"01a11bf9-7f6e-76b0-9995-435b1f17f807","client_id":"96df7c48-9718-4239-9f93-162c40f90e93","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$ai-code-intelligence $ai-update-knowledge $laravel-fullstack-admin-suite Чат, создай пожалуйста для этого проекта `symbol relations`, и запиши их в БД в таблицу `aic_symbol_relations` в проект `ai-code-intelligence`\n\nIf you have any questions, problems, doubts, or are having trouble making a decision—stop and reach out to me; I'll help you solve the problem.\n\nDon't forget about Laravel Boost, functions, methods, services, helpers, and other tools designed to simplify development and facilitate the reuse of existing functionality.\n\nUse `cua_repl`/`node_rep` mcp tool for connecting to browser using my session if it needs.\n\nUse `rg`, `Serena/LSP`, `ast-grep`, `Tree-sitter` from my PC and `PostgreSQL` (it it needs) from the `ai-code-intelligence-postgres-postgresql` dockers container for finding any code that you need.\n\nYou can delegate the work to `AI subagents`. Use no more than 4 sub-agents! Reuse sub-agents if any are available! If there are no available agents and 4 sub-agents have already been created, then you absolutely must not create any new sub-agents! Read `docs/ai-ai-agent-collaboration.md`.\n\nYou must call the file `/app/embeddin file .js` from the `ai-code-intelligence-postgres` docker container that it determines in the `/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/getting-started.md` documentation!","text_elements":[{"byte_range":{"start":0,"end":21},"placeholder":"$ai-code-intelligence"},{"byte_range":{"start":22,"end":42},"placeholder":"$ai-update-knowledge"},{"byte_range":{"start":43,"end":73},"placeholder":"$laravel-fullstack-admin-suite"}]},{"type":"skill","name":"ai-code-intelligence","path":"/home/kamaz/www/strateg-projects/strateg-export-products/.codex/skills/ai-code-intelligence/SKILL.md"},{"type":"skill","name":"ai-update-knowledge","path":"/home/kamaz/www/strateg-projects/strateg-export-products/.codex/skills/ai-update-knowledge/SKILL.md"},{"type":"skill","name":"laravel-fullstack-admin-suite","path":"/home/kamaz/www/strateg-projects/strateg-export-products/.codex/skills/laravel-fullstack-admin-suite/SKILL.md"}]},"started_at_ms":1791470698351,"completed_at_ms":1791470698351}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-08T14:44:58.356Z","ordinal":11764,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a11bf9-7f74-7dd3-b22d-443e46b1d1dd","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"<skill>\n<name>ai-code-intelligence</name>\n<path>/home/kamaz/www/strateg-projects/strateg-export-products/.codex/skills/ai-code-intelligence/SKILL.md</path>\n---\nname: ai-code-intelligence\ndescription: Explains how to use the shared ai-code-intelligence documentation, host-side code tools, PostgreSQL retrieval, and paid embeddings safely. Use when indexing or searching a project in the shared intelligence database, or when creating/updating project documentation about that integration, especially docs/ai-code-intelligence.md.\nargument-hint: \"[task or repository]\"\nmetadata:\n  author: AI Factory\n  version: \"1.0\"\n  category: documentation-and-code-intelligence\n---\n\n# AI Code Intelligence\n\nUse this skill to work with the shared `ai-code-intelligence` project and document that workflow accurately. Treat the repository as an evidence store, not as an authority over the source code.\n\n## 1. Read the Documentation in Context\n\n1. Identify the current repository root and read its `AGENTS.md` first. Follow direct links to the shared `ai-code-intelligence` project; do not guess its path.\n2. Read the shared project's `AGENTS.md` and `README.md`. Then read only the pages needed for the task:\n   - `docs/getting-started.md` for Docker, database, and embedding setup.\n   - `docs/architecture.md` for tool responsibilities and retrieval boundaries.\n   - `docs/database.md` for table ownership, constraints, and project-scoped records.\n   - `docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md` for search selection, index creation, retry behavior, and chunking.\n   - `docs/agent-workflow.md` for investigation and verification sequence.\n   - `docs/security.md` for secret handling and shared-data safeguards.\n   - `docs/integrations.md` for available database/MCP surfaces.\n3. Check the current database schema and scripts when documentation might be stale. Prefer the checked-in DDL and functions over copied examples.\n4. Resolve the target project by its stable slug, then scope every project-owned database read/write by `project_id`.\n\nFor tool commands, index recovery, vector search, and common failures, read [the detailed workflow reference](references/AI-CODE-INTELLIGENCE.md).\n\n## 2. Choose and Invoke Tools Correctly\n\nUse the cheapest precise search first:\n\n- Exact path, string, route, config key, or error: `rg`.\n- Class/method definitions and references: Serena/LSP.\n- Repeated code shape: `ast-grep`.\n- Syntax-aware PHP metadata and chunk boundaries: Tree-sitter.\n- Unknown business wording: PostgreSQL full-text and vector retrieval.\n- Runtime page behavior: the available browser inspection tool.\n\nCheck which environment has the tools. In this setup, PostgreSQL and Node.js run in Docker; `ast-grep`, Tree-sitter Python bindings, and Serena are host tools. A missing command inside the database container does not establish that it is absent on the host. Check `command -v`, `serena --version`, and the Python interpreter that can import `tree_sitter_language_pack`.\n\nSerena may need to download a PHP language server on its first run. A DNS or package-registry failure is a tool-installation problem; retry after network access is available. Tree-sitter bindings can fail during large or repeated AST traversals. Parse a representative file first. If a fallback is required, record it and do not describe line-based chunks as syntax-aware.\n\n## 3. Treat Embeddings as a Paid, Persistent Artifact\n\nEmbedding calls use the OpenAI API and can incur cost. Generate or refresh them only when the user's request authorizes that work. Before any call:\n\n1. Inspect existing result files, project/chunk counts, chunk hashes, model registration, and indexing-run status.\n2. Reuse complete results. Do not re-embed unchanged chunks just because a later database step failed.\n3. Exclude secrets, credentials, local environment files, generated/runtime data, and unapproved source. Embeddings remain source-derived data.\n4. Write each batch immediately to the configured persistent `app/embedding-results/` directory. Never keep the only copy in container `/tmp`.\n5. Keep a batch manifest mapping each result file to chunk IDs and content hashes so interrupted database writes can resume without new API calls.\n\nThe current `app/embedding.js` prints its API key and input texts to stdout. Suppress that output and do not expose captured logs. In the local Compose setup, the host results directory is mounted at `/app/embedding-results/` inside the PostgreSQL/Node container. See [the paid-embedding and recovery instructions](references/AI-CODE-INTELLIGENCE.md#paid-embeddings-and-safe-retries).\n\n## 4. Write and Verify the Index in Safe Stages\n\n1. Create or resolve the project row; never infer a project ID from another project.\n2. Upsert model metadata and persist files, symbols, documents, knowledge, and chunks with the same project ID.\n3. Generate and persist embedding batch files, then write vectors to PostgreSQL.\n4. Commit vector rows before creating the project/model HNSW index. In the local service, the restricted `$POSTGRES_SIMPLE_USER` can write rows but may not own `aic_embeddings`; use the table-owner role for `aic_create_project_embedding_hnsw_index(...)`.\n5. Verify file, symbol, chunk, embedding, document, knowledge, and index counts. Mark the `aic_indexing_runs` record `completed` only after those checks pass.\n6. If a stage fails, inspect the database and persistent batch files before retrying. Do not broadly delete project data or recreate a project as a shortcut.\n\nFor vector retrieval, generate the query vector with the same registered model and dimensions as stored vectors. Resolve the project's slug and pass its project ID and model ID to `aic_search_chunks_vector_hnsw(...)` (or the exact-search helper when required). Review source files for all important results; vector similarity proposes candidates and does not prove behavior.\n\n## 5. Create and Maintain Project Documentation\n\nFor a first documentation pass, inspect the real repository structure, framework, dependencies, runtime configuration, database access, tests, integrations, and existing docs before writing. Never invent available tools or commands. Keep the README a concise landing page and put setup, architecture, configuration, API, deployment, testing, security, and code-intelligence details in focused pages under the configured documentation directory.\n\nWhen a project uses the shared intelligence database, create a `docs/ai-code-intelligence.md` page. Document:\n\n- the shared database's purpose and the project's stable slug;\n- how to locate and read the shared documentation;\n- which tools run on the host and which services run in Docker;\n- verified commands, model, dimensions, result-file location, and project-scoped search function;\n- API cost, stdout/logging, secret exclusion, persistent batch recovery, and database-role nuances;\n- actual index counts, limitations, parser fallbacks, and whether symbol relations were populated;\n- relative links to relevant project docs and shared documentation.\n\nFor existing docs, read the configured docs path, README, AGENTS.md, and linked pages first. Make targeted updates without replacing unrelated content. Keep every claim tied to source/config/DDL or a verified run. Update the README documentation table and the `AGENTS.md` Documentation table when pages are added or removed. External shared-project docs are a separate project: edit them only when the user explicitly asks.\n\nUse [the documentation checklist and page outline](references/PROJECT-DOCUMENTATION.md) for first-time documentation and `docs/ai-code-intelligence.md` updates.\n\n## 6. Final Review\n\nBefore finishing, check all modified pages and examples against the actual project and database schema. Verify Markdown links, commands, code-fence balance, and README/AGENTS documentation indexes. Report any tool failure, fallback, missing relation data, or incomplete indexing clearly. Do not claim successful semantic indexing when only line chunks or declaration-line matches were stored.\n</skill>"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a11bf9-7de2-72a3-b949-e54787d105e6","create_time":1791470698.356097,"content_item_kinds":["skills.selected_skill_instructions"]}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-08T14:44:58.356Z","ordinal":11765,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a11bf9-7f74-7dd3-b22d-444889d5e4fa","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"<skill>\n<name>ai-update-knowledge</name>\n<path>/home/kamaz/www/strateg-projects/strateg-export-products/.codex/skills/ai-update-knowledge/SKILL.md</path>\n---\nname: ai-update-knowledge\ndescription: Refreshes changed project files, knowledge, symbols, relations, chunks, and embeddings in the shared ai-code-intelligence database. Use for fast, evidence-based incremental index updates that avoid reprocessing unchanged files or paying to re-embed unchanged chunks.\nargument-hint: \"[project slug or refresh scope]\"\nmetadata:\n  author: AI Factory\n  version: \"1.0\"\n  category: code-intelligence\n---\n\n# Incremental AI Knowledge Refresh\n\nUse this workflow to refresh existing project index data. For initial project indexing or knowledge cleanup beyond changed sources, use `$ai-create-knowledge` and its full workflow. The project guide [docs/ai-knowledge-refresh.md](../../../docs/ai-knowledge-refresh.md) intentionally mirrors these instructions.\n\n## Required workflow\n\n### 1. Establish the project and baseline\n\n1. Read `AGENTS.md`, `docs/ai-code-intelligence.md`, and the shared AIC `AGENTS.md` plus relevant getting-started, database, retrieval/indexing, and security docs. Confirm the live service, schema, embedding utility path, and persistent output mount from documentation.\n2. Resolve the requested slug from `aic_projects` and retain its active `project_id`. Never infer or reuse an ID.\n3. Inspect Git status, `HEAD`, the latest completed indexing run, indexed commit, counts, file/chunk hashes, model, HNSW state, and existing manifests/results. Include untracked/requested documentation edits explicitly.\n4. Compare the indexed commit to the intended source state. Build one exact list of changed, approved, indexable files.\n\nUse scoped summary queries and keep environment values, secrets, source dumps, and vector values out of tool output.\n\n### 2. Freeze and hash inputs before processing\n\nFinish source/docs/link/knowledge edits first. Avoid volatile batch-count documentation. Exclude credentials, `.env`, private configuration, dependencies, build/runtime/generated files, binary content, and unapproved paths. Record the source filters, commit, parsers, and chunking settings.\n\nDo not change indexed inputs after calculating chunk hashes. If source changes mid-run, recompute the affected chunks and embeddings before completion.\n\n### 3. Incrementally parse source structure\n\n- Use `rg` for exact searches, Tree-sitter for batch syntax parsing and evidence, and Serena/LSP only where semantic symbol/reference information is needed.\n- Reuse valid caches. Do not run a full Serena index for a small change; run one project-wide pass only if its cache is absent or broadly stale. Use targeted `index-file` work only on files needing LSP evidence. Do not run concurrent writers against the same Serena cache.\n- Parse changed files in one Tree-sitter host-side pass when possible. Never describe line chunks as AST chunks.\n- Upsert only changed declarations, preserving symbol IDs where possible. If IDs change, rebuild affected incoming and outgoing relations because foreign-key cascades can remove edges.\n- Replace outgoing edges only for refreshed source symbols, within a transaction. Resolve targets in the same project only when matching is unambiguous; otherwise retain `target_qualified_name`. Store extractor, source path, line, confidence, and short evidence.\n- Never fabricate call paths. Record parser errors, unmapped matches, dynamic dispatch limits, and unresolved targets.\n\n### 4. Persist changed content and evidence-backed knowledge\n\nCreate an `aic_indexing_runs` row with `running`, project ID, commit, and refresh scope. In a project-scoped transaction:\n\n- update changed `aic_files` and `aic_documents` metadata;\n- update existing knowledge records rather than duplicating them; create only genuinely new facts;\n- link knowledge chunks to `aic_knowledge_items`, document chunks to `aic_documents`, and code chunks to `aic_files`;\n- compare source identity, sequence, chunk type, and content hash; upsert changed chunks and remove only obsolete sequences for the exact changed source;\n- persist only verified symbols and relation deltas.\n\nKnowledge must be concise, current, evidence-backed, and include `source_reference` and justified confidence. Keep replaced items `superseded` and current facts `active`. Do not delete broad project data or reset shared tables.\n\n### 5. Embed changed chunks only\n\nSelect chunks that lack an embedding for the registered model or whose `source_content_hash` differs from the chunk hash. Inspect existing manifests before retrying. Reuse completed results; do not incur API cost for unchanged chunks.\n\nEmbedding calls use the OpenAI API and can incur cost. Proceed only when the user's request authorizes embedding generation; an index-only request does not automatically authorize paid embedding calls.\n\nBefore an API call, persist a manifest mapping project slug/ID, indexing run, commit, model ID/name, dimensions, batch files, chunk IDs, and content hashes. Store all outputs in the project's persistent directory:\n\n```text\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/app/embedding-results/strateg-export-products/{year}/{month}/\n```\n\nConsult shared `docs/getting-started.md` for the exact container and script. In the current setup, invoke `/app/embedding.js` in `ai-code-intelligence-postgres-postgresql` with the registered model. Keep the API key in container environment variables, suppress stdout, and keep tool logs private. Reuse saved output if database insertion fails.\n\nInsert vectors scoped by project, chunk, and model, with expected dimensions and the exact source hash. Verify/reuse the existing project/model HNSW index; create it only if absent and with its documented owner role, in a separate stage from vector inserts.\n\n### 6. Verify before completion\n\nCheck the current project counts, file/document hashes, symbol and relation coverage, knowledge/chunk references, vector model and dimensions, and that no current chunk lacks a matching embedding. Confirm HNSW index and manifest mappings. Update indexing-run stats/limitations and mark it `completed` only after all required stages succeed.\n\nFor failures, inspect run status, committed rows, and persistent manifests/results, then resume at the failed stage. Do not retry successful API batches or broadly delete/rebuild project data.\n\n## Efficiency rules\n\n- One preflight inventory and a small final verification set are preferable to repeated ad hoc queries.\n- Freeze documentation before chunking; avoid volatile exact batch counts in indexed docs.\n- Hash before embedding and select only missing or stale vectors.\n- Use parser caches and changed-file scope; full-project work is only for a demonstrated stale or absent baseline.\n- Send concise progress updates during long parser or embedding stages.\n\n## Final report\n\nState project slug, commit, refreshed file/document/knowledge/symbol/relation/chunk/embedding counts, model and dimensions, persistent manifest path, HNSW status, and known parser/coverage limits. Mention whether tests were run only when relevant; indexing is not application code verification.\n</skill>"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a11bf9-7de2-72a3-b949-e54787d105e6","create_time":1791470698.3565671,"content_item_kinds":["skills.selected_skill_instructions"]}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-08T14:44:58.357Z","ordinal":11766,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a11bf9-7f75-71c3-a51b-05fee3fae12b","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"<skill>\n<name>laravel-fullstack-admin-suite</name>\n<path>/home/kamaz/www/strateg-projects/strateg-export-products/.codex/skills/laravel-fullstack-admin-suite/SKILL.md</path>\n---\nname: laravel-fullstack-admin-suite\ndescription: Universal Laravel fullstack skill for Filament, Livewire, Tailwind UI, and backend logic with mandatory code-quality and security focus.\nallowed-tools: Read Write Bash(php *) Bash(vendor/bin/pint *) Bash(vendor/bin/phpstan *) Bash(composer *)\nmetadata:\n  version: \"1.0\"\n  category: laravel-fullstack\n  includes:\n    - filament-resource\n    - livewire-development\n    - php-best-practices\n    - aif-best-practices\n    - security-best-practices\n    - laravel-best-practices\n    - aif-security-checklist\n    - testing-best-practices\n    - tailwindcss-development\n---\n\n# Laravel Fullstack Admin Suite\n\n## When to use\n- New features that combine Filament admin, Livewire, and backend changes.\n- UI changes in the storefront/admin areas with Tailwind CSS v4.\n- Complex tasks requiring code quality, maintainability, and secure defaults.\n- Project-aligned fullstack work for Laravel 13 + Filament 5 + Livewire 4.\n\n## Composition and order\n1. `laravel-best-practices` for architecture and backend flow.\n2. `filament-resource` for Filament resources/pages/tables.\n3. `livewire-development` for reactive component logic.\n4. `php-best-practices` + `aif-best-practices` for style and maintainability.\n5. `security-best-practices` for secure-by-default checks on relevant paths.\n\n## Required quality gates\n- `vendor/bin/pint --dirty --format=agent`\n- `vendor/bin/phpstan analyse app --memory-limit=1G`\n- Targeted `php artisan test --compact ...` for modified flows.\n\n## Project adaptation notes\n- Preserve existing Filament v5 and Livewire v4 conventions already used in the project.\n- Minimize breaking changes for existing TS hooks (`data-*`, IDs, and class contracts).\n- Reuse existing Blade components and shared services before introducing new abstractions.\n</skill>"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a11bf9-7de2-72a3-b949-e54787d105e6","create_time":1791470698.357011,"content_item_kinds":["skills.selected_skill_instructions"]}}}
