{"timestamp":"2026-10-08T14:45:01.740Z","ordinal":11771,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a11bf9-8cac-72f1-9085-c7f786087ba4","call_id":"call_c309a5b5bef445dbb0840f13890f1a75","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.0 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"[{\"name\":\"exec_command\",\"description\":\"Runs a command in a PTY, returning output or a session ID for ongoing interaction.\\n\\nexec tool declaration:\\n```ts\\ndeclare const tools: { exec_command(args: {\\n  // Shell command to execute.\\n  cmd: string;\\n  // User-facing approval question for `require_escalated`; omit otherwise.\\n  justification?: string;\\n  // True runs the shell with -l/-i semantics; false disables them. Defaults to true.\\n  login?: boolean;\\n  // Output token budget. Defaults to 10000 tokens; larger requests may be capped by policy.\\n  max_output_tokens?: number;\\n  // Reusable approval prefix for `cmd`, only with `sandbox_permissions: \\\"require_escalated\\\"`; for example [\\\"git\\\", \\\"pull\\\"].\\n  prefix_rule?: Array<string>;\\n  // Per-command sandbox override. Defaults to `use_default`; use `require_escalated` for unsandboxed execution.\\n  sandbox_permissions?: \\\"use_default\\\" | \\\"require_escalated\\\";\\n  // Shell binary to launch. Defaults to the user's default shell.\\n  shell?: string;\\n  // True allocates a PTY for the command; false or omitted uses plain pipes.\\n  tty?: boolean;\\n  // Working directory for the command. Defaults to the turn cwd.\\n  workdir?: string;\\n  // Wait before yielding output. Defaults to 10000 ms; effective range is 250-30000 ms.\\n  yield_time_ms?: number;\\n}): Promise<{\\n  // Chunk identifier included when the response reports one.\\n  chunk_id?: string;\\n  // Process exit code when the command finished during this call.\\n  exit_code?: number;\\n  // Approximate token count before output truncation.\\n  original_token_count?: number;\\n  // Command output text, possibly truncated.\\n  output: string;\\n  // Session identifier to pass to write_stdin when the process is still running.\\n  session_id?: number;\\n  // Elapsed wall time spent waiting for output in seconds.\\n  wall_time_seconds: number;\\n}>; };\\n```\"},{\"name\":\"mcp__chrome_devtools__lighthouse_audit\",\"description\":\"Get Lighthouse score and reports for accessibility, SEO, best practices, and agentic browsing. This excludes performance. For performance audits, run performance_start_trace\\n\\nexec tool declaration:\\n```ts\\ndeclare const tools: { mcp__chrome_devtools__lighthouse_audit(args: {\\n  // Device to emulate.\\n  device?: \\\"desktop\\\" | \\\"mobile\\\";\\n  // \\\"navigation\\\" reloads & audits. \\\"snapshot\\\" analyzes current state.\\n  mode?: \\\"navigation\\\" | \\\"snapshot\\\";\\n  // Directory for reports. If omitted, uses temporary files.\\n  outputDirPath?: string;\\n  // Targets a specific page by ID.\\n  pageId: number;\\n  [key: string]: unknown;\\n}): Promise<CallToolResult>; };\\n```\"},{\"name\":\"mcp__dev_strateg_export_products_laravel_boost__application_info\",\"description\":\"Laravel ecosystem MCP server offering database schema access, error logs, semantic documentation search, and more. Boost helps with code generation.\\n\\nGet comprehensive application information including PHP version, Laravel version, database engine, and all installed packages with their versions. You should use this tool on each new chat, and use the package & version data to write version specific code for the packages that exist.\\n\\nexec tool declaration:\\n```ts\\ndeclare const tools: { mcp__dev_strateg_export_products_laravel_boost__application_info(args: { [key: string]: unknown; }): Promise<CallToolResult>; };\\n```\"},{\"name\":\"mcp__dev_strateg_export_products_laravel_boost__browser_logs\",\"description\":\"Laravel ecosystem MCP server offering database schema access, error logs, semantic documentation search, and more. Boost helps with code generation.\\n\\nRead the last N log entries from the BROWSER log. Very helpful for debugging the frontend and JS/Javascript\\n\\nexec tool declaration:\\n```ts\\ndeclare const tools: { mcp__dev_strateg_export_products_laravel_boost__browser_logs(args: {\\n  // Number of log entries to return.\\n  entries: number;\\n}): Promise<CallToolResult>; };\\n```\"},{\"name\":\"mcp__dev_strateg_export_products_laravel_boost__database_connections\",\"description\":\"Laravel ecosystem MCP server offering database schema access, error logs, semantic documentation search, and more. Boost helps with code generation.\\n\\nList the configured database connection names for this application.\\n\\nexec tool declaration:\\n```ts\\ndeclare const tools: { mcp__dev_strateg_export_products_laravel_boost__database_connections(args: { [key: string]: unknown; }): Promise<CallToolResult>; };\\n```\"},{\"name\":\"mcp__dev_strateg_export_products_laravel_boost__database_query\",\"description\":\"Laravel ecosystem MCP server offering database schema access, error logs, semantic documentation search, and more. Boost helps with code generation.\\n\\nExecute a read-only SQL query against the configured database.\\n\\nexec tool declaration:\\n```ts\\ndeclare const tools: { mcp__dev_strateg_export_products_laravel_boost__database_query(args: {\\n  // Optional database connection name to use. Defaults to the application's default connection.\\n  database?: string;\\n  // The SQL query to execute. Only read-only queries are allowed (i.e. SELECT, SHOW, EXPLAIN, DESCRIBE).\\n  query: string;\\n}): Promise<CallToolResult>; };\\n```\"},{\"name\":\"mcp__dev_strateg_export_products_laravel_boost__database_schema\",\"description\":\"Laravel ecosystem MCP server offering database schema access, error logs, semantic documentation search, and more. Boost helps with code generation.\\n\\nRead the database schema for this application. Returns table names, columns, indexes, and foreign keys. Use \\\"summary\\\" mode first to get an overview (table names with column types only), then call again without \\\"summary\\\" and with a \\\"filter\\\" to get full details for specific tables. Params: \\\"summary\\\" (default false) - returns only table names and column types; \\\"database\\\" - connection name; \\\"filter\\\" - substring match on table names; \\\"include_column_details\\\" (default false) - adds nullable, default, auto_increment, comments; \\\"include_views\\\" (default false); \\\"include_routines\\\" (default false) - stored procedures, functions, sequences.\\n\\nexec tool declaration:\\n```ts\\ndeclare const tools: { mcp__dev_strateg_export_products_laravel_boost__database_schema(args: {\\n  // Name of the database connection to dump (defaults to app's default connection, often not needed)\\n  database?: string;\\n  // Filter tables by name (substring match).\\n  filter?: string;\\n  // Include full column metadata (nullable, default, auto_increment, comments, generation). Defaults to false.\\n  include_column_details?: boolean;\\n  // Include stored procedures, functions, and sequences. Defaults to false.\\n  include_routines?: boolean;\\n  // Include database views. Defaults to false.\\n  include_views?: boolean;\\n  // Return only table names and their column types. Use this first to understand the database structure, then request full details for specific tables using \\\"filter\\\". Defaults to false.\\n  summary?: boolean;\\n}): Promise<CallToolResult>; };\\n```\"},{\"name\":\"mcp__dev_strateg_export_products_laravel_boost__get_absolute_url\",\"description\":\"Laravel ecosystem MCP server offering database schema access, error logs, semantic documentation search, and more. Boost helps with code generation.\\n\\nGet the absolute URL for a given relative path or named route. If no arguments are provided, you will get the absolute URL for \\\"/\\\"\\n\\nexec tool declaration:\\n```ts\\ndeclare const tools: { mcp__dev_strateg_export_products_laravel_boost__get_absolute_url(args: {\\n  // The relative URL/path (e.g. \\\"/dashboard\\\") to convert to an absolute URL.\\n  path?: string;\\n  // The named route to generate an absolute URL for (e.g. \\\"home\\\").\\n  route?: string;\\n}): Promise<CallToolResult>; };\\n```\"},{\"name\":\"mcp__dev_strateg_export_products_laravel_boost__last_error\",\"description\":\"Laravel ecosystem MCP server offering database schema access, error logs, semantic documentation search, and more. Boost helps with code generation.\\n\\nGet details of the last error/exception created in this application on the backend. Use browser-logs tool for browser errors.\\n\\nexec tool declaration:\\n```ts\\ndeclare const tools: { mcp__dev_strateg_export_products_laravel_boost__last_error(args: { [key: string]: unknown; }): Promise<CallToolResult>; };\\n```\"},{\"name\":\"mcp__dev_strateg_export_products_laravel_boost__read_log_entries\",\"description\":\"Laravel ecosystem MCP server offering database schema access, error logs, semantic documentation search, and more. Boost helps with code generation.\\n\\nRead the last N log entries from the application log, correctly handling multi-line PSR-3 formatted logs and JSON-formatted logs. Only works for log files.\\n\\nexec tool declaration:\\n```ts\\ndeclare const tools: { mcp__dev_strateg_export_products_laravel_boost__read_log_entries(args: {\\n  // Number of log entries to return.\\n  entries: number;\\n}): Promise<CallToolResult>; };\\n```\"},{\"name\":\"mcp__dev_strateg_export_products_laravel_boost__record_rule\",\"description\":\"Laravel ecosystem MCP server offering database schema access, error logs, semantic documentation search, and more. Boost helps with code generation.\\n\\nRecord a durable project rule in the shared, committed markdown notes in .ai/rules, grouped by area. Only call this when the user explicitly asks for a rule to be recorded, remembered, or documented. Instructions for the work at hand are not rules, no matter how emphatic: \\\"remove this typo\\\", \\\"use X here\\\", and \\\"don't do that again\\\" are work to do, not rules to record. A rule constrains future work across many files; it never describes a single fix. Never call this on your own initiative, as a byproduct of a change, or to summarize what you just did. When in doubt, do not call it. Pass a glob for the files it applies to (e.g. app/Http/Controllers/**). Keep the note to a few lines. Do not record secrets, transient state, or anything already obvious from the code.\\n\\nexec tool declaration:\\n```ts\\ndeclare const tools: { mcp__dev_strateg_export_products_laravel_boost__record_rule(args: {\\n  // Glob for the files this rule applies to, for example \\\"app/Http/Controllers/**\\\" or \\\"app/Models/*.php\\\". This routes the rule into a shared area file and is how agents find it later.\\n  glob: string;\\n  // A few lines stating the rule plainly. No essays.\\n  note: string;\\n  // A short, specific heading, for example \\\"Extend BaseController for tenant scoping\\\".\\n  title: string;\\n}): Promise<CallToolResult>; };\\n```\"},{\"name\":\"mcp__dev_strateg_export_products_laravel_boost__search_docs\",\"description\":\"Laravel ecosystem MCP server offering database schema access, error logs, semantic documentation search, and more. Boost helps with code generation.\\n\\nSearch for up-to-date version-specific documentation related to this project and its packages. This tool will search Laravel hosted documentation based on the packages installed and is perfect for all Laravel ecosystem packages. Laravel, Inertia, Pest, Livewire, Filament, Nova, Tailwind, and more. You must use this tool to search for Laravel-ecosystem docs before using other approaches. The results provided are for this project's package version and does not cover all versions of the package.\\n\\nexec tool declaration:\\n```ts\\ndeclare const tools: { mcp__dev_strateg_export_products_laravel_boost__search_docs(args: {\\n  // Package names to limit searching to from application-info. Useful if you know the package(s) you need. i.e. laravel/framework, inertiajs/inertia-laravel, @inertiajs/react\\n  packages?: Array<string>;\\n  // List of queries to perform, pass multiple if you aren't sure if it is \\\"toggle\\\" or \\\"switch\\\", for example\\n  queries: Array<string>;\\n  // Maximum number of tokens to return in the response. Defaults to 3,000 tokens, maximum 1,000,000 tokens. If results are truncated, or you need more complete documentation, increase this value (e.g.5000, 10000)\\n  token_limit?: number;\\n}): Promise<CallToolResult>; };\\n```\"}]"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a11bf9-7de2-72a3-b949-e54787d105e6","create_time":1791470701.7401993}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
