{"timestamp":"2026-10-08T14:45:09.517Z","ordinal":11784,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a0a02b-47ee-7913-b18b-d4d4b0be8b60","turn_id":"01a11bf9-7de2-72a3-b949-e54787d105e6","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-20162654-0f78-42bf-8b62-44df318062b8","process_id":"79109","command":["/bin/bash","-lc","sed -n '1,240p' /home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/AGENTS.md && sed -n '1,220p' /home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/README.md && sed -n '1,280p' /home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/getting-started.md && sed -n '1,280p' /home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/database.md && sed -n '1,280p' /home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md && sed -n '1,220p' /home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/security.md && sed -n '1,180p' docs/ai-agent-collaboration.md"],"cwd":"file:///home/kamaz/www/strateg-projects/strateg-export-products","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,240p' /home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/AGENTS.md","name":"AGENTS.md","path":"/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/AGENTS.md"},{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,220p' /home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/README.md","name":"README.md","path":"/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/README.md"},{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,280p' /home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/getting-started.md","name":"getting-started.md","path":"/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/getting-started.md"},{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,280p' /home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/database.md","name":"database.md","path":"/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/database.md"},{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,280p' /home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md","name":"retrieval-and-indexing.md","path":"/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md"},{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,220p' /home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/security.md","name":"security.md","path":"/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/security.md"},{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,180p' docs/ai-agent-collaboration.md","name":"ai-agent-collaboration.md","path":"docs/ai-agent-collaboration.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"# Repository Guidelines\n\n## Project Structure & Module Organization\n\nThis repository packages a shared PostgreSQL database for code intelligence. The root `docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/` contains ordered SQL initialization scripts: extensions, schema, indexes, search functions, and vector helpers. `.docker/dev/` contains the local development image, PostgreSQL configuration, Compose file, and environment files. `ai-code-intelligence/` is the standalone deployment package with its own Dockerfile, Compose file, SQL initialization scripts, example environment file, and README. `app/` contains the small Node.js embedding utility and provider implementation. The root README documents the schema and recommended agent workflow.\n\n## Build, Test, and Development Commands\n\n- `make up-dev` starts the development PostgreSQL service.\n- `make down-dev` stops the development service; it does not remove stored volumes.\n- `make build-dev` builds the development image.\n- `make restart-dev` restarts the service; `make rebuild-dev` rebuilds and starts it.\n- `npm install --prefix app` installs the embedding utility dependencies.\n- `node app/embedding.js <API_KEY> text-embedding-3-small \"text to embed\"` generates embeddings.\n\nThere is no configured automated test or lint command. For database changes, review SQL ordering and validate initialization against a disposable local database. Do not use destructive volume/database cleanup commands against the shared development data.\n\n## Coding Style & Naming Conventions\n\nKeep SQL readable, use uppercase SQL keywords, explicit column lists, and descriptive `snake_case` identifiers. Preserve the numeric prefix and dependency order of initialization scripts (for example, `003_indexes.sql`). Use two spaces for JSON and JavaScript indentation, ES modules, and descriptive camelCase JavaScript names. Keep deployment examples aligned between the root and standalone package when both represent the same behavior.\n\n## Testing Guidelines\n\nNo dedicated test suite is present. Verify SQL changes by applying initialization on a clean local database and checking the affected tables, indexes, or functions. For changes to `app/`, run the relevant command manually and avoid committing generated credentials or real API keys.\n\n## Commit & Pull Request Guidelines\n\nGit history is unavailable in this checkout, so no established commit pattern could be confirmed. Use concise imperative commit subjects, such as `Add vector search helper index`. Pull requests should explain the schema or runtime impact, list validation performed, call out environment/configuration changes, and include logs or screenshots only when they clarify behavior.\n\n## Security & Configuration\n\nNever commit `.docker/dev/env/` secrets or expose their values in documentation and logs. Use `.env.example` for safe placeholders. Scope all project-specific database queries by `project_id` or resolve a project by slug first. Persist paid embedding results under `app/embedding-results/`; inspect and reuse saved batches before retries. The current embedding script echoes its API key and input texts to stdout, so suppress stdout. Create project HNSW indexes as the table owner, after committing vector rows.\n\n## Documentation\n\n| Document               | Path                             | Description                                   |\n|------------------------|----------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|\n| README                 | `README.md`                      | Project landing page and guide index          |\n| Getting Started        | `docs/getting-started.md`        | Local database startup and embedding setup    |\n| Architecture           | `docs/architecture.md`           | Retrieval model and system responsibilities   |\n| Database Reference     | `docs/database.md`               | Schema, ownership, and SQL examples           |\n| Retrieval and Indexing | `docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md` | Search, indexing, and code intelligence tools |\n| Agent Workflow         | `docs/agent-workflow.md`         | Investigation and verification practices      |\n| Integrations           | `docs/integrations.md`           | MCP surface and upstream references           |\n| Security               | `docs/security.md`               | Local database and data protection guidance   |\n\n## AI Context Files\n\n| File                          | Purpose                                    |\n|-------------------------------|--------------------------------------------|\n| `.ai-factory/DESCRIPTION.md`  | Project scope and technology summary       |\n| `.ai-factory/ARCHITECTURE.md` | Repository architecture guidelines         |\n| `.ai-factory/rules/base.md`   | Detected naming and structural conventions |# AI Code Intelligence Database\n\n> Local, multi-project PostgreSQL storage for AI-assisted software development.\n\nThis repository combines PostgreSQL full-text search, fuzzy lookup, pgvector retrieval, and relational code relationships. It includes a Docker-based development database and a small Node.js embedding utility. Always scope project data by `project_id` or resolve the project by slug.\n\n## Quick Start\n\nRequires Docker Compose. Start the local database:\n\n```bash\nmake up-dev\n```\n\nCheck the service state:\n\n```bash\ndocker compose -f .docker/dev/docker-compose.yml ps\n```\n\nThe embedding utility uses the OpenAI API and `text-embedding-3-small`; see [Getting Started](docs/getting-started.md) for safe setup details.\n\n## Capabilities\n\n- PostgreSQL 18 with pgvector, full-text search, and pg_trgm.\n- Project, file, symbol, relationship, document, chunk, embedding, and indexing-run storage.\n- Ordered database initialization scripts for local development and standalone deployment.\n- A hybrid retrieval workflow that combines exact, structural, semantic, and graph search.\n\n## Documentation\n\n| Guide                                                    | Description                                          |\n|----------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------|\n| [Getting Started](docs/getting-started.md)               | Local database startup and embedding setup           |\n| [Architecture](docs/architecture.md)                     | Retrieval model and system responsibilities          |\n| [Database Reference](docs/database.md)                   | Schema, ownership, and SQL examples                  |\n| [Retrieval and Indexing](docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md) | Search, indexing, and code intelligence tools        |\n| [Agent Workflow](docs/agent-workflow.md)                 | Investigation, verification, and knowledge practices |\n| [Integrations](docs/integrations.md)                     | MCP surface and upstream references                  |\n| [Security](docs/security.md)                             | Local database and data protection guidance          |[Back to README](../README.md) · [Architecture and Retrieval Model →](./architecture.md)\n\n# Getting Started\n\n## Safe embedding workflow\n\n- Development environment files live in `.docker/dev/env/`. Never copy their values into docs, chat, logs, or command output.\n- The database container runs PostgreSQL and Node.js. Tools such as `/home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep`, Tree-sitter Python bindings, and Serena/LSP may be installed on the host; check the host environment and the exact Python interpreter instead of assuming they must exist in Docker.\n- Keep paid embedding results in the host directory `app/embedding-results/project-name/{year}/{month}/`, mounted in the container at `/app/embedding-results/project-name/{year}/{month}/`. Use stable batch filenames, check which files already exist before retrying, and resume from completed batches. Do not store paid results only in `/tmp` or another ephemeral container path.\n- The current `app/embedding.js` prints its API key and input texts to standard output. Suppress stdout for the embedding call; do not share captured logs. Only index sanitized, non-secret source content.\n- Use `text-embedding-3-small` for the existing model configuration. Keep batches bounded and persist each result file before inserting vectors into PostgreSQL.\n- The restricted `$POSTGRES_SIMPLE_USER` can write project records but may not own `aic_embeddings`. Create project/model HNSW indexes as `$POSTGRES_USER` (table owner). Commit vector rows before index creation and mark the indexing run complete only after the index is verified.\n- Never run `docker system prune`, `docker volume rm`, or `DROP DATABASE` against the shared development data. Before retrying, inspect project counts, run status, and saved embedding files; do not delete a project as a shortcut.\n\n### Run SQL through the PostgreSQL container\n\nFrom the ai-code-intelligence repository root, execute SQL with `psql` inside the database container:\n\n```bash\ndocker compose -f .docker/dev/docker-compose.yml exec -T \\\n  ai-code-intelligence-postgres-postgresql sh -lc \\\n  'psql -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -P pager=off -U \"$POSTGRES_SIMPLE_USER\" -d \"$POSTGRES_DB\" -c \"SELECT slug, id FROM aic_projects ORDER BY slug;\"'\n```\n\nUse `$POSTGRES_SIMPLE_USER` for normal project-scoped reads and writes. Resolve the target project's ID by slug, then include `project_id` in queries against its files, chunks, and embeddings. For vector and HNSW queries, see [Retrieval and Indexing](./retrieval-and-indexing.md).\n\nExample for one sanitized batch:\n\n```bash\ndocker compose -f /home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/.docker/dev/docker-compose.yml \\\n  exec -T ai-code-intelligence-postgres-postgresql sh -lc '\n    node /app/embedding.js \"$OPENAI_API_KEY\" \\\n      /app/embedding-results/project-name/{year}/{month}/example-batch-0000.json \\\n      text-embedding-3-small \"class OrderService {}\" >/dev/null\n  '\n```\n\nThe embedding file persists on the host at `/app/embedding-results/project-name/{year}/{month}/example-batch-0000.json`.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Database startup\n\n### Files about DB tables\n\nDB tables schemas:\ndocker-entrypoint-initdb.d:\n - 001_extensions.sql\n - 002_schema.sql\n - 003_indexes.sql\n - 004_views_and_search_functions.sql\n - 005_vector_index_helpers.sql\n\n---\n\n## See Also\n\n- [Architecture and Retrieval Model](./architecture.md)\n- [Database Reference](./database.md)\n- [Retrieval and Indexing](./retrieval-and-indexing.md)[← Architecture and Retrieval Model](./architecture.md) · [Back to README](../README.md) · [Retrieval and Indexing →](./retrieval-and-indexing.md)\n\n# Database Reference\n\n## 6. PostgreSQL extensions\n\n`001_extensions.sql` enables:\n\n| Extension  | Purpose                                       |\n|------------|-----------------------------------------------|\n| `vector`   | Embedding storage and nearest-neighbor search |\n| `pgcrypto` | `gen_random_uuid()`                           |\n| `pg_trgm`  | Fuzzy symbol/path lookup                      |\n| `unaccent` | Optional normalization support                |\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 7. DDL model\n\nThe authoritative DDL is in `002_schema.sql`.\n\n### `projects`\n\nRoot tenant/entity for all stored projects.\n\n| Column           | Type        | Purpose                       |\n|------------------|-------------|-------------------------------|\n| `id`             | UUID PK     | Project identifier            |\n| `name`           | TEXT        | Human-readable name           |\n| `slug`           | TEXT UNIQUE | Stable local identifier       |\n| `root_path`      | TEXT        | Local repository path         |\n| `repository_url` | TEXT        | Optional Git remote           |\n| `default_branch` | TEXT        | Default branch                |\n| `active`         | BOOLEAN     | Whether the project is active |\n| `metadata`       | JSONB       | Project-specific metadata     |\n| timestamps       | TIMESTAMPTZ | Audit timestamps              |\n\nAll project-owned data cascades when a project is deleted. Composite project-scoped foreign keys also prevent accidental cross-project links between files, symbols, chunks, documents, knowledge, and embeddings.\n\n### `files`\n\nOne row per indexed file in a project.\n\nImportant columns:\n\n```text\nproject_id\npath\nlanguage\nfile_type\ncontent_hash\ngit_blob_sha\nlast_indexed_commit\nis_generated\nis_deleted\nindexed_at\n```\n\nUnique key:\n\n```text\n(project_id, path)\n```\n\n### `symbols`\n\nTree-sitter/LSP/Serena-derived symbols.\n\nTypical `kind` values:\n\n```text\nnamespace\nmodule\nclass\ninterface\ntrait\nenum\nmethod\nfunction\nproperty\nconstant\ntype\ncomponent\n```\n\nImportant columns:\n\n```text\nproject_id\nfile_id\nparent_symbol_id\nkind\nname\nqualified_name\nsignature\nvisibility\nstart_line/start_column\nend_line/end_column\ncontent_hash\nmetadata\n```\n\nThe schema intentionally does not constrain `kind` to a fixed enum because language ecosystems differ.\n\n### `aic_symbol_relations`\n\nStores directed edges in the project's code graph. A row means that `source_symbol_id` has a `relation_type` relationship to a target. For example, a method that calls another method points from the caller to the callee; a class that implements an interface points from the class to the interface.\n\n| Column                  |    Required | Meaning                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n|-------------------------|------------:|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| `id`                    |   Generated | Relation row UUID.                                                                                                                                                                      |\n| `project_id`            |         Yes | Owning project; must match both endpoint symbols.                                                                                                                                       |\n| `source_symbol_id`      |         Yes | Symbol where the relationship originates.                                                                                                                                               |\n| `target_symbol_id`      |          No | Resolved target symbol in the same project.                                                                                                                                             |\n| `target_qualified_name` | Conditional | Target name when it cannot be resolved to an indexed symbol.                                                                                                                            |\n| `relation_type`         |         Yes | Relationship kind, such as `calls`, `references`, `imports`, `extends`, `implements`, `uses`, `dispatches`, `listens`, `publishes`, `subscribes`, `reads`, `writes`, or `instantiates`. |\n| `source_line`           |          No | One-based line in the source file where the relationship is evidenced.                                                                                                                  |\n| `confidence`            |         Yes | Confidence score from `0.0` to `1.0`; defaults to `1.0`.                                                                                                                                |\n| `evidence`              |         Yes | JSONB details supporting the edge; defaults to `{}`.                                                                                                                                    |\n| `created_at`            |   Generated | Row creation time.                                                                                                                                                                      |\n\n`relation_type` is free text, not a database enum. Use the canonical lowercase values above consistently; add a new value only when the extractor has a distinct, useful relationship to represent. The database enforces nonblank types, valid confidence and line ranges, project-matched foreign keys, and at least one target identifier (`target_symbol_id` or a nonblank `target_qualified_name`).\n\nUse the actual table name `aic_symbol_relations` in SQL. The relation indexes support project/type filtering and incoming/outgoing traversal. There is no natural unique constraint for an edge, so `ON CONFLICT` cannot make relation writes idempotent by itself. Refresh a source symbol's outgoing edges by deleting its old rows and inserting the complete newly extracted set in one transaction.\n\nFor the extraction workflow and evidence/confidence guidance, see [relation collection and persistence](retrieval-and-indexing.md#required-relation-collection-workflow).\n\n```sql\nBEGIN;\n\nDELETE FROM aic_symbol_relations\nWHERE project_id = :project_id\n  AND source_symbol_id = :source_symbol_id;\n\nINSERT INTO aic_symbol_relations (\n    project_id,\n    source_symbol_id,\n    target_symbol_id,\n    target_qualified_name,\n    relation_type,\n    source_line,\n    confidence,\n    evidence\n)\nVALUES (\n    :project_id,\n    :source_symbol_id,\n    :target_symbol_id,\n    :target_qualified_name,\n    :relation_type,\n    :source_line,\n    :confidence,\n    :evidence::jsonb\n);\n\nCOMMIT;\n```\n\nRun the `DELETE` once for each refreshed source symbol, then insert all of its current edges before committing. If the extraction finds no edges, the delete alone correctly leaves that source with no outgoing relations. Do not delete rows for sources outside the refreshed set.\n\nAn indexed same-project target should use `target_symbol_id`; an external or unresolved target should use `NULL` for that column and a stable qualified name. `source_line` is one-based. Keep `evidence` structured, for example with extractor, evidence kind, source path, and a short source fragment or AST/LSP reference. Avoid storing secrets or large source bodies.\n\nWhen rebuilding symbols, account for `ON DELETE CASCADE` on both source and target foreign keys: deleting a symbol also deletes edges from other files that point to it. Re-resolve and rebuild both outgoing relations for refreshed symbols and incoming relations affected by changed/deleted targets in the same logical indexing run. Do not report graph indexing as complete while those dependent edges are stale.\n\n### `documents`\n\nProject documentation and external/local reference material.\n\nExamples:\n\n```text\nREADME\nADR\nOpenAPI document\ninternal architecture document\nvendor API reference\nAI Factory generated research\n```\n\nA document must have either `path` or `source_uri`.\n\n### `knowledge_items`\n\nDurable, explicit project knowledge.\n\nRecommended `kind` values:\n\n```text\narchitecture\nbusiness_rule\nconstraint\nconvention\ndecision\nincident\nintegration\nsecurity\ndeployment\n```\n\nUse this table for concise, evidence-backed facts that should persist across agent sessions.\n\nDo not put temporary guesses here.\n\n### `chunks`\n\nCanonical RAG chunk table for all source types.\n\n`source_type` is one of:\n\n```text\ncode\ndocument\nknowledge\n```\n\nA chunk points to exactly one primary source:\n\n```text\ncode      -> file_id (+ optional symbol_id)\ndocument  -> document_id\nknowledge -> knowledge_item_id\n```\n\nImportant columns:\n\n```text\nchunk_type\nsequence_number\nstart_line/end_line\ncontent\ncontent_hash\ntoken_count\nmetadata\nsearch_vector\n```\n\n`search_vector` is generated automatically for PostgreSQL Full Text Search.\n\n### `embedding_models`\n\nRegistry of embedding configurations.\n\n```text\nprovider\nmodel\ndimensions\ndistance_metric\nenabled\n```\n\nSupported `distance_metric` values in this schema:\n\n```text\ncosine\nl2\ninner_product\n```\n\nThe model registry avoids hard-coding a single embedding dimension into the schema.\n\n### `embeddings`\n\nEmbeddings for chunks.\n\n```text\nproject_id\nchunk_id\nembedding_model_id\ndimensions\nembedding\nsource_content_hash\n```\n\nThe `embedding` column uses unconstrained `VECTOR`, allowing different models with different dimensions in one table.\n\n`source_content_hash` is critical: if it differs from the current chunk hash, the embedding is stale and must be regenerated.\n\n### `indexing_runs`\n[← Database Reference](./database.md) · [Back to README](../README.md) · [Agent Workflow →](./agent-workflow.md)\n\n# Retrieval and Indexing\n\n## 4. Search decision matrix\n\n| Need                             | Preferred tool                                    |\n|----------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------|\n| Exact string/error/config key    | ripgrep                                           |\n| Class/method definition          | Serena/LSP                                        |\n| Find references/implementations  | Serena/LSP                                        |\n| Caller/callee path               | Serena/LSP + relational graph                     |\n| Blast radius                     | PostgreSQL relational graph                       |\n| AST code shape                   | /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep                    |\n| Large codemod                    | /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep + Git diff + tests |\n| Syntax-aware chunk boundaries    | Tree-sitter                                       |\n| Unknown business concept         | FTS + pgvector                                    |\n| Documentation/ADR/business rules | PostgreSQL RAG                                    |\n| Why/when code changed            | Git                                               |\n| Prove correctness                | tests/static analysis                             |\n| Rendered UI/layout/usability     | `cua_repl` / `node_repl`                          |\n| DOM/computed styles/geometry     | `cua_repl` / `node_repl`                          |\n| Browser console/runtime errors   | `cua_repl` / `node_repl`                          |\n| Network/API behavior in browser  | `cua_repl` / `node_repl`                          |\n| Browser runtime alternative      | Chrome DevTools MCP                               |\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## Reliable end-to-end indexing\n\nIndexing crosses host tools, a Docker container, a paid embedding API, and PostgreSQL. Verify each boundary explicitly:\n\n1. Check host tools with `command -v /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep`, `serena --version`, and the Python interpreter that has `tree_sitter_language_pack`. The AI database container is expected to provide PostgreSQL and Node.js, not every parser or language server.\n2. Run Serena/LSP indexing locally. A first run may try to download a language server (for PHP, Intelephense); DNS or registry failures indicate a tool-installation problem. Retry after network access is available and distinguish this from source parse errors.\n3. Parse a small representative file before processing the whole repository. Some Tree-sitter bindings can fail in batch traversal. If a fallback parser or line chunking is used, record the fallback and resulting limitations; do not label those chunks syntax-aware. Populate `aic_symbol_relations` for every edge class that was actually derived, and record any extraction gaps.\n4. Resolve the project by slug and scope every file, symbol, chunk, knowledge, embedding, and run query by `project_id`. Exclude credentials, environment files, generated/runtime content, and any source not approved for the shared database.\n5. Generate embeddings in bounded batches. `app/embedding.js` currently prints the API key and input text to stdout, so suppress stdout and keep command logs private. Persist each batch under `/app/embedding-results/{year}/{month}/` (container path `/app/embedding-results/{year}/{month}/`) before starting another batch. Never rely on container `/tmp` for paid results.\n6. Before retrying, compare existing result files, chunk hashes, project counts, model ID, and indexing-run state. Reuse completed batches; do not call the API again for unchanged input. Keep a manifest mapping batch files to chunk IDs/content hashes when building a new index.\n7. Commit embeddings first. Then create the project/model HNSW index with `aic_create_project_embedding_hnsw_index(...)` using the table-owner role. The restricted `$POSTGRES_SIMPLE_USER` may insert rows but fail with `must be owner of table aic_embeddings` when creating the index. Mark the run `completed` only after the index exists and counts are checked.\n\nA failed index-creation statement inside the same transaction as vector inserts rolls back those inserts. Check database state before recovery; preserve saved batch files and resume the database write without generating embeddings again. Avoid broad deletes or project recreation when recovering partial runs.\n\n### Office Easyfit indexing record (2026-10-02)\n\nThe `office-easyfit` project uses `text-embedding-3-small` (1536 dimensions) and a project-specific HNSW index. The completed run contains 1,182 PHP files, 4,221 approximate symbol rows, 3,896 chunks/embeddings, four documents, and four knowledge items. Configuration, test, runtime, vendor/dependency, web asset/upload paths, and files matching the credential-like literal filter were excluded. Serena indexed 964 PHP files. The Tree-sitter Python binding segfaulted during batch node-field extraction, so code chunks used 80-line boundaries and symbol names used a declaration-line fallback; symbol relations were not populated. Use vector and symbol results as candidates and verify findings in the original source. The 325 embedding result JSON files were persisted under `app/embedding-results/`. This run did not produce a separate batch-to-chunk manifest; the database rows are complete, so do not regenerate them. Future runs should persist that mapping alongside each batch file.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Search rules\n\n### Rule 1 — Always scope by project\n\nBad:\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM chunks\nLIMIT 20;\n```\n\nGood:\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM chunks\nWHERE project_id = :project_id\nLIMIT 20;\n```\n\nCross-project retrieval is allowed only when the task explicitly requires it.\n\n### Rule 2 — Prefer exact symbol lookup before fuzzy/vector search\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n    s.*,\n    f.path\nFROM symbols s\nJOIN files f ON f.id = s.file_id\nWHERE s.project_id = :project_id\n  AND s.qualified_name = :qualified_name;\n```\n\n### Rule 3 — Fuzzy symbol search for uncertain names\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM search_symbols_fuzzy(\n    :project_id,\n    'PaymentServce',\n    20\n);\n```\n\n### Rule 4 — Full Text Search for lexical/business terms\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM search_chunks_text(\n    :project_id,\n    'failed payment retry',\n    20\n);\n```\n\n### Rule 5 — Exact vector retrieval\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM search_chunks_vector_exact(\n    :project_id,\n    :embedding_model_id,\n    :query_embedding::vector,\n    20\n);\n```\n\nThe application is responsible for generating `:query_embedding` with the same model used for stored embeddings.\n\n### Rule 6 — Graph traversal\n\nOutgoing dependencies:\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM aic_symbol_relations_walk(\n    :project_id,\n    :symbol_id,\n    'outgoing',\n    3\n);\n```\n\nIncoming dependants / impact candidates:\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM aic_symbol_relations_walk(\n    :project_id,\n    :symbol_id,\n    'incoming',\n    3\n);\n```\n\nTreat heuristic relations according to their `confidence`.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 12. Vector indexing\n\nA single `VECTOR` column can store embeddings of different dimensions. Approximate vector indexes, however, must target rows with a consistent dimension.\n\nThis repository therefore does **not** create one global HNSW index automatically.\n\nAfter registering an embedding model:\n\n```sql\nSELECT id, provider, model, dimensions\nFROM embedding_models;\n```\n\nCreate a **project + model specific** HNSW index:\n\n```sql\nSELECT create_project_embedding_hnsw_index(\n    :project_id,\n    :embedding_model_id\n);\n```\n\nProject-specific partial indexes are intentional. In a shared multi-project database, a single approximate index shared by all projects can lose recall when PostgreSQL applies `project_id` filtering after ANN candidate selection.\n\nThe helper uses:\n\n- `vector(...)` for dimensions up to 2000\n- `halfvec(...)` for dimensions from 2001 through 4000\n\nFor larger vectors, choose a deliberate strategy such as dimensionality reduction, binary quantization, or another model.\n\nTo remove an automatically named project/model index:\n\n```sql\nSELECT drop_project_embedding_hnsw_index(\n    :project_id,\n    :embedding_model_id\n);\n```\n\n### Important query note\n\nAn expression HNSW index is useful only when the retrieval query uses the corresponding cast/expression and matches the partial-index predicate. `search_chunks_vector_exact()` is deliberately an exact, generic fallback.\n\nFor high-volume approximate retrieval, use the helper after creating the HNSW index:\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM search_chunks_vector_hnsw(\n    :project_id,\n    :embedding_model_id,\n    :query_embedding::vector,\n    20\n);\n```\n\nThis function uses the same `vector(n)` or `halfvec(n)` expression as the model-specific HNSW index.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 13. Recommended hybrid RAG flow\n\nFor a natural-language question:\n\n```text\n\"Where is failed payment retry logic implemented?\"\n```\n\nRecommended pipeline:\n\n```text\n1. Resolve project_id\n2. FTS top N\n3. Vector top N\n4. Fuzzy symbol candidates\n5. Optional graph expansion around strong symbol candidates\n6. Merge / rerank candidates\n7. Read original source\n8. Verify references with Serena/LSP and PostgreSQL `aic_symbol_relations`\n9. Answer or modify code\n```\n\nA practical RRF-style merge can happen in application code. Do not blindly concatenate dozens of chunks into the LLM context.\n\nPrefer small, evidence-rich context.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 14. Tree-sitter indexing rules\n\nTree-sitter should primarily produce structural metadata and semantic chunk boundaries.\n\nDo not embed a serialized raw AST unless a specific experiment proves it useful.\n\nPreferred code chunk:\n\n```text\nLanguage: PHP\nFile: app/Services/PaymentService.php\nNamespace: App\\Services\nClass: PaymentService\nMethod: processPayment\nSignature: processPayment(Order $order): Payment\nLines: 34-81\n\n<source code>\n```\n\nRecommended chunk boundaries:\n\n```text\nclass/interface/trait/enum\nmethod/function\ncomponent\ntemplate block\nconfiguration object\nquery/migration unit\n```\n\nFor very large symbols, split only after preserving:\n\n- symbol identity\n- parent symbol\n[← Integrations and References](./integrations.md) · [Back to README](../README.md)\n\n# Security\n\n## 23. Security\n\nThis stack is intended for local development.\n\nThe Compose file binds PostgreSQL to:\n\n```text\n127.0.0.1\n```\n\nrather than all network interfaces.\n\nStill:\n\n- change the default password\n- do not commit `.env`\n- do not expose port `5432` publicly\n- use a separate database user with restricted permissions if an untrusted agent/client can connect\n- back up the Docker volume if the accumulated project knowledge matters\n- never index secrets from source code in chunks, knowledge, or embeddings\n- treat embedding result files as source-derived data: keep them in `app/embedding-results/`, restrict access, and do not commit them unless the repository explicitly requires that\n- suppress stdout from the current embedding script because it prints the API key and input texts\n\n---\n\n## See Also\n\n- [Getting Started](./getting-started.md)\n- [Architecture and Retrieval Model](./architecture.md)\n- [Database Reference](./database.md)\n[← Users](user-resource.md) · [Back to README](../README.md) · [Code Intelligence →](ai-code-intelligence.md)\n\n# Agent Collaboration\n\nThis guide defines how the primary AI agent delegates complete, independently executable work to subagents in this repository.\n\nA subagent owns its assigned scope from investigation through implementation and verification unless the task brief explicitly limits it to research, review, testing, or another narrower responsibility.\n\nThe primary agent remains responsible for orchestration, integration, conflict resolution, final verification, and communication with the user.\n\n## Subagent orchestration policy\n\nUse subagents conservatively.\n\nSubagents exist to reduce context pressure, parallelize genuinely independent work, and provide specialized investigation or implementation. They must not be created merely because additional concurrency is available.\n\n### Hard limits\n\n- A root task may use **at most 4 subagents** unless the user explicitly requests a different limit.\n- The limit of 4 is a ceiling, not a target.\n- Do not spawn subagents for simple or straightforward tasks.\n- Prefer solving the task directly when delegation provides no clear benefit.\n- Prefer 1 subagent when one independent investigation or implementation scope is sufficient.\n- Prefer 2-4 subagents only when there are genuinely independent workstreams.\n- Never spawn additional subagents merely to increase parallelism.\n- Never spawn duplicate subagents investigating or implementing substantially the same scope.\n- Do not replace a completed subagent with another unless new independent work has actually been identified.\n- Prefer continuing an existing suitable subagent instead of spawning another one for closely related follow-up work.\n\nRecommended default:\n\n```text\nSimple task:\nMain Agent only\n\nModerate task:\nMain Agent\n└── 1 Subagent if useful\n\nComplex task:\nMain Agent\n├── Subagent 1\n└── Subagent 2\n\nLarge task with independent workstreams:\nMain Agent\n├── Subagent 1\n├── Subagent 2\n├── Subagent 3\n└── Subagent 4\n```\n\nNever create four subagents simply because four slots are available.\n\n### Nested delegation\n\nSubagents MUST NOT spawn other subagents.\n\nOnly the root/main agent may create subagents.\n\nEvery subagent task brief must explicitly include:\n\n> Do not spawn additional subagents. Complete the assigned task yourself.\n\nThe intended hierarchy is always:\n\n```text\nMain Agent\n├── Subagent\n├── Subagent\n├── Subagent\n└── Subagent\n```\n\nThe following is forbidden:\n\n```text\nMain Agent\n└── Subagent\n    └── Subagent\n        └── Subagent\n```\n\n### Before spawning\n\nBefore creating a subagent, the primary agent must verify that:\n\n1. The work is sufficiently independent to delegate.\n2. The scope can be clearly bounded.\n3. No existing subagent is already responsible for substantially the same work.\n4. Delegation provides meaningful benefit over completing the work directly.\n5. The expected benefit justifies the additional token, tool, and execution cost.\n6. The work can be performed without unsafe write conflicts with other active agents.\n\nIf these conditions are not satisfied, do not spawn a new subagent.\n\n### Token and cost discipline\n\nSubagents consume independent model context and tool execution.\n\nThe primary agent must therefore:\n\n- prefer the smallest useful number of subagents;\n- avoid assigning the same background investigation to multiple agents;\n- provide focused starting context rather than asking every subagent to rediscover the entire project;\n- give each subagent explicit files, symbols, services, or questions when known;\n- avoid broad prompts such as \"analyze the whole project\" unless that breadth is genuinely required;\n- request concise completion reports instead of unnecessary raw logs;\n- avoid spawning a new agent for a task that can be completed by extending an existing agent's assignment; and\n- stop delegating when sufficient evidence already exists to complete the task safely.\n\n### Lifecycle\n\nWhen a subagent completes its assigned task:\n\n1. collect its completion report;\n2. inspect the resulting changes or evidence;\n3. close/release the agent when supported;\n4. do not automatically create a replacement agent;\n5. integrate the result with findings from other agents;\n6. resolve contradictions before implementation or final reporting.\n\nA completed subagent's conclusion is evidence, not automatically accepted truth.\n\n### Default strategy\n\nFor a complex development task, a reasonable maximum decomposition is:\n\n```text\nMain Agent\n├── Subagent 1: primary investigation or backend scope\n├── Subagent 2: independent subsystem or database scope\n├── Subagent 3: verification, UI, testing, or review if necessary\n└── Subagent 4: independent implementation scope if necessary\n```\n\nThese roles are examples only.\n\nDo not automatically assign all four roles. The primary agent should create only the agents required by the actual task.\n\nA subagent may already own investigation, implementation, and verification for its bounded scope. A separate implementation subagent is unnecessary when an existing subagent can safely complete the work end to end.\n\n## Roles and authority\n\n| Role          | Responsibility                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    |\n|---------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Primary agent | Understands the user's goal, performs initial reconnaissance, divides work into bounded tasks, controls subagent count, assigns ownership, integrates and reviews results, resolves conflicts, performs final verification, reports to the user, and is the only agent allowed to run `git push`. |\n| Subagent      | Completes its assigned work within the defined scope, uses available project tools, may modify authorized files or services, verifies its result, and reports evidence and remaining risks to the primary agent.                                                                                  |\n\nSubagents are full contributors, not inherently read-only reviewers.\n\nHowever, their authority is limited by the task brief.\n\nA subagent may be assigned as:\n\n```text\nresearch-only\nimplementation\nreview\ntesting\ndatabase\nUI/runtime investigation\ninfrastructure\ndocumentation\n```\n\nIf the task brief states that the assignment is read-only or research-only, the subagent must not modify project files, database state, services, Git state, or external systems.\n\nWithin their assignment and the current session's available tools and permissions, subagents may:\n\n- analyze requirements;\n- read source code and documentation;\n- search with `rg`, Serena/LSP, ast-grep, Tree-sitter, and the project index;\n- collect evidence from PostgreSQL and configured services while respecting project scope and read/write authorization;\n- inspect Docker services, PHP-FPM, NGINX, application servers, queues, and other runtime components when tools and permissions are available;\n- use host and container utilities, package managers, Node.js, Python, PHP, and other installed tools;\n- work with the shared `ai-code-intelligence` project to index this application, update project-scoped knowledge, or create embeddings when explicitly included in the assignment and authorized;\n- inspect browser/UI/runtime state using the configured browser tooling;\n- create, edit, or delete files within the assigned write scope; and\n- use Git to inspect changes and, when explicitly included in the assignment, stage and commit them.\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"# Repository Guidelines\n\n## Project Structure & Module Organization\n\nThis repository packages a shared PostgreSQL database for code intelligence. The root `docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/` contains ordered SQL initialization scripts: extensions, schema, indexes, search functions, and vector helpers. `.docker/dev/` contains the local development image, PostgreSQL configuration, Compose file, and environment files. `ai-code-intelligence/` is the standalone deployment package with its own Dockerfile, Compose file, SQL initialization scripts, example environment file, and README. `app/` contains the small Node.js embedding utility and provider implementation. The root README documents the schema and recommended agent workflow.\n\n## Build, Test, and Development Commands\n\n- `make up-dev` starts the development PostgreSQL service.\n- `make down-dev` stops the development service; it does not remove stored volumes.\n- `make build-dev` builds the development image.\n- `make restart-dev` restarts the service; `make rebuild-dev` rebuilds and starts it.\n- `npm install --prefix app` installs the embedding utility dependencies.\n- `node app/embedding.js <API_KEY> text-embedding-3-small \"text to embed\"` generates embeddings.\n\nThere is no configured automated test or lint command. For database changes, review SQL ordering and validate initialization against a disposable local database. Do not use destructive volume/database cleanup commands against the shared development data.\n\n## Coding Style & Naming Conventions\n\nKeep SQL readable, use uppercase SQL keywords, explicit column lists, and descriptive `snake_case` identifiers. Preserve the numeric prefix and dependency order of initialization scripts (for example, `003_indexes.sql`). Use two spaces for JSON and JavaScript indentation, ES modules, and descriptive camelCase JavaScript names. Keep deployment examples aligned between the root and standalone package when both represent the same behavior.\n\n## Testing Guidelines\n\nNo dedicated test suite is present. Verify SQL changes by applying initialization on a clean local database and checking the affected tables, indexes, or functions. For changes to `app/`, run the relevant command manually and avoid committing generated credentials or real API keys.\n\n## Commit & Pull Request Guidelines\n\nGit history is unavailable in this checkout, so no established commit pattern could be confirmed. Use concise imperative commit subjects, such as `Add vector search helper index`. Pull requests should explain the schema or runtime impact, list validation performed, call out environment/configuration changes, and include logs or screenshots only when they clarify behavior.\n\n## Security & Configuration\n\nNever commit `.docker/dev/env/` secrets or expose their values in documentation and logs. Use `.env.example` for safe placeholders. Scope all project-specific database queries by `project_id` or resolve a project by slug first. Persist paid embedding results under `app/embedding-results/`; inspect and reuse saved batches before retries. The current embedding script echoes its API key and input texts to stdout, so suppress stdout. Create project HNSW indexes as the table owner, after committing vector rows.\n\n## Documentation\n\n| Document               | Path                             | Description                                   |\n|------------------------|----------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|\n| README                 | `README.md`                      | Project landing page and guide index          |\n| Getting Started        | `docs/getting-started.md`        | Local database startup and embedding setup    |\n| Architecture           | `docs/architecture.md`           | Retrieval model and system responsibilities   |\n| Database Reference     | `docs/database.md`               | Schema, ownership, and SQL examples           |\n| Retrieval and Indexing | `docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md` | Search, indexing, and code intelligence tools |\n| Agent Workflow         | `docs/agent-workflow.md`         | Investigation and verification practices      |\n| Integrations           | `docs/integrations.md`           | MCP surface and upstream references           |\n| Security               | `docs/security.md`               | Local database and data protection guidance   |\n\n## AI Context Files\n\n| File                          | Purpose                                    |\n|-------------------------------|--------------------------------------------|\n| `.ai-factory/DESCRIPTION.md`  | Project scope and technology summary       |\n| `.ai-factory/ARCHITECTURE.md` | Repository architecture guidelines         |\n| `.ai-factory/rules/base.md`   | Detected naming and structural conventions |# AI Code Intelligence Database\n\n> Local, multi-project PostgreSQL storage for AI-assisted software development.\n\nThis repository combines PostgreSQL full-text search, fuzzy lookup, pgvector retrieval, and relational code relationships. It includes a Docker-based development database and a small Node.js embedding utility. Always scope project data by `project_id` or resolve the project by slug.\n\n## Quick Start\n\nRequires Docker Compose. Start the local database:\n\n```bash\nmake up-dev\n```\n\nCheck the service state:\n\n```bash\ndocker compose -f .docker/dev/docker-compose.yml ps\n```\n\nThe embedding utility uses the OpenAI API and `text-embedding-3-small`; see [Getting Started](docs/getting-started.md) for safe setup details.\n\n## Capabilities\n\n- PostgreSQL 18 with pgvector, full-text search, and pg_trgm.\n- Project, file, symbol, relationship, document, chunk, embedding, and indexing-run storage.\n- Ordered database initialization scripts for local development and standalone deployment.\n- A hybrid retrieval workflow that combines exact, structural, semantic, and graph search.\n\n## Documentation\n\n| Guide                                                    | Description                                          |\n|----------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------|\n| [Getting Started](docs/getting-started.md)               | Local database startup and embedding setup           |\n| [Architecture](docs/architecture.md)                     | Retrieval model and system responsibilities          |\n| [Database Reference](docs/database.md)                   | Schema, ownership, and SQL examples                  |\n| [Retrieval and Indexing](docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md) | Search, indexing, and code intelligence tools        |\n| [Agent Workflow](docs/agent-workflow.md)                 | Investigation, verification, and knowledge practices |\n| [Integrations](docs/integrations.md)                     | MCP surface and upstream references                  |\n| [Security](docs/security.md)                             | Local database and data protection guidance          |[Back to README](../README.md) · [Architecture and Retrieval Model →](./architecture.md)\n\n# Getting Started\n\n## Safe embedding workflow\n\n- Development environment files live in `.docker/dev/env/`. Never copy their values into docs, chat, logs, or command output.\n- The database container runs PostgreSQL and Node.js. Tools such as `/home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep`, Tree-sitter Python bindings, and Serena/LSP may be installed on the host; check the host environment and the exact Python interpreter instead of assuming they must exist in Docker.\n- Keep paid embedding results in the host directory `app/embedding-results/project-name/{year}/{month}/`, mounted in the container at `/app/embedding-results/project-name/{year}/{month}/`. Use stable batch filenames, check which files already exist before retrying, and resume from completed batches. Do not store paid results only in `/tmp` or another ephemeral container path.\n- The current `app/embedding.js` prints its API key and input texts to standard output. Suppress stdout for the embedding call; do not share captured logs. Only index sanitized, non-secret source content.\n- Use `text-embedding-3-small` for the existing model configuration. Keep batches bounded and persist each result file before inserting vectors into PostgreSQL.\n- The restricted `$POSTGRES_SIMPLE_USER` can write project records but may not own `aic_embeddings`. Create project/model HNSW indexes as `$POSTGRES_USER` (table owner). Commit vector rows before index creation and mark the indexing run complete only after the index is verified.\n- Never run `docker system prune`, `docker volume rm`, or `DROP DATABASE` against the shared development data. Before retrying, inspect project counts, run status, and saved embedding files; do not delete a project as a shortcut.\n\n### Run SQL through the PostgreSQL container\n\nFrom the ai-code-intelligence repository root, execute SQL with `psql` inside the database container:\n\n```bash\ndocker compose -f .docker/dev/docker-compose.yml exec -T \\\n  ai-code-intelligence-postgres-postgresql sh -lc \\\n  'psql -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -P pager=off -U \"$POSTGRES_SIMPLE_USER\" -d \"$POSTGRES_DB\" -c \"SELECT slug, id FROM aic_projects ORDER BY slug;\"'\n```\n\nUse `$POSTGRES_SIMPLE_USER` for normal project-scoped reads and writes. Resolve the target project's ID by slug, then include `project_id` in queries against its files, chunks, and embeddings. For vector and HNSW queries, see [Retrieval and Indexing](./retrieval-and-indexing.md).\n\nExample for one sanitized batch:\n\n```bash\ndocker compose -f /home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/.docker/dev/docker-compose.yml \\\n  exec -T ai-code-intelligence-postgres-postgresql sh -lc '\n    node /app/embedding.js \"$OPENAI_API_KEY\" \\\n      /app/embedding-results/project-name/{year}/{month}/example-batch-0000.json \\\n      text-embedding-3-small \"class OrderService {}\" >/dev/null\n  '\n```\n\nThe embedding file persists on the host at `/app/embedding-results/project-name/{year}/{month}/example-batch-0000.json`.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Database startup\n\n### Files about DB tables\n\nDB tables schemas:\ndocker-entrypoint-initdb.d:\n - 001_extensions.sql\n - 002_schema.sql\n - 003_indexes.sql\n - 004_views_and_search_functions.sql\n - 005_vector_index_helpers.sql\n\n---\n\n## See Also\n\n- [Architecture and Retrieval Model](./architecture.md)\n- [Database Reference](./database.md)\n- [Retrieval and Indexing](./retrieval-and-indexing.md)[← Architecture and Retrieval Model](./architecture.md) · [Back to README](../README.md) · [Retrieval and Indexing →](./retrieval-and-indexing.md)\n\n# Database Reference\n\n## 6. PostgreSQL extensions\n\n`001_extensions.sql` enables:\n\n| Extension  | Purpose                                       |\n|------------|-----------------------------------------------|\n| `vector`   | Embedding storage and nearest-neighbor search |\n| `pgcrypto` | `gen_random_uuid()`                           |\n| `pg_trgm`  | Fuzzy symbol/path lookup                      |\n| `unaccent` | Optional normalization support                |\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 7. DDL model\n\nThe authoritative DDL is in `002_schema.sql`.\n\n### `projects`\n\nRoot tenant/entity for all stored projects.\n\n| Column           | Type        | Purpose                       |\n|------------------|-------------|-------------------------------|\n| `id`             | UUID PK     | Project identifier            |\n| `name`           | TEXT        | Human-readable name           |\n| `slug`           | TEXT UNIQUE | Stable local identifier       |\n| `root_path`      | TEXT        | Local repository path         |\n| `repository_url` | TEXT        | Optional Git remote           |\n| `default_branch` | TEXT        | Default branch                |\n| `active`         | BOOLEAN     | Whether the project is active |\n| `metadata`       | JSONB       | Project-specific metadata     |\n| timestamps       | TIMESTAMPTZ | Audit timestamps              |\n\nAll project-owned data cascades when a project is deleted. Composite project-scoped foreign keys also prevent accidental cross-project links between files, symbols, chunks, documents, knowledge, and embeddings.\n\n### `files`\n\nOne row per indexed file in a project.\n\nImportant columns:\n\n```text\nproject_id\npath\nlanguage\nfile_type\ncontent_hash\ngit_blob_sha\nlast_indexed_commit\nis_generated\nis_deleted\nindexed_at\n```\n\nUnique key:\n\n```text\n(project_id, path)\n```\n\n### `symbols`\n\nTree-sitter/LSP/Serena-derived symbols.\n\nTypical `kind` values:\n\n```text\nnamespace\nmodule\nclass\ninterface\ntrait\nenum\nmethod\nfunction\nproperty\nconstant\ntype\ncomponent\n```\n\nImportant columns:\n\n```text\nproject_id\nfile_id\nparent_symbol_id\nkind\nname\nqualified_name\nsignature\nvisibility\nstart_line/start_column\nend_line/end_column\ncontent_hash\nmetadata\n```\n\nThe schema intentionally does not constrain `kind` to a fixed enum because language ecosystems differ.\n\n### `aic_symbol_relations`\n\nStores directed edges in the project's code graph. A row means that `source_symbol_id` has a `relation_type` relationship to a target. For example, a method that calls another method points from the caller to the callee; a class that implements an interface points from the class to the interface.\n\n| Column                  |    Required | Meaning                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n|-------------------------|------------:|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| `id`                    |   Generated | Relation row UUID.                                                                                                                                                                      |\n| `project_id`            |         Yes | Owning project; must match both endpoint symbols.                                                                                                                                       |\n| `source_symbol_id`      |         Yes | Symbol where the relationship originates.                                                                                                                                               |\n| `target_symbol_id`      |          No | Resolved target symbol in the same project.                                                                                                                                             |\n| `target_qualified_name` | Conditional | Target name when it cannot be resolved to an indexed symbol.                                                                                                                            |\n| `relation_type`         |         Yes | Relationship kind, such as `calls`, `references`, `imports`, `extends`, `implements`, `uses`, `dispatches`, `listens`, `publishes`, `subscribes`, `reads`, `writes`, or `instantiates`. |\n| `source_line`           |          No | One-based line in the source file where the relationship is evidenced.                                                                                                                  |\n| `confidence`            |         Yes | Confidence score from `0.0` to `1.0`; defaults to `1.0`.                                                                                                                                |\n| `evidence`              |         Yes | JSONB details supporting the edge; defaults to `{}`.                                                                                                                                    |\n| `created_at`            |   Generated | Row creation time.                                                                                                                                                                      |\n\n`relation_type` is free text, not a database enum. Use the canonical lowercase values above consistently; add a new value only when the extractor has a distinct, useful relationship to represent. The database enforces nonblank types, valid confidence and line ranges, project-matched foreign keys, and at least one target identifier (`target_symbol_id` or a nonblank `target_qualified_name`).\n\nUse the actual table name `aic_symbol_relations` in SQL. The relation indexes support project/type filtering and incoming/outgoing traversal. There is no natural unique constraint for an edge, so `ON CONFLICT` cannot make relation writes idempotent by itself. Refresh a source symbol's outgoing edges by deleting its old rows and inserting the complete newly extracted set in one transaction.\n\nFor the extraction workflow and evidence/confidence guidance, see [relation collection and persistence](retrieval-and-indexing.md#required-relation-collection-workflow).\n\n```sql\nBEGIN;\n\nDELETE FROM aic_symbol_relations\nWHERE project_id = :project_id\n  AND source_symbol_id = :source_symbol_id;\n\nINSERT INTO aic_symbol_relations (\n    project_id,\n    source_symbol_id,\n    target_symbol_id,\n    target_qualified_name,\n    relation_type,\n    source_line,\n    confidence,\n    evidence\n)\nVALUES (\n    :project_id,\n    :source_symbol_id,\n    :target_symbol_id,\n    :target_qualified_name,\n    :relation_type,\n    :source_line,\n    :confidence,\n    :evidence::jsonb\n);\n\nCOMMIT;\n```\n\nRun the `DELETE` once for each refreshed source symbol, then insert all of its current edges before committing. If the extraction finds no edges, the delete alone correctly leaves that source with no outgoing relations. Do not delete rows for sources outside the refreshed set.\n\nAn indexed same-project target should use `target_symbol_id`; an external or unresolved target should use `NULL` for that column and a stable qualified name. `source_line` is one-based. Keep `evidence` structured, for example with extractor, evidence kind, source path, and a short source fragment or AST/LSP reference. Avoid storing secrets or large source bodies.\n\nWhen rebuilding symbols, account for `ON DELETE CASCADE` on both source and target foreign keys: deleting a symbol also deletes edges from other files that point to it. Re-resolve and rebuild both outgoing relations for refreshed symbols and incoming relations affected by changed/deleted targets in the same logical indexing run. Do not report graph indexing as complete while those dependent edges are stale.\n\n### `documents`\n\nProject documentation and external/local reference material.\n\nExamples:\n\n```text\nREADME\nADR\nOpenAPI document\ninternal architecture document\nvendor API reference\nAI Factory generated research\n```\n\nA document must have either `path` or `source_uri`.\n\n### `knowledge_items`\n\nDurable, explicit project knowledge.\n\nRecommended `kind` values:\n\n```text\narchitecture\nbusiness_rule\nconstraint\nconvention\ndecision\nincident\nintegration\nsecurity\ndeployment\n```\n\nUse this table for concise, evidence-backed facts that should persist across agent sessions.\n\nDo not put temporary guesses here.\n\n### `chunks`\n\nCanonical RAG chunk table for all source types.\n\n`source_type` is one of:\n\n```text\ncode\ndocument\nknowledge\n```\n\nA chunk points to exactly one primary source:\n\n```text\ncode      -> file_id (+ optional symbol_id)\ndocument  -> document_id\nknowledge -> knowledge_item_id\n```\n\nImportant columns:\n\n```text\nchunk_type\nsequence_number\nstart_line/end_line\ncontent\ncontent_hash\ntoken_count\nmetadata\nsearch_vector\n```\n\n`search_vector` is generated automatically for PostgreSQL Full Text Search.\n\n### `embedding_models`\n\nRegistry of embedding configurations.\n\n```text\nprovider\nmodel\ndimensions\ndistance_metric\nenabled\n```\n\nSupported `distance_metric` values in this schema:\n\n```text\ncosine\nl2\ninner_product\n```\n\nThe model registry avoids hard-coding a single embedding dimension into the schema.\n\n### `embeddings`\n\nEmbeddings for chunks.\n\n```text\nproject_id\nchunk_id\nembedding_model_id\ndimensions\nembedding\nsource_content_hash\n```\n\nThe `embedding` column uses unconstrained `VECTOR`, allowing different models with different dimensions in one table.\n\n`source_content_hash` is critical: if it differs from the current chunk hash, the embedding is stale and must be regenerated.\n\n### `indexing_runs`\n[← Database Reference](./database.md) · [Back to README](../README.md) · [Agent Workflow →](./agent-workflow.md)\n\n# Retrieval and Indexing\n\n## 4. Search decision matrix\n\n| Need                             | Preferred tool                                    |\n|----------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------|\n| Exact string/error/config key    | ripgrep                                           |\n| Class/method definition          | Serena/LSP                                        |\n| Find references/implementations  | Serena/LSP                                        |\n| Caller/callee path               | Serena/LSP + relational graph                     |\n| Blast radius                     | PostgreSQL relational graph                       |\n| AST code shape                   | /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep                    |\n| Large codemod                    | /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep + Git diff + tests |\n| Syntax-aware chunk boundaries    | Tree-sitter                                       |\n| Unknown business concept         | FTS + pgvector                                    |\n| Documentation/ADR/business rules | PostgreSQL RAG                                    |\n| Why/when code changed            | Git                                               |\n| Prove correctness                | tests/static analysis                             |\n| Rendered UI/layout/usability     | `cua_repl` / `node_repl`                          |\n| DOM/computed styles/geometry     | `cua_repl` / `node_repl`                          |\n| Browser console/runtime errors   | `cua_repl` / `node_repl`                          |\n| Network/API behavior in browser  | `cua_repl` / `node_repl`                          |\n| Browser runtime alternative      | Chrome DevTools MCP                               |\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## Reliable end-to-end indexing\n\nIndexing crosses host tools, a Docker container, a paid embedding API, and PostgreSQL. Verify each boundary explicitly:\n\n1. Check host tools with `command -v /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep`, `serena --version`, and the Python interpreter that has `tree_sitter_language_pack`. The AI database container is expected to provide PostgreSQL and Node.js, not every parser or language server.\n2. Run Serena/LSP indexing locally. A first run may try to download a language server (for PHP, Intelephense); DNS or registry failures indicate a tool-installation problem. Retry after network access is available and distinguish this from source parse errors.\n3. Parse a small representative file before processing the whole repository. Some Tree-sitter bindings can fail in batch traversal. If a fallback parser or line chunking is used, record the fallback and resulting limitations; do not label those chunks syntax-aware. Populate `aic_symbol_relations` for every edge class that was actually derived, and record any extraction gaps.\n4. Resolve the project by slug and scope every file, symbol, chunk, knowledge, embedding, and run query by `project_id`. Exclude credentials, environment files, generated/runtime content, and any source not approved for the shared database.\n5. Generate embeddings in bounded batches. `app/embedding.js` currently prints the API key and input text to stdout, so suppress stdout and keep command logs private. Persist each batch under `/app/embedding-results/{year}/{month}/` (container path `/app/embedding-results/{year}/{month}/`) before starting another batch. Never rely on container `/tmp` for paid results.\n6. Before retrying, compare existing result files, chunk hashes, project counts, model ID, and indexing-run state. Reuse completed batches; do not call the API again for unchanged input. Keep a manifest mapping batch files to chunk IDs/content hashes when building a new index.\n7. Commit embeddings first. Then create the project/model HNSW index with `aic_create_project_embedding_hnsw_index(...)` using the table-owner role. The restricted `$POSTGRES_SIMPLE_USER` may insert rows but fail with `must be owner of table aic_embeddings` when creating the index. Mark the run `completed` only after the index exists and counts are checked.\n\nA failed index-creation statement inside the same transaction as vector inserts rolls back those inserts. Check database state before recovery; preserve saved batch files and resume the database write without generating embeddings again. Avoid broad deletes or project recreation when recovering partial runs.\n\n### Office Easyfit indexing record (2026-10-02)\n\nThe `office-easyfit` project uses `text-embedding-3-small` (1536 dimensions) and a project-specific HNSW index. The completed run contains 1,182 PHP files, 4,221 approximate symbol rows, 3,896 chunks/embeddings, four documents, and four knowledge items. Configuration, test, runtime, vendor/dependency, web asset/upload paths, and files matching the credential-like literal filter were excluded. Serena indexed 964 PHP files. The Tree-sitter Python binding segfaulted during batch node-field extraction, so code chunks used 80-line boundaries and symbol names used a declaration-line fallback; symbol relations were not populated. Use vector and symbol results as candidates and verify findings in the original source. The 325 embedding result JSON files were persisted under `app/embedding-results/`. This run did not produce a separate batch-to-chunk manifest; the database rows are complete, so do not regenerate them. Future runs should persist that mapping alongside each batch file.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Search rules\n\n### Rule 1 — Always scope by project\n\nBad:\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM chunks\nLIMIT 20;\n```\n\nGood:\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM chunks\nWHERE project_id = :project_id\nLIMIT 20;\n```\n\nCross-project retrieval is allowed only when the task explicitly requires it.\n\n### Rule 2 — Prefer exact symbol lookup before fuzzy/vector search\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n    s.*,\n    f.path\nFROM symbols s\nJOIN files f ON f.id = s.file_id\nWHERE s.project_id = :project_id\n  AND s.qualified_name = :qualified_name;\n```\n\n### Rule 3 — Fuzzy symbol search for uncertain names\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM search_symbols_fuzzy(\n    :project_id,\n    'PaymentServce',\n    20\n);\n```\n\n### Rule 4 — Full Text Search for lexical/business terms\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM search_chunks_text(\n    :project_id,\n    'failed payment retry',\n    20\n);\n```\n\n### Rule 5 — Exact vector retrieval\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM search_chunks_vector_exact(\n    :project_id,\n    :embedding_model_id,\n    :query_embedding::vector,\n    20\n);\n```\n\nThe application is responsible for generating `:query_embedding` with the same model used for stored embeddings.\n\n### Rule 6 — Graph traversal\n\nOutgoing dependencies:\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM aic_symbol_relations_walk(\n    :project_id,\n    :symbol_id,\n    'outgoing',\n    3\n);\n```\n\nIncoming dependants / impact candidates:\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM aic_symbol_relations_walk(\n    :project_id,\n    :symbol_id,\n    'incoming',\n    3\n);\n```\n\nTreat heuristic relations according to their `confidence`.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 12. Vector indexing\n\nA single `VECTOR` column can store embeddings of different dimensions. Approximate vector indexes, however, must target rows with a consistent dimension.\n\nThis repository therefore does **not** create one global HNSW index automatically.\n\nAfter registering an embedding model:\n\n```sql\nSELECT id, provider, model, dimensions\nFROM embedding_models;\n```\n\nCreate a **project + model specific** HNSW index:\n\n```sql\nSELECT create_project_embedding_hnsw_index(\n    :project_id,\n    :embedding_model_id\n);\n```\n\nProject-specific partial indexes are intentional. In a shared multi-project database, a single approximate index shared by all projects can lose recall when PostgreSQL applies `project_id` filtering after ANN candidate selection.\n\nThe helper uses:\n\n- `vector(...)` for dimensions up to 2000\n- `halfvec(...)` for dimensions from 2001 through 4000\n\nFor larger vectors, choose a deliberate strategy such as dimensionality reduction, binary quantization, or another model.\n\nTo remove an automatically named project/model index:\n\n```sql\nSELECT drop_project_embedding_hnsw_index(\n    :project_id,\n    :embedding_model_id\n);\n```\n\n### Important query note\n\nAn expression HNSW index is useful only when the retrieval query uses the corresponding cast/expression and matches the partial-index predicate. `search_chunks_vector_exact()` is deliberately an exact, generic fallback.\n\nFor high-volume approximate retrieval, use the helper after creating the HNSW index:\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM search_chunks_vector_hnsw(\n    :project_id,\n    :embedding_model_id,\n    :query_embedding::vector,\n    20\n);\n```\n\nThis function uses the same `vector(n)` or `halfvec(n)` expression as the model-specific HNSW index.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 13. Recommended hybrid RAG flow\n\nFor a natural-language question:\n\n```text\n\"Where is failed payment retry logic implemented?\"\n```\n\nRecommended pipeline:\n\n```text\n1. Resolve project_id\n2. FTS top N\n3. Vector top N\n4. Fuzzy symbol candidates\n5. Optional graph expansion around strong symbol candidates\n6. Merge / rerank candidates\n7. Read original source\n8. Verify references with Serena/LSP and PostgreSQL `aic_symbol_relations`\n9. Answer or modify code\n```\n\nA practical RRF-style merge can happen in application code. Do not blindly concatenate dozens of chunks into the LLM context.\n\nPrefer small, evidence-rich context.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 14. Tree-sitter indexing rules\n\nTree-sitter should primarily produce structural metadata and semantic chunk boundaries.\n\nDo not embed a serialized raw AST unless a specific experiment proves it useful.\n\nPreferred code chunk:\n\n```text\nLanguage: PHP\nFile: app/Services/PaymentService.php\nNamespace: App\\Services\nClass: PaymentService\nMethod: processPayment\nSignature: processPayment(Order $order): Payment\nLines: 34-81\n\n<source code>\n```\n\nRecommended chunk boundaries:\n\n```text\nclass/interface/trait/enum\nmethod/function\ncomponent\ntemplate block\nconfiguration object\nquery/migration unit\n```\n\nFor very large symbols, split only after preserving:\n\n- symbol identity\n- parent symbol\n[← Integrations and References](./integrations.md) · [Back to README](../README.md)\n\n# Security\n\n## 23. Security\n\nThis stack is intended for local development.\n\nThe Compose file binds PostgreSQL to:\n\n```text\n127.0.0.1\n```\n\nrather than all network interfaces.\n\nStill:\n\n- change the default password\n- do not commit `.env`\n- do not expose port `5432` publicly\n- use a separate database user with restricted permissions if an untrusted agent/client can connect\n- back up the Docker volume if the accumulated project knowledge matters\n- never index secrets from source code in chunks, knowledge, or embeddings\n- treat embedding result files as source-derived data: keep them in `app/embedding-results/`, restrict access, and do not commit them unless the repository explicitly requires that\n- suppress stdout from the current embedding script because it prints the API key and input texts\n\n---\n\n## See Also\n\n- [Getting Started](./getting-started.md)\n- [Architecture and Retrieval Model](./architecture.md)\n- [Database Reference](./database.md)\n[← Users](user-resource.md) · [Back to README](../README.md) · [Code Intelligence →](ai-code-intelligence.md)\n\n# Agent Collaboration\n\nThis guide defines how the primary AI agent delegates complete, independently executable work to subagents in this repository.\n\nA subagent owns its assigned scope from investigation through implementation and verification unless the task brief explicitly limits it to research, review, testing, or another narrower responsibility.\n\nThe primary agent remains responsible for orchestration, integration, conflict resolution, final verification, and communication with the user.\n\n## Subagent orchestration policy\n\nUse subagents conservatively.\n\nSubagents exist to reduce context pressure, parallelize genuinely independent work, and provide specialized investigation or implementation. They must not be created merely because additional concurrency is available.\n\n### Hard limits\n\n- A root task may use **at most 4 subagents** unless the user explicitly requests a different limit.\n- The limit of 4 is a ceiling, not a target.\n- Do not spawn subagents for simple or straightforward tasks.\n- Prefer solving the task directly when delegation provides no clear benefit.\n- Prefer 1 subagent when one independent investigation or implementation scope is sufficient.\n- Prefer 2-4 subagents only when there are genuinely independent workstreams.\n- Never spawn additional subagents merely to increase parallelism.\n- Never spawn duplicate subagents investigating or implementing substantially the same scope.\n- Do not replace a completed subagent with another unless new independent work has actually been identified.\n- Prefer continuing an existing suitable subagent instead of spawning another one for closely related follow-up work.\n\nRecommended default:\n\n```text\nSimple task:\nMain Agent only\n\nModerate task:\nMain Agent\n└── 1 Subagent if useful\n\nComplex task:\nMain Agent\n├── Subagent 1\n└── Subagent 2\n\nLarge task with independent workstreams:\nMain Agent\n├── Subagent 1\n├── Subagent 2\n├── Subagent 3\n└── Subagent 4\n```\n\nNever create four subagents simply because four slots are available.\n\n### Nested delegation\n\nSubagents MUST NOT spawn other subagents.\n\nOnly the root/main agent may create subagents.\n\nEvery subagent task brief must explicitly include:\n\n> Do not spawn additional subagents. Complete the assigned task yourself.\n\nThe intended hierarchy is always:\n\n```text\nMain Agent\n├── Subagent\n├── Subagent\n├── Subagent\n└── Subagent\n```\n\nThe following is forbidden:\n\n```text\nMain Agent\n└── Subagent\n    └── Subagent\n        └── Subagent\n```\n\n### Before spawning\n\nBefore creating a subagent, the primary agent must verify that:\n\n1. The work is sufficiently independent to delegate.\n2. The scope can be clearly bounded.\n3. No existing subagent is already responsible for substantially the same work.\n4. Delegation provides meaningful benefit over completing the work directly.\n5. The expected benefit justifies the additional token, tool, and execution cost.\n6. The work can be performed without unsafe write conflicts with other active agents.\n\nIf these conditions are not satisfied, do not spawn a new subagent.\n\n### Token and cost discipline\n\nSubagents consume independent model context and tool execution.\n\nThe primary agent must therefore:\n\n- prefer the smallest useful number of subagents;\n- avoid assigning the same background investigation to multiple agents;\n- provide focused starting context rather than asking every subagent to rediscover the entire project;\n- give each subagent explicit files, symbols, services, or questions when known;\n- avoid broad prompts such as \"analyze the whole project\" unless that breadth is genuinely required;\n- request concise completion reports instead of unnecessary raw logs;\n- avoid spawning a new agent for a task that can be completed by extending an existing agent's assignment; and\n- stop delegating when sufficient evidence already exists to complete the task safely.\n\n### Lifecycle\n\nWhen a subagent completes its assigned task:\n\n1. collect its completion report;\n2. inspect the resulting changes or evidence;\n3. close/release the agent when supported;\n4. do not automatically create a replacement agent;\n5. integrate the result with findings from other agents;\n6. resolve contradictions before implementation or final reporting.\n\nA completed subagent's conclusion is evidence, not automatically accepted truth.\n\n### Default strategy\n\nFor a complex development task, a reasonable maximum decomposition is:\n\n```text\nMain Agent\n├── Subagent 1: primary investigation or backend scope\n├── Subagent 2: independent subsystem or database scope\n├── Subagent 3: verification, UI, testing, or review if necessary\n└── Subagent 4: independent implementation scope if necessary\n```\n\nThese roles are examples only.\n\nDo not automatically assign all four roles. The primary agent should create only the agents required by the actual task.\n\nA subagent may already own investigation, implementation, and verification for its bounded scope. A separate implementation subagent is unnecessary when an existing subagent can safely complete the work end to end.\n\n## Roles and authority\n\n| Role          | Responsibility                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    |\n|---------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Primary agent | Understands the user's goal, performs initial reconnaissance, divides work into bounded tasks, controls subagent count, assigns ownership, integrates and reviews results, resolves conflicts, performs final verification, reports to the user, and is the only agent allowed to run `git push`. |\n| Subagent      | Completes its assigned work within the defined scope, uses available project tools, may modify authorized files or services, verifies its result, and reports evidence and remaining risks to the primary agent.                                                                                  |\n\nSubagents are full contributors, not inherently read-only reviewers.\n\nHowever, their authority is limited by the task brief.\n\nA subagent may be assigned as:\n\n```text\nresearch-only\nimplementation\nreview\ntesting\ndatabase\nUI/runtime investigation\ninfrastructure\ndocumentation\n```\n\nIf the task brief states that the assignment is read-only or research-only, the subagent must not modify project files, database state, services, Git state, or external systems.\n\nWithin their assignment and the current session's available tools and permissions, subagents may:\n\n- analyze requirements;\n- read source code and documentation;\n- search with `rg`, Serena/LSP, ast-grep, Tree-sitter, and the project index;\n- collect evidence from PostgreSQL and configured services while respecting project scope and read/write authorization;\n- inspect Docker services, PHP-FPM, NGINX, application servers, queues, and other runtime components when tools and permissions are available;\n- use host and container utilities, package managers, Node.js, Python, PHP, and other installed tools;\n- work with the shared `ai-code-intelligence` project to index this application, update project-scoped knowledge, or create embeddings when explicitly included in the assignment and authorized;\n- inspect browser/UI/runtime state using the configured browser tooling;\n- create, edit, or delete files within the assigned write scope; and\n- use Git to inspect changes and, when explicitly included in the assignment, stage and commit them.\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":5861},"formatted_output":"# Repository Guidelines\n\n## Project Structure & Module Organization\n\nThis repository packages a shared PostgreSQL database for code intelligence. The root `docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/` contains ordered SQL initialization scripts: extensions, schema, indexes, search functions, and vector helpers. `.docker/dev/` contains the local development image, PostgreSQL configuration, Compose file, and environment files. `ai-code-intelligence/` is the standalone deployment package with its own Dockerfile, Compose file, SQL initialization scripts, example environment file, and README. `app/` contains the small Node.js embedding utility and provider implementation. The root README documents the schema and recommended agent workflow.\n\n## Build, Test, and Development Commands\n\n- `make up-dev` starts the development PostgreSQL service.\n- `make down-dev` stops the development service; it does not remove stored volumes.\n- `make build-dev` builds the development image.\n- `make restart-dev` restarts the service; `make rebuild-dev` rebuilds and starts it.\n- `npm install --prefix app` installs the embedding utility dependencies.\n- `node app/embedding.js <API_KEY> text-embedding-3-small \"text to embed\"` generates embeddings.\n\nThere is no configured automated test or lint command. For database changes, review SQL ordering and validate initialization against a disposable local database. Do not use destructive volume/database cleanup commands against the shared development data.\n\n## Coding Style & Naming Conventions\n\nKeep SQL readable, use uppercase SQL keywords, explicit column lists, and descriptive `snake_case` identifiers. Preserve the numeric prefix and dependency order of initialization scripts (for example, `003_indexes.sql`). Use two spaces for JSON and JavaScript indentation, ES modules, and descriptive camelCase JavaScript names. Keep deployment examples aligned between the root and standalone package when both represent the same behavior.\n\n## Testing Guidelines\n\nNo dedicated test suite is present. Verify SQL changes by applying initialization on a clean local database and checking the affected tables, indexes, or functions. For changes to `app/`, run the relevant command manually and avoid committing generated credentials or real API keys.\n\n## Commit & Pull Request Guidelines\n\nGit history is unavailable in this checkout, so no established commit pattern could be confirmed. Use concise imperative commit subjects, such as `Add vector search helper index`. Pull requests should explain the schema or runtime impact, list validation performed, call out environment/configuration changes, and include logs or screenshots only when they clarify behavior.\n\n## Security & Configuration\n\nNever commit `.docker/dev/env/` secrets or expose their values in documentation and logs. Use `.env.example` for safe placeholders. Scope all project-specific database queries by `project_id` or resolve a project by slug first. Persist paid embedding results under `app/embedding-results/`; inspect and reuse saved batches before retries. The current embedding script echoes its API key and input texts to stdout, so suppress stdout. Create project HNSW indexes as the table owner, after committing vector rows.\n\n## Documentation\n\n| Document               | Path                             | Description                                   |\n|------------------------|----------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|\n| README                 | `README.md`                      | Project landing page and guide index          |\n| Getting Started        | `docs/getting-started.md`        | Local database startup and embedding setup    |\n| Architecture           | `docs/architecture.md`           | Retrieval model and system responsibilities   |\n| Database Reference     | `docs/database.md`               | Schema, ownership, and SQL examples           |\n| Retrieval and Indexing | `docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md` | Search, indexing, and code intelligence tools |\n| Agent Workflow         | `docs/agent-workflow.md`         | Investigation and verification practices      |\n| Integrations           | `docs/integrations.md`           | MCP surface and upstream references           |\n| Security               | `docs/security.md`               | Local database and data protection guidance   |\n\n## AI Context Files\n\n| File                          | Purpose                                    |\n|-------------------------------|--------------------------------------------|\n| `.ai-factory/DESCRIPTION.md`  | Project scope and technology summary       |\n| `.ai-factory/ARCHITECTURE.md` | Repository architecture guidelines         |\n| `.ai-factory/rules/base.md`   | Detected naming and structural conventions |# AI Code Intelligence Database\n\n> Local, multi-project PostgreSQL storage for AI-assisted software development.\n\nThis repository combines PostgreSQL full-text search, fuzzy lookup, pgvector retrieval, and relational code relationships. It includes a Docker-based development database and a small Node.js embedding utility. Always scope project data by `project_id` or resolve the project by slug.\n\n## Quick Start\n\nRequires Docker Compose. Start the local database:\n\n```bash\nmake up-dev\n```\n\nCheck the service state:\n\n```bash\ndocker compose -f .docker/dev/docker-compose.yml ps\n```\n\nThe embedding utility uses the OpenAI API and `text-embedding-3-small`; see [Getting Started](docs/getting-started.md) for safe setup details.\n\n## Capabilities\n\n- PostgreSQL 18 with pgvector, full-text search, and pg_trgm.\n- Project, file, symbol, relationship, document, chunk, embedding, and indexing-run storage.\n- Ordered database initialization scripts for local development and standalone deployment.\n- A hybrid retrieval workflow that combines exact, structural, semantic, and graph search.\n\n## Documentation\n\n| Guide                                                    | Description                                          |\n|----------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------|\n| [Getting Started](docs/getting-started.md)               | Local database startup and embedding setup           |\n| [Architecture](docs/architecture.md)                     | Retrieval model and system responsibilities          |\n| [Database Reference](docs/database.md)                   | Schema, ownership, and SQL examples                  |\n| [Retrieval and Indexing](docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md) | Search, indexing, and code intelligence tools        |\n| [Agent Workflow](docs/agent-workflow.md)                 | Investigation, verification, and knowledge practices |\n| [Integrations](docs/integrations.md)                     | MCP surface and upstream references                  |\n| [Security](docs/security.md)                             | Local database and data protection guidance          |[Back to README](../README.md) · [Architecture and Retrieval Model →](./architecture.md)\n\n# Getting Started\n\n## Safe embedding workflow\n\n- Development environment files live in `.docker/dev/env/`. Never copy their values into docs, chat, logs, or command output.\n- The database container runs PostgreSQL and Node.js. Tools such as `/home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep`, Tree-sitter Python bindings, and Serena/LSP may be installed on the host; check the host environment and the exact Python interpreter instead of assuming they must exist in Docker.\n- Keep paid embedding results in the host directory `app/embedding-results/project-name/{year}/{month}/`, mounted in the container at `/app/embedding-results/project-name/{year}/{month}/`. Use stable batch filenames, check which files already exist before retrying, and resume from completed batches. Do not store paid results only in `/tmp` or another ephemeral container path.\n- The current `app/embedding.js` prints its API key and input texts to standard output. Suppress stdout for the embedding call; do not share captured logs. Only index sanitized, non-secret source content.\n- Use `text-embedding-3-small` for the existing model configuration. Keep batches bounded and persist each result file before inserting vectors into PostgreSQL.\n- The restricted `$POSTGRES_SIMPLE_USER` can write project records but may not own `aic_embeddings`. Create project/model HNSW indexes as `$POSTGRES_USER` (table owner). Commit vector rows before index creation and mark the indexing run complete only after the index is verified.\n- Never run `docker system prune`, `docker volume rm`, or `DROP DATABASE` against the shared development data. Before retrying, inspect project counts, run status, and saved embedding files; do not delete a project as a shortcut.\n\n### Run SQL through the PostgreSQL container\n\nFrom the ai-code-intelligence repository root, execute SQL with `psql` inside the database container:\n\n```bash\ndocker compose -f .docker/dev/docker-compose.yml exec -T \\\n  ai-code-intelligence-postgres-postgresql sh -lc \\\n  'psql -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -P pager=off -U \"$POSTGRES_SIMPLE_USER\" -d \"$POSTGRES_DB\" -c \"SELECT slug, id FROM aic_projects ORDER BY slug;\"'\n```\n\nUse `$POSTGRES_SIMPLE_USER` for normal project-scoped reads and writes. Resolve the target project's ID by slug, then include `project_id` in queries against its files, chunks, and embeddings. For vector and HNSW queries, see [Retrieval and Indexing](./retrieval-and-indexing.md).\n\nExample for one sanitized batch:\n\n```bash\ndocker compose -f /home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/.docker/dev/docker-compose.yml \\\n  exec -T ai-code-intelligence-postgres-postgresql sh -lc '\n    node /app/embedding.js \"$OPENAI_API_KEY\" \\\n      /app/embedding-results/project-name/{year}/{month}/example-batch-0000.json \\\n      text-embedding-3-small \"class OrderService {}\" >/dev/null\n  '\n```\n\nThe embedding file persists on the host at `/app/embedding-results/project-name/{year}/{month}/example-batch-0000.json`.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Database startup\n\n### Files about DB tables\n\nDB tables schemas:\ndocker-entrypoint-initdb.d:\n - 001_extensions.sql\n - 002_schema.sql\n - 003_indexes.sql\n - 004_views_and_search_functions.sql\n - 005_vector_index_helpers.sql\n\n---\n\n## See Also\n\n- [Architecture and Retrieval Model](./architecture.md)\n- [Database Reference](./database.md)\n- [Retrieval and Indexing](./retrieval-and-indexing.md)[← Architecture and Retrieval Model](./architecture.md) · [Back to README](../README.md) · [Retrieval and Indexing →](./retrieval-and-indexing.md)\n\n# Database Reference\n\n## 6. PostgreSQL extensions\n\n`001_extensions.sql` enables:\n\n| Extension  | Purpose                                       |\n|------------|-----------------------------------------------|\n| `vector`   | Embedding storage and nearest-neighbor search |\n| `pgcrypto` | `gen_random_uuid()`                           |\n| `pg_trgm`  | Fuzzy symbol/path lookup                      |\n| `unaccent` | Optional normalization support                |\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 7. DDL model\n\nThe authoritative DDL is in `002_schema.sql`.\n\n### `projects`\n\nRoot tenant/entity for all stored projects.\n\n| Column           | Type        | Purpose                       |\n|------------------|-------------|-------------------------------|\n| `id`             | UUID PK     | Project identifier            |\n| `name`           | TEXT        | Human-readable name           |\n| `slug`           | TEXT UNIQUE | Stable local identifier       |\n| `root_path`      | TEXT        | Local repository path         |\n| `repository_url` | TEXT        | Optional Git remote           |\n| `default_branch` | TEXT        | Default branch                |\n| `active`         | BOOLEAN     | Whether the project is active |\n| `metadata`       | JSONB       | Project-specific metadata     |\n| timestamps       | TIMESTAMPTZ | Audit timestamps              |\n\nAll project-owned data cascades when a project is deleted. Composite project-scoped foreign keys also prevent accidental cross-project links between files, symbols, chunks, documents, knowledge, and embeddings.\n\n### `files`\n\nOne row per indexed file in a project.\n\nImportant columns:\n\n```text\nproject_id\npath\nlanguage\nfile_type\ncontent_hash\ngit_blob_sha\nlast_indexed_commit\nis_generated\nis_deleted\nindexed_at\n```\n\nUnique key:\n\n```text\n(project_id, path)\n```\n\n### `symbols`\n\nTree-sitter/LSP/Serena-derived symbols.\n\nTypical `kind` values:\n\n```text\nnamespace\nmodule\nclass\ninterface\ntrait\nenum\nmethod\nfunction\nproperty\nconstant\ntype\ncomponent\n```\n\nImportant columns:\n\n```text\nproject_id\nfile_id\nparent_symbol_id\nkind\nname\nqualified_name\nsignature\nvisibility\nstart_line/start_column\nend_line/end_column\ncontent_hash\nmetadata\n```\n\nThe schema intentionally does not constrain `kind` to a fixed enum because language ecosystems differ.\n\n### `aic_symbol_relations`\n\nStores directed edges in the project's code graph. A row means that `source_symbol_id` has a `relation_type` relationship to a target. For example, a method that calls another method points from the caller to the callee; a class that implements an interface points from the class to the interface.\n\n| Column                  |    Required | Meaning                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n|-------------------------|------------:|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| `id`                    |   Generated | Relation row UUID.                                                                                                                                                                      |\n| `project_id`            |         Yes | Owning project; must match both endpoint symbols.                                                                                                                                       |\n| `source_symbol_id`      |         Yes | Symbol where the relationship originates.                                                                                                                                               |\n| `target_symbol_id`      |          No | Resolved target symbol in the same project.                                                                                                                                             |\n| `target_qualified_name` | Conditional | Target name when it cannot be resolved to an indexed symbol.                                                                                                                            |\n| `relation_type`         |         Yes | Relationship kind, such as `calls`, `references`, `imports`, `extends`, `implements`, `uses`, `dispatches`, `listens`, `publishes`, `subscribes`, `reads`, `writes`, or `instantiates`. |\n| `source_line`           |          No | One-based line in the source file where the relationship is evidenced.                                                                                                                  |\n| `confidence`            |         Yes | Confidence score from `0.0` to `1.0`; defaults to `1.0`.                                                                                                                                |\n| `evidence`              |         Yes | JSONB details supporting the edge; defaults to `{}`.                                                                                                                                    |\n| `created_at`            |   Generated | Row creation time.                                                                                                                                                                      |\n\n`relation_type` is free text, not a database enum. Use the canonical lowercase values above consistently; add a new value only when the extractor has a distinct, useful relationship to represent. The database enforces nonblank types, valid confidence and line ranges, project-matched foreign keys, and at least one target identifier (`target_symbol_id` or a nonblank `target_qualified_name`).\n\nUse the actual table name `aic_symbol_relations` in SQL. The relation indexes support project/type filtering and incoming/outgoing traversal. There is no natural unique constraint for an edge, so `ON CONFLICT` cannot make relation writes idempotent by itself. Refresh a source symbol's outgoing edges by deleting its old rows and inserting the complete newly extracted set in one transaction.\n\nFor the extraction workflow and evidence/confidence guidance, see [relation collection and persistence](retrieval-and-indexing.md#required-relation-collection-workflow).\n\n```sql\nBEGIN;\n\nDELETE FROM aic_symbol_relations\nWHERE project_id = :project_id\n  AND source_symbol_id = :source_symbol_id;\n\nINSERT INTO aic_symbol_relations (\n    project_id,\n    source_symbol_id,\n    target_symbol_id,\n    target_qualified_name,\n    relation_type,\n    source_line,\n    confidence,\n    evidence\n)\nVALUES (\n    :project_id,\n    :source_symbol_id,\n    :target_symbol_id,\n    :target_qualified_name,\n    :relation_type,\n    :source_line,\n    :confidence,\n    :evidence::jsonb\n);\n\nCOMMIT;\n```\n\nRun the `DELETE` once for each refreshed source symbol, then insert all of its current edges before committing. If the extraction finds no edges, the delete alone correctly leaves that source with no outgoing relations. Do not delete rows for sources outside the refreshed set.\n\nAn indexed same-project target should use `target_symbol_id`; an external or unresolved target should use `NULL` for that column and a stable qualified name. `source_line` is one-based. Keep `evidence` structured, for example with extractor, evidence kind, source path, and a short source fragment or AST/LSP reference. Avoid storing secrets or large source bodies.\n\nWhen rebuilding symbols, account for `ON DELETE CASCADE` on both source and target foreign keys: deleting a symbol also deletes edges from other files that point to it. Re-resolve and rebuild both outgoing relations for refreshed symbols and incoming relations affected by changed/deleted targets in the same logical indexing run. Do not report graph indexing as complete while those dependent edges are stale.\n\n### `documents`\n\nProject documentation and external/local reference material.\n\nExamples:\n\n```text\nREADME\nADR\nOpenAPI document\ninternal architecture document\nvendor API reference\nAI Factory generated research\n```\n\nA document must have either `path` or `source_uri`.\n\n### `knowledge_items`\n\nDurable, explicit project knowledge.\n\nRecommended `kind` values:\n\n```text\narchitecture\nbusiness_rule\nconstraint\nconvention\ndecision\nincident\nintegration\nsecurity\ndeployment\n```\n\nUse this table for concise, evidence-backed facts that should persist across agent sessions.\n\nDo not put temporary guesses here.\n\n### `chunks`\n\nCanonical RAG chunk table for all source types.\n\n`source_type` is one of:\n\n```text\ncode\ndocument\nknowledge\n```\n\nA chunk points to exactly one primary source:\n\n```text\ncode      -> file_id (+ optional symbol_id)\ndocument  -> document_id\nknowledge -> knowledge_item_id\n```\n\nImportant columns:\n\n```text\nchunk_type\nsequence_number\nstart_line/end_line\ncontent\ncontent_hash\ntoken_count\nmetadata\nsearch_vector\n```\n\n`search_vector` is generated automatically for PostgreSQL Full Text Search.\n\n### `embedding_models`\n\nRegistry of embedding configurations.\n\n```text\nprovider\nmodel\ndimensions\ndistance_metric\nenabled\n```\n\nSupported `distance_metric` values in this schema:\n\n```text\ncosine\nl2\ninner_product\n```\n\nThe model registry avoids hard-coding a single embedding dimension into the schema.\n\n### `embeddings`\n\nEmbeddings for chunks.\n\n```text\nproject_id\nchunk_id\nembedding_model_id\ndimensions\nembedding\nsource_content_hash\n```\n\nThe `embedding` column uses unconstrained `VECTOR`, allowing different models with different dimensions in one table.\n\n`source_content_hash` is critical: if it differs from the current chunk hash, the embedding is stale and must be regenerated.\n\n### `indexing_runs`\n[← Database Reference](./database.md) · [Back to README](../README.md) · [Agent Workflow →](./agent-workflow.md)\n\n# Retrieval and Indexing\n\n## 4. Search decision matrix\n\n| Need                             | Preferred tool                                    |\n|----------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------|\n| Exact string/error/config key    | ripgrep                                           |\n| Class/method definition          | Serena/LSP                                        |\n| Find references/implementations  | Serena/LSP                                        |\n| Caller/callee path               | Serena/LSP + relational graph                     |\n| Blast radius                     | PostgreSQL relational graph                       |\n| AST code shape                   | /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep                    |\n| Large codemod                    | /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep + Git diff + tests |\n| Syntax-aware chunk boundaries    | Tree-sitter                                       |\n| Unknown business concept         | FTS + pgvector                                    |\n| Documentation/ADR/business rules | PostgreSQL RAG                                    |\n| Why/when code changed            | Git                                               |\n| Prove correctness                | tests/static analysis                             |\n| Rendered UI/layout/usability     | `cua_repl` / `node_repl`                          |\n| DOM/computed styles/geometry     | `cua_repl` / `node_repl`                          |\n| Browser console/runtime errors   | `cua_repl` / `node_repl`                          |\n| Network/API behavior in browser  | `cua_repl` / `node_repl`                          |\n| Browser runtime alternative      | Chrome DevTools MCP                               |\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## Reliable end-to-end indexing\n\nIndexing crosses host tools, a Docker container, a paid embedding API, and PostgreSQL. Verify each boundary explicitly:\n\n1. Check host tools with `command -v /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep`, `serena --version`, and the Python interpreter that has `tree_sitter_language_pack`. The AI database container is expected to provide PostgreSQL and Node.js, not every parser or language server.\n2. Run Serena/LSP indexing locally. A first run may try to download a language server (for PHP, Intelephense); DNS or registry failures indicate a tool-installation problem. Retry after network access is available and distinguish this from source parse errors.\n3. Parse a small representative file before processing the whole repository. Some Tree-sitter bindings can fail in batch traversal. If a fallback parser or line chunking is used, record the fallback and resulting limitations; do not label those chunks syntax-aware. Populate `aic_symbol_relations` for every edge class that was actually derived, and record any extraction gaps.\n4. Resolve the project by slug and scope every file, symbol, chunk, knowledge, embedding, and run query by `project_id`. Exclude credentials, environment files, generated/runtime content, and any source not approved for the shared database.\n5. Generate embeddings in bounded batches. `app/embedding.js` currently prints the API key and input text to stdout, so suppress stdout and keep command logs private. Persist each batch under `/app/embedding-results/{year}/{month}/` (container path `/app/embedding-results/{year}/{month}/`) before starting another batch. Never rely on container `/tmp` for paid results.\n6. Before retrying, compare existing result files, chunk hashes, project counts, model ID, and indexing-run state. Reuse completed batches; do not call the API again for unchanged input. Keep a manifest mapping batch files to chunk IDs/content hashes when building a new index.\n7. Commit embeddings first. Then create the project/model HNSW index with `aic_create_project_embedding_hnsw_index(...)` using the table-owner role. The restricted `$POSTGRES_SIMPLE_USER` may insert rows but fail with `must be owner of table aic_embeddings` when creating the index. Mark the run `completed` only after the index exists and counts are checked.\n\nA failed index-creation statement inside the same transaction as vector inserts rolls back those inserts. Check database state before recovery; preserve saved batch files and resume the database write without generating embeddings again. Avoid broad deletes or project recreation when recovering partial runs.\n\n### Office Easyfit indexing record (2026-10-02)\n\nThe `office-easyfit` project uses `text-embedding-3-small` (1536 dimensions) and a project-specific HNSW index. The completed run contains 1,182 PHP files, 4,221 approximate symbol rows, 3,896 chunks/embeddings, four documents, and four knowledge items. Configuration, test, runtime, vendor/dependency, web asset/upload paths, and files matching the credential-like literal filter were excluded. Serena indexed 964 PHP files. The Tree-sitter Python binding segfaulted during batch node-field extraction, so code chunks used 80-line boundaries and symbol names used a declaration-line fallback; symbol relations were not populated. Use vector and symbol results as candidates and verify findings in the original source. The 325 embedding result JSON files were persisted under `app/embedding-results/`. This run did not produce a separate batch-to-chunk manifest; the database rows are complete, so do not regenerate them. Future runs should persist that mapping alongside each batch file.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Search rules\n\n### Rule 1 — Always scope by project\n\nBad:\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM chunks\nLIMIT 20;\n```\n\nGood:\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM chunks\nWHERE project_id = :project_id\nLIMIT 20;\n```\n\nCross-project retrieval is allowed only when the task explicitly requires it.\n\n### Rule 2 — Prefer exact symbol lookup before fuzzy/vector search\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n    s.*,\n    f.path\nFROM symbols s\nJOIN files f ON f.id = s.file_id\nWHERE s.project_id = :project_id\n  AND s.qualified_name = :qualified_name;\n```\n\n### Rule 3 — Fuzzy symbol search for uncertain names\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM search_symbols_fuzzy(\n    :project_id,\n    'PaymentServce',\n    20\n);\n```\n\n### Rule 4 — Full Text Search for lexical/business terms\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM search_chunks_text(\n    :project_id,\n    'failed payment retry',\n    20\n);\n```\n\n### Rule 5 — Exact vector retrieval\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM search_chunks_vector_exact(\n    :project_id,\n    :embedding_model_id,\n    :query_embedding::vector,\n    20\n);\n```\n\nThe application is responsible for generating `:query_embedding` with the same model used for stored embeddings.\n\n### Rule 6 — Graph traversal\n\nOutgoing dependencies:\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM aic_symbol_relations_walk(\n    :project_id,\n    :symbol_id,\n    'outgoing',\n    3\n);\n```\n\nIncoming dependants / impact candidates:\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM aic_symbol_relations_walk(\n    :project_id,\n    :symbol_id,\n    'incoming',\n    3\n);\n```\n\nTreat heuristic relations according to their `confidence`.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 12. Vector indexing\n\nA single `VECTOR` column can store embeddings of different dimensions. Approximate vector indexes, however, must target rows with a consistent dimension.\n\nThis repository therefore does **not** create one global HNSW index automatically.\n\nAfter registering an embedding model:\n\n```sql\nSELECT id, provider, model, dimensions\nFROM embedding_models;\n```\n\nCreate a **project + model specific** HNSW index:\n\n```sql\nSELECT create_project_embedding_hnsw_index(\n    :project_id,\n    :embedding_model_id\n);\n```\n\nProject-specific partial indexes are intentional. In a shared multi-project database, a single approximate index shared by all projects can lose recall when PostgreSQL applies `project_id` filtering after ANN candidate selection.\n\nThe helper uses:\n\n- `vector(...)` for dimensions up to 2000\n- `halfvec(...)` for dimensions from 2001 through 4000\n\nFor larger vectors, choose a deliberate strategy such as dimensionality reduction, binary quantization, or another model.\n\nTo remove an automatically named project/model index:\n\n```sql\nSELECT drop_project_embedding_hnsw_index(\n    :project_id,\n    :embedding_model_id\n);\n```\n\n### Important query note\n\nAn expression HNSW index is useful only when the retrieval query uses the corresponding cast/expression and matches the partial-index predicate. `search_chunks_vector_exact()` is deliberately an exact, generic fallback.\n\nFor high-volume approximate retrieval, use the helper after creating the HNSW index:\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM search_chunks_vector_hnsw(\n    :project_id,\n    :embedding_model_id,\n    :query_embedding::vector,\n    20\n);\n```\n\nThis function uses the same `vector(n)` or `halfvec(n)` expression as the model-specific HNSW index.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 13. Recommended hybrid RAG flow\n\nFor a natural-language question:\n\n```text\n\"Where is failed payment retry logic implemented?\"\n```\n\nRecommended pipeline:\n\n```text\n1. Resolve project_id\n2. FTS top N\n3. Vector top N\n4. Fuzzy symbol candidates\n5. Optional graph expansion around strong symbol candidates\n6. Merge / rerank candidates\n7. Read original source\n8. Verify references with Serena/LSP and PostgreSQL `aic_symbol_relations`\n9. Answer or modify code\n```\n\nA practical RRF-style merge can happen in application code. Do not blindly concatenate dozens of chunks into the LLM context.\n\nPrefer small, evidence-rich context.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 14. Tree-sitter indexing rules\n\nTree-sitter should primarily produce structural metadata and semantic chunk boundaries.\n\nDo not embed a serialized raw AST unless a specific experiment proves it useful.\n\nPreferred code chunk:\n\n```text\nLanguage: PHP\nFile: app/Services/PaymentService.php\nNamespace: App\\Services\nClass: PaymentService\nMethod: processPayment\nSignature: processPayment(Order $order): Payment\nLines: 34-81\n\n<source code>\n```\n\nRecommended chunk boundaries:\n\n```text\nclass/interface/trait/enum\nmethod/function\ncomponent\ntemplate block\nconfiguration object\nquery/migration unit\n```\n\nFor very large symbols, split only after preserving:\n\n- symbol identity\n- parent symbol\n[← Integrations and References](./integrations.md) · [Back to README](../README.md)\n\n# Security\n\n## 23. Security\n\nThis stack is intended for local development.\n\nThe Compose file binds PostgreSQL to:\n\n```text\n127.0.0.1\n```\n\nrather than all network interfaces.\n\nStill:\n\n- change the default password\n- do not commit `.env`\n- do not expose port `5432` publicly\n- use a separate database user with restricted permissions if an untrusted agent/client can connect\n- back up the Docker volume if the accumulated project knowledge matters\n- never index secrets from source code in chunks, knowledge, or embeddings\n- treat embedding result files as source-derived data: keep them in `app/embedding-results/`, restrict access, and do not commit them unless the repository explicitly requires that\n- suppress stdout from the current embedding script because it prints the API key and input texts\n\n---\n\n## See Also\n\n- [Getting Started](./getting-started.md)\n- [Architecture and Retrieval Model](./architecture.md)\n- [Database Reference](./database.md)\n[← Users](user-resource.md) · [Back to README](../README.md) · [Code Intelligence →](ai-code-intelligence.md)\n\n# Agent Collaboration\n\nThis guide defines how the primary AI agent delegates complete, independently executable work to subagents in this repository.\n\nA subagent owns its assigned scope from investigation through implementation and verification unless the task brief explicitly limits it to research, review, testing, or another narrower responsibility.\n\nThe primary agent remains responsible for orchestration, integration, conflict resolution, final verification, and communication with the user.\n\n## Subagent orchestration policy\n\nUse subagents conservatively.\n\nSubagents exist to reduce context pressure, parallelize genuinely independent work, and provide specialized investigation or implementation. They must not be created merely because additional concurrency is available.\n\n### Hard limits\n\n- A root task may use **at most 4 subagents** unless the user explicitly requests a different limit.\n- The limit of 4 is a ceiling, not a target.\n- Do not spawn subagents for simple or straightforward tasks.\n- Prefer solving the task directly when delegation provides no clear benefit.\n- Prefer 1 subagent when one independent investigation or implementation scope is sufficient.\n- Prefer 2-4 subagents only when there are genuinely independent workstreams.\n- Never spawn additional subagents merely to increase parallelism.\n- Never spawn duplicate subagents investigating or implementing substantially the same scope.\n- Do not replace a completed subagent with another unless new independent work has actually been identified.\n- Prefer continuing an existing suitable subagent instead of spawning another one for closely related follow-up work.\n\nRecommended default:\n\n```text\nSimple task:\nMain Agent only\n\nModerate task:\nMain Agent\n└── 1 Subagent if useful\n\nComplex task:\nMain Agent\n├── Subagent 1\n└── Subagent 2\n\nLarge task with independent workstreams:\nMain Agent\n├── Subagent 1\n├── Subagent 2\n├── Subagent 3\n└── Subagent 4\n```\n\nNever create four subagents simply because four slots are available.\n\n### Nested delegation\n\nSubagents MUST NOT spawn other subagents.\n\nOnly the root/main agent may create subagents.\n\nEvery subagent task brief must explicitly include:\n\n> Do not spawn additional subagents. Complete the assigned task yourself.\n\nThe intended hierarchy is always:\n\n```text\nMain Agent\n├── Subagent\n├── Subagent\n├── Subagent\n└── Subagent\n```\n\nThe following is forbidden:\n\n```text\nMain Agent\n└── Subagent\n    └── Subagent\n        └── Subagent\n```\n\n### Before spawning\n\nBefore creating a subagent, the primary agent must verify that:\n\n1. The work is sufficiently independent to delegate.\n2. The scope can be clearly bounded.\n3. No existing subagent is already responsible for substantially the same work.\n4. Delegation provides meaningful benefit over completing the work directly.\n5. The expected benefit justifies the additional token, tool, and execution cost.\n6. The work can be performed without unsafe write conflicts with other active agents.\n\nIf these conditions are not satisfied, do not spawn a new subagent.\n\n### Token and cost discipline\n\nSubagents consume independent model context and tool execution.\n\nThe primary agent must therefore:\n\n- prefer the smallest useful number of subagents;\n- avoid assigning the same background investigation to multiple agents;\n- provide focused starting context rather than asking every subagent to rediscover the entire project;\n- give each subagent explicit files, symbols, services, or questions when known;\n- avoid broad prompts such as \"analyze the whole project\" unless that breadth is genuinely required;\n- request concise completion reports instead of unnecessary raw logs;\n- avoid spawning a new agent for a task that can be completed by extending an existing agent's assignment; and\n- stop delegating when sufficient evidence already exists to complete the task safely.\n\n### Lifecycle\n\nWhen a subagent completes its assigned task:\n\n1. collect its completion report;\n2. inspect the resulting changes or evidence;\n3. close/release the agent when supported;\n4. do not automatically create a replacement agent;\n5. integrate the result with findings from other agents;\n6. resolve contradictions before implementation or final reporting.\n\nA completed subagent's conclusion is evidence, not automatically accepted truth.\n\n### Default strategy\n\nFor a complex development task, a reasonable maximum decomposition is:\n\n```text\nMain Agent\n├── Subagent 1: primary investigation or backend scope\n├── Subagent 2: independent subsystem or database scope\n├── Subagent 3: verification, UI, testing, or review if necessary\n└── Subagent 4: independent implementation scope if necessary\n```\n\nThese roles are examples only.\n\nDo not automatically assign all four roles. The primary agent should create only the agents required by the actual task.\n\nA subagent may already own investigation, implementation, and verification for its bounded scope. A separate implementation subagent is unnecessary when an existing subagent can safely complete the work end to end.\n\n## Roles and authority\n\n| Role          | Responsibility                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    |\n|---------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Primary agent | Understands the user's goal, performs initial reconnaissance, divides work into bounded tasks, controls subagent count, assigns ownership, integrates and reviews results, resolves conflicts, performs final verification, reports to the user, and is the only agent allowed to run `git push`. |\n| Subagent      | Completes its assigned work within the defined scope, uses available project tools, may modify authorized files or services, verifies its result, and reports evidence and remaining risks to the primary agent.                                                                                  |\n\nSubagents are full contributors, not inherently read-only reviewers.\n\nHowever, their authority is limited by the task brief.\n\nA subagent may be assigned as:\n\n```text\nresearch-only\nimplementation\nreview\ntesting\ndatabase\nUI/runtime investigation\ninfrastructure\ndocumentation\n```\n\nIf the task brief states that the assignment is read-only or research-only, the subagent must not modify project files, database state, services, Git state, or external systems.\n\nWithin their assignment and the current session's available tools and permissions, subagents may:\n\n- analyze requirements;\n- read source code and documentation;\n- search with `rg`, Serena/LSP, ast-grep, Tree-sitter, and the project index;\n- collect evidence from PostgreSQL and configured services while respecting project scope and read/write authorization;\n- inspect Docker services, PHP-FPM, NGINX, application servers, queues, and other runtime components when tools and permissions are available;\n- use host and container utilities, package managers, Node.js, Python, PHP, and other installed tools;\n- work with the shared `ai-code-intelligence` project to index this application, update project-scoped knowledge, or create embeddings when explicitly included in the assignment and authorized;\n- inspect browser/UI/runtime state using the configured browser tooling;\n- create, edit, or delete files within the assigned write scope; and\n- use Git to inspect changes and, when explicitly included in the assignment, stage and commit them.\n"},"started_at_ms":1791470709517,"completed_at_ms":1791470709517}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-08T14:45:09.523Z","ordinal":11785,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a11bf9-ab13-7850-bce2-ae7e90c895ca","call_id":"call_2052bd4e2ee9427daf274049d1176bbb","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.1 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"# Repository Guidelines\n\n## Project Structure & Module Organization\n\nThis repository packages a shared PostgreSQL database for code intelligence. The root `docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/` contains ordered SQL initialization scripts: extensions, schema, indexes, search functions, and vector helpers. `.docker/dev/` contains the local development image, PostgreSQL configuration, Compose file, and environment files. `ai-code-intelligence/` is the standalone deployment package with its own Dockerfile, Compose file, SQL initialization scripts, example environment file, and README. `app/` contains the small Node.js embedding utility and provider implementation. The root README documents the schema and recommended agent workflow.\n\n## Build, Test, and Development Commands\n\n- `make up-dev` starts the development PostgreSQL service.\n- `make down-dev` stops the development service; it does not remove stored volumes.\n- `make build-dev` builds the development image.\n- `make restart-dev` restarts the service; `make rebuild-dev` rebuilds and starts it.\n- `npm install --prefix app` installs the embedding utility dependencies.\n- `node app/embedding.js <API_KEY> text-embedding-3-small \"text to embed\"` generates embeddings.\n\nThere is no configured automated test or lint command. For database changes, review SQL ordering and validate initialization against a disposable local database. Do not use destructive volume/database cleanup commands against the shared development data.\n\n## Coding Style & Naming Conventions\n\nKeep SQL readable, use uppercase SQL keywords, explicit column lists, and descriptive `snake_case` identifiers. Preserve the numeric prefix and dependency order of initialization scripts (for example, `003_indexes.sql`). Use two spaces for JSON and JavaScript indentation, ES modules, and descriptive camelCase JavaScript names. Keep deployment examples aligned between the root and standalone package when both represent the same behavior.\n\n## Testing Guidelines\n\nNo dedicated test suite is present. Verify SQL changes by applying initialization on a clean local database and checking the affected tables, indexes, or functions. For changes to `app/`, run the relevant command manually and avoid committing generated credentials or real API keys.\n\n## Commit & Pull Request Guidelines\n\nGit history is unavailable in this checkout, so no established commit pattern could be confirmed. Use concise imperative commit subjects, such as `Add vector search helper index`. Pull requests should explain the schema or runtime impact, list validation performed, call out environment/configuration changes, and include logs or screenshots only when they clarify behavior.\n\n## Security & Configuration\n\nNever commit `.docker/dev/env/` secrets or expose their values in documentation and logs. Use `.env.example` for safe placeholders. Scope all project-specific database queries by `project_id` or resolve a project by slug first. Persist paid embedding results under `app/embedding-results/`; inspect and reuse saved batches before retries. The current embedding script echoes its API key and input texts to stdout, so suppress stdout. Create project HNSW indexes as the table owner, after committing vector rows.\n\n## Documentation\n\n| Document               | Path                             | Description                                   |\n|------------------------|----------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|\n| README                 | `README.md`                      | Project landing page and guide index          |\n| Getting Started        | `docs/getting-started.md`        | Local database startup and embedding setup    |\n| Architecture           | `docs/architecture.md`           | Retrieval model and system responsibilities   |\n| Database Reference     | `docs/database.md`               | Schema, ownership, and SQL examples           |\n| Retrieval and Indexing | `docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md` | Search, indexing, and code intelligence tools |\n| Agent Workflow         | `docs/agent-workflow.md`         | Investigation and verification practices      |\n| Integrations           | `docs/integrations.md`           | MCP surface and upstream references           |\n| Security               | `docs/security.md`               | Local database and data protection guidance   |\n\n## AI Context Files\n\n| File                          | Purpose                                    |\n|-------------------------------|--------------------------------------------|\n| `.ai-factory/DESCRIPTION.md`  | Project scope and technology summary       |\n| `.ai-factory/ARCHITECTURE.md` | Repository architecture guidelines         |\n| `.ai-factory/rules/base.md`   | Detected naming and structural conventions |# AI Code Intelligence Database\n\n> Local, multi-project PostgreSQL storage for AI-assisted software development.\n\nThis repository combines PostgreSQL full-text search, fuzzy lookup, pgvector retrieval, and relational code relationships. It includes a Docker-based development database and a small Node.js embedding utility. Always scope project data by `project_id` or resolve the project by slug.\n\n## Quick Start\n\nRequires Docker Compose. Start the local database:\n\n```bash\nmake up-dev\n```\n\nCheck the service state:\n\n```bash\ndocker compose -f .docker/dev/docker-compose.yml ps\n```\n\nThe embedding utility uses the OpenAI API and `text-embedding-3-small`; see [Getting Started](docs/getting-started.md) for safe setup details.\n\n## Capabilities\n\n- PostgreSQL 18 with pgvector, full-text search, and pg_trgm.\n- Project, file, symbol, relationship, document, chunk, embedding, and indexing-run storage.\n- Ordered database initialization scripts for local development and standalone deployment.\n- A hybrid retrieval workflow that combines exact, structural, semantic, and graph search.\n\n## Documentation\n\n| Guide                                                    | Description                                          |\n|----------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------|\n| [Getting Started](docs/getting-started.md)               | Local database startup and embedding setup           |\n| [Architecture](docs/architecture.md)                     | Retrieval model and system responsibilities          |\n| [Database Reference](docs/database.md)                   | Schema, ownership, and SQL examples                  |\n| [Retrieval and Indexing](docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md) | Search, indexing, and code intelligence tools        |\n| [Agent Workflow](docs/agent-workflow.md)                 | Investigation, verification, and knowledge practices |\n| [Integrations](docs/integrations.md)                     | MCP surface and upstream references                  |\n| [Security](docs/security.md)                             | Local database and data protection guidance          |[Back to README](../README.md) · [Architecture and Retrieval Model →](./architecture.md)\n\n# Getting Started\n\n## Safe embedding workflow\n\n- Development environment files live in `.docker/dev/env/`. Never copy their values into docs, chat, logs, or command output.\n- The database container runs PostgreSQL and Node.js. Tools such as `/home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep`, Tree-sitter Python bindings, and Serena/LSP may be installed on the host; check the host environment and the exact Python interpreter instead of assuming they must exist in Docker.\n- Keep paid embedding results in the host directory `app/embedding-results/project-name/{year}/{month}/`, mounted in the container at `/app/embedding-results/project-name/{year}/{month}/`. Use stable batch filenames, check which files already exist before retrying, and resume from completed batches. Do not store paid results only in `/tmp` or another ephemeral container path.\n- The current `app/embedding.js` prints its API key and input texts to standard output. Suppress stdout for the embedding call; do not share captured logs. Only index sanitized, non-secret source content.\n- Use `text-embedding-3-small` for the existing model configuration. Keep batches bounded and persist each result file before inserting vectors into PostgreSQL.\n- The restricted `$POSTGRES_SIMPLE_USER` can write project records but may not own `aic_embeddings`. Create project/model HNSW indexes as `$POSTGRES_USER` (table owner). Commit vector rows before index creation and mark the indexing run complete only after the index is verified.\n- Never run `docker system prune`, `docker volume rm`, or `DROP DATABASE` against the shared development data. Before retrying, inspect project counts, run status, and saved embedding files; do not delete a project as a shortcut.\n\n### Run SQL through the PostgreSQL container\n\nFrom the ai-code-intelligence repository root, execute SQL with `psql` inside the database container:\n\n```bash\ndocker compose -f .docker/dev/docker-compose.yml exec -T \\\n  ai-code-intelligence-postgres-postgresql sh -lc \\\n  'psql -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -P pager=off -U \"$POSTGRES_SIMPLE_USER\" -d \"$POSTGRES_DB\" -c \"SELECT slug, id FROM aic_projects ORDER BY slug;\"'\n```\n\nUse `$POSTGRES_SIMPLE_USER` for normal project-scoped reads and writes. Resolve the target project's ID by slug, then include `project_id` in queries against its files, chunks, and embeddings. For vector and HNSW queries, see [Retrieval and Indexing](./retrieval-and-indexing.md).\n\nExample for one sanitized batch:\n\n```bash\ndocker compose -f /home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/.docker/dev/docker-compose.yml \\\n  exec -T ai-code-intelligence-postgres-postgresql sh -lc '\n    node /app/embedding.js \"$OPENAI_API_KEY\" \\\n      /app/embedding-results/project-name/{year}/{month}/example-batch-0000.json \\\n      text-embedding-3-small \"class OrderService {}\" >/dev/null\n  '\n```\n\nThe embedding file persists on the host at `/app/embedding-results/project-name/{year}/{month}/example-batch-0000.json`.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Database startup\n\n### Files about DB tables\n\nDB tables schemas:\ndocker-entrypoint-initdb.d:\n - 001_extensions.sql\n - 002_schema.sql\n - 003_indexes.sql\n - 004_views_and_search_functions.sql\n - 005_vector_index_helpers.sql\n\n---\n\n## See Also\n\n- [Architecture and Retrieval Model](./architecture.md)\n- [Database Reference](./database.md)\n- [Retrieval and Indexing](./retrieval-and-indexing.md)[← Architecture and Retrieval Model](./architecture.md) · [Back to README](../README.md) · [Retrieval and Indexing →](./retrieval-and-indexing.md)\n\n# Database Reference\n\n## 6. PostgreSQL extensions\n\n`001_extensions.sql` enables:\n\n| Extension  | Purpose                                       |\n|------------|-----------------------------------------------|\n| `vector`   | Embedding storage and nearest-neighbor search |\n| `pgcrypto` | `gen_random_uuid()`                           |\n| `pg_trgm`  | Fuzzy symbol/path lookup                      |\n| `unaccent` | Optional normalization support                |\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 7. DDL model\n\nThe authoritative DDL is in `002_schema.sql`.\n\n### `projects`\n\nRoot tenant/entity for all stored projects.\n\n| Column           | Type        | Purpose                       |\n|------------------|-------------|-------------------------------|\n| `id`             | UUID PK     | Project identifier            |\n| `name`           | TEXT        | Human-readable name           |\n| `slug`           | TEXT UNIQUE | Stable local identifier       |\n| `root_path`      | TEXT        | Local repository path         |\n| `repository_url` | TEXT        | Optional Git remote           |\n| `default_branch` | TEXT        | Default branch                |\n| `active`         | BOOLEAN     | Whether the project is active |\n| `metadata`       | JSONB       | Project-specific metadata     |\n| timestamps       | TIMESTAMPTZ | Audit timestamps              |\n\nAll project-owned data cascades when a project is deleted. Composite project-scoped foreign keys also prevent accidental cross-project links between files, symbols, chunks, documents, knowledge, and embeddings.\n\n### `files`\n\nOne row per indexed file in a project.\n\nImportant columns:\n\n```text\nproject_id\npath\nlanguage\nfile_type\ncontent_hash\ngit_blob_sha\nlast_indexed_commit\nis_generated\nis_deleted\nindexed_at\n```\n\nUnique key:\n\n```text\n(project_id, path)\n```\n\n### `symbols`\n\nTree-sitter/LSP/Serena-derived symbols.\n\nTypical `kind` values:\n\n```text\nnamespace\nmodule\nclass\ninterface\ntrait\nenum\nmethod\nfunction\nproperty\nconstant\ntype\ncomponent\n```\n\nImportant columns:\n\n```text\nproject_id\nfile_id\nparent_symbol_id\nkind\nname\nqualified_name\nsignature\nvisibility\nstart_line/start_column\nend_line/end_column\ncontent_hash\nmetadata\n```\n\nThe schema intentionally does not constrain `kind` to a fixed enum because language ecosystems differ.\n\n### `aic_symbol_relations`\n\nStores directed edges in the project's code graph. A row means that `source_symbol_id` has a `relation_type` relationship to a target. For example, a method that calls another method points from the caller to the callee; a class that implements an interface points from the class to the interface.\n\n| Column                  |    Required | Meaning                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n|-------------------------|------------:|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| `id`                    |   Generated | Relation row UUID.                                                                                                                                                                      |\n| `project_id`            |         Yes | Owning project; must match both endpoint symbols.                                                                                                                                       |\n| `source_symbol_id`      |         Yes | Symbol where the relationship originates.                                                                                                                                               |\n| `target_symbol_id`      |          No | Resolved target symbol in the same project.                                                                                                                                             |\n| `target_qualified_name` | Conditional | Target name when it cannot be resolved to an indexed symbol.                                                                                                                            |\n| `relation_type`         |         Yes | Relationship kind, such as `calls`, `references`, `imports`, `extends`, `implements`, `uses`, `dispatches`, `listens`, `publishes`, `subscribes`, `reads`, `writes`, or `instantiates`. |\n| `source_line`           |          No | One-based line in the source file where the relationship is evidenced.                                                                                                                  |\n| `confidence`            |         Yes | Confidence score from `0.0` to `1.0`; defaults to `1.0`.                                                                                                                                |\n| `evidence`              |         Yes | JSONB details supporting the edge; defaults to `{}`.                                                                                                                                    |\n| `created_at`            |   Generated | Row creation time.                                                                                                                                                                      |\n\n`relation_type` is free text, not a database enum. Use the canonical lowercase values above consistently; add a new value only when the extractor has a distinct, useful relationship to represent. The database enforces nonblank types, valid confidence and line ranges, project-matched foreign keys, and at least one target identifier (`target_symbol_id` or a nonblank `target_qualified_name`).\n\nUse the actual table name `aic_symbol_relations` in SQL. The relation indexes support project/type filtering and incoming/outgoing traversal. There is no natural unique constraint for an edge, so `ON CONFLICT` cannot make relation writes idempotent by itself. Refresh a source symbol's outgoing edges by deleting its old rows and inserting the complete newly extracted set in one transaction.\n\nFor the extraction workflow and evidence/confidence guidance, see [relation collection and persistence](retrieval-and-indexing.md#required-relation-collection-workflow).\n\n```sql\nBEGIN;\n\nDELETE FROM aic_symbol_relations\nWHERE project_id = :project_id\n  AND source_symbol_id = :source_symbol_id;\n\nINSERT INTO aic_symbol_relations (\n    project_id,\n    source_symbol_id,\n    target_symbol_id,\n    target_qualified_name,\n    relation_type,\n    source_line,\n    confidence,\n    evidence\n)\nVALUES (\n    :project_id,\n    :source_symbol_id,\n    :target_symbol_id,\n    :target_qualified_name,\n    :relation_type,\n    :source_line,\n    :confidence,\n    :evidence::jsonb\n);\n\nCOMMIT;\n```\n\nRun the `DELETE` once for each refreshed source symbol, then insert all of its current edges before committing. If the extraction finds no edges, the delete alone correctly leaves that source with no outgoing relations. Do not delete rows for sources outside the refreshed set.\n\nAn indexed same-project target should use `target_symbol_id`; an external or unresolved target should use `NULL` for that column and a stable qualified name. `source_line` is one-based. Keep `evidence` structured, for example with extractor, evidence kind, source path, and a short source fragment or AST/LSP reference. Avoid storing secrets or large source bodies.\n\nWhen rebuilding symbols, account for `ON DELETE CASCADE` on both source and target foreign keys: deleting a symbol also deletes edges from other files that point to it. Re-resolve and rebuild both outgoing relations for refreshed symbols and incoming relations affected by changed/deleted targets in the same logical indexing run. Do not report graph indexing as complete while those dependent edges are stale.\n\n### `documents`\n\nProject documentation and external/local reference material.\n\nExamples:\n\n```text\nREADME\nADR\nOpenAPI document\ninternal architecture document\nvendor API reference\nAI Factory generated research\n```\n\nA document must have either `path` or `source_uri`.\n\n### `knowledge_items`\n\nDurable, explicit project knowledge.\n\nRecommended `kind` values:\n\n```text\narchitecture\nbusiness_rule\nconstraint\nconvention\ndecision\nincident\nintegration\nsecurity\ndeployment\n```\n\nUse this table for concise, evidence-backed facts that should persist across agent sessions.\n\nDo not put temporary guesses here.\n\n### `chunks`\n\nCanonical RAG chunk table for all source types.\n\n`source_type` is one of:\n\n```text\ncode\ndocument\nknowledge\n```\n\nA chunk points to exactly one primary source:\n\n```text\ncode      -> file_id (+ optional symbol_id)\ndocument  -> document_id\nknowledge -> knowledge_item_id\n```\n\nImportant columns:\n\n```text\nchunk_type\nsequence_number\nstart_line/end_line\ncontent\ncontent_hash\ntoken_count\nmetadata\nsearch_vector\n```\n\n`search_vector` is generated automatically for PostgreSQL Full Text Search.\n\n### `embedding_models`\n\nRegistry of embedding configurations.\n\n```text\nprovider\nmodel\ndimensions\ndistance_metric\nenabled\n```\n\nSupported `distance_metric` values in this schema:\n\n```text\ncosine\nl2\ninner_product\n```\n\nThe model registry avoids hard-coding a single embedding dimension into the schema.\n\n### `embeddings`\n\nEmbeddings for chunks.\n\n```text\nproject_id\nchunk_id\nembedding_model_id\ndimensions\nembedding\nsource_content_hash\n```\n\nThe `embedding` column uses unconstrained `VECTOR`, allowing different models with different dimensions in one table.\n\n`source_content_hash` is critical: if it differs from the current chunk hash, the embedding is stale and must be regenerated.\n\n### `indexing_runs`\n[← Database Reference](./database.md) · [Back to README](../README.md) · [Agent Workflow →](./agent-workflow.md)\n\n# Retrieval and Indexing\n\n## 4. Search decision matrix\n\n| Need                             | Preferred tool                                    |\n|----------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------|\n| Exact string/error/config key    | ripgrep                                           |\n| Class/method definition          | Serena/LSP                                        |\n| Find references/implementations  | Serena/LSP                                        |\n| Caller/callee path               | Serena/LSP + relational graph                     |\n| Blast radius                     | PostgreSQL relational graph                       |\n| AST code shape                   | /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep                    |\n| Large codemod                    | /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep + Git diff + tests |\n| Syntax-aware chunk boundaries    | Tree-sitter                                       |\n| Unknown business concept         | FTS + pgvector                                    |\n| Documentation/ADR/business rules | PostgreSQL RAG                                    |\n| Why/when code changed            | Git                                               |\n| Prove correctness                | tests/static analysis                             |\n| Rendered UI/layout/usability     | `cua_repl` / `node_repl`                          |\n| DOM/computed styles/geometry     | `cua_repl` / `node_repl`                          |\n| Browser console/runtime errors   | `cua_repl` / `node_repl`                          |\n| Network/API behavior in browser  | `cua_repl` / `node_repl`                          |\n| Browser runtime alternative      | Chrome DevTools MCP                               |\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## Reliable end-to-end indexing\n\nIndexing crosses host tools, a Docker container, a paid embedding API, and PostgreSQL. Verify each boundary explicitly:\n\n1. Check host tools with `command -v /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep`, `serena --version`, and the Python interpreter that has `tree_sitter_language_pack`. The AI database container is expected to provide PostgreSQL and Node.js, not every parser or language server.\n2. Run Serena/LSP indexing locally. A first run may try to download a language server (for PHP, Intelephense); DNS or registry failures indicate a tool-installation problem. Retry after network access is available and distinguish this from source parse errors.\n3. Parse a small representative file before processing the whole repository. Some Tree-sitter bindings can fail in batch traversal. If a fallback parser or line chunking is used, record the fallback and resulting limitations; do not label those chunks syntax-aware. Populate `aic_symbol_relations` for every edge class that was actually derived, and record any extraction gaps.\n4. Resolve the project by slug and scope every file, symbol, chunk, knowledge, embedding, and run query by `project_id`. Exclude credentials, environment files, generated/runtime content, and any source not approved for the shared database.\n5. Generate embeddings in bounded batches. `app/embedding.js` currently prints the API key and input text to stdout, so suppress stdout and keep command logs private. Persist each batch under `/app/embedding-results/{year}/{month}/` (container path `/app/embedding-results/{year}/{month}/`) before starting another batch. Never rely on container `/tmp` for paid results.\n6. Before retrying, compare existing result files, chunk hashes, project counts, model ID, and indexing-run state. Reuse completed batches; do not call the API again for unchanged input. Keep a manifest mapping batch files to chunk IDs/content hashes when building a new index.\n7. Commit embeddings first. Then create the project/model HNSW index with `aic_create_project_embedding_hnsw_index(...)` using the table-owner role. The restricted `$POSTGRES_SIMPLE_USER` may insert rows but fail with `must be owner of table aic_embeddings` when creating the index. Mark the run `completed` only after the index exists and counts are checked.\n\nA failed index-creation statement inside the same transaction as vector inserts rolls back those inserts. Check database state before recovery; preserve saved batch files and resume the database write without generating embeddings again. Avoid broad deletes or project recreation when recovering partial runs.\n\n### Office Easyfit indexing record (2026-10-02)\n\nThe `office-easyfit` project uses `text-embedding-3-small` (1536 dimensions) and a project-specific HNSW index. The completed run contains 1,182 PHP files, 4,221 approximate symbol rows, 3,896 chunks/embeddings, four documents, and four knowledge items. Configuration, test, runtime, vendor/dependency, web asset/upload paths, and files matching the credential-like literal filter were excluded. Serena indexed 964 PHP files. The Tree-sitter Python binding segfaulted during batch node-field extraction, so code chunks used 80-line boundaries and symbol names used a declaration-line fallback; symbol relations were not populated. Use vector and symbol results as candidates and verify findings in the original source. The 325 embedding result JSON files were persisted under `app/embedding-results/`. This run did not produce a separate batch-to-chunk manifest; the database rows are complete, so do not regenerate them. Future runs should persist that mapping alongside each batch file.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Search rules\n\n### Rule 1 — Always scope by project\n\nBad:\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM chunks\nLIMIT 20;\n```\n\nGood:\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM chunks\nWHERE project_id = :project_id\nLIMIT 20;\n```\n\nCross-project retrieval is allowed only when the task explicitly requires it.\n\n### Rule 2 — Prefer exact symbol lookup before fuzzy/vector search\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n    s.*,\n    f.path\nFROM symbols s\nJOIN files f ON f.id = s.file_id\nWHERE s.project_id = :project_id\n  AND s.qualified_name = :qualified_name;\n```\n\n### Rule 3 — Fuzzy symbol search for uncertain names\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM search_symbols_fuzzy(\n    :project_id,\n    'PaymentServce',\n    20\n);\n```\n\n### Rule 4 — Full Text Search for lexical/business terms\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM search_chunks_text(\n    :project_id,\n    'failed payment retry',\n    20\n);\n```\n\n### Rule 5 — Exact vector retrieval\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM search_chunks_vector_exact(\n    :project_id,\n    :embedding_model_id,\n    :query_embedding::vector,\n    20\n);\n```\n\nThe application is responsible for generating `:query_embedding` with the same model used for stored embeddings.\n\n### Rule 6 — Graph traversal\n\nOutgoing dependencies:\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM aic_symbol_relations_walk(\n    :project_id,\n    :symbol_id,\n    'outgoing',\n    3\n);\n```\n\nIncoming dependants / impact candidates:\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM aic_symbol_relations_walk(\n    :project_id,\n    :symbol_id,\n    'incoming',\n    3\n);\n```\n\nTreat heuristic relations according to their `confidence`.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 12. Vector indexing\n\nA single `VECTOR` column can store embeddings of different dimensions. Approximate vector indexes, however, must target rows with a consistent dimension.\n\nThis repository therefore does **not** create one global HNSW index automatically.\n\nAfter registering an embedding model:\n\n```sql\nSELECT id, provider, model, dimensions\nFROM embedding_models;\n```\n\nCreate a **project + model specific** HNSW index:\n\n```sql\nSELECT create_project_embedding_hnsw_index(\n    :project_id,\n    :embedding_model_id\n);\n```\n\nProject-specific partial indexes are intentional. In a shared multi-project database, a single approximate index shared by all projects can lose recall when PostgreSQL applies `project_id` filtering after ANN candidate selection.\n\nThe helper uses:\n\n- `vector(...)` for dimensions up to 2000\n- `halfvec(...)` for dimensions from 2001 through 4000\n\nFor larger vectors, choose a deliberate strategy such as dimensionality reduction, binary quantization, or another model.\n\nTo remove an automatically named project/model index:\n\n```sql\nSELECT drop_project_embedding_hnsw_index(\n    :project_id,\n    :embedding_model_id\n);\n```\n\n### Important query note\n\nAn expression HNSW index is useful only when the retrieval query uses the corresponding cast/expression and matches the partial-index predicate. `search_chunks_vector_exact()` is deliberately an exact, generic fallback.\n\nFor high-volume approximate retrieval, use the helper after creating the HNSW index:\n\n```sql\nSELECT *\nFROM search_chunks_vector_hnsw(\n    :project_id,\n    :embedding_model_id,\n    :query_embedding::vector,\n    20\n);\n```\n\nThis function uses the same `vector(n)` or `halfvec(n)` expression as the model-specific HNSW index.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 13. Recommended hybrid RAG flow\n\nFor a natural-language question:\n\n```text\n\"Where is failed payment retry logic implemented?\"\n```\n\nRecommended pipeline:\n\n```text\n1. Resolve project_id\n2. FTS top N\n3. Vector top N\n4. Fuzzy symbol candidates\n5. Optional graph expansion around strong symbol candidates\n6. Merge / rerank candidates\n7. Read original source\n8. Verify references with Serena/LSP and PostgreSQL `aic_symbol_relations`\n9. Answer or modify code\n```\n\nA practical RRF-style merge can happen in application code. Do not blindly concatenate dozens of chunks into the LLM context.\n\nPrefer small, evidence-rich context.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 14. Tree-sitter indexing rules\n\nTree-sitter should primarily produce structural metadata and semantic chunk boundaries.\n\nDo not embed a serialized raw AST unless a specific experiment proves it useful.\n\nPreferred code chunk:\n\n```text\nLanguage: PHP\nFile: app/Services/PaymentService.php\nNamespace: App\\Services\nClass: PaymentService\nMethod: processPayment\nSignature: processPayment(Order $order): Payment\nLines: 34-81\n\n<source code>\n```\n\nRecommended chunk boundaries:\n\n```text\nclass/interface/trait/enum\nmethod/function\ncomponent\ntemplate block\nconfiguration object\nquery/migration unit\n```\n\nFor very large symbols, split only after preserving:\n\n- symbol identity\n- parent symbol\n[← Integrations and References](./integrations.md) · [Back to README](../README.md)\n\n# Security\n\n## 23. Security\n\nThis stack is intended for local development.\n\nThe Compose file binds PostgreSQL to:\n\n```text\n127.0.0.1\n```\n\nrather than all network interfaces.\n\nStill:\n\n- change the default password\n- do not commit `.env`\n- do not expose port `5432` publicly\n- use a separate database user with restricted permissions if an untrusted agent/client can connect\n- back up the Docker volume if the accumulated project knowledge matters\n- never index secrets from source code in chunks, knowledge, or embeddings\n- treat embedding result files as source-derived data: keep them in `app/embedding-results/`, restrict access, and do not commit them unless the repository explicitly requires that\n- suppress stdout from the current embedding script because it prints the API key and input texts\n\n---\n\n## See Also\n\n- [Getting Started](./getting-started.md)\n- [Architecture and Retrieval Model](./architecture.md)\n- [Database Reference](./database.md)\n[← Users](user-resource.md) · [Back to README](../README.md) · [Code Intelligence →](ai-code-intelligence.md)\n\n# Agent Collaboration\n\nThis guide defines how the primary AI agent delegates complete, independently executable work to subagents in this repository.\n\nA subagent owns its assigned scope from investigation through implementation and verification unless the task brief explicitly limits it to research, review, testing, or another narrower responsibility.\n\nThe primary agent remains responsible for orchestration, integration, conflict resolution, final verification, and communication with the user.\n\n## Subagent orchestration policy\n\nUse subagents conservatively.\n\nSubagents exist to reduce context pressure, parallelize genuinely independent work, and provide specialized investigation or implementation. They must not be created merely because additional concurrency is available.\n\n### Hard limits\n\n- A root task may use **at most 4 subagents** unless the user explicitly requests a different limit.\n- The limit of 4 is a ceiling, not a target.\n- Do not spawn subagents for simple or straightforward tasks.\n- Prefer solving the task directly when delegation provides no clear benefit.\n- Prefer 1 subagent when one independent investigation or implementation scope is sufficient.\n- Prefer 2-4 subagents only when there are genuinely independent workstreams.\n- Never spawn additional subagents merely to increase parallelism.\n- Never spawn duplicate subagents investigating or implementing substantially the same scope.\n- Do not replace a completed subagent with another unless new independent work has actually been identified.\n- Prefer continuing an existing suitable subagent instead of spawning another one for closely related follow-up work.\n\nRecommended default:\n\n```text\nSimple task:\nMain Agent only\n\nModerate task:\nMain Agent\n└── 1 Subagent if useful\n\nComplex task:\nMain Agent\n├── Subagent 1\n└── Subagent 2\n\nLarge task with independent workstreams:\nMain Agent\n├── Subagent 1\n├── Subagent 2\n├── Subagent 3\n└── Subagent 4\n```\n\nNever create four subagents simply because four slots are available.\n\n### Nested delegation\n\nSubagents MUST NOT spawn other subagents.\n\nOnly the root/main agent may create subagents.\n\nEvery subagent task brief must explicitly include:\n\n> Do not spawn additional subagents. Complete the assigned task yourself.\n\nThe intended hierarchy is always:\n\n```text\nMain Agent\n├── Subagent\n├── Subagent\n├── Subagent\n└── Subagent\n```\n\nThe following is forbidden:\n\n```text\nMain Agent\n└── Subagent\n    └── Subagent\n        └── Subagent\n```\n\n### Before spawning\n\nBefore creating a subagent, the primary agent must verify that:\n\n1. The work is sufficiently independent to delegate.\n2. The scope can be clearly bounded.\n3. No existing subagent is already responsible for substantially the same work.\n4. Delegation provides meaningful benefit over completing the work directly.\n5. The expected benefit justifies the additional token, tool, and execution cost.\n6. The work can be performed without unsafe write conflicts with other active agents.\n\nIf these conditions are not satisfied, do not spawn a new subagent.\n\n### Token and cost discipline\n\nSubagents consume independent model context and tool execution.\n\nThe primary agent must therefore:\n\n- prefer the smallest useful number of subagents;\n- avoid assigning the same background investigation to multiple agents;\n- provide focused starting context rather than asking every subagent to rediscover the entire project;\n- give each subagent explicit files, symbols, services, or questions when known;\n- avoid broad prompts such as \"analyze the whole project\" unless that breadth is genuinely required;\n- request concise completion reports instead of unnecessary raw logs;\n- avoid spawning a new agent for a task that can be completed by extending an existing agent's assignment; and\n- stop delegating when sufficient evidence already exists to complete the task safely.\n\n### Lifecycle\n\nWhen a subagent completes its assigned task:\n\n1. collect its completion report;\n2. inspect the resulting changes or evidence;\n3. close/release the agent when supported;\n4. do not automatically create a replacement agent;\n5. integrate the result with findings from other agents;\n6. resolve contradictions before implementation or final reporting.\n\nA completed subagent's conclusion is evidence, not automatically accepted truth.\n\n### Default strategy\n\nFor a complex development task, a reasonable maximum decomposition is:\n\n```text\nMain Agent\n├── Subagent 1: primary investigation or backend scope\n├── Subagent 2: independent subsystem or database scope\n├── Subagent 3: verification, UI, testing, or review if necessary\n└── Subagent 4: independent implementation scope if necessary\n```\n\nThese roles are examples only.\n\nDo not automatically assign all four roles. The primary agent should create only the agents required by the actual task.\n\nA subagent may already own investigation, implementation, and verification for its bounded scope. A separate implementation subagent is unnecessary when an existing subagent can safely complete the work end to end.\n\n## Roles and authority\n\n| Role          | Responsibility                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    |\n|---------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Primary agent | Understands the user's goal, performs initial reconnaissance, divides work into bounded tasks, controls subagent count, assigns ownership, integrates and reviews results, resolves conflicts, performs final verification, reports to the user, and is the only agent allowed to run `git push`. |\n| Subagent      | Completes its assigned work within the defined scope, uses available project tools, may modify authorized files or services, verifies its result, and reports evidence and remaining risks to the primary agent.                                                                                  |\n\nSubagents are full contributors, not inherently read-only reviewers.\n\nHowever, their authority is limited by the task brief.\n\nA subagent may be assigned as:\n\n```text\nresearch-only\nimplementation\nreview\ntesting\ndatabase\nUI/runtime investigation\ninfrastructure\ndocumentation\n```\n\nIf the task brief states that the assignment is read-only or research-only, the subagent must not modify project files, database state, services, Git state, or external systems.\n\nWithin their assignment and the current session's available tools and permissions, subagents may:\n\n- analyze requirements;\n- read source code and documentation;\n- search with `rg`, Serena/LSP, ast-grep, Tree-sitter, and the project index;\n- collect evidence from PostgreSQL and configured services while respecting project scope and read/write authorization;\n- inspect Docker services, PHP-FPM, NGINX, application servers, queues, and other runtime components when tools and permissions are available;\n- use host and container utilities, package managers, Node.js, Python, PHP, and other installed tools;\n- work with the shared `ai-code-intelligence` project to index this application, update project-scoped knowledge, or create embeddings when explicitly included in the assignment and authorized;\n- inspect browser/UI/runtime state using the configured browser tooling;\n- create, edit, or delete files within the assigned write scope; and\n- use Git to inspect changes and, when explicitly included in the assignment, stage and commit them.\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a11bf9-7de2-72a3-b949-e54787d105e6","create_time":1791470709.523087}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
