{"timestamp":"2026-10-08T14:47:06.642Z","ordinal":11851,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_094cad5b47a7c03f016ac7ace93d0487d2978a5f350496aa86","status":"completed","call_id":"call_8c6ef57ae9ee4a178e6c8b4505c3512e","name":"exec","input":"const r=await tools.exec_command({cmd:\"rg -n \\\"Required relation collection workflow|relation_type|Tree-sitter|edge\\\" /home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md /home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql /home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/app/embedding.js && sed -n '250,410p' /home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md && sed -n '1,220p' /home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/app/embedding.js\",\"workdir\":\"/home/kamaz/www/strateg-projects/strateg-export-products\",\"yield_time_ms\":10000,\"max_output_tokens\":6000}); text(r.output)\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a11bf9-7de2-72a3-b949-e54787d105e6","create_time":1791470825.2407684}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-08T14:47:06.749Z","ordinal":11852,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a0a02b-47ee-7913-b18b-d4d4b0be8b60","turn_id":"01a11bf9-7de2-72a3-b949-e54787d105e6","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-b2753abc-243c-4f5e-b281-8b350d42e817","process_id":"31286","command":["/bin/bash","-lc","rg -n \"Required relation collection workflow|relation_type|Tree-sitter|edge\" /home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md /home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql /home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/app/embedding.js && sed -n '250,410p' /home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md && sed -n '1,220p' /home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/app/embedding.js"],"cwd":"file:///home/kamaz/www/strateg-projects/strateg-export-products","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"search","cmd":"rg -n 'Required relation collection workflow|relation_type|Tree-sitter|edge' /home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md /home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql /home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/app/embedding.js","query":"Required relation collection workflow|relation_type|Tree-sitter|edge","path":"retrieval-and-indexing.md"},{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '250,410p' /home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md","name":"retrieval-and-indexing.md","path":"/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md"},{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,220p' /home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/app/embedding.js","name":"embedding.js","path":"/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/app/embedding.js"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:16:| Syntax-aware chunk boundaries    | Tree-sitter                                       |\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:37:3. Parse a small representative file before processing the whole repository. Some Tree-sitter bindings can fail in batch traversal. If a fallback parser or line chunking is used, record the fallback and resulting limitations; do not label those chunks syntax-aware. Populate `aic_symbol_relations` for every edge class that was actually derived, and record any extraction gaps.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:38:4. Resolve the project by slug and scope every file, symbol, chunk, knowledge, embedding, and run query by `project_id`. Exclude credentials, environment files, generated/runtime content, and any source not approved for the shared database.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:47:The `office-easyfit` project uses `text-embedding-3-small` (1536 dimensions) and a project-specific HNSW index. The completed run contains 1,182 PHP files, 4,221 approximate symbol rows, 3,896 chunks/embeddings, four documents, and four knowledge items. Configuration, test, runtime, vendor/dependency, web asset/upload paths, and files matching the credential-like literal filter were excluded. Serena indexed 964 PHP files. The Tree-sitter Python binding segfaulted during batch node-field extraction, so code chunks used 80-line boundaries and symbol names used a declaration-line fallback; symbol relations were not populated. Use vector and symbol results as candidates and verify findings in the original source. The 325 embedding result JSON files were persisted under `app/embedding-results/`. This run did not produce a separate batch-to-chunk manifest; the database rows are complete, so do not regenerate them. Future runs should persist that mapping alongside each batch file.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:246:## 14. Tree-sitter indexing rules\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:248:Tree-sitter should primarily produce structural metadata and semantic chunk boundaries.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:379:Populate relations from Tree-sitter, Serena/LSP, framework-specific extractors, and other verified indexing logic. Store unresolved targets through `target_qualified_name`, and use `confidence` plus `evidence` for heuristic relationships.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:383:### Required relation collection workflow\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:385:For every file whose symbols are indexed, collect outgoing relations for each extracted source symbol when evidence is available. Do not silently skip the graph phase because semantic parsing is incomplete: use available AST/LSP evidence, record any supported framework-specific edges, and state which edge classes could not be resolved. Never invent edges just to make the graph look complete.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:391:5. Attach evidence describing how the edge was found. A suggested JSON shape is `{\"extractor\":\"tree-sitter\",\"evidence_kind\":\"direct_call\",\"source_path\":\"src/Order.php\",\"snippet\":\"$gateway->charge($order)\"}`. Keep snippets short and exclude secrets.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:392:6. Assign `confidence` according to evidence: use `1.0` for a direct, unambiguous AST/LSP edge; lower it for aliases, framework conventions, or heuristic resolution. The schema only enforces the range `0.0..1.0`; these scoring levels are indexing guidance, not database rules.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:393:7. Persist the complete current outgoing edge set for refreshed source symbols transactionally, then verify counts and unresolved targets before marking the indexing run complete. Record extracted, inserted, and unresolved edge counts plus parser/extractor limitations in `indexing_runs.stats`.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:395:Use a consistent edge direction: the symbol that performs or declares the relationship is the source. Examples: caller → callee, importing file symbol → imported symbol, implementing class → interface, event publisher → event/listener target. `relation_type` is free text in the schema; use the documented lowercase vocabulary (`calls`, `references`, `imports`, `extends`, `implements`, `uses`, `dispatches`, `listens`, `publishes`, `subscribes`, `reads`, `writes`, `instantiates`) consistently. Add other values only when the extractor can explain their meaning.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:397:### Persist and refresh edges\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:407:    relation_type,\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:434:    target_qualified_name, relation_type, source_line, confidence, evidence\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:447:There is no unique constraint for an edge, so do not depend on `ON CONFLICT`. Before inserting a refreshed source symbol's full edge set, replace its outgoing rows in the same transaction:\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:456:-- Insert every currently extracted outgoing edge for this source symbol.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:461:When symbol IDs are replaced, the source and target foreign keys use `ON DELETE CASCADE`. Deleting a symbol therefore also removes incoming relations whose source is in another file. Capture affected target names before deletion and rebuild those incoming edges after the replacement symbols are inserted. A refresh that only recreates outgoing edges can leave the graph incomplete.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:465:Run checks scoped to the project. Compare relation counts with extracted edge counts and inspect unresolved targets rather than assuming every target should resolve locally.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:468:SELECT relation_type, COUNT(*) AS edge_count\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:471:GROUP BY relation_type\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:472:ORDER BY relation_type;\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:474:SELECT relation_type, target_qualified_name, COUNT(*) AS unresolved_count\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:478:GROUP BY relation_type, target_qualified_name\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:548:Use Serena/LSP, `rg`, /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep, and Tree-sitter to move from the runtime evidence back to the relevant source code.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:634:Never copy secrets, authorization tokens, session cookies, credentials, or other sensitive values into persistent project knowledge.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:701:10. Locate the owning source component/template with Serena/LSP, `rg`, /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep, or Tree-sitter.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:801:Tree-sitter and PostgreSQL remain useful for locating and understanding project structure and durable code intelligence. They are not substitutes for inspecting the rendered browser state.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:823:Do not automatically store transient visual observations in `knowledge_items`.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:825:Good candidates for durable knowledge include verified project conventions such as:\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:104:    relation_type TEXT NOT NULL,\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:129:    CONSTRAINT symbol_relations_type_not_blank CHECK (BTRIM(relation_type) <> ''),\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:160:CREATE TABLE aic_knowledge_items (\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:175:    CONSTRAINT knowledge_items_kind_not_blank CHECK (BTRIM(kind) <> ''),\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:176:    CONSTRAINT knowledge_items_title_not_blank CHECK (BTRIM(title) <> ''),\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:177:    CONSTRAINT knowledge_items_content_not_blank CHECK (BTRIM(content) <> ''),\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:178:    CONSTRAINT knowledge_items_status_valid CHECK (\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:181:    CONSTRAINT knowledge_items_confidence_valid CHECK (\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:184:    CONSTRAINT knowledge_items_validity_valid CHECK (\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:187:    CONSTRAINT knowledge_items_project_id_id_unique UNIQUE (project_id, id)\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:197:    knowledge_item_id UUID,\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:232:    CONSTRAINT chunks_knowledge_project_fk\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:233:        FOREIGN KEY (project_id, knowledge_item_id)\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:234:        REFERENCES aic_knowledge_items(project_id, id)\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:238:        source_type IN ('code', 'document', 'knowledge')\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:245:            AND knowledge_item_id IS NULL\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:253:            AND knowledge_item_id IS NULL\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:257:            source_type = 'knowledge'\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:261:            AND knowledge_item_id IS NOT NULL\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:287:        knowledge_item_id,\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:364:            'knowledge',\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:407:CREATE TRIGGER knowledge_items_set_updated_at\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:408:BEFORE UPDATE ON aic_knowledge_items\nDo not embed a serialized raw AST unless a specific experiment proves it useful.\n\nPreferred code chunk:\n\n```text\nLanguage: PHP\nFile: app/Services/PaymentService.php\nNamespace: App\\Services\nClass: PaymentService\nMethod: processPayment\nSignature: processPayment(Order $order): Payment\nLines: 34-81\n\n<source code>\n```\n\nRecommended chunk boundaries:\n\n```text\nclass/interface/trait/enum\nmethod/function\ncomponent\ntemplate block\nconfiguration object\nquery/migration unit\n```\n\nFor very large symbols, split only after preserving:\n\n- symbol identity\n- parent symbol\n- signature\n- file path\n- line range\n- overlap where necessary\n\nFor mixed-language files such as Vue, Blade, or Twig, preserve the outer document structure and use language injections/sub-parsers where available.\n\nFor JSONL/NDJSON, parse each non-empty line as a separate JSON record rather than treating the entire file as one JSON document.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 15. ast-grep rules\n\nUse ast-grep when syntax matters.\n\nGood cases:\n\n```text\nfind a specific method-call pattern\nfind unsafe API usage\nlocate a framework-specific code shape\nperform a repeated mechanical rewrite\nenforce a local AST rule\n```\n\nBefore a rewrite:\n\n1. run search-only mode\n2. inspect representative matches\n3. narrow the rule\n4. commit/stash current work if needed\n5. run rewrite\n6. inspect `git diff`\n7. run relevant tests/static analysis\n\nDo not use a broad codemod without checking its match set.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 16. ripgrep rules\n\nUse `rg` for exact strings and regex.\n\nExamples:\n\n```bash\nrg \"PaymentCompleted\"\nrg \"processPayment\\(\" app tests\nrg --glob '*.php' \"dispatch\\(\"\nrg --glob '!vendor/**' \"retry\"\n```\n\nRemember that ripgrep respects ignore rules by default. Hidden/ignored/generated content should not be searched unless there is a specific reason.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 17. Serena / LSP rules\n\nUse Serena/LSP for semantic navigation:\n\n```text\nfind symbol\ngo to definition\nfind references\nfind implementations\ninspect symbol body\nrename/refactor\ntype-aware navigation\n```\n\nPrefer Serena/LSP over text search when a precise symbol is already known.\n\nDo not assume LSP is complete for dynamic framework relationships. Laravel container bindings, events, queues, Blade/Twig/Vue conventions, callbacks, reflection, or runtime configuration may require PostgreSQL `aic_symbol_relations`, framework-specific heuristics, `rg`, and direct source inspection.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 18. Relational code graph rules\n\nThe PostgreSQL `aic_symbols` and `aic_symbol_relations` tables are the persistent structural graph for indexed projects.\n\nUse them for:\n\n```text\ncall paths\ncallers/callees\nstructural exploration\nimpact/blast radius\ndependency context\n```\n\nPopulate relations from Tree-sitter, Serena/LSP, framework-specific extractors, and other verified indexing logic. Store unresolved targets through `target_qualified_name`, and use `confidence` plus `evidence` for heuristic relationships.\n\nDo not treat a stored relation as stronger evidence than its source permits. For dynamic framework behavior, verify important paths against source code, Serena/LSP, `rg`, configuration, tests, and runtime/framework conventions.\n\n### Required relation collection workflow\n\nFor every file whose symbols are indexed, collect outgoing relations for each extracted source symbol when evidence is available. Do not silently skip the graph phase because semantic parsing is incomplete: use available AST/LSP evidence, record any supported framework-specific edges, and state which edge classes could not be resolved. Never invent edges just to make the graph look complete.\n\n1. Resolve the project by slug once and retain its `project_id`. Load the current, non-deleted file and symbol rows for that project.\n2. Parse each symbol body or declaration range. For each call, reference, import, inheritance/implementation, dispatch, event, read/write, or instantiation that is actually present, record the source symbol ID, target expression, relation type, and one-based source line.\n3. Resolve the target against `aic_symbols` in the same project, preferring exact qualified name and language-server identity. Never resolve a target by name across projects.\n4. If the target is indexed, store its ID in `target_symbol_id`. If it is external, dynamic, or not yet indexed, set `target_symbol_id` to `NULL` and preserve its stable qualified name in `target_qualified_name`.\n5. Attach evidence describing how the edge was found. A suggested JSON shape is `{\"extractor\":\"tree-sitter\",\"evidence_kind\":\"direct_call\",\"source_path\":\"src/Order.php\",\"snippet\":\"$gateway->charge($order)\"}`. Keep snippets short and exclude secrets.\n6. Assign `confidence` according to evidence: use `1.0` for a direct, unambiguous AST/LSP edge; lower it for aliases, framework conventions, or heuristic resolution. The schema only enforces the range `0.0..1.0`; these scoring levels are indexing guidance, not database rules.\n7. Persist the complete current outgoing edge set for refreshed source symbols transactionally, then verify counts and unresolved targets before marking the indexing run complete. Record extracted, inserted, and unresolved edge counts plus parser/extractor limitations in `indexing_runs.stats`.\n\nUse a consistent edge direction: the symbol that performs or declares the relationship is the source. Examples: caller → callee, importing file symbol → imported symbol, implementing class → interface, event publisher → event/listener target. `relation_type` is free text in the schema; use the documented lowercase vocabulary (`calls`, `references`, `imports`, `extends`, `implements`, `uses`, `dispatches`, `listens`, `publishes`, `subscribes`, `reads`, `writes`, `instantiates`) consistently. Add other values only when the extractor can explain their meaning.\n\n### Persist and refresh edges\n\nUse the real table name and explicit columns. Resolve symbol IDs before this insert; the composite foreign keys ensure both symbols belong to `:project_id`.\n\n```sql\nINSERT INTO aic_symbol_relations (\n    project_id,\n    source_symbol_id,\n    target_symbol_id,\n    target_qualified_name,\n    relation_type,\n    source_line,\n    confidence,\n    evidence\nimport { OpenAiEmbeddingProvider } from './providers/open-ai.js';\n\nconst args = process.argv.slice(2);\n\nconst apiKey = REDACTED;\nconst pathToEmbedFile = args[1];\nconst model = args[2];\nconst texts = args.slice(3);\n\nif (!apiKey || !pathToEmbedFile || !model || texts.length === 0) {\n    console.error(\n        'Usage: node /app/embedding.js <api-key> <embedding-project-name.json> <model> <text> [text...]',\n    );\n\n    process.exit(1);\n}\n\nconst embeddingProvider = new OpenAiEmbeddingProvider(\n    apiKey,\n    model,\n);\n\ntry {\n    const embeddings = await embeddingProvider.createBatch(texts);\n\n    embeddingProvider.saveEmbedding(pathToEmbedFile, embeddings);\n\n    console.log('See the file' + pathToEmbedFile);\n} catch (error) {\n    console.error(error instanceof Error ? error.message : error);\n\n    process.exit(1);\n}","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:16:| Syntax-aware chunk boundaries    | Tree-sitter                                       |\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:37:3. Parse a small representative file before processing the whole repository. Some Tree-sitter bindings can fail in batch traversal. If a fallback parser or line chunking is used, record the fallback and resulting limitations; do not label those chunks syntax-aware. Populate `aic_symbol_relations` for every edge class that was actually derived, and record any extraction gaps.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:38:4. Resolve the project by slug and scope every file, symbol, chunk, knowledge, embedding, and run query by `project_id`. Exclude credentials, environment files, generated/runtime content, and any source not approved for the shared database.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:47:The `office-easyfit` project uses `text-embedding-3-small` (1536 dimensions) and a project-specific HNSW index. The completed run contains 1,182 PHP files, 4,221 approximate symbol rows, 3,896 chunks/embeddings, four documents, and four knowledge items. Configuration, test, runtime, vendor/dependency, web asset/upload paths, and files matching the credential-like literal filter were excluded. Serena indexed 964 PHP files. The Tree-sitter Python binding segfaulted during batch node-field extraction, so code chunks used 80-line boundaries and symbol names used a declaration-line fallback; symbol relations were not populated. Use vector and symbol results as candidates and verify findings in the original source. The 325 embedding result JSON files were persisted under `app/embedding-results/`. This run did not produce a separate batch-to-chunk manifest; the database rows are complete, so do not regenerate them. Future runs should persist that mapping alongside each batch file.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:246:## 14. Tree-sitter indexing rules\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:248:Tree-sitter should primarily produce structural metadata and semantic chunk boundaries.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:379:Populate relations from Tree-sitter, Serena/LSP, framework-specific extractors, and other verified indexing logic. Store unresolved targets through `target_qualified_name`, and use `confidence` plus `evidence` for heuristic relationships.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:383:### Required relation collection workflow\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:385:For every file whose symbols are indexed, collect outgoing relations for each extracted source symbol when evidence is available. Do not silently skip the graph phase because semantic parsing is incomplete: use available AST/LSP evidence, record any supported framework-specific edges, and state which edge classes could not be resolved. Never invent edges just to make the graph look complete.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:391:5. Attach evidence describing how the edge was found. A suggested JSON shape is `{\"extractor\":\"tree-sitter\",\"evidence_kind\":\"direct_call\",\"source_path\":\"src/Order.php\",\"snippet\":\"$gateway->charge($order)\"}`. Keep snippets short and exclude secrets.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:392:6. Assign `confidence` according to evidence: use `1.0` for a direct, unambiguous AST/LSP edge; lower it for aliases, framework conventions, or heuristic resolution. The schema only enforces the range `0.0..1.0`; these scoring levels are indexing guidance, not database rules.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:393:7. Persist the complete current outgoing edge set for refreshed source symbols transactionally, then verify counts and unresolved targets before marking the indexing run complete. Record extracted, inserted, and unresolved edge counts plus parser/extractor limitations in `indexing_runs.stats`.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:395:Use a consistent edge direction: the symbol that performs or declares the relationship is the source. Examples: caller → callee, importing file symbol → imported symbol, implementing class → interface, event publisher → event/listener target. `relation_type` is free text in the schema; use the documented lowercase vocabulary (`calls`, `references`, `imports`, `extends`, `implements`, `uses`, `dispatches`, `listens`, `publishes`, `subscribes`, `reads`, `writes`, `instantiates`) consistently. Add other values only when the extractor can explain their meaning.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:397:### Persist and refresh edges\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:407:    relation_type,\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:434:    target_qualified_name, relation_type, source_line, confidence, evidence\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:447:There is no unique constraint for an edge, so do not depend on `ON CONFLICT`. Before inserting a refreshed source symbol's full edge set, replace its outgoing rows in the same transaction:\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:456:-- Insert every currently extracted outgoing edge for this source symbol.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:461:When symbol IDs are replaced, the source and target foreign keys use `ON DELETE CASCADE`. Deleting a symbol therefore also removes incoming relations whose source is in another file. Capture affected target names before deletion and rebuild those incoming edges after the replacement symbols are inserted. A refresh that only recreates outgoing edges can leave the graph incomplete.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:465:Run checks scoped to the project. Compare relation counts with extracted edge counts and inspect unresolved targets rather than assuming every target should resolve locally.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:468:SELECT relation_type, COUNT(*) AS edge_count\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:471:GROUP BY relation_type\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:472:ORDER BY relation_type;\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:474:SELECT relation_type, target_qualified_name, COUNT(*) AS unresolved_count\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:478:GROUP BY relation_type, target_qualified_name\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:548:Use Serena/LSP, `rg`, /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep, and Tree-sitter to move from the runtime evidence back to the relevant source code.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:634:Never copy secrets, authorization tokens, session cookies, credentials, or other sensitive values into persistent project knowledge.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:701:10. Locate the owning source component/template with Serena/LSP, `rg`, /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep, or Tree-sitter.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:801:Tree-sitter and PostgreSQL remain useful for locating and understanding project structure and durable code intelligence. They are not substitutes for inspecting the rendered browser state.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:823:Do not automatically store transient visual observations in `knowledge_items`.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:825:Good candidates for durable knowledge include verified project conventions such as:\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:104:    relation_type TEXT NOT NULL,\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:129:    CONSTRAINT symbol_relations_type_not_blank CHECK (BTRIM(relation_type) <> ''),\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:160:CREATE TABLE aic_knowledge_items (\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:175:    CONSTRAINT knowledge_items_kind_not_blank CHECK (BTRIM(kind) <> ''),\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:176:    CONSTRAINT knowledge_items_title_not_blank CHECK (BTRIM(title) <> ''),\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:177:    CONSTRAINT knowledge_items_content_not_blank CHECK (BTRIM(content) <> ''),\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:178:    CONSTRAINT knowledge_items_status_valid CHECK (\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:181:    CONSTRAINT knowledge_items_confidence_valid CHECK (\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:184:    CONSTRAINT knowledge_items_validity_valid CHECK (\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:187:    CONSTRAINT knowledge_items_project_id_id_unique UNIQUE (project_id, id)\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:197:    knowledge_item_id UUID,\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:232:    CONSTRAINT chunks_knowledge_project_fk\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:233:        FOREIGN KEY (project_id, knowledge_item_id)\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:234:        REFERENCES aic_knowledge_items(project_id, id)\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:238:        source_type IN ('code', 'document', 'knowledge')\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:245:            AND knowledge_item_id IS NULL\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:253:            AND knowledge_item_id IS NULL\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:257:            source_type = 'knowledge'\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:261:            AND knowledge_item_id IS NOT NULL\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:287:        knowledge_item_id,\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:364:            'knowledge',\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:407:CREATE TRIGGER knowledge_items_set_updated_at\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:408:BEFORE UPDATE ON aic_knowledge_items\nDo not embed a serialized raw AST unless a specific experiment proves it useful.\n\nPreferred code chunk:\n\n```text\nLanguage: PHP\nFile: app/Services/PaymentService.php\nNamespace: App\\Services\nClass: PaymentService\nMethod: processPayment\nSignature: processPayment(Order $order): Payment\nLines: 34-81\n\n<source code>\n```\n\nRecommended chunk boundaries:\n\n```text\nclass/interface/trait/enum\nmethod/function\ncomponent\ntemplate block\nconfiguration object\nquery/migration unit\n```\n\nFor very large symbols, split only after preserving:\n\n- symbol identity\n- parent symbol\n- signature\n- file path\n- line range\n- overlap where necessary\n\nFor mixed-language files such as Vue, Blade, or Twig, preserve the outer document structure and use language injections/sub-parsers where available.\n\nFor JSONL/NDJSON, parse each non-empty line as a separate JSON record rather than treating the entire file as one JSON document.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 15. ast-grep rules\n\nUse ast-grep when syntax matters.\n\nGood cases:\n\n```text\nfind a specific method-call pattern\nfind unsafe API usage\nlocate a framework-specific code shape\nperform a repeated mechanical rewrite\nenforce a local AST rule\n```\n\nBefore a rewrite:\n\n1. run search-only mode\n2. inspect representative matches\n3. narrow the rule\n4. commit/stash current work if needed\n5. run rewrite\n6. inspect `git diff`\n7. run relevant tests/static analysis\n\nDo not use a broad codemod without checking its match set.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 16. ripgrep rules\n\nUse `rg` for exact strings and regex.\n\nExamples:\n\n```bash\nrg \"PaymentCompleted\"\nrg \"processPayment\\(\" app tests\nrg --glob '*.php' \"dispatch\\(\"\nrg --glob '!vendor/**' \"retry\"\n```\n\nRemember that ripgrep respects ignore rules by default. Hidden/ignored/generated content should not be searched unless there is a specific reason.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 17. Serena / LSP rules\n\nUse Serena/LSP for semantic navigation:\n\n```text\nfind symbol\ngo to definition\nfind references\nfind implementations\ninspect symbol body\nrename/refactor\ntype-aware navigation\n```\n\nPrefer Serena/LSP over text search when a precise symbol is already known.\n\nDo not assume LSP is complete for dynamic framework relationships. Laravel container bindings, events, queues, Blade/Twig/Vue conventions, callbacks, reflection, or runtime configuration may require PostgreSQL `aic_symbol_relations`, framework-specific heuristics, `rg`, and direct source inspection.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 18. Relational code graph rules\n\nThe PostgreSQL `aic_symbols` and `aic_symbol_relations` tables are the persistent structural graph for indexed projects.\n\nUse them for:\n\n```text\ncall paths\ncallers/callees\nstructural exploration\nimpact/blast radius\ndependency context\n```\n\nPopulate relations from Tree-sitter, Serena/LSP, framework-specific extractors, and other verified indexing logic. Store unresolved targets through `target_qualified_name`, and use `confidence` plus `evidence` for heuristic relationships.\n\nDo not treat a stored relation as stronger evidence than its source permits. For dynamic framework behavior, verify important paths against source code, Serena/LSP, `rg`, configuration, tests, and runtime/framework conventions.\n\n### Required relation collection workflow\n\nFor every file whose symbols are indexed, collect outgoing relations for each extracted source symbol when evidence is available. Do not silently skip the graph phase because semantic parsing is incomplete: use available AST/LSP evidence, record any supported framework-specific edges, and state which edge classes could not be resolved. Never invent edges just to make the graph look complete.\n\n1. Resolve the project by slug once and retain its `project_id`. Load the current, non-deleted file and symbol rows for that project.\n2. Parse each symbol body or declaration range. For each call, reference, import, inheritance/implementation, dispatch, event, read/write, or instantiation that is actually present, record the source symbol ID, target expression, relation type, and one-based source line.\n3. Resolve the target against `aic_symbols` in the same project, preferring exact qualified name and language-server identity. Never resolve a target by name across projects.\n4. If the target is indexed, store its ID in `target_symbol_id`. If it is external, dynamic, or not yet indexed, set `target_symbol_id` to `NULL` and preserve its stable qualified name in `target_qualified_name`.\n5. Attach evidence describing how the edge was found. A suggested JSON shape is `{\"extractor\":\"tree-sitter\",\"evidence_kind\":\"direct_call\",\"source_path\":\"src/Order.php\",\"snippet\":\"$gateway->charge($order)\"}`. Keep snippets short and exclude secrets.\n6. Assign `confidence` according to evidence: use `1.0` for a direct, unambiguous AST/LSP edge; lower it for aliases, framework conventions, or heuristic resolution. The schema only enforces the range `0.0..1.0`; these scoring levels are indexing guidance, not database rules.\n7. Persist the complete current outgoing edge set for refreshed source symbols transactionally, then verify counts and unresolved targets before marking the indexing run complete. Record extracted, inserted, and unresolved edge counts plus parser/extractor limitations in `indexing_runs.stats`.\n\nUse a consistent edge direction: the symbol that performs or declares the relationship is the source. Examples: caller → callee, importing file symbol → imported symbol, implementing class → interface, event publisher → event/listener target. `relation_type` is free text in the schema; use the documented lowercase vocabulary (`calls`, `references`, `imports`, `extends`, `implements`, `uses`, `dispatches`, `listens`, `publishes`, `subscribes`, `reads`, `writes`, `instantiates`) consistently. Add other values only when the extractor can explain their meaning.\n\n### Persist and refresh edges\n\nUse the real table name and explicit columns. Resolve symbol IDs before this insert; the composite foreign keys ensure both symbols belong to `:project_id`.\n\n```sql\nINSERT INTO aic_symbol_relations (\n    project_id,\n    source_symbol_id,\n    target_symbol_id,\n    target_qualified_name,\n    relation_type,\n    source_line,\n    confidence,\n    evidence\nimport { OpenAiEmbeddingProvider } from './providers/open-ai.js';\n\nconst args = process.argv.slice(2);\n\nconst apiKey = REDACTED;\nconst pathToEmbedFile = args[1];\nconst model = args[2];\nconst texts = args.slice(3);\n\nif (!apiKey || !pathToEmbedFile || !model || texts.length === 0) {\n    console.error(\n        'Usage: node /app/embedding.js <api-key> <embedding-project-name.json> <model> <text> [text...]',\n    );\n\n    process.exit(1);\n}\n\nconst embeddingProvider = new OpenAiEmbeddingProvider(\n    apiKey,\n    model,\n);\n\ntry {\n    const embeddings = await embeddingProvider.createBatch(texts);\n\n    embeddingProvider.saveEmbedding(pathToEmbedFile, embeddings);\n\n    console.log('See the file' + pathToEmbedFile);\n} catch (error) {\n    console.error(error instanceof Error ? error.message : error);\n\n    process.exit(1);\n}","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":7304},"formatted_output":"/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:16:| Syntax-aware chunk boundaries    | Tree-sitter                                       |\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:37:3. Parse a small representative file before processing the whole repository. Some Tree-sitter bindings can fail in batch traversal. If a fallback parser or line chunking is used, record the fallback and resulting limitations; do not label those chunks syntax-aware. Populate `aic_symbol_relations` for every edge class that was actually derived, and record any extraction gaps.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:38:4. Resolve the project by slug and scope every file, symbol, chunk, knowledge, embedding, and run query by `project_id`. Exclude credentials, environment files, generated/runtime content, and any source not approved for the shared database.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:47:The `office-easyfit` project uses `text-embedding-3-small` (1536 dimensions) and a project-specific HNSW index. The completed run contains 1,182 PHP files, 4,221 approximate symbol rows, 3,896 chunks/embeddings, four documents, and four knowledge items. Configuration, test, runtime, vendor/dependency, web asset/upload paths, and files matching the credential-like literal filter were excluded. Serena indexed 964 PHP files. The Tree-sitter Python binding segfaulted during batch node-field extraction, so code chunks used 80-line boundaries and symbol names used a declaration-line fallback; symbol relations were not populated. Use vector and symbol results as candidates and verify findings in the original source. The 325 embedding result JSON files were persisted under `app/embedding-results/`. This run did not produce a separate batch-to-chunk manifest; the database rows are complete, so do not regenerate them. Future runs should persist that mapping alongside each batch file.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:246:## 14. Tree-sitter indexing rules\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:248:Tree-sitter should primarily produce structural metadata and semantic chunk boundaries.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:379:Populate relations from Tree-sitter, Serena/LSP, framework-specific extractors, and other verified indexing logic. Store unresolved targets through `target_qualified_name`, and use `confidence` plus `evidence` for heuristic relationships.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:383:### Required relation collection workflow\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:385:For every file whose symbols are indexed, collect outgoing relations for each extracted source symbol when evidence is available. Do not silently skip the graph phase because semantic parsing is incomplete: use available AST/LSP evidence, record any supported framework-specific edges, and state which edge classes could not be resolved. Never invent edges just to make the graph look complete.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:391:5. Attach evidence describing how the edge was found. A suggested JSON shape is `{\"extractor\":\"tree-sitter\",\"evidence_kind\":\"direct_call\",\"source_path\":\"src/Order.php\",\"snippet\":\"$gateway->charge($order)\"}`. Keep snippets short and exclude secrets.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:392:6. Assign `confidence` according to evidence: use `1.0` for a direct, unambiguous AST/LSP edge; lower it for aliases, framework conventions, or heuristic resolution. The schema only enforces the range `0.0..1.0`; these scoring levels are indexing guidance, not database rules.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:393:7. Persist the complete current outgoing edge set for refreshed source symbols transactionally, then verify counts and unresolved targets before marking the indexing run complete. Record extracted, inserted, and unresolved edge counts plus parser/extractor limitations in `indexing_runs.stats`.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:395:Use a consistent edge direction: the symbol that performs or declares the relationship is the source. Examples: caller → callee, importing file symbol → imported symbol, implementing class → interface, event publisher → event/listener target. `relation_type` is free text in the schema; use the documented lowercase vocabulary (`calls`, `references`, `imports`, `extends`, `implements`, `uses`, `dispatches`, `listens`, `publishes`, `subscribes`, `reads`, `writes`, `instantiates`) consistently. Add other values only when the extractor can explain their meaning.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:397:### Persist and refresh edges\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:407:    relation_type,\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:434:    target_qualified_name, relation_type, source_line, confidence, evidence\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:447:There is no unique constraint for an edge, so do not depend on `ON CONFLICT`. Before inserting a refreshed source symbol's full edge set, replace its outgoing rows in the same transaction:\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:456:-- Insert every currently extracted outgoing edge for this source symbol.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:461:When symbol IDs are replaced, the source and target foreign keys use `ON DELETE CASCADE`. Deleting a symbol therefore also removes incoming relations whose source is in another file. Capture affected target names before deletion and rebuild those incoming edges after the replacement symbols are inserted. A refresh that only recreates outgoing edges can leave the graph incomplete.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:465:Run checks scoped to the project. Compare relation counts with extracted edge counts and inspect unresolved targets rather than assuming every target should resolve locally.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:468:SELECT relation_type, COUNT(*) AS edge_count\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:471:GROUP BY relation_type\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:472:ORDER BY relation_type;\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:474:SELECT relation_type, target_qualified_name, COUNT(*) AS unresolved_count\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:478:GROUP BY relation_type, target_qualified_name\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:548:Use Serena/LSP, `rg`, /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep, and Tree-sitter to move from the runtime evidence back to the relevant source code.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:634:Never copy secrets, authorization tokens, session cookies, credentials, or other sensitive values into persistent project knowledge.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:701:10. Locate the owning source component/template with Serena/LSP, `rg`, /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep, or Tree-sitter.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:801:Tree-sitter and PostgreSQL remain useful for locating and understanding project structure and durable code intelligence. They are not substitutes for inspecting the rendered browser state.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:823:Do not automatically store transient visual observations in `knowledge_items`.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:825:Good candidates for durable knowledge include verified project conventions such as:\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:104:    relation_type TEXT NOT NULL,\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:129:    CONSTRAINT symbol_relations_type_not_blank CHECK (BTRIM(relation_type) <> ''),\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:160:CREATE TABLE aic_knowledge_items (\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:175:    CONSTRAINT knowledge_items_kind_not_blank CHECK (BTRIM(kind) <> ''),\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:176:    CONSTRAINT knowledge_items_title_not_blank CHECK (BTRIM(title) <> ''),\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:177:    CONSTRAINT knowledge_items_content_not_blank CHECK (BTRIM(content) <> ''),\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:178:    CONSTRAINT knowledge_items_status_valid CHECK (\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:181:    CONSTRAINT knowledge_items_confidence_valid CHECK (\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:184:    CONSTRAINT knowledge_items_validity_valid CHECK (\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:187:    CONSTRAINT knowledge_items_project_id_id_unique UNIQUE (project_id, id)\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:197:    knowledge_item_id UUID,\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:232:    CONSTRAINT chunks_knowledge_project_fk\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:233:        FOREIGN KEY (project_id, knowledge_item_id)\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:234:        REFERENCES aic_knowledge_items(project_id, id)\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:238:        source_type IN ('code', 'document', 'knowledge')\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:245:            AND knowledge_item_id IS NULL\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:253:            AND knowledge_item_id IS NULL\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:257:            source_type = 'knowledge'\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:261:            AND knowledge_item_id IS NOT NULL\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:287:        knowledge_item_id,\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:364:            'knowledge',\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:407:CREATE TRIGGER knowledge_items_set_updated_at\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:408:BEFORE UPDATE ON aic_knowledge_items\nDo not embed a serialized raw AST unless a specific experiment proves it useful.\n\nPreferred code chunk:\n\n```text\nLanguage: PHP\nFile: app/Services/PaymentService.php\nNamespace: App\\Services\nClass: PaymentService\nMethod: processPayment\nSignature: processPayment(Order $order): Payment\nLines: 34-81\n\n<source code>\n```\n\nRecommended chunk boundaries:\n\n```text\nclass/interface/trait/enum\nmethod/function\ncomponent\ntemplate block\nconfiguration object\nquery/migration unit\n```\n\nFor very large symbols, split only after preserving:\n\n- symbol identity\n- parent symbol\n- signature\n- file path\n- line range\n- overlap where necessary\n\nFor mixed-language files such as Vue, Blade, or Twig, preserve the outer document structure and use language injections/sub-parsers where available.\n\nFor JSONL/NDJSON, parse each non-empty line as a separate JSON record rather than treating the entire file as one JSON document.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 15. ast-grep rules\n\nUse ast-grep when syntax matters.\n\nGood cases:\n\n```text\nfind a specific method-call pattern\nfind unsafe API usage\nlocate a framework-specific code shape\nperform a repeated mechanical rewrite\nenforce a local AST rule\n```\n\nBefore a rewrite:\n\n1. run search-only mode\n2. inspect representative matches\n3. narrow the rule\n4. commit/stash current work if needed\n5. run rewrite\n6. inspect `git diff`\n7. run relevant tests/static analysis\n\nDo not use a broad codemod without checking its match set.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 16. ripgrep rules\n\nUse `rg` for exact strings and regex.\n\nExamples:\n\n```bash\nrg \"PaymentCompleted\"\nrg \"processPayment\\(\" app tests\nrg --glob '*.php' \"dispatch\\(\"\nrg --glob '!vendor/**' \"retry\"\n```\n\nRemember that ripgrep respects ignore rules by default. Hidden/ignored/generated content should not be searched unless there is a specific reason.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 17. Serena / LSP rules\n\nUse Serena/LSP for semantic navigation:\n\n```text\nfind symbol\ngo to definition\nfind references\nfind implementations\ninspect symbol body\nrename/refactor\ntype-aware navigation\n```\n\nPrefer Serena/LSP over text search when a precise symbol is already known.\n\nDo not assume LSP is complete for dynamic framework relationships. Laravel container bindings, events, queues, Blade/Twig/Vue conventions, callbacks, reflection, or runtime configuration may require PostgreSQL `aic_symbol_relations`, framework-specific heuristics, `rg`, and direct source inspection.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 18. Relational code graph rules\n\nThe PostgreSQL `aic_symbols` and `aic_symbol_relations` tables are the persistent structural graph for indexed projects.\n\nUse them for:\n\n```text\ncall paths\ncallers/callees\nstructural exploration\nimpact/blast radius\ndependency context\n```\n\nPopulate relations from Tree-sitter, Serena/LSP, framework-specific extractors, and other verified indexing logic. Store unresolved targets through `target_qualified_name`, and use `confidence` plus `evidence` for heuristic relationships.\n\nDo not treat a stored relation as stronger evidence than its source permits. For dynamic framework behavior, verify important paths against source code, Serena/LSP, `rg`, configuration, tests, and runtime/framework conventions.\n\n### Required relation collection workflow\n\nFor every file whose symbols are indexed, collect outgoing relations for each extracted source symbol when evidence is available. Do not silently skip the graph phase because semantic parsing is incomplete: use available AST/LSP evidence, record any supported framework-specific edges, and state which edge classes could not be resolved. Never invent edges just to make the graph look complete.\n\n1. Resolve the project by slug once and retain its `project_id`. Load the current, non-deleted file and symbol rows for that project.\n2. Parse each symbol body or declaration range. For each call, reference, import, inheritance/implementation, dispatch, event, read/write, or instantiation that is actually present, record the source symbol ID, target expression, relation type, and one-based source line.\n3. Resolve the target against `aic_symbols` in the same project, preferring exact qualified name and language-server identity. Never resolve a target by name across projects.\n4. If the target is indexed, store its ID in `target_symbol_id`. If it is external, dynamic, or not yet indexed, set `target_symbol_id` to `NULL` and preserve its stable qualified name in `target_qualified_name`.\n5. Attach evidence describing how the edge was found. A suggested JSON shape is `{\"extractor\":\"tree-sitter\",\"evidence_kind\":\"direct_call\",\"source_path\":\"src/Order.php\",\"snippet\":\"$gateway->charge($order)\"}`. Keep snippets short and exclude secrets.\n6. Assign `confidence` according to evidence: use `1.0` for a direct, unambiguous AST/LSP edge; lower it for aliases, framework conventions, or heuristic resolution. The schema only enforces the range `0.0..1.0`; these scoring levels are indexing guidance, not database rules.\n7. Persist the complete current outgoing edge set for refreshed source symbols transactionally, then verify counts and unresolved targets before marking the indexing run complete. Record extracted, inserted, and unresolved edge counts plus parser/extractor limitations in `indexing_runs.stats`.\n\nUse a consistent edge direction: the symbol that performs or declares the relationship is the source. Examples: caller → callee, importing file symbol → imported symbol, implementing class → interface, event publisher → event/listener target. `relation_type` is free text in the schema; use the documented lowercase vocabulary (`calls`, `references`, `imports`, `extends`, `implements`, `uses`, `dispatches`, `listens`, `publishes`, `subscribes`, `reads`, `writes`, `instantiates`) consistently. Add other values only when the extractor can explain their meaning.\n\n### Persist and refresh edges\n\nUse the real table name and explicit columns. Resolve symbol IDs before this insert; the composite foreign keys ensure both symbols belong to `:project_id`.\n\n```sql\nINSERT INTO aic_symbol_relations (\n    project_id,\n    source_symbol_id,\n    target_symbol_id,\n    target_qualified_name,\n    relation_type,\n    source_line,\n    confidence,\n    evidence\nimport { OpenAiEmbeddingProvider } from './providers/open-ai.js';\n\nconst args = process.argv.slice(2);\n\nconst apiKey = REDACTED;\nconst pathToEmbedFile = args[1];\nconst model = args[2];\nconst texts = args.slice(3);\n\nif (!apiKey || !pathToEmbedFile || !model || texts.length === 0) {\n    console.error(\n        'Usage: node /app/embedding.js <api-key> <embedding-project-name.json> <model> <text> [text...]',\n    );\n\n    process.exit(1);\n}\n\nconst embeddingProvider = new OpenAiEmbeddingProvider(\n    apiKey,\n    model,\n);\n\ntry {\n    const embeddings = await embeddingProvider.createBatch(texts);\n\n    embeddingProvider.saveEmbedding(pathToEmbedFile, embeddings);\n\n    console.log('See the file' + pathToEmbedFile);\n} catch (error) {\n    console.error(error instanceof Error ? error.message : error);\n\n    process.exit(1);\n}"},"started_at_ms":1791470826749,"completed_at_ms":1791470826749}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-08T14:47:06.797Z","ordinal":11853,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a0a02b-47ee-7913-b18b-d4d4b0be8b60","turn_id":"01a11bf9-7de2-72a3-b949-e54787d105e6","session_id":"01a0a02b-47ee-7913-b18b-d4d4b0be8b60","root_turn_id":"01a11bf9-7de2-72a3-b949-e54787d105e6","response_id":"resp_094cad5b47a7c03f016ac7acdd7d9c87d29b4a9dc7b697b93b","usage":{"input_tokens":60643,"cached_input_tokens":59136,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":1096,"reasoning_output_tokens":906,"total_tokens":61739},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":587815,"cached_input_tokens":528640,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":4434,"reasoning_output_tokens":1916,"total_tokens":592249},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":165700858,"cached_input_tokens":160979840,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":704653,"reasoning_output_tokens":372261,"total_tokens":166405511}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-08T14:47:06.797Z","ordinal":11854,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a11bfb-752d-7f60-8a1f-ea5de2c76f55","call_id":"call_8c6ef57ae9ee4a178e6c8b4505c3512e","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.1 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:16:| Syntax-aware chunk boundaries    | Tree-sitter                                       |\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:37:3. Parse a small representative file before processing the whole repository. Some Tree-sitter bindings can fail in batch traversal. If a fallback parser or line chunking is used, record the fallback and resulting limitations; do not label those chunks syntax-aware. Populate `aic_symbol_relations` for every edge class that was actually derived, and record any extraction gaps.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:38:4. Resolve the project by slug and scope every file, symbol, chunk, knowledge, embedding, and run query by `project_id`. Exclude credentials, environment files, generated/runtime content, and any source not approved for the shared database.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:47:The `office-easyfit` project uses `text-embedding-3-small` (1536 dimensions) and a project-specific HNSW index. The completed run contains 1,182 PHP files, 4,221 approximate symbol rows, 3,896 chunks/embeddings, four documents, and four knowledge items. Configuration, test, runtime, vendor/dependency, web asset/upload paths, and files matching the credential-like literal filter were excluded. Serena indexed 964 PHP files. The Tree-sitter Python binding segfaulted during batch node-field extraction, so code chunks used 80-line boundaries and symbol names used a declaration-line fallback; symbol relations were not populated. Use vector and symbol results as candidates and verify findings in the original source. The 325 embedding result JSON files were persisted under `app/embedding-results/`. This run did not produce a separate batch-to-chunk manifest; the database rows are complete, so do not regenerate them. Future runs should persist that mapping alongside each batch file.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:246:## 14. Tree-sitter indexing rules\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:248:Tree-sitter should primarily produce structural metadata and semantic chunk boundaries.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:379:Populate relations from Tree-sitter, Serena/LSP, framework-specific extractors, and other verified indexing logic. Store unresolved targets through `target_qualified_name`, and use `confidence` plus `evidence` for heuristic relationships.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:383:### Required relation collection workflow\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:385:For every file whose symbols are indexed, collect outgoing relations for each extracted source symbol when evidence is available. Do not silently skip the graph phase because semantic parsing is incomplete: use available AST/LSP evidence, record any supported framework-specific edges, and state which edge classes could not be resolved. Never invent edges just to make the graph look complete.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:391:5. Attach evidence describing how the edge was found. A suggested JSON shape is `{\"extractor\":\"tree-sitter\",\"evidence_kind\":\"direct_call\",\"source_path\":\"src/Order.php\",\"snippet\":\"$gateway->charge($order)\"}`. Keep snippets short and exclude secrets.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:392:6. Assign `confidence` according to evidence: use `1.0` for a direct, unambiguous AST/LSP edge; lower it for aliases, framework conventions, or heuristic resolution. The schema only enforces the range `0.0..1.0`; these scoring levels are indexing guidance, not database rules.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:393:7. Persist the complete current outgoing edge set for refreshed source symbols transactionally, then verify counts and unresolved targets before marking the indexing run complete. Record extracted, inserted, and unresolved edge counts plus parser/extractor limitations in `indexing_runs.stats`.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:395:Use a consistent edge direction: the symbol that performs or declares the relationship is the source. Examples: caller → callee, importing file symbol → imported symbol, implementing class → interface, event publisher → event/listener target. `relation_type` is free text in the schema; use the documented lowercase vocabulary (`calls`, `references`, `imports`, `extends`, `implements`, `uses`, `dispatches`, `listens`, `publishes`, `subscribes`, `reads`, `writes`, `instantiates`) consistently. Add other values only when the extractor can explain their meaning.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:397:### Persist and refresh edges\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:407:    relation_type,\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:434:    target_qualified_name, relation_type, source_line, confidence, evidence\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:447:There is no unique constraint for an edge, so do not depend on `ON CONFLICT`. Before inserting a refreshed source symbol's full edge set, replace its outgoing rows in the same transaction:\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:456:-- Insert every currently extracted outgoing edge for this source symbol.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:461:When symbol IDs are replaced, the source and target foreign keys use `ON DELETE CASCADE`. Deleting a symbol therefore also removes incoming relations whose source is in another file. Capture affected target names before deletion and rebuild those incoming edges after the replacement symbols are inserted. A refresh that only recreates outgoing edges can leave the graph incomplete.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:465:Run checks scoped to the project. Compare relation counts with extracted edge counts and inspect unresolved targets rather than assuming every target should resolve locally.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:468:SELECT relation_type, COUNT(*) AS edge_count\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:471:GROUP BY relation_type\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:472:ORDER BY relation_type;\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:474:SELECT relation_type, target_qualified_name, COUNT(*) AS unresolved_count\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:478:GROUP BY relation_type, target_qualified_name\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:548:Use Serena/LSP, `rg`, /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep, and Tree-sitter to move from the runtime evidence back to the relevant source code.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:634:Never copy secrets, authorization tokens, session cookies, credentials, or other sensitive values into persistent project knowledge.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:701:10. Locate the owning source component/template with Serena/LSP, `rg`, /home/kamaz/.venv/bin/ast-grep, or Tree-sitter.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:801:Tree-sitter and PostgreSQL remain useful for locating and understanding project structure and durable code intelligence. They are not substitutes for inspecting the rendered browser state.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:823:Do not automatically store transient visual observations in `knowledge_items`.\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docs/retrieval-and-indexing.md:825:Good candidates for durable knowledge include verified project conventions such as:\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:104:    relation_type TEXT NOT NULL,\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:129:    CONSTRAINT symbol_relations_type_not_blank CHECK (BTRIM(relation_type) <> ''),\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:160:CREATE TABLE aic_knowledge_items (\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:175:    CONSTRAINT knowledge_items_kind_not_blank CHECK (BTRIM(kind) <> ''),\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:176:    CONSTRAINT knowledge_items_title_not_blank CHECK (BTRIM(title) <> ''),\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:177:    CONSTRAINT knowledge_items_content_not_blank CHECK (BTRIM(content) <> ''),\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:178:    CONSTRAINT knowledge_items_status_valid CHECK (\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:181:    CONSTRAINT knowledge_items_confidence_valid CHECK (\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:184:    CONSTRAINT knowledge_items_validity_valid CHECK (\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:187:    CONSTRAINT knowledge_items_project_id_id_unique UNIQUE (project_id, id)\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:197:    knowledge_item_id UUID,\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:232:    CONSTRAINT chunks_knowledge_project_fk\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:233:        FOREIGN KEY (project_id, knowledge_item_id)\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:234:        REFERENCES aic_knowledge_items(project_id, id)\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:238:        source_type IN ('code', 'document', 'knowledge')\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:245:            AND knowledge_item_id IS NULL\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:253:            AND knowledge_item_id IS NULL\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:257:            source_type = 'knowledge'\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:261:            AND knowledge_item_id IS NOT NULL\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:287:        knowledge_item_id,\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:364:            'knowledge',\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:407:CREATE TRIGGER knowledge_items_set_updated_at\n/home/kamaz/www/hobby/ai-code-intelligence/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/002_schema.sql:408:BEFORE UPDATE ON aic_knowledge_items\nDo not embed a serialized raw AST unless a specific experiment proves it useful.\n\nPreferred code chunk:\n\n```text\nLanguage: PHP\nFile: app/Services/PaymentService.php\nNamespace: App\\Services\nClass: PaymentService\nMethod: processPayment\nSignature: processPayment(Order $order): Payment\nLines: 34-81\n\n<source code>\n```\n\nRecommended chunk boundaries:\n\n```text\nclass/interface/trait/enum\nmethod/function\ncomponent\ntemplate block\nconfiguration object\nquery/migration unit\n```\n\nFor very large symbols, split only after preserving:\n\n- symbol identity\n- parent symbol\n- signature\n- file path\n- line range\n- overlap where necessary\n\nFor mixed-language files such as Vue, Blade, or Twig, preserve the outer document structure and use language injections/sub-parsers where available.\n\nFor JSONL/NDJSON, parse each non-empty line as a separate JSON record rather than treating the entire file as one JSON document.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 15. ast-grep rules\n\nUse ast-grep when syntax matters.\n\nGood cases:\n\n```text\nfind a specific method-call pattern\nfind unsafe API usage\nlocate a framework-specific code shape\nperform a repeated mechanical rewrite\nenforce a local AST rule\n```\n\nBefore a rewrite:\n\n1. run search-only mode\n2. inspect representative matches\n3. narrow the rule\n4. commit/stash current work if needed\n5. run rewrite\n6. inspect `git diff`\n7. run relevant tests/static analysis\n\nDo not use a broad codemod without checking its match set.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 16. ripgrep rules\n\nUse `rg` for exact strings and regex.\n\nExamples:\n\n```bash\nrg \"PaymentCompleted\"\nrg \"processPayment\\(\" app tests\nrg --glob '*.php' \"dispatch\\(\"\nrg --glob '!vendor/**' \"retry\"\n```\n\nRemember that ripgrep respects ignore rules by default. Hidden/ignored/generated content should not be searched unless there is a specific reason.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 17. Serena / LSP rules\n\nUse Serena/LSP for semantic navigation:\n\n```text\nfind symbol\ngo to definition\nfind references\nfind implementations\ninspect symbol body\nrename/refactor\ntype-aware navigation\n```\n\nPrefer Serena/LSP over text search when a precise symbol is already known.\n\nDo not assume LSP is complete for dynamic framework relationships. Laravel container bindings, events, queues, Blade/Twig/Vue conventions, callbacks, reflection, or runtime configuration may require PostgreSQL `aic_symbol_relations`, framework-specific heuristics, `rg`, and direct source inspection.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 18. Relational code graph rules\n\nThe PostgreSQL `aic_symbols` and `aic_symbol_relations` tables are the persistent structural graph for indexed projects.\n\nUse them for:\n\n```text\ncall paths\ncallers/callees\nstructural exploration\nimpact/blast radius\ndependency context\n```\n\nPopulate relations from Tree-sitter, Serena/LSP, framework-specific extractors, and other verified indexing logic. Store unresolved targets through `target_qualified_name`, and use `confidence` plus `evidence` for heuristic relationships.\n\nDo not treat a stored relation as stronger evidence than its source permits. For dynamic framework behavior, verify important paths against source code, Serena/LSP, `rg`, configuration, tests, and runtime/framework conventions.\n\n### Required relation collection workflow\n\nFor every file whose symbols are indexed, collect outgoing relations for each extracted source symbol when evidence is available. Do not silently skip the graph phase because semantic parsing is incomplete: use available AST/LSP evidence, record any supported framework-specific edges, and state which edge classes could not be resolved. Never invent edges just to make the graph look complete.\n\n1. Resolve the project by slug once and retain its `project_id`. Load the current, non-deleted file and symbol rows for that project.\n2. Parse each symbol body or declaration range. For each call, reference, import, inheritance/implementation, dispatch, event, read/write, or instantiation that is actually present, record the source symbol ID, target expression, relation type, and one-based source line.\n3. Resolve the target against `aic_symbols` in the same project, preferring exact qualified name and language-server identity. Never resolve a target by name across projects.\n4. If the target is indexed, store its ID in `target_symbol_id`. If it is external, dynamic, or not yet indexed, set `target_symbol_id` to `NULL` and preserve its stable qualified name in `target_qualified_name`.\n5. Attach evidence describing how the edge was found. A suggested JSON shape is `{\"extractor\":\"tree-sitter\",\"evidence_kind\":\"direct_call\",\"source_path\":\"src/Order.php\",\"snippet\":\"$gateway->charge($order)\"}`. Keep snippets short and exclude secrets.\n6. Assign `confidence` according to evidence: use `1.0` for a direct, unambiguous AST/LSP edge; lower it for aliases, framework conventions, or heuristic resolution. The schema only enforces the range `0.0..1.0`; these scoring levels are indexing guidance, not database rules.\n7. Persist the complete current outgoing edge set for refreshed source symbols transactionally, then verify counts and unresolved targets before marking the indexing run complete. Record extracted, inserted, and unresolved edge counts plus parser/extractor limitations in `indexing_runs.stats`.\n\nUse a consistent edge direction: the symbol that performs or declares the relationship is the source. Examples: caller → callee, importing file symbol → imported symbol, implementing class → interface, event publisher → event/listener target. `relation_type` is free text in the schema; use the documented lowercase vocabulary (`calls`, `references`, `imports`, `extends`, `implements`, `uses`, `dispatches`, `listens`, `publishes`, `subscribes`, `reads`, `writes`, `instantiates`) consistently. Add other values only when the extractor can explain their meaning.\n\n### Persist and refresh edges\n\nUse the real table name and explicit columns. Resolve symbol IDs before this insert; the composite foreign keys ensure both symbols belong to `:project_id`.\n\n```sql\nINSERT INTO aic_symbol_relations (\n    project_id,\n    source_symbol_id,\n    target_symbol_id,\n    target_qualified_name,\n    relation_type,\n    source_line,\n    confidence,\n    evidence\nimport { OpenAiEmbeddingProvider } from './providers/open-ai.js';\n\nconst args = process.argv.slice(2);\n\nconst apiKey = REDACTED;\nconst pathToEmbedFile = args[1];\nconst model = args[2];\nconst texts = args.slice(3);\n\nif (!apiKey || !pathToEmbedFile || !model || texts.length === 0) {\n    console.error(\n        'Usage: node /app/embedding.js <api-key> <embedding-project-name.json> <model> <text> [text...]',\n    );\n\n    process.exit(1);\n}\n\nconst embeddingProvider = new OpenAiEmbeddingProvider(\n    apiKey,\n    model,\n);\n\ntry {\n    const embeddings = await embeddingProvider.createBatch(texts);\n\n    embeddingProvider.saveEmbedding(pathToEmbedFile, embeddings);\n\n    console.log('See the file' + pathToEmbedFile);\n} catch (error) {\n    console.error(error instanceof Error ? error.message : error);\n\n    process.exit(1);\n}"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a11bf9-7de2-72a3-b949-e54787d105e6","create_time":1791470826.7978973}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
