targeted document artifacts inside the container, with a strict render-and-verify workflow. Use `render_docx.py` to generate page PNGs (and optional PDF) for  (file: r8/documents/26.1025.11240/skills/documents/SKILL.md)\n- figma-implement-design: Translates Figma designs into production-ready application code with 1:1 visual fidelity. Use when implementing UI code from Figma files, when user mentions \"implement design\", \"generate code\", \"implement component\", provides  (file: r0/figma-implement-design/SKILL.md)\n- find-docs: Retrieves up-to-date documentation, API references, and code examples for any developer technology. Use this skill whenever the user asks about a specific library, framework, SDK, CLI tool, or cloud service — even for well-kno (file: r1/find-docs/SKILL.md)\n- find-skills: Helps users discover and install agent skills when they ask questions like \"how do I do X\", \"find a skill for X\", \"is there a skill that can...\", or express interest in extending capabilities. This skill should be used when the  (file: r1/find-skills/SKILL.md)\n- google-drive:google-docs: Create and edit native Google Docs through connected tools. (file: r4/google-docs/SKILL.md)\n- google-drive:google-drive: Use connected Google Drive as the single entrypoint for Drive, Docs, Sheets, and Slides work. Use when the user wants to find, fetch, organize, share, export, copy, or delete Drive files, or summarize and edit Google Docs, Google (file: r4/google-drive/SKILL.md)\n- google-drive:google-drive-comments: Write, reply to, and resolve Google Drive comments on Docs, Sheets, Slides, and Drive files with evidence-backed location context. Use when the user asks to leave comments, review a file with comments, respond to comment thread (file: r4/google-drive-comments/SKILL.md)\n- google-drive:google-sheets: Analyze and edit connected Google Sheets with range precision. Use when the user wants to create Google Sheets, find a spreadsheet, inspect tabs or ranges, search rows, plan formulas, create or repair charts, clean or restructu (file: r4/google-sheets/SKILL.md)\n- google-drive:google-slides: Route Google Slides authoring requests and derive a design system from a native template or reference deck. Use this skill when the user provides an existing native Google Slides deck as a template, reference, or prior-period s (file: r4/google-slides/SKILL.md)\n- graphify: Use for any question about a codebase, its architecture, file relationships, or project content — especially when graphify-out/ exists, where the question should be treated as a graphify query first. Turns any input (code, docs, (file: r1/graphify/SKILL.md)\n- local-mem0: Retrieves and preserves project-tagged knowledge through the local-mem0 CLI. Use when searching prior decisions, saving reusable lessons, or diagnosing local memory access. (file: r1/local-mem0/SKILL.md)\n- openspec-apply-change: Implement tasks from an OpenSpec change. Use when the user wants to start implementing, continue implementation, or work through tasks. Also use when the user says \"openspec apply\", \"opsx apply\", or \"openspec implement\". (file: r1/openspec-apply-change/SKILL.md)\n- openspec-archive-change: Archive a completed OpenSpec change in the experimental workflow. Use when the user wants to finalize and archive a change after implementation is complete. Also use when the user says \"openspec archive\" or \"opsx archive\". (file: r1/openspec-archive-change/SKILL.md)\n- openspec-explore: Enter OpenSpec explore mode - a thinking partner for exploring ideas, investigating problems, and clarifying requirements in a project that uses OpenSpec. Use when the user wants to think through something before or during an  (file: r1/openspec-explore/SKILL.md)\n- openspec-propose: Propose a new OpenSpec change with all artifacts generated in one step. Use when the user wants to quickly describe what they want to build and get a complete proposal with design, specs, and tasks ready for implementation. Al (file: r1/openspec-propose/SKILL.md)\n- openspec-sync-specs: Sync delta specs from an OpenSpec change to main specs. Use when the user wants to update main specs with changes from a delta spec, without archiving the change. Also use when the user says \"openspec sync\" or \"opsx sync\". (file: r1/openspec-sync-specs/SKILL.md)\n- openspec-update-change: Update an OpenSpec change by revising its existing planning artifacts and keeping them coherent with one another. Use when the user wants to revise a change's plan, fold new decisions into it, or reconcile its artifacts after  (file: r1/openspec-update-change/SKILL.md)\n- pdf:pdf: Read, create, inspect, render, and verify PDF files where visual layout matters, including fillable AcroForms. Use Poppler rendering plus Python tools such as reportlab, pdfplumber, and pypdf for generation and extraction. (file: r8/pdf/26.1025.11240/skills/pdf/SKILL.md)\n- plugin-management:plugin-management: Discover and suggest relevant plugins, inspect app permissions and dependencies, and manage plugin connections or removal. Use when the user asks about plugins or when a task would materially benefit from an external app, accou (file: r5/plugin-management/0.1.0/skills/plugin-management/SKILL.md)\n- presentations:Presentations: Read, create or edit PowerPoint or Google Slides decks. Use for presentation, slide deck, PowerPoint, PPT, PPTX, or Google Slides requests. (file: r8/presentations/26.1025.11240/skills/presentations/SKILL.md)\n- screenshot: Use when the user explicitly asks for a desktop or system screenshot (full screen, specific app or window, or a pixel region), or when tool-specific capture capabilities are unavailable and an OS-level capture is needed. (file: r0/screenshot/SKILL.md)\n- security-best-practices: Perform language and framework specific security best-practice reviews and suggest improvements. Trigger only when the user explicitly requests security best practices guidance, a security review/report, or secure-by-default cod (file: r0/security-best-practices/SKILL.md)\n- sites:sites: Use Sites when the user wants a website built for them (including a landing page, portfolio, dashboard, portal, tracker, hub, game, event registration, or tool), requests changes to a website already built with Sites, wants to (file: r6/sites/SKILL.md)\n- sites:sites-remix: Remix a Site built with the Sites plugin into a new Site. Use when the user has identified the source Site and asks to remix it. (file: r6/sites-remix/SKILL.md)\n- spreadsheets:Spreadsheets: Use skill when user requests to create, modify, analyze, visualize, or work with spreadsheet files (`.xlsx`, `.xls`, `.csv`, `.tsv`) or Google Sheets with formulas, formatting, charts, tables, and recalculation. Do not use for li (file: r9/spreadsheets/SKILL.md)\n- spreadsheets:excel-live-control: Control an open or active Microsoft Excel workbook through the ChatGPT add-in or connected session. Use when the user tags the Microsoft Excel app in Codex or follows up on an established live Excel task. Do not use for standalon (file: r9/excel-live-control/SKILL.md)\n- template-creator:template-creator: Create or update a reusable personal Codex artifact-template skill. Use when the user invokes $template-creator or asks in natural language to create a reusable template from a reference document, presentation, spreadsheet, Goo (file: r8/template-creator/26.1025.11240/skills/template-creator/SKILL.md)\n- visualize:visualize: Create visualizations and interactive tools directly in conversation. Proactively use to show how something works; explore 'what happens when', 'what changes', or 'help me understand'; compare or inspect; create simulations, m (file: r3/visualize/1.0.55/skills/visualize/SKILL.md)\n- work-pets:create-pet: Create, repair, validate, preview, upload, or activate an animated v2 pet in ChatGPT Work mode from a character idea, brand cue, or reference image. Use for new ChatGPT Pets, custom mascots, non-pixel styles, and unattached spri (file: r7/create-pet/SKILL.md)\n- work-pets:pets: List, inspect, select, download, or delete animated pets in ChatGPT Work mode. Use for an explicit pet in ChatGPT or established ChatGPT Pets context. Exclude real-world and other-app pets; clarify before invoking. (file: r7/pets/SKILL.md)\n- work-pets:update-pet: Inspect, validate, preview, repair, or update a custom pet in ChatGPT Work mode, including its name, description, or sprite sheet. Use for a pet in ChatGPT or established ChatGPT Pets context. Exclude real-world and other-app pe (file: r7/update-pet/SKILL.md)\n</skills_instructions>"},{"type":"input_text","text":"<permissions instructions>\nFilesystem sandboxing defines which files can be read or written. `sandbox_mode` is `workspace-write`: The sandbox permits reading files, and editing files in `cwd` and `writable_roots`. Editing files in other directories requires approval. Network access is restricted.\n# Escalation Requests\n\nCommands are run outside the sandbox if they are approved by the user, or match an existing rule that allows it to run unrestricted. The command string is split into independent command segments at shell control operators, including but not limited to:\n\n- Pipes: |\n- Logical operators: &&, ||\n- Command separators: ;\n- Subshell boundaries: (...), $(...)\n\nEach resulting segment is evaluated independently for sandbox restrictions and approval requirements.\n\nExample:\n\ngit pull | tee output.txt\n\nThis is treated as two command segments:\n\n[\"git\", \"pull\"]\n\n[\"tee\", \"output.txt\"]\n\nCommands that use more advanced shell features like redirection (>, >>, <), substitutions ($(...), ...), environment variables (FOO=bar), or wildcard patterns (*, ?) will not be evaluated against rules, to limit the scope of what an approved rule allows.\n\n## How to request escalation\n\nIMPORTANT: To request approval to execute a command that will require escalated privileges:\n\n- Provide the `sandbox_permissions` parameter with the value `\"require_escalated\"`\n- Include a short question asking the user if they want to allow the action in `justification` parameter. e.g. \"Do you want to download and install dependencies for this project?\"\n- Optionally suggest a `prefix_rule` - this will be shown to the user with an option to persist the rule approval for future sessions.\n\nIf you run a command that is important to solving the user's query, but it fails because of sandboxing or with a likely sandbox-related network error (for example DNS/host resolution, registry/index access, or dependency download failure), rerun the command with \"require_escalated\". ALWAYS proceed to use the `justification` parameter - do not message the user before requesting approval for the command.\n\n## When to request escalation\n\nWhile commands are running inside the sandbox, here are some scenarios that will require escalation outside the sandbox:\n\n- You need to run a command that writes to a directory that requires it (e.g. running tests that write to /var)\n- You need to run a GUI app (e.g., open/xdg-open/osascript) to open browsers or files.\n- If you run a command that is important to solving the user's query, but it fails because of sandboxing or with a likely sandbox-related network error (for example DNS/host resolution, registry/index access, or dependency download failure), rerun the command with `require_escalated`. ALWAYS proceed to use the `sandbox_permissions` and `justification` parameters. do not message the user before requesting approval for the command.\n- You are about to take a potentially destructive action such as an `rm` or `git reset` that the user did not explicitly ask for.\n- Be judicious with escalating, but if completing the user's request requires it, you should do so - don't try and circumvent approvals by using other tools.\n\n## prefix_rule guidance\n\nWhen choosing a `prefix_rule`, request one that will allow you to fulfill similar requests from the user in the future without re-requesting escalation. It should be categorical and reasonably scoped to similar capabilities. You should rarely pass the entire command into `prefix_rule`.\n\n### Banned prefix_rules \nAvoid requesting overly broad prefixes that the user would be ill-advised to approve. For example, do not request [\"python3\"], [\"python\", \"-\"], or other similar prefixes that would allow arbitrary scripting.\nNEVER provide a prefix_rule argument for destructive commands like rm.\nNEVER provide a prefix_rule if your command uses a heredoc or herestring. \n\n### Examples\nGood examples of prefixes:\n- [\"npm\", \"run\", \"dev\"]\n- [\"gh\", \"pr\", \"check\"]\n- [\"cargo\", \"test\"]\n\n\n## Approved command prefixes\nThe following prefix rules have already been approved: - [\"pwd\"]\n- [\"sort\"]\n- [\"true\"]\n- [\"rsync\"]\n- [\"/home/kamaz/.venv/bin/python\"]\n- [\"/home/kamaz/.local/share/uv/python/cpython-3.13.16-linux-x86_64-gnu/bin/python\"]\n- [\"git\", \"-C\"]\n- [\"ls\", \"-la\"]\n- [\"bd\", \"show\"]\n- [\"curl\", \"-I\"]\n- [\"git\", \"add\"]\n- [\"head\", \"-1\"]\n- [\"sleep\", \"8\"]\n- [\"bd\", \"prime\"]\n- [\"bd\", \"where\"]\n- [\"git\", \"push\"]\n- [\"head\", \"-12\"]\n- [\"head\", \"-20\"]\n- [\"head\", \"-30\"]\n- [\"head\", \"-35\"]\n- [\"head\", \"-40\"]\n- [\"head\", \"-80\"]\n- [\"make\", \"e2e\"]\n- [\"mkdir\", \"-p\"]\n- [\"npm\", \"test\"]\n- [\"sleep\", \"10\"]\n- [\"sleep\", \"12\"]\n- [\"sleep\", \"15\"]\n- [\"sleep\", \"20\"]\n- [\"sleep\", \"35\"]\n- [\"tail\", \"-30\"]\n- [\"tail\", \"-35\"]\n- [\"tail\", \"-40\"]\n- [\"tail\", \"-45\"]\n- [\"tail\", \"-50\"]\n- [\"docker\", \"cp\"]\n- [\"docker\", \"ps\"]\n- [\"git\", \"apply\"]\n- [\"head\", \"-120\"]\n- [\"docker\", \"run\"]\n- [\"git\", \"commit\"]\n- [\"npx\", \"skills\"]\n- [\"rg\", \"^boost:\"]\n- [\"docker\", \"exec\"]\n- [\"docker\", \"logs\"]\n- [\"docker\", \"stop\"]\n- [\"git\", \"restore\"]\n- [\"make\", \"mob-ci\"]\n- [\"make\", \"up-dev\"]\n- [\"npm\", \"install\"]\n- [\"npx\", \"vue-tsc\"]\n- [\"sed\", \"s/=.*//\"]\n- [\"bwrap\", \"--help\"]\n- [\"docker\", \"build\"]\n- [\"docker\", \"start\"]\n- [\"printf\", \"php\\\\n\"]\n- [\"rg\", \"^tinker\\\\s\"]\n- [\"sed\", \"s#^\\\\./##\"]\n- [\"make\", \"mob-down\"]\n- [\"printf\", \"n\\\\ny\\\\n\"]\n- [\"rg\", \"\\\\.env|vite\"]\n- [\"docker\", \"compose\"]\n- [\"docker\", \"inspect\"]\n- [\"make\", \"build-dev\"]\n- [\"make\", \"mob-build\"]\n- [\"rg\", \"mate/\\\\.env$\"]\n- [\"bwrap\", \"--version\"]\n- [\"cd\", \"app-code/app\"]\n- [\"make\", \"mob-gradle\"]\n- [\"make\", \"vite-build\"]\n- [\"npx\", \"ctx7@latest\"]\n- [\"composer\", \"phpstan\"]\n- [\"make\", \"mob-wrapper\"]\n- [\"make\", \"rebuild-dev\"]\n- [\"printf\", \"n\\\\nn\\\\ny\\\\n\"]\n- [\"rg\", \"^boost|boost:\"]\n- [\"local-mem0\", \"--help\"]\n- [\"local-mem0\", \"search\"]\n- [\"ls\", \"db-sync-worker\"]\n- [\"rg\", \"^TRAEFIK_HOST=\"]\n- [\"make\", \"backend-check\"]\n- [\"make\", \"frontend-lint\"]\n- [\"make\", \"backend-format\"]\n- [\"make\", \"backend-tooling\"]\n- [\"rg\", \"^DB_WRITE_JOURNAL_\"]\n- [\"make\", \"backend-typecheck\"]\n- [\"make\", \"mob-phone-devices\"]\n- [\"make\", \"mob-ui-test-smoke\"]\n- [\"make\", \"frontend-api-types\"]\n- [\"make\", \"mob-assemble-debug\"]\n- [\"printf\", \"\\\\n====FILE====\\\\n\"]\n- [\"printf\", \"\\\\nSTAGED FILES\\\\n\"]\n- [\"rg\", \"^portfolio-e2e-data$\"]\n- [\"cat\", \"openspec/config.yaml\"]\n- [\"printf\", \"\\\\n====PSALM====\\\\n\"]\n- [\"rg\", \"(^|/)(\\\\.env|.*\\\\.env$)\"]\n- [\"printf\", \"\\\\n--- BRANCH ---\\\\n\"]\n- [\"printf\", \"n\\\\ny\\\\nn\\\\nn\\\\nn\\\\ny\\\\n\"]\n- [\"printf\", \"y\\\\nn\\\\nn\\\\nn\\\\nn\\\\ny\\\\n\"]\n- [\"make\", \"frontend-format-check\"]...\n[Some commands were truncated]\n The writable roots are `/home/kamaz/www/strateg-projects/strateg-export-products`, `/tmp`.\n</permissions instructions>"},{"type":"input_text","text":"<collaboration_mode># Collaboration Mode: Default\n\nYou are now in Default mode. Any previous instructions for other modes (e.g. Plan mode) are no longer active.\n\nYour active mode changes only when new developer instructions with a different `<collaboration_mode>...</collaboration_mode>` change it; user requests or tool descriptions do not change mode by themselves. Known mode names are Default and Plan.\n\n## request_user_input availability\n\nUse the `request_user_input` tool only when it is listed in the available tools for this turn.\n\nIn Default mode, strongly prefer making reasonable assumptions and executing the user's request rather than stopping to ask questions.\n\nUse the `request_user_input` tool only for optional questions where the answer would materially improve the quality of the work.\n\nIf `request_user_input` returns no answers, continue with best judgment instead of asking again or treating the turn as blocked.\n\nNever use the `request_user_input` tool for permission requests or permission-related escalations.\n</collaboration_mode>"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a17dfb-d4fa-7642-9c18-64cf77325bf8","create_time":1793115018.8599017,"content_item_kinds":["host_skills.instructions","permissions.instructions","collaboration_mode.instructions"],"content_item_metadata":[{"provenance":{"type":"skills"}},{"provenance":{"type":"harness","hook":false}},{"provenance":{"type":"harness","hook":false}}]}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-27T15:30:18.860Z","ordinal":12368,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","id":"msg_01a17dfb-d66b-7113-b92e-731eb9e94130","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"# AGENTS.md instructions for /home/kamaz/www/strateg-projects/strateg-export-products\n\n<INSTRUCTIONS>\n# Project instructions for AI agents\n\nThis is the canonical project-wide instruction file. `CLAUDE.md` points here; do not duplicate shared rules in other tool instruction files.\n\n## Start here\n\nBegin every work session by reading [<current project>/docs/setup/README.md](<current project>/docs/setup/README.md), then apply these rules. Before starting assigned work, every subagent must also read the [AI Agent Collaboration guide](~/.agents/docs/ai-agent-collaboration.md) and this file. The primary agent must include those readings in each subagent brief. The application name is **<current project name>**; use exactly `<current project name>:` for shared Local Mem0 records. All documentation, task decomposition, plans, ADRs, and skill instructions must be in English.\n\n## Required work sequence: Beads → Context7 → Mem0\n\nProcess these stages in order before engineering or documentation changes. State explicitly when a stage is not applicable. Complete the setup guide's tool health gate when onboarding or when context or tool health is stale. Installation output alone is not an operational check.\n\n## Application Problem Diagnosis\n\nWhen diagnosing an application error, unexpected behavior, or a reported failure, inspect and analyze the relevant application logs before proposing a root cause or implementing a fix. Do not assume logs are unavailable, stale, incomplete, or unreliable without checking.\n\n- Locate the logs for the correct local development application and runtime using the documented setup, application configuration, and container or service paths. Check relevant time windows, rotated files, and the request or record context when available.\n- Read the matching exception or error and its stack trace, then correlate it with the request, database effects, and current source. Treat logs as evidence to assess against those sources; do not dismiss them by assumption.\n- Keep log inspection targeted. Do not expose or copy credentials, customer data, full request payloads, or unrelated personal information into reports.\n- If relevant logs cannot be accessed or do not cover the failure, report the paths and commands checked, the exact sanitized error or access limitation, and what remains unverified. Separate source-based hypotheses from confirmed runtime findings, and ask for the access or evidence needed before making a consequential diagnosis.\n\n\n### 1. Beads — establish and decompose the work\n\n- **Project boundary — absolute:** Beads may be used only for the project currently assigned in this repository. Before any Beads operation, confirm the repository root and run `bd where`; verify the selected workspace and project prefix belong to this exact project. For <current project name>, the workspace must be this repository's `.beads` workspace with the `<current project name>` prefix. Never read, search, list, create, update, close, export, import, sync, diagnose, or otherwise handle another project's Beads data. For example, while working on Project 1, accessing Project 3's Beads workspace is strictly forbidden, even for a read-only lookup or because an issue ID appears relevant. If the workspace identity is wrong or uncertain, stop immediately; do not inspect or mutate that workspace. Resolve the current repository's configuration or report the blocker.\n- After verifying the project workspace, run `bd prime` when the workflow context is missing or stale. Find matching work with `bd ready`, `bd list`, or `bd show` in this same workspace; claim it before implementation or documentation work. Create an issue first when no match exists.\n- Decompose work in English and store every actionable task, its description, acceptance criteria, dependencies, blockers, and progress in Beads. Use child issues for separately assignable work.\n- Do not create or maintain task-decomposition or task-tracking documents under `<current project>/docs/tasks/`. Beads is the sole source of truth for task content and status. Existing files under `<current project>/docs/tasks/` are retained as historical references; do not treat them as current task records.\n- Link relevant OpenSpec artifacts from the Beads issue description, design, or notes. Keep OpenSpec's native workspace at the repository root; do not use its checklist as a substitute for Beads issues that represent actionable work.\n- Diagnose workspace failure with `bd where` only in the current repository context; report the exact sanitized error without accessing another project's workspace. Do not claim creation or updates when the CLI failed.\n\n### 2. Context7 — verify external documentation (if it needs)\n\n- For external library, framework, SDK, API, CLI, or cloud-service behavior, fetch current documentation before implementation or version-sensitive guidance, even for familiar technology.\n- Run the Context7 CLI from the host, not a Docker container. This is an exception to the containerized application-tooling rule.\n- Resolve the official library name first, select the exact relevant `/org/project` ID (versioned when available and applicable), then fetch one specific topic:\n\n  ```bash\n  npx ctx7@latest library \"<official library name>\" \"<specific question>\"\n  npx ctx7@latest docs <library-id> \"<one specific topic>\"\n  ```\n\n- Keep queries free of credentials and secrets. This stage is not applicable to general programming, internal business logic, or refactoring when external API behavior is not in question.\n- On DNS/network restrictions, request an approved retry outside the sandbox. For authentication/quota failures, propose `npx ctx7@latest login` or secure `CONTEXT7_API_KEY` configuration. Never label guessed behavior documentation-backed.\n\n### 3. Mem0 — retrieve reusable project knowledge\n\n- After Beads and Context7, use the [local-mem0 skill](~/.agents/skills/local-mem0/SKILL.md). Run its CLI from the host, never from a Docker container, because its service is reached through host loopback. Search using only relevant topic text; do not prepend the current project name. Decide from the task and returned records whether checking for the current project's exact name or prefix is needed. For project-specific decisions, or when results appear to mix projects, accept only records whose memory text starts with the exact `<current project name>:` prefix. For general guidance, project-name filtering may be unnecessary. Semantic similarity is not proof of project ownership.\n- Check relevant memories against the user request, current sources, and external documentation. Memories never override those sources.\n- Save only stable reusable lessons, each prefixed `<current project name>: `. Use `add --no-infer` and read back the returned ID with `get` to verify the prefix. Do not store secrets, transient status, duplicate canonical documentation, or other projects' details.\n\n### Graphify execution location\n\n- Run all Graphify CLI commands directly on the host, including `graphify query`, `graphify path`, `graphify explain`, and `graphify update .`. This is an explicit workflow-tool exception to the containerized application-tooling rule.\n- Do not run Graphify from Docker containers or wrap it in `docker exec`, `docker compose exec`, or `docker compose run`. Use the host CLI and the repository's `graphify-out/` graph.\n\n## Mandatory tool failures\n\nCheck Beads, Graphify, Local Mem0, OpenSpec, and Context7 as described in the setup guide. If any required tool fails, stop implementation, investigate through read-only diagnostics, capture the command and sanitized error, record a blocker in Beads when possible, and propose specific solutions. Do not silently bypass failures. Resume after resolution or explicit user authorization for a revised workflow. An approved successful sandbox retry resolves the restriction.\n\n## Documentation\n\nRead [<current project>/docs/DOCUMENTS.md](<current project>/docs/DOCUMENTS.md) for ownership and artifact conventions. Keep human-authored documentation and all AI Factory project artifacts under `<current project>/docs/`. Tool configuration stays discoverable in `<current project>/.ai-factory/config.yaml`; skill packages stay in agent skill directories. Do not create duplicate project manuals at root. All maintained documentation is English. Keep the root README as a landing page and use topic pages under `<current project>/docs/`. Any application change that alters behavior or visual appearance, adds functionality, or removes functionality MUST be documented in an ADR at `<current project>/docs/adr/<YYYY>/<MM>/<NNNN>-<short-title>.md`, using the decision date for the year and month. `<NNNN>` is a zero-padded four-digit serial that increases globally across all ADRs; do not restart it by month or year. Follow it with a hyphen and concise kebab-case title. Include the rationale and consequences, and add the record to `<current project>/docs/adr/README.md`. Preserve existing ADR filenames and links; apply this naming pattern to new records. ADRs preserve decision rationale and history; they do not replace Beads. Keep the complete actionable task, acceptance criteria, status, dependencies, blockers, and progress in the current project's Beads issue(s). Do not create or maintain task decomposition or status documents under `<current project>/docs/tasks/`; that workflow is retired. Existing files there remain historical references only.\n\n\n| Document                  | Path                                                                               | Description                                                                                                                      |\n|---------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| README                    | `<current project>/README.md`                                                      | Project page                                                                                                                     |\n| Documentation index       | `<current project>/docs/README.md`                                                 | Navigation and ownership                                                                                                         |\n| Setup and operations      | `<current project>/docs/setup/README.md`                                           | Environment and required tool checks                                                                                             |\n| AI agent collaboration    | `~/.agents/<current project>/docs/ai-agent-collaboration.md`                       | Delegation, subagent authority, and handoff                                                                                      |\n| Project description       | `<current project>/docs/DESCRIPTION.md`                                            | Purpose and implemented capabilities                                                                                             |\n| Architecture              | `<current project>/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`                                           | Components and source boundaries                                                                                                 |\n| Document conventions      | `<current project>/docs/DOCUMENTS.md`                                              | Canonical sources and AI Factory locations                                                                                       |\n| Decision index            | `<current project>/docs/adr/README.md`                                             | Durable decision history                                                                                                         |\n| Workflow decision         | `<current project>/docs/adr/YYYY/MM/NNNN-short-title.md`                           | Historical workflow decision superseded by ADR NNNN                                                                              |\n| Reviews                   | `<current project>/docs/reviews/YYYY/MM/NNNN-short-title.md`                       | Code review history                                                                                                              |\n| Playground                | `<current project>/docs/playground/README.md`                                      | Provisional experiments                                                                                                          |\n| OpenSpec                  | `<current project>/openspec/`                                                      | Tool-managed changes and specifications                                                                                          |\n| API reference             | `<current project>/docs/api.md`                                                    | Integration routes and authentication                                                                                            |\n| Configuration             | `<current project>/docs/configuration.md`                                          | Connections and environment settings                                                                                             |\n| Testing                   | `<current project>/docs/testing.md`                                                | Suites and verification limits                                                                                                   |\n| OpenAPI                   | `<current project>/<dir with app code>/openapi/openapi.yaml`                       | Swagger-compatible contract                                                                                                      |\n| InPost Shipping           | `<current project>/docs/modules/<module name>.md`                                  | To describe module work                                                                                                          |\n| Twig Template Library     | `<current project>/docs/libraries/<library name>.md`                               | To describe library work                                                                                                         |\n| Deployment                | `<current project>/docs/deployment.md`                                             | Docker and release flow                                                                                                          |\n| Database Schema Migration | `<current project>/docs/database/YYYY/MM/<YYYY-MM-DD-HH-mm-ss-migration-name>.sql` | SQL migrations for creating, altering, and dropping database schema objects (tables, columns, indexes, constraints, views, etc.) |\n| Rules                     | `docs/RULES.md`                                                                    | Universal project rules                                                                                                          |\n\nRecord significant decisions in `<current project>/docs/adr/` with context, date, status, alternatives, and consequences. Preserve accepted records; supersede them through linked successor ADRs. Keep actionable work and its decomposition in Beads. Store supporting project documentation, patches, and optional roadmap, research, rules, QA, references, and evolution artifacts under their configured `<current project>/docs/` paths. Create optional artifacts only when needed.\n\n## AI Context Files\n\n| File                        | Purpose             |\n|-----------------------------|---------------------|\n| `.ai-factory/rules/base.md` | Project conventions |\n| `.ai-factory/config.yaml`   | AI Factory settings |\n\n## Workflow and safety\n\n- **Escalate difficulty and uncertainty:** If you encounter a blocker, unexpected complexity, unclear or conflicting requirements, insufficient evidence, or a decision you cannot make confidently, stop the affected work and any work that depends on it. Perform only the read-only diagnosis needed to explain the situation, then contact the developer with the issue, evidence, what remains uncertain, and the specific help or decision needed. Wait for guidance before resuming. Do not guess, silently bypass a constraint, or make a consequential choice on the developer's behalf. A subagent must stop its changes and report the blocker to the primary agent for developer escalation.\n- Follow the conservative local-only Git profile. Do not commit, push, pull, or sync remotely unless explicitly requested. A missing Dolt remote warning is expected in this profile; do not add a remote to suppress it.\n- Preserve unrelated user changes and secrets. Do not deploy or send real Telegram messages without authorization.\n- Do not add or run application tests unless the user requests testing or verification. Mandatory tool health checks and invoked-skill documentation/skill reviews are separate from application tests.\n- Before handoff, close completed Beads issues, record remaining work and blockers, run only authorized quality checks, inspect `git status`, and report changed files and handoff state. Never close incomplete work.\n\n## Knowledge graph\n\n- Use `graphify query \"<question>\"` for codebase questions when `graphify-out/graph.json` exists; use `graphify path \"<A>\" \"<B>\"` for relationships and `graphify explain \"<concept>\"` for focused explanations.\n- Use `graphify-out/wiki/index.md` for broad navigation when present. Read `GRAPH_REPORT.md` for broad architecture review or when scoped queries are insufficient.\n- Verify graph evidence against current files; dirty output can follow hooks. Skip queries only when diagnosing stale/incorrect graph output or when the user asks.\n- After modifying code, run `graphify update .` on the host and report any refusal or failure; do not force an overwrite without diagnosing it.\n\n## Pick a tool for the question\n\n| Question                                                                                                | First tool               |\n|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------|\n| Exact route, text, config key, or error                                                                 | `rg`                     |\n| Changed behavior in history                                                                             | Git log/blame/diff       |\n| Rendered UI, browser console, network, or layout                                                        | `cua_repl` / `node_repl` |\n| Run JavaScript code, retreive DOM tree/snaphot, network trace and other system information for web page | Chrome DevTools MCP      |\n\n## Entire CLI — checkpoints and session history\n\nEntire records agent sessions as checkpoints tied to Git commits. Use it for *why/when/by which session* questions; Git stays authoritative for repository state, OpenSpec for requirements, Beads for task status. Run `entire` on the host from the repository root. Do not guess flags: read them with `entire agent-help [<command>]`.\n\n### Submodule rule — check first\n\nBefore any Entire or Git-history work in a repository that has `.gitmodules`, look for `<current project>/docs/AGENT_SUBMODULE_HISTORY.md`.\n\n- **If it exists**, read it and follow it for everything involving a `git submodule`: history, checkpoints, metadata export, and run records. Its rules override the generic guidance in this section.\n- **If it does not exist**, use this section only. Do not enable Entire inside a submodule or change a submodule's hooks, remotes, or configuration. Report the missing file when the task depends on submodule history.\n\n### Read-only commands (safe to run)\n\n```bash\nentire status                                         # enabled state, agents, active session\nentire checkpoint list --json                         # checkpoints on the current branch\nentire checkpoint list --pending --json               # work not yet condensed into a checkpoint\nentire checkpoint explain <checkpoint-id|commit-sha> --json   # metadata only, no transcript\nentire session current                                # active session for this worktree\nentire session info <session-id> --json\nentire why <file>[:line]                              # commit, checkpoint, and session behind a line\nentire search \"<topic>\" --json --compact              # needs `entire login`\n```\n\n### History for a task\n\n1. Get the Beads issue ID and any OpenSpec change name; search checkpoints and commits by them (`entire search`, `git log --grep`, or match `entire checkpoint list --json` entries to the commits that touched the task's files).\n2. Run `entire checkpoint explain <id> --json` for the intent, files touched, and token usage.\n3. Compare with `git log`, `git diff`, and `git status`. Treat Entire output as evidence, not as proof of correctness or authorship.\n\n### Two agents: Codex and Claude Code\n\nEntire is set up for exactly two agents, **Codex** and **Claude Code** (`entire status` lists them). Both may work in the same worktree.\n\n- Each agent has its own session ID. Use only the ID of your own session (`entire session current`); never attribute another agent's session or checkpoint to yourself.\n- A Git commit condenses **every** session with pending work in that worktree. One commit can therefore produce checkpoint data for both agents; do not assume a checkpoint contains only your work. Read `entire checkpoint explain <id> --json` to see which sessions and files it covers.\n- Each agent's hooks must be approved in its own tool (for Codex, `entire status` may report \"hook(s) need approval\"; that is the user's action via `/hooks`). Until approved, that agent's sessions may not be recorded. Report it; do not edit hook files.\n\n### Creating checkpoints\n\nThere is no command that creates a checkpoint. Agent hooks record session data locally; at a Git commit, Entire's Git hooks condense the pending work into a checkpoint and link it to the commit with an `Entire-Checkpoint` trailer. Entire never creates commits itself.\n\n- Commit only when the user or the active Git profile authorizes it, so a checkpoint is a by-product of an authorized commit. Never commit solely to produce a checkpoint.\n- `commit_linking` defaults to `prompt` (alternative: `always`). Entire may therefore ask whether to link the commit to a session. Do not change the setting; if a commit is blocked by or waits on that prompt, stop and report it.\n- After an authorized commit, confirm the checkpoint with `entire checkpoint list --json` or `entire checkpoint explain <commit-sha> --json`. Never invent a checkpoint ID.\n\n### Submodules: Entire stays out\n\nEntire is enabled in the workspace root only. Never run `entire enable` or any Entire setup in a submodule, and never change a submodule's Git hooks. Upstream Entire documentation does not describe submodule handling, so the following is an inference to verify on the first real commit: Entire's Git hooks are installed for the root repository, so a commit made inside a submodule creates no Entire checkpoint, and a root checkpoint sees the submodule only as a changed gitlink. Take submodule history from `git -C <submodule>` and from the project's `AGENT_SUBMODULE_HISTORY.md` (see the Submodule rule above).\n\n### Safety\n\n- Do not pass `--full`, `--transcript`, or `--raw-transcript` unless the user asks; transcripts can contain secrets and customer data.\n- `entire search` sends the query to the Entire service: keep it free of secrets and customer data.\n- Do not run `enable`, `disable`, `configure`, `clean`, `agent`, `login`, `logout`, or `checkpoint delete`; suggest them to the user.\n- Do not push checkpoints or sync Entire data without authorization.\n- If `entire` is missing or fails, continue without it, report the exact sanitized error, and do not install it automatically.\n\n<!-- bv-agent-instructions-v7 -->\n\n---\n\n## Beads Workflow Integration\n\nThis project uses a Beads tracker—either the Go `bd` CLI or the Rust `br` CLI—for issue tracking, plus [beads_viewer](https://github.com/Dicklesworthstone/beads_viewer) (`bv`) for graph-aware triage. Issues are stored in `.beads/`. `bv` auto-discovers supported JSONL exports, including `.beads/issues.jsonl` and legacy `.beads/beads.jsonl`.\n\n**Project boundary:** The absolute project-isolation rule above also applies to `bv` and every suggested working-directory or tracker route. Use `bv` only for the verified current-project snapshot; reject any route that points outside this repository. This applies to primary agents and subagents.\n\n**Choose the tracker CLI from this repository's instructions and configuration.** Use `bd` commands in a Go Beads workspace and `br` commands in a beads_rust workspace. Do not run both trackers against the same workspace or infer the tracker solely from the JSONL filename.\n\n### Using bv as an AI sidecar\n\nbv is a graph-aware triage engine for Beads projects. Instead of parsing .beads/issues.jsonl / .beads/beads.jsonl directly or hallucinating graph traversal, use robot flags for deterministic, dependency-aware outputs with precomputed metrics (PageRank, betweenness, critical path, cycles, HITS, eigenvector, k-core).\n\n**Scope boundary:** bv handles *what to work on* (triage, priority, planning) inside the verified current-project snapshot only. The selected tracker CLI (`bd` or `br`) handles creating, claiming, modifying, and closing beads inside the same project workspace. Reject any suggested working-directory or tracker route that points outside the current project.\n\n**CRITICAL: Use ONLY --robot-* flags. Bare bv launches an interactive TUI that blocks your session.**\n\n#### The Workflow: Start With Triage\n\n**`bv --robot-triage` is your single entry point.** Its `triage` object contains:\n- `quick_ref`: at-a-glance counts + top 3 picks\n- `recommendations`: ranked actionable items with scores, reasons, unblock info\n- `quick_wins`: low-effort high-impact items\n- `blockers_to_clear`: items that unblock the most downstream work\n- `project_health`: status/type/priority distributions, graph metrics\n- `commands`: copy-paste shell commands for next steps\n\n```bash\nbv --robot-triage        # THE MEGA-COMMAND: start here\nbv --robot-next          # Minimal: just the single top pick + claim command\n\n# TOON output (--format toon): a compact tabular encoding. Measured on this\n# repository it is 7% smaller than JSON for --robot-graph but 9-15% LARGER for\n# nested payloads (--robot-triage, --robot-plan, --robot-insights,\n# --robot-label-health); use --stats to see both sizes before adopting it.\n# TOON encoding shells out to the tru binary. With no encoder installed,\n# --format toon prints a fallback warning, emits JSON with output_format \"json\",\n# and --stats prints no sizes at all.\nbv --robot-graph --format toon\nbv --robot-triage --format toon --stats\n```\n\nRecommendations can include blocked or assigned work; `triage.quick_ref.top_picks` reflects snapshot readiness. A suggested action records its original local ID, working directory, and tracker route. Use that route rather than a namespaced display ID or an unrelated current directory. Inspect current tracker state before execution: analysis does not reserve work or guarantee that a later claim succeeds.\n\n#### Other bv Commands\n\n| Command                                             | Returns                                                                               |\n|-----------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| `--robot-plan`                                      | Parallel execution tracks with unblocks lists                                         |\n| `--robot-priority`                                  | Priority misalignment detection with confidence                                       |\n| `--robot-insights`                                  | Full metrics: PageRank, betweenness, HITS, eigenvector, critical path, cycles, k-core |\n| `--robot-alerts`                                    | Stale issues, blocking cascades, priority mismatches                                  |\n| `--robot-suggest`                                   | Hygiene: duplicates, missing deps, label suggestions, cycle breaks                    |\n| `--robot-diff --diff-since <ref>`                   | Changes since ref: new/closed/modified issues                                         |\n| `--robot-graph [--graph-format=json\\|dot\\|mermaid]` | Dependency graph export                                                               |\n\nRobot analysis commands default to JSON; `--format toon` selects TOON, and `--robot-help` defaults to text. In JSON mode, `--graph-format=dot` or `mermaid` puts diagram text in the `graph` field (`bv --robot-graph --graph-format=dot | jq -r .graph`).\n\n#### Scoping & Filtering\n\n```bash\nbv --robot-plan --label backend              # Scope to label's subgraph\nbv --robot-insights --as-of HEAD~30          # Historical point-in-time\nbv --recipe actionable --robot-plan          # Pre-filter: ready to work (no blockers)\nbv --recipe high-impact --robot-triage       # Pre-filter: top PageRank scores\n```\n\n### Tracker Commands for Issue Management\n\nUse exactly one command family, matching the tracker configured for the repository.\n\n#### Rust beads_rust (`br`)\n\nUse `br` 0.6.0 or newer when executing saved claim commands. It rechecks\ndeferred status and future `defer_until` values when the claim runs, so a\nrecommendation captured before a deferral cannot bypass it. This requirement\napplies to executing tracker claims.\n\n```bash\nbr ready --json                       # Show issues ready to work (no blockers)\nbr list --status=open --json          # All open issues\nbr show <id> --json                   # Full issue details with dependencies\nbr create --title=\"...\" --type=task --priority=2 --json\nbr update <id> --claim --json         # Claim for the current actor and start work\nbr close <id> --reason=\"Completed\" --json\nbr close <id1> <id2> --reason=\"Completed\" --json\nbr sync --flush-only                  # Export DB to JSONL after Beads mutations\n```\n\n#### Go Beads (`bd`)\n\n```bash\nbd ready --json                       # Show issues ready to work\nbd show <id> --json                   # Full issue details\nbd create \"...\" -t task -p 2 --json\nbd update <id> --claim --json         # Atomically claim work\nbd close <id> --json\nbd dep add <issue> <depends-on>\nbd export -o .beads/issues.jsonl        # Refresh the compatibility export read by bv\n```\n\n### Workflow Pattern\n\n1. **Triage**: Run `bv --robot-triage` to find the highest-impact actionable work\n2. **Verify**: Check the selected tracker's `show`/`ready` output before claiming\n3. **Claim**: Use `br update <id> --claim --json` or `bd update <id> --claim --json`\n4. **Work**: Implement the task\n5. **Complete**: Use the selected tracker's `close` command\n6. **Refresh for bv**: Run `br sync --flush-only` or the `bd export` command above so the JSONL export is current\n\n### Key Concepts\n\n- **Dependencies**: Issues can block other issues. `br ready --json` and `bd ready --json` show unblocked work.\n- **Priority**: P0=critical, P1=high, P2=medium, P3=low, P4=backlog (use numbers 0-4, not words)\n- **Types**: task, bug, feature, epic, chore, docs, question\n- **Blocking**: Use `br dep add <issue> <depends-on>` or `bd dep add <issue> <depends-on>` to add dependencies\n\n### Git Policy\n\nTracker commands do not grant permission to commit or push application code. Follow this repository's own git and tracker instructions before staging, committing, syncing, or pushing. If the repository says \"commit only when asked,\" that rule overrides any generic workflow advice.\n\n<!-- end-bv-agent-instructions -->\n\n--- project-doc ---\n\n# Project Instructions for AI Agents\n\nThis is the canonical project-wide instruction file. `CLAUDE.md` is a pointer to this file; keep shared rules here only.\n\n## Start Here\n\nThe shared workflow (Beads → Context7 → Mem0, tool health gate, subagent briefing, documentation and ADR rules, Git profile, testing policy) is defined in `~/.codex/AGENTS.md` and is not repeated here. Read [docs/setup/README.md](docs/setup/README.md) for this project's environment.\n\nThe application name is **Strateg Export Products**. Use exactly `Strateg Export Products: ` as the prefix for shared Local Mem0 records.\n\n## Coding Conventions\n\n1. You generate the best answers.\n2. You are a very good know the [revolution/laravel-google-sheets](https://github.com/invokable/laravel-google-sheets) library.\n3. You know the [openai-php/client](https://github.com/openai-php/client) php library very well.\n4. You can develop a Telegram bot using the [Telegram bot docs](https://core.telegram.org/bots/api).\n5. You know the [longman/telegram-bot](https://github.com/php-telegram-bot/core) php library very well.\n6. You know the [phpoffice/phpspreadsheet](https://phpspreadsheet.readthedocs.io/en/latest/) php library very well.\n7. Follow the coding style if you are processing a finished piece of code.\n8. Write comments only on `English language`.\n9. Use `camelCase` for writing function names.\n10. Use `Snake case` for writing variables for `PHP`.\n11. Use `camelCase` for writing variables for `JS/TS`.\n12. Use `PascalCase` for writing `Classes`, `Interfaces`, `Traits`, and `Enums` names.\n13. We work in a `Linux environment`.\n14. Be `attentive to the details of the question` to you and be attentive to the details of our dialogue.\n15. Pay attention to `small (subtle) details` that can be very important in solving the problem.\n16. Before returning the answer, `analyze your prepared answer and think again whether it is correct`!\n17. If you are not sure about the answer, you do not understand something in my message, you have doubts about my message or need some details for the best answer - then ask me!\n18. For logging, use the follow code:\n```php\n\\Illuminate\\Support\\Facades\\Log::channel('stack')->$method_name(...); // for errors and warnings\n\\Illuminate\\Support\\Facades\\Log::channel('daily')->$method_name(...); // for other logs\n```\n\n## Safety dev workflow\n\n- You can use system variables from `/home/kamaz/www/strateg-projects/strateg-export-products/.docker/dev/env/`\n- Never run: `docker system prune`, `docker volume rm`, `DROP DATABASE`, `TRUNCATE`\n- Prefer SELECT-only unless explicitly asked to modify data\n- DB read-only queries:\n    * `docker compose -f /home/kamaz/www/strateg-projects/strateg-export-products/.docker/dev/docker-compose.yml exec -T dev-strateg-export-products-postgresql sh -lc 'psql -U \"${POSTGRES_SIMPLE_USER}\" -d \"${POSTGRES_DB}\" -c \"SELECT ...'`\"\n- To execute commands related to the application or server, use the following command:\n    * `docker compose -f /home/kamaz/www/strateg-projects/strateg-export-products/.docker/dev/docker-compose.yml exec -T dev-strateg-export-products-php-fpm <command and args>`. The Docker container already has everything you need installed, including PHP.\n- You are strictly prohibited from `installing` or `updating` `composer` and `npm dependencies`, or `Docker images` yourself! You are strictly prohibited from restarting `Docker containers`!\n  To perform these operations, you must `notify` the developer about them. The developer will then follow all instructions and `notify` you when they are ready. After that, you can continue working.\n\n# Laravel Boost Guidelines\n\nThe Laravel Boost guidelines are specifically curated by Laravel maintainers for this application. These guidelines should be followed closely to enhance the user's satisfaction building Laravel applications.\n\n## Foundational Context\nThis application is a Laravel application and its main Laravel ecosystems package & versions are below. You are an expert with them all. Ensure you abide by these specific packages & versions.\n\n- PHP - 8.5\n- PostgreSQL - 18.1\n- Node.js - 25.6.1\n- filament/filament (FILAMENT) - v5\n- laravel/framework (LARAVEL) - v13.33\n- laravel/prompts (PROMPTS) - v0.3\n- livewire/livewire (LIVEWIRE) - v4.4\n- larastan/larastan (LARASTAN) - v3.12\n- laravel/boost (BOOST) - v2.10\n- laravel/mcp (MCP) - v1\n- laravel/pint (PINT) - v1.32\n- laravel/sail (SAIL) - v1.68\n- phpunit/phpunit (PHPUNIT) - v13.3\n- rector/rector (RECTOR) - v2.6\n- tailwindcss (TAILWINDCSS) - v4.3\n- phpoffice/phpspreadsheet - v5.10\n- revolution/laravel-google-sheets - v7.2\n\n## Documentation\n\n| Document                          | Path                                        | Description                                     |\n|-----------------------------------|---------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------|\n| Documentation Index               | `docs/README.md`                            | Navigation and ownership                        |\n| Setup and Operations              | `docs/setup/README.md`                      | Environment and required tool checks            |\n| Document Conventions              | `docs/DOCUMENTS.md`                         | Canonical sources and artifact locations        |\n| Decisions (ADR)                   | `docs/adr/README.md`                        | Durable decision history                        |\n| Getting Started                   | `docs/getting-started.md`                   | Installation and local startup                  |\n| Architecture                      | `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`                      | Module boundaries and dependency rules          |\n| Configuration                     | `docs/configuration.md`                     | Environment, stores, and queues                 |\n| API Reference                     | `docs/api.md`                               | Routes and authentication                       |\n| Testing                           | `docs/testing.md`                           | Suites and verification limits                  |\n| Deployment                        | `docs/deployment.md`                        | Docker and release flow                         |\n| Helper Functions                  | `docs/helpers.md`                           | Shared helper reference                         |\n| Supports Reference                | `docs/supports.md`                          | All Supports service methods                    |\n| Product Data Workflows            | `docs/product-data-workflows.md`            | Import, export, update, delete, and restore     |\n| Product Import                    | `docs/product-import-batch-resource.md`     | Import batches and items                        |\n| Product Updates                   | `docs/product-update-batch-resource.md`     | Batch and API updates                           |\n| Product Deletes                   | `docs/product-delete-batch-resource.md`     | Remote shop deletion batches                    |\n| Product Catalog                   | `docs/product-resource.md`                  | Local catalog operations                        |\n| Remote API Integration            | `docs/remote-api-integration.md`            | Outbound shop requests and API status           |\n| OpenCart API Token Lifecycle      | `docs/opencart-api-token-lifecycle.md`      | Session token login, reuse, and refresh         |\n| OpenAPI Specification             | `docs/openapi.yaml`                         | Swagger-readable integration contract           |\n| Categories                        | `docs/category-resource.md`                 | Category hierarchy and binding                  |\n| Attributes                        | `docs/attribute-resource.md`                | Attribute management                            |\n| Shops                             | `docs/shop-resource.md`                     | External shop configuration                     |\n| Shop Languages                    | `docs/shop-language-resource.md`            | Shop language settings                          |\n| Users                             | `docs/user-resource.md`                     | Admin user management                           |\n| Project Description               | `docs/DESCRIPTION.md`                       | Business logic, modules, and tables             |\n| Security                          | `docs/SECURITY.md`                          | Secrets, access rules, and known risks          |\n| Roadmap                           | `docs/ROADMAP.md`                           | Completed and planned milestones                |\n| Project Rules                     | `docs/RULES.md`                             | Coding and workflow rules                       |\n\n## Agent Rules\n\n- Do not combine independent shell commands into a single line when each step should be verified separately.\n    - Incorrect: `git checkout main && git pull`\n    - Correct: first `git checkout main`, then `git pull origin main`\n- Use the project Docker containers for runtime checks and direct database access (run from the repository root):\n    * PHP and Artisan: `docker compose -f .docker/dev/docker-compose.yml exec -T dev-strateg-export-products-php-fpm <command>`\n    * PostgreSQL client: see the read-only query command in \"Safety dev workflow\". The database is PostgreSQL, not MariaDB.\n    * Do not rely on host PHP or `psql` binaries.\n- Host paths and container paths:\n    * `app-code/app` -> `/var/webroot/sites/export-products.strateg.ua/app`\n    * `app-code/httpdocs` -> `/var/webroot/sites/export-products.strateg.ua/httpdocs`\n    * `app-code/storage` -> `/var/webroot/sites/export-products.strateg.ua/storage`\n    Use the container path for PHP CLI commands such as linting.\n- Read database credentials from `.docker/dev/env/` or the container environment; never write real credentials into documentation, skills, committed shell commands, or logs.\n- Queued jobs are processed by the `cron` container (`schedule:run` every minute), not by a separate worker.\n\n## Security & Configuration\n\nDo not commit credentials, production configuration, generated runtime data, or local environment files.\n\n## AI Context Files\n\n| File                      | Purpose                                                                                       |\n|---------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| `docs/DESCRIPTION.md`     | High-level project purpose, workflows, data model map, and where to look first when debugging |\n| `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`    | Architecture decisions, module boundaries, dependency rules, and workflow ownership           |\n| `docs/patches/*.md`       | Important regression history, business invariants, and lessons from previous fixes            |\n| `docs/RULES.md`           | Universal project rules                                                                       |\n| `docs/rules/base.md`      | Project conventions for AI-assisted changes                                                   |\n| `.ai-factory/config.yaml` | AI Factory settings and artifact paths                                                        |\n\n## Project Overrides of Generated Guidelines\n\nThe generated Laravel Boost guidelines below are subordinate to this section and to `~/.codex/AGENTS.md` (workflow, documentation, ADR, Git, and testing policy). Where the guidelines conflict with them:\n\n- **Tests:** the guidelines' \"write, update, or run tests\" steps (including \"run the singular test\" and \"ask whether to run the whole suite\") apply only when the user requests testing. Run requested tests in the PHP container.\n- **Verification scripts:** do not create ad hoc verification scripts. Use `tinker` or `database-query` only for read-only diagnosis.\n- **Formatting:** run `vendor/bin/pint --dirty` in the PHP container only when quality checks are authorized.\n- **Documentation:** documentation is required, not \"only on request\"; follow `docs/DOCUMENTS.md`.\n\n<laravel-boost-guidelines>\n=== foundation rules ===\n\n## Conventions\n- You must follow all existing code conventions used in this application. When creating or editing a file, check sibling files for the correct structure, approach, and naming.\n- Use descriptive names for variables and methods. For example, `isRegisteredForDiscounts`, not `discount()`.\n- Check for existing components to reuse before writing a new one.\n\n## Verification Scripts\n- Do not create ad hoc verification scripts. Use `tinker` only for read-only diagnosis. Add or run tests only when the user requests them (see \"Project Overrides of Generated Guidelines\").\n\n## Application Structure & Architecture\n- Stick to existing directory structure; don't create new base folders without approval.\n- Do not change the application's dependencies without approval.\n\n### Project-specific product workflows\n- This is a modular Laravel monolith. Keep import, binding, export, update, delete, restore, translation, and remote-shop integration rules in their existing jobs and services.\n- Product import entry points are Excel upload, Google Sheets URL, and the Filament admin form. The import preparation job is `app-code/app/app/Jobs/ProcessProductImportBatchJob.php`.\n- Excel and Google Sheets imports use the required sheets `Product`, `Description`, `Image`, `Product Category`, `Product Attribute`, `Seo Url`, `Special`, and `Discount`.\n- Long-running operations must use batch and item records with persisted statuses, counters, payloads, and error audit metadata. Do not hide row failures in notifications only.\n- `spme_product_shop` is the authoritative local product-to-shop binding. `external_product_id` is required for remote update, delete, and restore operations.\n- `family_ulid` tracks one logical product across shop-specific duplicate rows. Preserve it when changing binding or duplication behavior.\n- Use `ProductPayloadBuilderService` for remote product payloads and the queue services for export, update, delete, and restore preparation. Filament resources should collect input and dispatch actions, not implement remote API rules.\n- Shop-scoped categories, attributes, manufacturers, brands, languages, and SEO rows must be resolved for the selected `shop_id` and active shop languages.\n- The project has no inbound `routes/api.php`. `docs/openapi.yaml` documents the configurable outbound remote-shop integration; each remote shop's actual contract remains authoritative.\n\n### Current code locations\n- Admin UI: `app-code/app/app/Filament/Resources/`\n- Queued workflows: `app-code/app/app/Jobs/`\n- Product application services: `app-code/app/app/Services/Products/`\n- Shared product, translation, deletion, restore, and SEO services: `app-code/app/app/Supports/Services/`\n- Shared helpers: `app-code/app/app/Supports/helpers.php`\n- Tests: `app-code/app/tests/`\n\n## Frontend Bundling\n- If the user doesn't see a frontend change reflected in the UI, it could mean they need to run `npm run build`, `npm run dev`, or `composer run dev`. Ask them.\n\n## Replies\n- Be concise in your explanations - focus on what's important rather than explaining obvious details.\n\n## Documentation Files\n- Project documentation is required. Follow `docs/DOCUMENTS.md` and \"Project Overrides of Generated Guidelines\"; do not create task documents (tasks live in Beads).\n\n=== boost rules ===\n\n## Laravel Boost\n- Laravel Boost is an MCP server that comes with powerful tools designed specifically for this application. Use them.\n\n## Artisan\n- Use the `list-artisan-commands` tool when you need to call an Artisan command to double-check the available parameters.\n\n## URLs\n- Whenever you share a project URL with the user, you should use the `get-absolute-url` tool to ensure you're using the correct scheme, domain/IP, and port.\n\n## Tinker / Debugging\n- You should use the `tinker` tool when you need to execute PHP to debug code or query Eloquent models directly.\n- Use the `database-query` tool when you only need to read from the database.\n\n## Reading Browser Logs With the `browser-logs` Tool\n- You can read browser logs, errors, and exceptions using the `browser-logs` tool from Boost.\n- Only recent browser logs will be useful - ignore old logs.\n\n## Searching Documentation (Critically Important)\n- Boost comes with a powerful `search-docs` tool you should use before any other approaches when dealing with Laravel or Laravel ecosystem packages. This tool automatically passes a list of installed packages and their versions to the remote Boost API, so it returns only version-specific documentation for the user's circumstance. You should pass an array of packages to filter on if you know you need docs for particular packages.\n- The `search-docs` tool is perfect for all Laravel-related packages, including Laravel, Inertia, Livewire, Filament, Tailwind, Pest, Nova, Nightwatch, etc.\n- You must use this tool to search for Laravel ecosystem documentation before falling back to other approaches.\n- Search the documentation before making code changes to ensure we are taking the correct approach.\n- Use multiple, broad, simple, topic-based queries to start. For example: `['rate limiting', 'routing rate limiting', 'routing']`.\n- Do not add package names to queries; package information is already shared. For example, use `test resource table`, not `filament 4 test resource table`.\n\n### Available Search Syntax\n- You can and should pass multiple queries at once. The most relevant results will be returned first.\n\n1. Simple Word Searches with auto-stemming - query=authentication - finds 'authenticate' and 'auth'.\n2. Multiple Words (AND Logic) - query=rate limit - finds knowledge containing both \"rate\" AND \"limit\".\n3. Quoted Phrases (Exact Position) - query=\"infinite scroll\" - words must be adjacent and in that order.\n4. Mixed Queries - query=middleware \"rate limit\" - \"middleware\" AND exact phrase \"rate limit\".\n5. Multiple Queries - queries=[\"authentication\", \"middleware\"] - ANY of these terms.\n\n=== php rules ===\n\n## PHP\n\n- Always use strict typing at the head of a `.php` file: `declare(strict_types=1);`.\n- Always use curly braces for control structures, even if it has one line.\n\n### Constructors\n- Use PHP 8 constructor property promotion in `__construct()`.\n      <code-snippet name=\"Constructor Property Promotion Example\" lang=\"php\">\n          public function __construct(public GitHub $github) { }\n      </code-snippet>\n- Do not allow empty `__construct()` methods with zero parameters unless the constructor is private.\n\n### Type Declarations\n- Always use explicit return type declarations for methods and functions.\n- Use appropriate PHP type hints for method parameters.\n\n<code-snippet name=\"Explicit Return Types and Method Params\" lang=\"php\">\n    protected function isAccessible(User $user, ?string $path = null): bool\n    {\n        ...\n    }\n</code-snippet>\n\n## Comments\n- Prefer PHPDoc blocks over inline comments. Never use comments within the code itself unless there is something very complex going on.\n\n## PHPDoc Blocks\n- Add useful array shape type definitions for arrays when appropriate.\n\n## Enums\n- That being said, keys in an Enum should follow existing application Enum conventions.\n\n=== laravel/core rules ===\n\n## Do Things the Laravel Way\n\n- Use `php artisan make:` commands to create new files (i.e. migrations, controllers, models, etc.). You can list available Artisan commands using the `list-artisan-commands` tool.\n- If you're creating a generic PHP class, use `php artisan make:class`.\n- Pass `--no-interaction` to all Artisan commands to ensure they work without user input. You should also pass the correct `--options` to ensure correct behavior.\n\n### Database\n- Always use proper Eloquent relationship methods with return type hints. Prefer relationship methods over raw queries or manual joins.\n- Use Eloquent models and relationships before suggesting raw database queries.\n- Avoid `DB::`; prefer `Model::query()`. Generate code that leverages Laravel's ORM capabilities rather than bypassing them.\n- Generate code that prevents N+1 query problems by using eager loading.\n- Use Laravel's query builder for very complex database operations.\n\n### Model Creation\n- When creating new models, create useful factories and seeders for them too. Ask the user if they need any other things, using `list-artisan-commands` to check the available options to `php artisan make:model`.\n\n### APIs & Eloquent Resources\n- For APIs, default to using Eloquent API Resources and API versioning unless existing API routes do not, then you should follow existing application convention.\n\n### Controllers & Validation\n- Always create Form Request classes for validation rather than inline validation in controllers. Include both validation rules and custom error messages.\n- Check sibling Form Requests to see if the application uses array or string based validation rules.\n\n### Queues\n- Use queued jobs for time-consuming operations with the `ShouldQueue` interface.\n\n### Authentication & Authorization\n- Use Laravel's built-in authentication and authorization features (gates, policies, Sanctum, etc.).\n\n### URL Generation\n- When generating links to other pages, prefer named routes and the `route()` function.\n\n### Configuration\n- Use environment variables only in configuration files - never use the `env()` function directly outside of config files. Always use `config('app.name')`, not `env('APP_NAME')`.\n\n### Testing\n- When creating models for tests, use the factories for the models. Check if the factory has custom states that can be used before manually setting up the model.\n- Faker: Use methods such as `$this->faker->word()` or `fake()->randomDigit()`. Follow existing conventions whether to use `$this->faker` or `fake()`.\n- When creating tests, make use of `php artisan make:test [options] {name}` to create a feature test, and pass `--unit` to create a unit test. Most tests should be feature tests.\n\n### Vite Error\n- If you receive an \"Illuminate\\Foundation\\ViteException: Unable to locate file in Vite manifest\" error, you can run `npm run build` or ask the user to run `npm run dev` or `composer run dev`.\n\n=== laravel/v13 rules ===\n\n## Laravel 13\n\n- Use the `search-docs` tool to get version-specific documentation.\n- Since Laravel 11, Laravel has a new streamlined file structure which this project uses.\n\n### Laravel 13 Structure\n- In Laravel 13, middleware are no longer registered in `app/Http/Kernel.php`.\n- Middleware are configured declaratively in `bootstrap/app.php` using `Application::configure()->withMiddleware()`.\n- `bootstrap/app.php` is the file to register middleware, exceptions, and routing files.\n- `bootstrap/providers.php` contains application specific service providers.\n- The `app\\Console\\Kernel.php` file no longer exists; use `bootstrap/app.php` or `routes/console.php` for console configuration.\n- Console commands in `app/Console/Commands/` are automatically available and do not require manual registration.\n\n### Database\n- When modifying a column, the migration must include all of the attributes that were previously defined on the column. Otherwise, they will be dropped and lost.\n- Laravel 13 allows limiting eagerly loaded records natively, without external packages: `$query->latest()->limit(10);`.\n\n### Models\n- Casts can and likely should be set in a `casts()` method on a model rather than the `$casts` property. Follow existing conventions from other models.\n\n=== livewire/core rules ===\n\n## Livewire\n\n- Use the `search-docs` tool to find exact version-specific documentation for how to write Livewire and Livewire tests.\n- Use the `php artisan make:livewire [Posts\\CreatePost]` Artisan command to create new components.\n- State should live on the server, with the UI reflecting it.\n- All Livewire requests hit the Laravel backend; they're like regular HTTP requests. Always validate form data and run authorization checks in Livewire actions.\n\n## Livewire Best Practices\n- Livewire components require a single root element.\n- Use `wire:loading` and `wire:dirty` for delightful loading states.\n- Add `wire:key` in loops:\n\n```blade\n    @foreach ($items as $item)\n        <div wire:key=\"item-{{ $item->id }}\">\n            {{ $item->name }}\n        </div>\n    @endforeach\n```\n\n- Prefer lifecycle hooks like `mount()`, `updatedFoo()` for initialization and reactive side effects:\n\n<code-snippet name=\"Lifecycle Hook Examples\" lang=\"php\">\n    public function mount(User $user) { $this->user = $user; }\n    public function updatedSearch() { $this->resetPage(); }\n</code-snippet>\n\n## Testing Livewire\n\n<code-snippet name=\"Example Livewire Component Test\" lang=\"php\">\n    Livewire::test(Counter::class)\n        ->assertSet('count', 0)\n        ->call('increment')\n        ->assertSet('count', 1)\n        ->assertSee(1)\n        ->assertStatus(200);\n</code-snippet>\n\n<code-snippet name=\"Testing Livewire Component Exists on Page\" lang=\"php\">\n    $this->get('/posts/create')\n    ->assertSeeLivewire(CreatePost::class);\n</code-snippet>\n\n=== livewire/v4 rules ===\n\n## Livewire 4\n\n### Upgrade Notes\n- These rules are for Livewire 4. Prefer Livewire 4 documentation and examples, not v2/v3 snippets from memory.\n- Components still typically live in the `App\\Livewire` namespace for class-based components.\n- Use `wire:model.live` for real-time updates. Plain `wire:model` remains deferred by default.\n- Use `$this->dispatch()` for events and `assertDispatched()` in tests.\n- Prefer `Route::livewire()` when routing directly to Livewire components, especially for view-based or single-file components.\n- Do not hardcode assumptions about `/livewire/` endpoints. In Livewire 4 the endpoint prefix is hash-based, like `/livewire-{hash}/...`.\n\n### JavaScript And Directives\n- `wire:show`, `wire:transition`, `wire:cloak`, `wire:offline`, `wire:target`, and `wire:intersect` are available. Use the docs for the exact syntax before implementing non-trivial behavior.\n- Alpine is bundled with Livewire; do not install or include Alpine separately unless the project already has a justified custom setup.\n- Prefer the new JavaScript APIs in Livewire 4:\n  - Use `$wire.$js.actionName = () => { ... }` instead of deprecated `$wire.$js('actionName', ...)`.\n  - Use `Livewire.interceptRequest()` / `Livewire.interceptMessage()` instead of deprecated `Livewire.hook('request', ...)` / `Livewire.hook('commit', ...)` for new code.\n\n### Initialization Example\n- Hook into `livewire:init` for client-side setup:\n\n<code-snippet name=\"Livewire Init Example\" lang=\"js\">\n    document.addEventListener('livewire:init', function () {\n        Livewire.interceptRequest(({ onError }) => {\n            onError(({ response, preventDefault }) => {\n                if (response && response.status === 419) {\n                    preventDefault();\n                    alert('Your session expired');\n                }\n            });\n        });\n    });\n</code-snippet>\n\n=== pint/core rules ===\n\n## Laravel Pint Code Formatter\n\n- When the user authorizes quality checks, run `vendor/bin/pint --dirty` in the PHP container before finalizing changes to ensure your code matches the project's expected style.\n- Do not run `vendor/bin/pint --test`, simply run `vendor/bin/pint` to fix any formatting issues.\n\n=== phpunit/core rules ===\n\n## PHPUnit\n\n- This application uses PHPUnit for testing. All tests must be written as PHPUnit classes. Use `php artisan make:test --phpunit {name}` to create a new test.\n- If you see a test using \"Pest\", convert it to PHPUnit.\n- Add, update, or run tests only when the user requests testing or verification. When requested, run the singular updated test first, then ask whether to run the entire suite.\n- Tests should test all of the happy paths, failure paths, and weird paths.\n- You must not remove any tests or test files from the tests directory without approval. These are not temporary or helper files; these are core to the application.\n\n### Running Tests\n- Run the minimal number of tests, using an appropriate filter, before finalizing.\n- To run all tests: `php artisan test --compact`.\n- To run all tests in a file: `php artisan test --compact tests/Feature/ExampleTest.php`.\n- To filter on a particular test name: `php artisan test --compact --filter=testName` (recommended after making a change to a related file).\n\n=== tailwindcss/core rules ===\n\n## Tailwind CSS\n\n- Use Tailwind CSS classes to style HTML; check and use existing Tailwind conventions within the project before writing your own.\n- Offer to extract repeated patterns into components that match the project's conventions (i.e. Blade, JSX, Vue, etc.).\n- Think through class placement, order, priority, and defaults. Remove redundant classes, add classes to parent or child carefully to limit repetition, and group elements logically.\n- You can use the `search-docs` tool to get exact examples from the official documentation when needed.\n\n### Spacing\n- When listing items, use gap utilities for spacing; don't use margins.\n\n<code-snippet name=\"Valid Flex Gap Spacing Example\" lang=\"html\">\n    <div class=\"flex gap-8\">\n        <div>Superior</div>\n        <div>Michigan</div>\n        <div>Erie</div>\n    </div>\n</code-snippet>\n\n### Dark Mode\n- If existing pages and components support dark mode, new pages and components must support dark mode in a similar way, typically using `dark:`.\n\n=== tailwindcss/v4 rules ===\n\n## Tailwind CSS 4\n\n- Always use Tailwind CSS v4; do not use the deprecated utilities.\n- `corePlugins` is not supported in Tailwind v4.\n- In Tailwind v4, configuration is CSS-first using the `@theme` directive — no separate `tailwind.config.js` file is needed.\n\n<code-snippet name=\"Extending Theme in CSS\" lang=\"css\">\n    @theme {\n      --color-brand: oklch(0.72 0.11 178);\n    }\n</code-snippet>\n\n- In Tailwind v4, you import Tailwind using a regular CSS `@import` statement, not using the `@tailwind` directives used in v3:\n\n<code-snippet name=\"Tailwind v4 Import Tailwind Diff\" lang=\"diff\">\n   - @tailwind base;\n   - @tailwind components;\n   - @tailwind utilities;\n   + @import \"tailwindcss\";\n</code-snippet>\n\n### Replaced Utilities\n- Tailwind v4 removed deprecated utilities. Do not use the deprecated option; use the replacement.\n- Opacity values are still numeric.\n\n\n| Deprecated            | Replacement          |\n|-----------------------|----------------------|\n| bg-opacity-*          | bg-black/*           |\n| text-opacity-*        | text-black/*         |\n| border-opacity-*      | border-black/*       |\n| divide-opacity-*      | divide-black/*       |\n| ring-opacity-*        | ring-black/*         |\n| placeholder-opacity-* | placeholder-black/*  |\n| flex-shrink-*         | shrink-*             |\n| flex-grow-*           | grow-*               |\n| overflow-ellipsis     | text-ellipsis        |\n| decoration-slice      | box-decoration-slice |\n| decoration-clone      | box-decoration-clone |\n\n=== filament/filament rules ===\n\n## Filament\n\n- Filament is used by this application. Follow existing conventions for how and where it's implemented.\n- Filament is a Server-Driven UI (SDUI) framework for Laravel that lets you define user interfaces in PHP using structured configuration objects. Built on Livewire, Alpine.js, and Tailwind CSS.\n- Use the `search-docs` tool for official documentation on Artisan commands, code examples, testing, relationships, and idiomatic practices.\n- Filament v5 requires Livewire v4+. When guidance conflicts, prefer the Filament 5 and Livewire 4 docs together.\n\n### Artisan\n\n- Use Filament-specific Artisan commands to create files. Find them with `list-artisan-commands` or `php artisan --help`.\n- Inspect required options and always pass `--no-interaction`.\n\n### Patterns\n\nUse static `make()` methods to initialize components. Most configuration methods accept a `Closure` for dynamic values.\n\nUse `Get $get` to read other form field values for conditional logic:\n\n<code-snippet name=\"Conditional form field\" lang=\"php\">\n    use Filament\\Forms\\Components\\Select;\n    use Filament\\Forms\\Components\\TextInput;\n    use Filament\\Schemas\\Components\\Utilities\\Get;\n    \n    Select::make('type')\n        ->options(CompanyType::class)\n        ->required()\n        ->live(),\n    \n    TextInput::make('company_name')\n        ->required()\n        ->visible(fn (Get $get): bool => $get('type') === 'business'),\n</code-snippet>\n\nUse `state()` with a `Closure` to compute derived column values:\n\n<code-snippet name=\"Computed table column\" lang=\"php\">\n    use Filament\\Tables\\Columns\\TextColumn;\n    \n    TextColumn::make('full_name')\n        ->state(fn (User $record): string => \"{$record->first_name} {$record->last_name}\"),\n</code-snippet>\n\nActions encapsulate a button with optional modal form and logic:\n\n<code-snippet name=\"Action with modal form\" lan\n</INSTRUCTIONS>"},{"type":"input_text","text":"<environment_context>\n  <cwd>/home/kamaz/www/strateg-projects/strateg-export-products</cwd>\n  <shell>bash</shell>\n  <current_date>2026-10-27</current_date>\n  <timezone>Europe/Kyiv</timezone>\n  <filesystem><workspace_roots><root>/home/kamaz/www/strateg-projects/strateg-export-products</root></workspace_roots><permission_profile type=\"managed\"><file_system type=\"restricted\"><entry access=\"read\"><special>:root</special></entry><entry access=\"write\"><path>/home/kamaz/www/strateg-projects/strateg-export-products</path></entry><entry access=\"write\"><special>:slash_tmp</special></entry><entry access=\"write\"><special>:tmpdir</special></entry><entry access=\"read\"><path>/home/kamaz/www/strateg-projects/strateg-export-products/.git</path></entry><entry access=\"read\"><path>/home/kamaz/www/strateg-projects/strateg-export-products/.agents</path></entry><entry access=\"read\"><path>/home/kamaz/www/strateg-projects/strateg-export-products/.codex</path></entry><entry access=\"read\"><path>/home/kamaz/www/strateg-projects/strateg-export-products/.aws</path></entry></file_system></permission_profile></filesystem>\n</environment_context>"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a17dfb-d4fa-7642-9c18-64cf77325bf8","create_time":1793115018.8599017,"content_item_kinds":["agents_md.instructions","environments.environment_context"],"content_item_metadata":[{"provenance":{"type":"agents_md"}},{"provenance":{"type":"harness","hook":false}}]}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-27T15:30:18.863Z","ordinal":12369,"type":"world_state","payload":{"full":true,"state":{"agents_md":{"directory":"/home/kamaz/www/strateg-projects/strateg-export-products","text":"# Project instructions for AI agents\n\nThis is the canonical project-wide instruction file. `CLAUDE.md` points here; do not duplicate shared rules in other tool instruction files.\n\n## Start here\n\nBegin every work session by reading [<current project>/docs/setup/README.md](<current project>/docs/setup/README.md), then apply these rules. Before starting assigned work, every subagent must also read the [AI Agent Collaboration guide](~/.agents/docs/ai-agent-collaboration.md) and this file. The primary agent must include those readings in each subagent brief. The application name is **<current project name>**; use exactly `<current project name>:` for shared Local Mem0 records. All documentation, task decomposition, plans, ADRs, and skill instructions must be in English.\n\n## Required work sequence: Beads → Context7 → Mem0\n\nProcess these stages in order before engineering or documentation changes. State explicitly when a stage is not applicable. Complete the setup guide's tool health gate when onboarding or when context or tool health is stale. Installation output alone is not an operational check.\n\n## Application Problem Diagnosis\n\nWhen diagnosing an application error, unexpected behavior, or a reported failure, inspect and analyze the relevant application logs before proposing a root cause or implementing a fix. Do not assume logs are unavailable, stale, incomplete, or unreliable without checking.\n\n- Locate the logs for the correct local development application and runtime using the documented setup, application configuration, and container or service paths. Check relevant time windows, rotated files, and the request or record context when available.\n- Read the matching exception or error and its stack trace, then correlate it with the request, database effects, and current source. Treat logs as evidence to assess against those sources; do not dismiss them by assumption.\n- Keep log inspection targeted. Do not expose or copy credentials, customer data, full request payloads, or unrelated personal information into reports.\n- If relevant logs cannot be accessed or do not cover the failure, report the paths and commands checked, the exact sanitized error or access limitation, and what remains unverified. Separate source-based hypotheses from confirmed runtime findings, and ask for the access or evidence needed before making a consequential diagnosis.\n\n\n### 1. Beads — establish and decompose the work\n\n- **Project boundary — absolute:** Beads may be used only for the project currently assigned in this repository. Before any Beads operation, confirm the repository root and run `bd where`; verify the selected workspace and project prefix belong to this exact project. For <current project name>, the workspace must be this repository's `.beads` workspace with the `<current project name>` prefix. Never read, search, list, create, update, close, export, import, sync, diagnose, or otherwise handle another project's Beads data. For example, while working on Project 1, accessing Project 3's Beads workspace is strictly forbidden, even for a read-only lookup or because an issue ID appears relevant. If the workspace identity is wrong or uncertain, stop immediately; do not inspect or mutate that workspace. Resolve the current repository's configuration or report the blocker.\n- After verifying the project workspace, run `bd prime` when the workflow context is missing or stale. Find matching work with `bd ready`, `bd list`, or `bd show` in this same workspace; claim it before implementation or documentation work. Create an issue first when no match exists.\n- Decompose work in English and store every actionable task, its description, acceptance criteria, dependencies, blockers, and progress in Beads. Use child issues for separately assignable work.\n- Do not create or maintain task-decomposition or task-tracking documents under `<current project>/docs/tasks/`. Beads is the sole source of truth for task content and status. Existing files under `<current project>/docs/tasks/` are retained as historical references; do not treat them as current task records.\n- Link relevant OpenSpec artifacts from the Beads issue description, design, or notes. Keep OpenSpec's native workspace at the repository root; do not use its checklist as a substitute for Beads issues that represent actionable work.\n- Diagnose workspace failure with `bd where` only in the current repository context; report the exact sanitized error without accessing another project's workspace. Do not claim creation or updates when the CLI failed.\n\n### 2. Context7 — verify external documentation (if it needs)\n\n- For external library, framework, SDK, API, CLI, or cloud-service behavior, fetch current documentation before implementation or version-sensitive guidance, even for familiar technology.\n- Run the Context7 CLI from the host, not a Docker container. This is an exception to the containerized application-tooling rule.\n- Resolve the official library name first, select the exact relevant `/org/project` ID (versioned when available and applicable), then fetch one specific topic:\n\n  ```bash\n  npx ctx7@latest library \"<official library name>\" \"<specific question>\"\n  npx ctx7@latest docs <library-id> \"<one specific topic>\"\n  ```\n\n- Keep queries free of credentials and secrets. This stage is not applicable to general programming, internal business logic, or refactoring when external API behavior is not in question.\n- On DNS/network restrictions, request an approved retry outside the sandbox. For authentication/quota failures, propose `npx ctx7@latest login` or secure `CONTEXT7_API_KEY` configuration. Never label guessed behavior documentation-backed.\n\n### 3. Mem0 — retrieve reusable project knowledge\n\n- After Beads and Context7, use the [local-mem0 skill](~/.agents/skills/local-mem0/SKILL.md). Run its CLI from the host, never from a Docker container, because its service is reached through host loopback. Search using only relevant topic text; do not prepend the current project name. Decide from the task and returned records whether checking for the current project's exact name or prefix is needed. For project-specific decisions, or when results appear to mix projects, accept only records whose memory text starts with the exact `<current project name>:` prefix. For general guidance, project-name filtering may be unnecessary. Semantic similarity is not proof of project ownership.\n- Check relevant memories against the user request, current sources, and external documentation. Memories never override those sources.\n- Save only stable reusable lessons, each prefixed `<current project name>: `. Use `add --no-infer` and read back the returned ID with `get` to verify the prefix. Do not store secrets, transient status, duplicate canonical documentation, or other projects' details.\n\n### Graphify execution location\n\n- Run all Graphify CLI commands directly on the host, including `graphify query`, `graphify path`, `graphify explain`, and `graphify update .`. This is an explicit workflow-tool exception to the containerized application-tooling rule.\n- Do not run Graphify from Docker containers or wrap it in `docker exec`, `docker compose exec`, or `docker compose run`. Use the host CLI and the repository's `graphify-out/` graph.\n\n## Mandatory tool failures\n\nCheck Beads, Graphify, Local Mem0, OpenSpec, and Context7 as described in the setup guide. If any required tool fails, stop implementation, investigate through read-only diagnostics, capture the command and sanitized error, record a blocker in Beads when possible, and propose specific solutions. Do not silently bypass failures. Resume after resolution or explicit user authorization for a revised workflow. An approved successful sandbox retry resolves the restriction.\n\n## Documentation\n\nRead [<current project>/docs/DOCUMENTS.md](<current project>/docs/DOCUMENTS.md) for ownership and artifact conventions. Keep human-authored documentation and all AI Factory project artifacts under `<current project>/docs/`. Tool configuration stays discoverable in `<current project>/.ai-factory/config.yaml`; skill packages stay in agent skill directories. Do not create duplicate project manuals at root. All maintained documentation is English. Keep the root README as a landing page and use topic pages under `<current project>/docs/`. Any application change that alters behavior or visual appearance, adds functionality, or removes functionality MUST be documented in an ADR at `<current project>/docs/adr/<YYYY>/<MM>/<NNNN>-<short-title>.md`, using the decision date for the year and month. `<NNNN>` is a zero-padded four-digit serial that increases globally across all ADRs; do not restart it by month or year. Follow it with a hyphen and concise kebab-case title. Include the rationale and consequences, and add the record to `<current project>/docs/adr/README.md`. Preserve existing ADR filenames and links; apply this naming pattern to new records. ADRs preserve decision rationale and history; they do not replace Beads. Keep the complete actionable task, acceptance criteria, status, dependencies, blockers, and progress in the current project's Beads issue(s). Do not create or maintain task decomposition or status documents under `<current project>/docs/tasks/`; that workflow is retired. Existing files there remain historical references only.\n\n\n| Document                  | Path                                                                               | Description                                                                                                                      |\n|---------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| README                    | `<current project>/README.md`                                                      | Project page                                                                                                                     |\n| Documentation index       | `<current project>/docs/README.md`                                                 | Navigation and ownership                                                                                                         |\n| Setup and operations      | `<current project>/docs/setup/README.md`                                           | Environment and required tool checks                                                                                             |\n| AI agent collaboration    | `~/.agents/<current project>/docs/ai-agent-collaboration.md`                       | Delegation, subagent authority, and handoff                                                                                      |\n| Project description       | `<current project>/docs/DESCRIPTION.md`                                            | Purpose and implemented capabilities                                                                                             |\n| Architecture              | `<current project>/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`                                           | Components and source boundaries                                                                                                 |\n| Document conventions      | `<current project>/docs/DOCUMENTS.md`                                              | Canonical sources and AI Factory locations                                                                                       |\n| Decision index            | `<current project>/docs/adr/README.md`                                             | Durable decision history                                                                                                         |\n| Workflow decision         | `<current project>/docs/adr/YYYY/MM/NNNN-short-title.md`                           | Historical workflow decision superseded by ADR NNNN                                                                              |\n| Reviews                   | `<current project>/docs/reviews/YYYY/MM/NNNN-short-title.md`                       | Code review history                                                                                                              |\n| Playground                | `<current project>/docs/playground/README.md`                                      | Provisional experiments                                                                                                          |\n| OpenSpec                  | `<current project>/openspec/`                                                      | Tool-managed changes and specifications                                                                                          |\n| API reference             | `<current project>/docs/api.md`                                                    | Integration routes and authentication                                                                                            |\n| Configuration             | `<current project>/docs/configuration.md`                                          | Connections and environment settings                                                                                             |\n| Testing                   | `<current project>/docs/testing.md`                                                | Suites and verification limits                                                                                                   |\n| OpenAPI                   | `<current project>/<dir with app code>/openapi/openapi.yaml`                       | Swagger-compatible contract                                                                                                      |\n| InPost Shipping           | `<current project>/docs/modules/<module name>.md`                                  | To describe module work                                                                                                          |\n| Twig Template Library     | `<current project>/docs/libraries/<library name>.md`                               | To describe library work                                                                                                         |\n| Deployment                | `<current project>/docs/deployment.md`                                             | Docker and release flow                                                                                                          |\n| Database Schema Migration | `<current project>/docs/database/YYYY/MM/<YYYY-MM-DD-HH-mm-ss-migration-name>.sql` | SQL migrations for creating, altering, and dropping database schema objects (tables, columns, indexes, constraints, views, etc.) |\n| Rules                     | `docs/RULES.md`                                                                    | Universal project rules                                                                                                          |\n\nRecord significant decisions in `<current project>/docs/adr/` with context, date, status, alternatives, and consequences. Preserve accepted records; supersede them through linked successor ADRs. Keep actionable work and its decomposition in Beads. Store supporting project documentation, patches, and optional roadmap, research, rules, QA, references, and evolution artifacts under their configured `<current project>/docs/` paths. Create optional artifacts only when needed.\n\n## AI Context Files\n\n| File                        | Purpose             |\n|-----------------------------|---------------------|\n| `.ai-factory/rules/base.md` | Project conventions |\n| `.ai-factory/config.yaml`   | AI Factory settings |\n\n## Workflow and safety\n\n- **Escalate difficulty and uncertainty:** If you encounter a blocker, unexpected complexity, unclear or conflicting requirements, insufficient evidence, or a decision you cannot make confidently, stop the affected work and any work that depends on it. Perform only the read-only diagnosis needed to explain the situation, then contact the developer with the issue, evidence, what remains uncertain, and the specific help or decision needed. Wait for guidance before resuming. Do not guess, silently bypass a constraint, or make a consequential choice on the developer's behalf. A subagent must stop its changes and report the blocker to the primary agent for developer escalation.\n- Follow the conservative local-only Git profile. Do not commit, push, pull, or sync remotely unless explicitly requested. A missing Dolt remote warning is expected in this profile; do not add a remote to suppress it.\n- Preserve unrelated user changes and secrets. Do not deploy or send real Telegram messages without authorization.\n- Do not add or run application tests unless the user requests testing or verification. Mandatory tool health checks and invoked-skill documentation/skill reviews are separate from application tests.\n- Before handoff, close completed Beads issues, record remaining work and blockers, run only authorized quality checks, inspect `git status`, and report changed files and handoff state. Never close incomplete work.\n\n## Knowledge graph\n\n- Use `graphify query \"<question>\"` for codebase questions when `graphify-out/graph.json` exists; use `graphify path \"<A>\" \"<B>\"` for relationships and `graphify explain \"<concept>\"` for focused explanations.\n- Use `graphify-out/wiki/index.md` for broad navigation when present. Read `GRAPH_REPORT.md` for broad architecture review or when scoped queries are insufficient.\n- Verify graph evidence against current files; dirty output can follow hooks. Skip queries only when diagnosing stale/incorrect graph output or when the user asks.\n- After modifying code, run `graphify update .` on the host and report any refusal or failure; do not force an overwrite without diagnosing it.\n\n## Pick a tool for the question\n\n| Question                                                                                                | First tool               |\n|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------|\n| Exact route, text, config key, or error                                                                 | `rg`                     |\n| Changed behavior in history                                                                             | Git log/blame/diff       |\n| Rendered UI, browser console, network, or layout                                                        | `cua_repl` / `node_repl` |\n| Run JavaScript code, retreive DOM tree/snaphot, network trace and other system information for web page | Chrome DevTools MCP      |\n\n## Entire CLI — checkpoints and session history\n\nEntire records agent sessions as checkpoints tied to Git commits. Use it for *why/when/by which session* questions; Git stays authoritative for repository state, OpenSpec for requirements, Beads for task status. Run `entire` on the host from the repository root. Do not guess flags: read them with `entire agent-help [<command>]`.\n\n### Submodule rule — check first\n\nBefore any Entire or Git-history work in a repository that has `.gitmodules`, look for `<current project>/docs/AGENT_SUBMODULE_HISTORY.md`.\n\n- **If it exists**, read it and follow it for everything involving a `git submodule`: history, checkpoints, metadata export, and run records. Its rules override the generic guidance in this section.\n- **If it does not exist**, use this section only. Do not enable Entire inside a submodule or change a submodule's hooks, remotes, or configuration. Report the missing file when the task depends on submodule history.\n\n### Read-only commands (safe to run)\n\n```bash\nentire status                                         # enabled state, agents, active session\nentire checkpoint list --json                         # checkpoints on the current branch\nentire checkpoint list --pending --json               # work not yet condensed into a checkpoint\nentire checkpoint explain <checkpoint-id|commit-sha> --json   # metadata only, no transcript\nentire session current                                # active session for this worktree\nentire session info <session-id> --json\nentire why <file>[:line]                              # commit, checkpoint, and session behind a line\nentire search \"<topic>\" --json --compact              # needs `entire login`\n```\n\n### History for a task\n\n1. Get the Beads issue ID and any OpenSpec change name; search checkpoints and commits by them (`entire search`, `git log --grep`, or match `entire checkpoint list --json` entries to the commits that touched the task's files).\n2. Run `entire checkpoint explain <id> --json` for the intent, files touched, and token usage.\n3. Compare with `git log`, `git diff`, and `git status`. Treat Entire output as evidence, not as proof of correctness or authorship.\n\n### Two agents: Codex and Claude Code\n\nEntire is set up for exactly two agents, **Codex** and **Claude Code** (`entire status` lists them). Both may work in the same worktree.\n\n- Each agent has its own session ID. Use only the ID of your own session (`entire session current`); never attribute another agent's session or checkpoint to yourself.\n- A Git commit condenses **every** session with pending work in that worktree. One commit can therefore produce checkpoint data for both agents; do not assume a checkpoint contains only your work. Read `entire checkpoint explain <id> --json` to see which sessions and files it covers.\n- Each agent's hooks must be approved in its own tool (for Codex, `entire status` may report \"hook(s) need approval\"; that is the user's action via `/hooks`). Until approved, that agent's sessions may not be recorded. Report it; do not edit hook files.\n\n### Creating checkpoints\n\nThere is no command that creates a checkpoint. Agent hooks record session data locally; at a Git commit, Entire's Git hooks condense the pending work into a checkpoint and link it to the commit with an `Entire-Checkpoint` trailer. Entire never creates commits itself.\n\n- Commit only when the user or the active Git profile authorizes it, so a checkpoint is a by-product of an authorized commit. Never commit solely to produce a checkpoint.\n- `commit_linking` defaults to `prompt` (alternative: `always`). Entire may therefore ask whether to link the commit to a session. Do not change the setting; if a commit is blocked by or waits on that prompt, stop and report it.\n- After an authorized commit, confirm the checkpoint with `entire checkpoint list --json` or `entire checkpoint explain <commit-sha> --json`. Never invent a checkpoint ID.\n\n### Submodules: Entire stays out\n\nEntire is enabled in the workspace root only. Never run `entire enable` or any Entire setup in a submodule, and never change a submodule's Git hooks. Upstream Entire documentation does not describe submodule handling, so the following is an inference to verify on the first real commit: Entire's Git hooks are installed for the root repository, so a commit made inside a submodule creates no Entire checkpoint, and a root checkpoint sees the submodule only as a changed gitlink. Take submodule history from `git -C <submodule>` and from the project's `AGENT_SUBMODULE_HISTORY.md` (see the Submodule rule above).\n\n### Safety\n\n- Do not pass `--full`, `--transcript`, or `--raw-transcript` unless the user asks; transcripts can contain secrets and customer data.\n- `entire search` sends the query to the Entire service: keep it free of secrets and customer data.\n- Do not run `enable`, `disable`, `configure`, `clean`, `agent`, `login`, `logout`, or `checkpoint delete`; suggest them to the user.\n- Do not push checkpoints or sync Entire data without authorization.\n- If `entire` is missing or fails, continue without it, report the exact sanitized error, and do not install it automatically.\n\n<!-- bv-agent-instructions-v7 -->\n\n---\n\n## Beads Workflow Integration\n\nThis project uses a Beads tracker—either the Go `bd` CLI or the Rust `br` CLI—for issue tracking, plus [beads_viewer](https://github.com/Dicklesworthstone/beads_viewer) (`bv`) for graph-aware triage. Issues are stored in `.beads/`. `bv` auto-discovers supported JSONL exports, including `.beads/issues.jsonl` and legacy `.beads/beads.jsonl`.\n\n**Project boundary:** The absolute project-isolation rule above also applies to `bv` and every suggested working-directory or tracker route. Use `bv` only for the verified current-project snapshot; reject any route that points outside this repository. This applies to primary agents and subagents.\n\n**Choose the tracker CLI from this repository's instructions and configuration.** Use `bd` commands in a Go Beads workspace and `br` commands in a beads_rust workspace. Do not run both trackers against the same workspace or infer the tracker solely from the JSONL filename.\n\n### Using bv as an AI sidecar\n\nbv is a graph-aware triage engine for Beads projects. Instead of parsing .beads/issues.jsonl / .beads/beads.jsonl directly or hallucinating graph traversal, use robot flags for deterministic, dependency-aware outputs with precomputed metrics (PageRank, betweenness, critical path, cycles, HITS, eigenvector, k-core).\n\n**Scope boundary:** bv handles *what to work on* (triage, priority, planning) inside the verified current-project snapshot only. The selected tracker CLI (`bd` or `br`) handles creating, claiming, modifying, and closing beads inside the same project workspace. Reject any suggested working-directory or tracker route that points outside the current project.\n\n**CRITICAL: Use ONLY --robot-* flags. Bare bv launches an interactive TUI that blocks your session.**\n\n#### The Workflow: Start With Triage\n\n**`bv --robot-triage` is your single entry point.** Its `triage` object contains:\n- `quick_ref`: at-a-glance counts + top 3 picks\n- `recommendations`: ranked actionable items with scores, reasons, unblock info\n- `quick_wins`: low-effort high-impact items\n- `blockers_to_clear`: items that unblock the most downstream work\n- `project_health`: status/type/priority distributions, graph metrics\n- `commands`: copy-paste shell commands for next steps\n\n```bash\nbv --robot-triage        # THE MEGA-COMMAND: start here\nbv --robot-next          # Minimal: just the single top pick + claim command\n\n# TOON output (--format toon): a compact tabular encoding. Measured on this\n# repository it is 7% smaller than JSON for --robot-graph but 9-15% LARGER for\n# nested payloads (--robot-triage, --robot-plan, --robot-insights,\n# --robot-label-health); use --stats to see both sizes before adopting it.\n# TOON encoding shells out to the tru binary. With no encoder installed,\n# --format toon prints a fallback warning, emits JSON with output_format \"json\",\n# and --stats prints no sizes at all.\nbv --robot-graph --format toon\nbv --robot-triage --format toon --stats\n```\n\nRecommendations can include blocked or assigned work; `triage.quick_ref.top_picks` reflects snapshot readiness. A suggested action records its original local ID, working directory, and tracker route. Use that route rather than a namespaced display ID or an unrelated current directory. Inspect current tracker state before execution: analysis does not reserve work or guarantee that a later claim succeeds.\n\n#### Other bv Commands\n\n| Command                                             | Returns                                                                               |\n|-----------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| `--robot-plan`                                      | Parallel execution tracks with unblocks lists                                         |\n| `--robot-priority`                                  | Priority misalignment detection with confidence                                       |\n| `--robot-insights`                                  | Full metrics: PageRank, betweenness, HITS, eigenvector, critical path, cycles, k-core |\n| `--robot-alerts`                                    | Stale issues, blocking cascades, priority mismatches                                  |\n| `--robot-suggest`                                   | Hygiene: duplicates, missing deps, label suggestions, cycle breaks                    |\n| `--robot-diff --diff-since <ref>`                   | Changes since ref: new/closed/modified issues                                         |\n| `--robot-graph [--graph-format=json\\|dot\\|mermaid]` | Dependency graph export                                                               |\n\nRobot analysis commands default to JSON; `--format toon` selects TOON, and `--robot-help` defaults to text. In JSON mode, `--graph-format=dot` or `mermaid` puts diagram text in the `graph` field (`bv --robot-graph --graph-format=dot | jq -r .graph`).\n\n#### Scoping & Filtering\n\n```bash\nbv --robot-plan --label backend              # Scope to label's subgraph\nbv --robot-insights --as-of HEAD~30          # Historical point-in-time\nbv --recipe actionable --robot-plan          # Pre-filter: ready to work (no blockers)\nbv --recipe high-impact --robot-triage       # Pre-filter: top PageRank scores\n```\n\n### Tracker Commands for Issue Management\n\nUse exactly one command family, matching the tracker configured for the repository.\n\n#### Rust beads_rust (`br`)\n\nUse `br` 0.6.0 or newer when executing saved claim commands. It rechecks\ndeferred status and future `defer_until` values when the claim runs, so a\nrecommendation captured before a deferral cannot bypass it. This requirement\napplies to executing tracker claims.\n\n```bash\nbr ready --json                       # Show issues ready to work (no blockers)\nbr list --status=open --json          # All open issues\nbr show <id> --json                   # Full issue details with dependencies\nbr create --title=\"...\" --type=task --priority=2 --json\nbr update <id> --claim --json         # Claim for the current actor and start work\nbr close <id> --reason=\"Completed\" --json\nbr close <id1> <id2> --reason=\"Completed\" --json\nbr sync --flush-only                  # Export DB to JSONL after Beads mutations\n```\n\n#### Go Beads (`bd`)\n\n```bash\nbd ready --json                       # Show issues ready to work\nbd show <id> --json                   # Full issue details\nbd create \"...\" -t task -p 2 --json\nbd update <id> --claim --json         # Atomically claim work\nbd close <id> --json\nbd dep add <issue> <depends-on>\nbd export -o .beads/issues.jsonl        # Refresh the compatibility export read by bv\n```\n\n### Workflow Pattern\n\n1. **Triage**: Run `bv --robot-triage` to find the highest-impact actionable work\n2. **Verify**: Check the selected tracker's `show`/`ready` output before claiming\n3. **Claim**: Use `br update <id> --claim --json` or `bd update <id> --claim --json`\n4. **Work**: Implement the task\n5. **Complete**: Use the selected tracker's `close` command\n6. **Refresh for bv**: Run `br sync --flush-only` or the `bd export` command above so the JSONL export is current\n\n### Key Concepts\n\n- **Dependencies**: Issues can block other issues. `br ready --json` and `bd ready --json` show unblocked work.\n- **Priority**: P0=critical, P1=high, P2=medium, P3=low, P4=backlog (use numbers 0-4, not words)\n- **Types**: task, bug, feature, epic, chore, docs, question\n- **Blocking**: Use `br dep add <issue> <depends-on>` or `bd dep add <issue> <depends-on>` to add dependencies\n\n### Git Policy\n\nTracker commands do not grant permission to commit or push application code. Follow this repository's own git and tracker instructions before staging, committing, syncing, or pushing. If the repository says \"commit only when asked,\" that rule overrides any generic workflow advice.\n\n<!-- end-bv-agent-instructions -->\n\n--- project-doc ---\n\n# Project Instructions for AI Agents\n\nThis is the canonical project-wide instruction file. `CLAUDE.md` is a pointer to this file; keep shared rules here only.\n\n## Start Here\n\nThe shared workflow (Beads → Context7 → Mem0, tool health gate, subagent briefing, documentation and ADR rules, Git profile, testing policy) is defined in `~/.codex/AGENTS.md` and is not repeated here. Read [docs/setup/README.md](docs/setup/README.md) for this project's environment.\n\nThe application name is **Strateg Export Products**. Use exactly `Strateg Export Products: ` as the prefix for shared Local Mem0 records.\n\n## Coding Conventions\n\n1. You generate the best answers.\n2. You are a very good know the [revolution/laravel-google-sheets](https://github.com/invokable/laravel-google-sheets) library.\n3. You know the [openai-php/client](https://github.com/openai-php/client) php library very well.\n4. You can develop a Telegram bot using the [Telegram bot docs](https://core.telegram.org/bots/api).\n5. You know the [longman/telegram-bot](https://github.com/php-telegram-bot/core) php library very well.\n6. You know the [phpoffice/phpspreadsheet](https://phpspreadsheet.readthedocs.io/en/latest/) php library very well.\n7. Follow the coding style if you are processing a finished piece of code.\n8. Write comments only on `English language`.\n9. Use `camelCase` for writing function names.\n10. Use `Snake case` for writing variables for `PHP`.\n11. Use `camelCase` for writing variables for `JS/TS`.\n12. Use `PascalCase` for writing `Classes`, `Interfaces`, `Traits`, and `Enums` names.\n13. We work in a `Linux environment`.\n14. Be `attentive to the details of the question` to you and be attentive to the details of our dialogue.\n15. Pay attention to `small (subtle) details` that can be very important in solving the problem.\n16. Before returning the answer, `analyze your prepared answer and think again whether it is correct`!\n17. If you are not sure about the answer, you do not understand something in my message, you have doubts about my message or need some details for the best answer - then ask me!\n18. For logging, use the follow code:\n```php\n\\Illuminate\\Support\\Facades\\Log::channel('stack')->$method_name(...); // for errors and warnings\n\\Illuminate\\Support\\Facades\\Log::channel('daily')->$method_name(...); // for other logs\n```\n\n## Safety dev workflow\n\n- You can use system variables from `/home/kamaz/www/strateg-projects/strateg-export-products/.docker/dev/env/`\n- Never run: `docker system prune`, `docker volume rm`, `DROP DATABASE`, `TRUNCATE`\n- Prefer SELECT-only unless explicitly asked to modify data\n- DB read-only queries:\n    * `docker compose -f /home/kamaz/www/strateg-projects/strateg-export-products/.docker/dev/docker-compose.yml exec -T dev-strateg-export-products-postgresql sh -lc 'psql -U \"${POSTGRES_SIMPLE_USER}\" -d \"${POSTGRES_DB}\" -c \"SELECT ...'`\"\n- To execute commands related to the application or server, use the following command:\n    * `docker compose -f /home/kamaz/www/strateg-projects/strateg-export-products/.docker/dev/docker-compose.yml exec -T dev-strateg-export-products-php-fpm <command and args>`. The Docker container already has everything you need installed, including PHP.\n- You are strictly prohibited from `installing` or `updating` `composer` and `npm dependencies`, or `Docker images` yourself! You are strictly prohibited from restarting `Docker containers`!\n  To perform these operations, you must `notify` the developer about them. The developer will then follow all instructions and `notify` you when they are ready. After that, you can continue working.\n\n# Laravel Boost Guidelines\n\nThe Laravel Boost guidelines are specifically curated by Laravel maintainers for this application. These guidelines should be followed closely to enhance the user's satisfaction building Laravel applications.\n\n## Foundational Context\nThis application is a Laravel application and its main Laravel ecosystems package & versions are below. You are an expert with them all. Ensure you abide by these specific packages & versions.\n\n- PHP - 8.5\n- PostgreSQL - 18.1\n- Node.js - 25.6.1\n- filament/filament (FILAMENT) - v5\n- laravel/framework (LARAVEL) - v13.33\n- laravel/prompts (PROMPTS) - v0.3\n- livewire/livewire (LIVEWIRE) - v4.4\n- larastan/larastan (LARASTAN) - v3.12\n- laravel/boost (BOOST) - v2.10\n- laravel/mcp (MCP) - v1\n- laravel/pint (PINT) - v1.32\n- laravel/sail (SAIL) - v1.68\n- phpunit/phpunit (PHPUNIT) - v13.3\n- rector/rector (RECTOR) - v2.6\n- tailwindcss (TAILWINDCSS) - v4.3\n- phpoffice/phpspreadsheet - v5.10\n- revolution/laravel-google-sheets - v7.2\n\n## Documentation\n\n| Document                          | Path                                        | Description                                     |\n|-----------------------------------|---------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------|\n| Documentation Index               | `docs/README.md`                            | Navigation and ownership                        |\n| Setup and Operations              | `docs/setup/README.md`                      | Environment and required tool checks            |\n| Document Conventions              | `docs/DOCUMENTS.md`                         | Canonical sources and artifact locations        |\n| Decisions (ADR)                   | `docs/adr/README.md`                        | Durable decision history                        |\n| Getting Started                   | `docs/getting-started.md`                   | Installation and local startup                  |\n| Architecture                      | `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`                      | Module boundaries and dependency rules          |\n| Configuration                     | `docs/configuration.md`                     | Environment, stores, and queues                 |\n| API Reference                     | `docs/api.md`                               | Routes and authentication                       |\n| Testing                           | `docs/testing.md`                           | Suites and verification limits                  |\n| Deployment                        | `docs/deployment.md`                        | Docker and release flow                         |\n| Helper Functions                  | `docs/helpers.md`                           | Shared helper reference                         |\n| Supports Reference                | `docs/supports.md`                          | All Supports service methods                    |\n| Product Data Workflows            | `docs/product-data-workflows.md`            | Import, export, update, delete, and restore     |\n| Product Import                    | `docs/product-import-batch-resource.md`     | Import batches and items                        |\n| Product Updates                   | `docs/product-update-batch-resource.md`     | Batch and API updates                           |\n| Product Deletes                   | `docs/product-delete-batch-resource.md`     | Remote shop deletion batches                    |\n| Product Catalog                   | `docs/product-resource.md`                  | Local catalog operations                        |\n| Remote API Integration            | `docs/remote-api-integration.md`            | Outbound shop requests and API status           |\n| OpenCart API Token Lifecycle      | `docs/opencart-api-token-lifecycle.md`      | Session token login, reuse, and refresh         |\n| OpenAPI Specification             | `docs/openapi.yaml`                         | Swagger-readable integration contract           |\n| Categories                        | `docs/category-resource.md`                 | Category hierarchy and binding                  |\n| Attributes                        | `docs/attribute-resource.md`                | Attribute management                            |\n| Shops                             | `docs/shop-resource.md`                     | External shop configuration                     |\n| Shop Languages                    | `docs/shop-language-resource.md`            | Shop language settings                          |\n| Users                             | `docs/user-resource.md`                     | Admin user management                           |\n| Project Description               | `docs/DESCRIPTION.md`                       | Business logic, modules, and tables             |\n| Security                          | `docs/SECURITY.md`                          | Secrets, access rules, and known risks          |\n| Roadmap                           | `docs/ROADMAP.md`                           | Completed and planned milestones                |\n| Project Rules                     | `docs/RULES.md`                             | Coding and workflow rules                       |\n\n## Agent Rules\n\n- Do not combine independent shell commands into a single line when each step should be verified separately.\n    - Incorrect: `git checkout main && git pull`\n    - Correct: first `git checkout main`, then `git pull origin main`\n- Use the project Docker containers for runtime checks and direct database access (run from the repository root):\n    * PHP and Artisan: `docker compose -f .docker/dev/docker-compose.yml exec -T dev-strateg-export-products-php-fpm <command>`\n    * PostgreSQL client: see the read-only query command in \"Safety dev workflow\". The database is PostgreSQL, not MariaDB.\n    * Do not rely on host PHP or `psql` binaries.\n- Host paths and container paths:\n    * `app-code/app` -> `/var/webroot/sites/export-products.strateg.ua/app`\n    * `app-code/httpdocs` -> `/var/webroot/sites/export-products.strateg.ua/httpdocs`\n    * `app-code/storage` -> `/var/webroot/sites/export-products.strateg.ua/storage`\n    Use the container path for PHP CLI commands such as linting.\n- Read database credentials from `.docker/dev/env/` or the container environment; never write real credentials into documentation, skills, committed shell commands, or logs.\n- Queued jobs are processed by the `cron` container (`schedule:run` every minute), not by a separate worker.\n\n## Security & Configuration\n\nDo not commit credentials, production configuration, generated runtime data, or local environment files.\n\n## AI Context Files\n\n| File                      | Purpose                                                                                       |\n|---------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| `docs/DESCRIPTION.md`     | High-level project purpose, workflows, data model map, and where to look first when debugging |\n| `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`    | Architecture decisions, module boundaries, dependency rules, and workflow ownership           |\n| `docs/patches/*.md`       | Important regression history, business invariants, and lessons from previous fixes            |\n| `docs/RULES.md`           | Universal project rules                                                                       |\n| `docs/rules/base.md`      | Project conventions for AI-assisted changes                                                   |\n| `.ai-factory/config.yaml` | AI Factory settings and artifact paths                                                        |\n\n## Project Overrides of Generated Guidelines\n\nThe generated Laravel Boost guidelines below are subordinate to this section and to `~/.codex/AGENTS.md` (workflow, documentation, ADR, Git, and testing policy). Where the guidelines conflict with them:\n\n- **Tests:** the guidelines' \"write, update, or run tests\" steps (including \"run the singular test\" and \"ask whether to run the whole suite\") apply only when the user requests testing. Run requested tests in the PHP container.\n- **Verification scripts:** do not create ad hoc verification scripts. Use `tinker` or `database-query` only for read-only diagnosis.\n- **Formatting:** run `vendor/bin/pint --dirty` in the PHP container only when quality checks are authorized.\n- **Documentation:** documentation is required, not \"only on request\"; follow `docs/DOCUMENTS.md`.\n\n<laravel-boost-guidelines>\n=== foundation rules ===\n\n## Conventions\n- You must follow all existing code conventions used in this application. When creating or editing a file, check sibling files for the correct structure, approach, and naming.\n- Use descriptive names for variables and methods. For example, `isRegisteredForDiscounts`, not `discount()`.\n- Check for existing components to reuse before writing a new one.\n\n## Verification Scripts\n- Do not create ad hoc verification scripts. Use `tinker` only for read-only diagnosis. Add or run tests only when the user requests them (see \"Project Overrides of Generated Guidelines\").\n\n## Application Structure & Architecture\n- Stick to existing directory structure; don't create new base folders without approval.\n- Do not change the application's dependencies without approval.\n\n### Project-specific product workflows\n- This is a modular Laravel monolith. Keep import, binding, export, update, delete, restore, translation, and remote-shop integration rules in their existing jobs and services.\n- Product import entry points are Excel upload, Google Sheets URL, and the Filament admin form. The import preparation job is `app-code/app/app/Jobs/ProcessProductImportBatchJob.php`.\n- Excel and Google Sheets imports use the required sheets `Product`, `Description`, `Image`, `Product Category`, `Product Attribute`, `Seo Url`, `Special`, and `Discount`.\n- Long-running operations must use batch and item records with persisted statuses, counters, payloads, and error audit metadata. Do not hide row failures in notifications only.\n- `spme_product_shop` is the authoritative local product-to-shop binding. `external_product_id` is required for remote update, delete, and restore operations.\n- `family_ulid` tracks one logical product across shop-specific duplicate rows. Preserve it when changing binding or duplication behavior.\n- Use `ProductPayloadBuilderService` for remote product payloads and the queue services for export, update, delete, and restore preparation. Filament resources should collect input and dispatch actions, not implement remote API rules.\n- Shop-scoped categories, attributes, manufacturers, brands, languages, and SEO rows must be resolved for the selected `shop_id` and active shop languages.\n- The project has no inbound `routes/api.php`. `docs/openapi.yaml` documents the configurable outbound remote-shop integration; each remote shop's actual contract remains authoritative.\n\n### Current code locations\n- Admin UI: `app-code/app/app/Filament/Resources/`\n- Queued workflows: `app-code/app/app/Jobs/`\n- Product application services: `app-code/app/app/Services/Products/`\n- Shared product, translation, deletion, restore, and SEO services: `app-code/app/app/Supports/Services/`\n- Shared helpers: `app-code/app/app/Supports/helpers.php`\n- Tests: `app-code/app/tests/`\n\n## Frontend Bundling\n- If the user doesn't see a frontend change reflected in the UI, it could mean they need to run `npm run build`, `npm run dev`, or `composer run dev`. Ask them.\n\n## Replies\n- Be concise in your explanations - focus on what's important rather than explaining obvious details.\n\n## Documentation Files\n- Project documentation is required. Follow `docs/DOCUMENTS.md` and \"Project Overrides of Generated Guidelines\"; do not create task documents (tasks live in Beads).\n\n=== boost rules ===\n\n## Laravel Boost\n- Laravel Boost is an MCP server that comes with powerful tools designed specifically for this application. Use them.\n\n## Artisan\n- Use the `list-artisan-commands` tool when you need to call an Artisan command to double-check the available parameters.\n\n## URLs\n- Whenever you share a project URL with the user, you should use the `get-absolute-url` tool to ensure you're using the correct scheme, domain/IP, and port.\n\n## Tinker / Debugging\n- You should use the `tinker` tool when you need to execute PHP to debug code or query Eloquent models directly.\n- Use the `database-query` tool when you only need to read from the database.\n\n## Reading Browser Logs With the `browser-logs` Tool\n- You can read browser logs, errors, and exceptions using the `browser-logs` tool from Boost.\n- Only recent browser logs will be useful - ignore old logs.\n\n## Searching Documentation (Critically Important)\n- Boost comes with a powerful `search-docs` tool you should use before any other approaches when dealing with Laravel or Laravel ecosystem packages. This tool automatically passes a list of installed packages and their versions to the remote Boost API, so it returns only version-specific documentation for the user's circumstance. You should pass an array of packages to filter on if you know you need docs for particular packages.\n- The `search-docs` tool is perfect for all Laravel-related packages, including Laravel, Inertia, Livewire, Filament, Tailwind, Pest, Nova, Nightwatch, etc.\n- You must use this tool to search for Laravel ecosystem documentation before falling back to other approaches.\n- Search the documentation before making code changes to ensure we are taking the correct approach.\n- Use multiple, broad, simple, topic-based queries to start. For example: `['rate limiting', 'routing rate limiting', 'routing']`.\n- Do not add package names to queries; package information is already shared. For example, use `test resource table`, not `filament 4 test resource table`.\n\n### Available Search Syntax\n- You can and should pass multiple queries at once. The most relevant results will be returned first.\n\n1. Simple Word Searches with auto-stemming - query=authentication - finds 'authenticate' and 'auth'.\n2. Multiple Words (AND Logic) - query=rate limit - finds knowledge containing both \"rate\" AND \"limit\".\n3. Quoted Phrases (Exact Position) - query=\"infinite scroll\" - words must be adjacent and in that order.\n4. Mixed Queries - query=middleware \"rate limit\" - \"middleware\" AND exact phrase \"rate limit\".\n5. Multiple Queries - queries=[\"authentication\", \"middleware\"] - ANY of these terms.\n\n=== php rules ===\n\n## PHP\n\n- Always use strict typing at the head of a `.php` file: `declare(strict_types=1);`.\n- Always use curly braces for control structures, even if it has one line.\n\n### Constructors\n- Use PHP 8 constructor property promotion in `__construct()`.\n      <code-snippet name=\"Constructor Property Promotion Example\" lang=\"php\">\n          public function __construct(public GitHub $github) { }\n      </code-snippet>\n- Do not allow empty `__construct()` methods with zero parameters unless the constructor is private.\n\n### Type Declarations\n- Always use explicit return type declarations for methods and functions.\n- Use appropriate PHP type hints for method parameters.\n\n<code-snippet name=\"Explicit Return Types and Method Params\" lang=\"php\">\n    protected function isAccessible(User $user, ?string $path = null): bool\n    {\n        ...\n    }\n</code-snippet>\n\n## Comments\n- Prefer PHPDoc blocks over inline comments. Never use comments within the code itself unless there is something very complex going on.\n\n## PHPDoc Blocks\n- Add useful array shape type definitions for arrays when appropriate.\n\n## Enums\n- That being said, keys in an Enum should follow existing application Enum conventions.\n\n=== laravel/core rules ===\n\n## Do Things the Laravel Way\n\n- Use `php artisan make:` commands to create new files (i.e. migrations, controllers, models, etc.). You can list available Artisan commands using the `list-artisan-commands` tool.\n- If you're creating a generic PHP class, use `php artisan make:class`.\n- Pass `--no-interaction` to all Artisan commands to ensure they work without user input. You should also pass the correct `--options` to ensure correct behavior.\n\n### Database\n- Always use proper Eloquent relationship methods with return type hints. Prefer relationship methods over raw queries or manual joins.\n- Use Eloquent models and relationships before suggesting raw database queries.\n- Avoid `DB::`; prefer `Model::query()`. Generate code that leverages Laravel's ORM capabilities rather than bypassing them.\n- Generate code that prevents N+1 query problems by using eager loading.\n- Use Laravel's query builder for very complex database operations.\n\n### Model Creation\n- When creating new models, create useful factories and seeders for them too. Ask the user if they need any other things, using `list-artisan-commands` to check the available options to `php artisan make:model`.\n\n### APIs & Eloquent Resources\n- For APIs, default to using Eloquent API Resources and API versioning unless existing API routes do not, then you should follow existing application convention.\n\n### Controllers & Validation\n- Always create Form Request classes for validation rather than inline validation in controllers. Include both validation rules and custom error messages.\n- Check sibling Form Requests to see if the application uses array or string based validation rules.\n\n### Queues\n- Use queued jobs for time-consuming operations with the `ShouldQueue` interface.\n\n### Authentication & Authorization\n- Use Laravel's built-in authentication and authorization features (gates, policies, Sanctum, etc.).\n\n### URL Generation\n- When generating links to other pages, prefer named routes and the `route()` function.\n\n### Configuration\n- Use environment variables only in configuration files - never use the `env()` function directly outside of config files. Always use `config('app.name')`, not `env('APP_NAME')`.\n\n### Testing\n- When creating models for tests, use the factories for the models. Check if the factory has custom states that can be used before manually setting up the model.\n- Faker: Use methods such as `$this->faker->word()` or `fake()->randomDigit()`. Follow existing conventions whether to use `$this->faker` or `fake()`.\n- When creating tests, make use of `php artisan make:test [options] {name}` to create a feature test, and pass `--unit` to create a unit test. Most tests should be feature tests.\n\n### Vite Error\n- If you receive an \"Illuminate\\Foundation\\ViteException: Unable to locate file in Vite manifest\" error, you can run `npm run build` or ask the user to run `npm run dev` or `composer run dev`.\n\n=== laravel/v13 rules ===\n\n## Laravel 13\n\n- Use the `search-docs` tool to get version-specific documentation.\n- Since Laravel 11, Laravel has a new streamlined file structure which this project uses.\n\n### Laravel 13 Structure\n- In Laravel 13, middleware are no longer registered in `app/Http/Kernel.php`.\n- Middleware are configured declaratively in `bootstrap/app.php` using `Application::configure()->withMiddleware()`.\n- `bootstrap/app.php` is the file to register middleware, exceptions, and routing files.\n- `bootstrap/providers.php` contains application specific service providers.\n- The `app\\Console\\Kernel.php` file no longer exists; use `bootstrap/app.php` or `routes/console.php` for console configuration.\n- Console commands in `app/Console/Commands/` are automatically available and do not require manual registration.\n\n### Database\n- When modifying a column, the migration must include all of the attributes that were previously defined on the column. Otherwise, they will be dropped and lost.\n- Laravel 13 allows limiting eagerly loaded records natively, without external packages: `$query->latest()->limit(10);`.\n\n### Models\n- Casts can and likely should be set in a `casts()` method on a model rather than the `$casts` property. Follow existing conventions from other models.\n\n=== livewire/core rules ===\n\n## Livewire\n\n- Use the `search-docs` tool to find exact version-specific documentation for how to write Livewire and Livewire tests.\n- Use the `php artisan make:livewire [Posts\\CreatePost]` Artisan command to create new components.\n- State should live on the server, with the UI reflecting it.\n- All Livewire requests hit the Laravel backend; they're like regular HTTP requests. Always validate form data and run authorization checks in Livewire actions.\n\n## Livewire Best Practices\n- Livewire components require a single root element.\n- Use `wire:loading` and `wire:dirty` for delightful loading states.\n- Add `wire:key` in loops:\n\n```blade\n    @foreach ($items as $item)\n        <div wire:key=\"item-{{ $item->id }}\">\n            {{ $item->name }}\n        </div>\n    @endforeach\n```\n\n- Prefer lifecycle hooks like `mount()`, `updatedFoo()` for initialization and reactive side effects:\n\n<code-snippet name=\"Lifecycle Hook Examples\" lang=\"php\">\n    public function mount(User $user) { $this->user = $user; }\n    public function updatedSearch() { $this->resetPage(); }\n</code-snippet>\n\n## Testing Livewire\n\n<code-snippet name=\"Example Livewire Component Test\" lang=\"php\">\n    Livewire::test(Counter::class)\n        ->assertSet('count', 0)\n        ->call('increment')\n        ->assertSet('count', 1)\n        ->assertSee(1)\n        ->assertStatus(200);\n</code-snippet>\n\n<code-snippet name=\"Testing Livewire Component Exists on Page\" lang=\"php\">\n    $this->get('/posts/create')\n    ->assertSeeLivewire(CreatePost::class);\n</code-snippet>\n\n=== livewire/v4 rules ===\n\n## Livewire 4\n\n### Upgrade Notes\n- These rules are for Livewire 4. Prefer Livewire 4 documentation and examples, not v2/v3 snippets from memory.\n- Components still typically live in the `App\\Livewire` namespace for class-based components.\n- Use `wire:model.live` for real-time updates. Plain `wire:model` remains deferred by default.\n- Use `$this->dispatch()` for events and `assertDispatched()` in tests.\n- Prefer `Route::livewire()` when routing directly to Livewire components, especially for view-based or single-file components.\n- Do not hardcode assumptions about `/livewire/` endpoints. In Livewire 4 the endpoint prefix is hash-based, like `/livewire-{hash}/...`.\n\n### JavaScript And Directives\n- `wire:show`, `wire:transition`, `wire:cloak`, `wire:offline`, `wire:target`, and `wire:intersect` are available. Use the docs for the exact syntax before implementing non-trivial behavior.\n- Alpine is bundled with Livewire; do not install or include Alpine separately unless the project already has a justified custom setup.\n- Prefer the new JavaScript APIs in Livewire 4:\n  - Use `$wire.$js.actionName = () => { ... }` instead of deprecated `$wire.$js('actionName', ...)`.\n  - Use `Livewire.interceptRequest()` / `Livewire.interceptMessage()` instead of deprecated `Livewire.hook('request', ...)` / `Livewire.hook('commit', ...)` for new code.\n\n### Initialization Example\n- Hook into `livewire:init` for client-side setup:\n\n<code-snippet name=\"Livewire Init Example\" lang=\"js\">\n    document.addEventListener('livewire:init', function () {\n        Livewire.interceptRequest(({ onError }) => {\n            onError(({ response, preventDefault }) => {\n                if (response && response.status === 419) {\n                    preventDefault();\n                    alert('Your session expired');\n                }\n            });\n        });\n    });\n</code-snippet>\n\n=== pint/core rules ===\n\n## Laravel Pint Code Formatter\n\n- When the user authorizes quality checks, run `vendor/bin/pint --dirty` in the PHP container before finalizing changes to ensure your code matches the project's expected style.\n- Do not run `vendor/bin/pint --test`, simply run `vendor/bin/pint` to fix any formatting issues.\n\n=== phpunit/core rules ===\n\n## PHPUnit\n\n- This application uses PHPUnit for testing. All tests must be written as PHPUnit classes. Use `php artisan make:test --phpunit {name}` to create a new test.\n- If you see a test using \"Pest\", convert it to PHPUnit.\n- Add, update, or run tests only when the user requests testing or verification. When requested, run the singular updated test first, then ask whether to run the entire suite.\n- Tests should test all of the happy paths, failure paths, and weird paths.\n- You must not remove any tests or test files from the tests directory without approval. These are not temporary or helper files; these are core to the application.\n\n### Running Tests\n- Run the minimal number of tests, using an appropriate filter, before finalizing.\n- To run all tests: `php artisan test --compact`.\n- To run all tests in a file: `php artisan test --compact tests/Feature/ExampleTest.php`.\n- To filter on a particular test name: `php artisan test --compact --filter=testName` (recommended after making a change to a related file).\n\n=== tailwindcss/core rules ===\n\n## Tailwind CSS\n\n- Use Tailwind CSS classes to style HTML; check and use existing Tailwind conventions within the project before writing your own.\n- Offer to extract repeated patterns into components that match the project's conventions (i.e. Blade, JSX, Vue, etc.).\n- Think through class placement, order, priority, and defaults. Remove redundant classes, add classes to parent or child carefully to limit repetition, and group elements logically.\n- You can use the `search-docs` tool to get exact examples from the official documentation when needed.\n\n### Spacing\n- When listing items, use gap utilities for spacing; don't use margins.\n\n<code-snippet name=\"Valid Flex Gap Spacing Example\" lang=\"html\">\n    <div class=\"flex gap-8\">\n        <div>Superior</div>\n        <div>Michigan</div>\n        <div>Erie</div>\n    </div>\n</code-snippet>\n\n### Dark Mode\n- If existing pages and components support dark mode, new pages and components must support dark mode in a similar way, typically using `dark:`.\n\n=== tailwindcss/v4 rules ===\n\n## Tailwind CSS 4\n\n- Always use Tailwind CSS v4; do not use the deprecated utilities.\n- `corePlugins` is not supported in Tailwind v4.\n- In Tailwind v4, configuration is CSS-first using the `@theme` directive — no separate `tailwind.config.js` file is needed.\n\n<code-snippet name=\"Extending Theme in CSS\" lang=\"css\">\n    @theme {\n      --color-brand: oklch(0.72 0.11 178);\n    }\n</code-snippet>\n\n- In Tailwind v4, you import Tailwind using a regular CSS `@import` statement, not using the `@tailwind` directives used in v3:\n\n<code-snippet name=\"Tailwind v4 Import Tailwind Diff\" lang=\"diff\">\n   - @tailwind base;\n   - @tailwind components;\n   - @tailwind utilities;\n   + @import \"tailwindcss\";\n</code-snippet>\n\n### Replaced Utilities\n- Tailwind v4 removed deprecated utilities. Do not use the deprecated option; use the replacement.\n- Opacity values are still numeric.\n\n\n| Deprecated            | Replacement          |\n|-----------------------|----------------------|\n| bg-opacity-*          | bg-black/*           |\n| text-opacity-*        | text-black/*         |\n| border-opacity-*      | border-black/*       |\n| divide-opacity-*      | divide-black/*       |\n| ring-opacity-*        | ring-black/*         |\n| placeholder-opacity-* | placeholder-black/*  |\n| flex-shrink-*         | shrink-*             |\n| flex-grow-*           | grow-*               |\n| overflow-ellipsis     | text-ellipsis        |\n| decoration-slice      | box-decoration-slice |\n| decoration-clone      | box-decoration-clone |\n\n=== filament/filament rules ===\n\n## Filament\n\n- Filament is used by this application. Follow existing conventions for how and where it's implemented.\n- Filament is a Server-Driven UI (SDUI) framework for Laravel that lets you define user interfaces in PHP using structured configuration objects. Built on Livewire, Alpine.js, and Tailwind CSS.\n- Use the `search-docs` tool for official documentation on Artisan commands, code examples, testing, relationships, and idiomatic practices.\n- Filament v5 requires Livewire v4+. When guidance conflicts, prefer the Filament 5 and Livewire 4 docs together.\n\n### Artisan\n\n- Use Filament-specific Artisan commands to create files. Find them with `list-artisan-commands` or `php artisan --help`.\n- Inspect required options and always pass `--no-interaction`.\n\n### Patterns\n\nUse static `make()` methods to initialize components. Most configuration methods accept a `Closure` for dynamic values.\n\nUse `Get $get` to read other form field values for conditional logic:\n\n<code-snippet name=\"Conditional form field\" lang=\"php\">\n    use Filament\\Forms\\Components\\Select;\n    use Filament\\Forms\\Components\\TextInput;\n    use Filament\\Schemas\\Components\\Utilities\\Get;\n    \n    Select::make('type')\n        ->options(CompanyType::class)\n        ->required()\n        ->live(),\n    \n    TextInput::make('company_name')\n        ->required()\n        ->visible(fn (Get $get): bool => $get('type') === 'business'),\n</code-snippet>\n\nUse `state()` with a `Closure` to compute derived column values:\n\n<code-snippet name=\"Computed table column\" lang=\"php\">\n    use Filament\\Tables\\Columns\\TextColumn;\n    \n    TextColumn::make('full_name')\n        ->state(fn (User $record): string => \"{$record->first_name} {$record->last_name}\"),\n</code-snippet>\n\nActions encapsulate a button with optional modal form and logic:\n\n<code-snippet name=\"Action with modal form\" lan"},"apps_instructions":false,"collaboration_mode":{"mode":"default","model":"gpt-6-luna","instructions":"afcdf1ff905fb4e61ff4d6d89af089dbda3d5e4e"},"context_window_guidance":"","environments":{"environments":{"local":{"cwd":"/home/kamaz/www/strateg-projects/strateg-export-products","status":"available","shell":"bash"}},"current_date":"2026-10-27","timezone":"Europe/Kyiv","filesystem":"<filesystem><workspace_roots><root>/home/kamaz/www/strateg-projects/strateg-export-products</root></workspace_roots><permission_profile type=\"managed\"><file_system type=\"restricted\"><entry access=\"read\"><special>:root</special></entry><entry access=\"write\"><path>/home/kamaz/www/strateg-projects/strateg-export-products</path></entry><entry access=\"write\"><special>:slash_tmp</special></entry><entry access=\"write\"><special>:tmpdir</special></entry><entry access=\"read\"><path>/home/kamaz/www/strateg-projects/strateg-export-products/.git</path></entry><entry access=\"read\"><path>/home/kamaz/www/strateg-projects/strateg-export-products/.agents</path></entry><entry access=\"read\"><path>/home/kamaz/www/strateg-projects/strateg-export-products/.codex</path></entry><entry access=\"read\"><path>/home/kamaz/www/strateg-projects/strateg-export-products/.aws</path></entry></file_system></permission_profile></filesystem>"},"environments_instructions":false,"git_attribution":false,"host_skills":{"body":"\n## Skills\nA skill is a set of local instructions to follow that is stored in a `SKILL.md` file. Below is the list of skills that can be used. Each entry includes a name, description, and a short path that can be expanded into an absolute path using the skill roots table.\n### Skill roots\n- `r0` = `/home/kamaz/.codex/skills`\n- `r1` = `/home/kamaz/.agents/skills`\n- `r2` = `/home/kamaz/.codex/skills/.system`\n- `r3` = `/home/kamaz/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-bundled`\n- `r4` = `/home/kamaz/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/google-drive/0.1.22/skills`\n- `r5` = `/home/kamaz/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote`\n- `r6` = `/home/kamaz/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/sites/1.1.2/skills`\n- `r7` = `/home/kamaz/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/work-pets/0.1.6/skills`\n- `r8` = `/home/kamaz/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-primary-runtime`\n- `r9` = `/home/kamaz/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-primary-runtime/spreadsheets/26.1025.11240/skills`\n- `r10` = `/home/kamaz/www/strateg-projects/strateg-export-products/.agents/skills`\n### Available skills\n- imagegen: Generate or edit raster images when the task benefits from AI-created bitmap visuals such as photos, illustrations, textures, sprites, mockups, or transparent-background cutouts. Use when Codex should create a brand-new image, tra (file: r2/imagegen/SKILL.md)\n- openai-docs: Use for Codex models/pricing, scheduled tasks, skills, settings, setup, troubleshooting, customization, automations, and self-knowledge—including 'you,' 'your,' 'this app,' or 'this coding agent' when they refer to Codex—and for (file: r2/openai-docs/SKILL.md)\n- skill-creator: Create or update a Codex skill with appropriately scoped instructions and any needed supporting resources. (file: r2/skill-creator/SKILL.md)\n- skill-installer: Install Codex skills into $CODEX_HOME/skills from a curated list or a GitHub repo path. Use when a user asks to list installable skills, install a curated skill, or install a skill from another repo (including private repos). (file: r2/skill-installer/SKILL.md)\n- infer-conventions: Use this skill to analyze how a Laravel application is actually written and record its conventions as shared rules. Trigger when the user wants to detect, infer, document, or standardize project conventions or coding style, set (file: r10/infer-conventions/SKILL.md)\n- laravel-backend: Build and maintain Laravel backend applications in PHP. Use for routes, controllers, requests, models, migrations, jobs, APIs, authentication, queues, and tests; do not use for Nuxt UI-only work. (file: r10/laravel-backend/SKILL.md)\n- laravel-backend-quality-suite: Specialized Laravel backend skill for services, models, APIs, queues, and business logic with strict quality and security controls. (file: r10/laravel-backend-quality-sui